('0e667365-9b3a-5bff-b312-35bbc131a763'::uuid,'91559d3e-3452-546c-ad4f-8b66c64cd08a'::uuid,'v1.0 introduction',E'![Manual of Operations cover](/moo-cover.webp)\n\n**INTRODUCTION**\n\nORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)\nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft\nSubject to institutional, operational, technical and legal validation*\n\n**Draft 1.0 | 12 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Document attribute** | **Control information** |\n|------------------------|-------------------------|\n| Document title         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Introduction |\n| Chapter title          | Introduction |\n| Document status        | Working draft for technical, institutional and legal validation |\n| Document owner         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office |\n| Technical custodian    | CDRRMO Head |\n| Reviewing offices      | City Legal Office; OCD Region VIII; City Planning and Development Office; City Budget Office; DILG City LGOO |\n| Version / date         | Draft 1.0 / 12 November 2026 |\n| Approval authority     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements |\n| Review cycle           | At least annually; after a significant emergency, exercise, material legal or technical change, or identified operational deficiency |\n\n## REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date** | **Description** | **Prepared / reviewed by** | **Approval** |\n|-------------|----------|-----------------|----------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0 | 12 Nov 2026 | Initial introduction: purpose, how the manual is organized (ten-chapter roadmap, five Parts, ten functional areas, four DRRM pillars), the assessment method stated once, document control and citation policy, and the manual''s relationship to the MODE platform. | Project drafting team | For review |\n\n# 1. Purpose of this Manual\n\nThis Manual of Operations (MOO) tells the City Government of Ormoc **who does what, when, on what authority, and to what standard** in disaster risk reduction and management (DRRM), before, during and after emergencies. It is the single controlled reference for the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC).\n\nThe manual exists to make the CDRRMO–EOC institution:\n\n- **Compliant** — every procedure traces to a legal or policy authority (RA 10121 and its IRR, RA 12287 and its IRR, NDRRMC issuances, and City ordinances and executive orders), and mandatory (M) indicators are separated from good-practice (GP) indicators so the City always knows what it *must* do and what it *should* do.\n- **Context-specific** — written for Ormoc''s actual hazards (typhoon, riverine and flash flood along the Anilao–Malbasag corridor, rain-induced landslide, storm surge, earthquake) and its **85 barangays** (PSA PSGC figure adopted by plebiscite on 8 October 2022; the City master list is being reconciled with CPDO, the City Secretary and the DILG City LGOO before the number is treated as final).\n- **Efficient** — built for a small office: the full institutional assessment takes about **five field days with a two-to-three person team**, and a short priority set of indicators carries most of the readiness gain (see §4.4).\n- **Evidence-based** — nothing is claimed without proof (see §4).\n- **Actionable** — every gap found by the assessment converts into a costed, owned, dated action in the manual''s action plan.\n\n# 2. How this Manual is Organized\n\n## 2.1 The ten-chapter roadmap\n\nThe full manual is compiled into **ten chapters**. Chapters 1–5 carry the drafted text to date; the remaining chapters are drafted next, drawing their requirements from the functional-area outlines in the MODE platform Library.\n\n| **Ch.** | **Title** | **Status (12 Nov 2026)** |\n|---------|-----------|--------------------------|\n| 0 | Introduction | Drafted (v1.0) — this chapter |\n| 1 | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability | Drafted (v1.2) |\n| 2 | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management | Drafted (v1.2) |\n| 3 | Risk Intelligence, DRRM and Contingency Planning, and Institutional Preparedness | Drafted (v1.1) |\n| 4 | Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response | Drafted (v1.1) |\n| 5 | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better | To be drafted |\n| 6 | DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability | To be drafted — a financing and procurement working draft exists and will be carried into this chapter at compilation |\n| 7 | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement | To be drafted |\n| 8 | Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships | To be drafted |\n| 9 | Public Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development | To be drafted |\n| 10 | Institutional Sustainability, Deep Resilience and Recognition | To be drafted |\n\nThe chapter order follows the life of an institution: its legal foundation (Ch. 1), its people (Ch. 2), its plans and risk knowledge (Ch. 3), its operations when hazards strike (Ch. 4), the return to normal and better (Ch. 5), the money and resources that make all of this possible (Ch. 6), the learning loop (Ch. 7), the external relationships that extend the City''s reach (Ch. 8), the resilience of communities themselves (Ch. 9), and the long-term sustainability and excellence agenda (Ch. 10).\n\nChapter numbers are assigned at compilation. Drafts keep their working numbers until the City adopts the full manual; where a working draft carries a different number (for example, the financing draft seeded as Chapter 5), it is renumbered without changing its content.\n\n## 2.2 Parts, functional areas and the four DRRM pillars\n\nUntil all ten chapters are drafted, the operational spine of the manual is the **ten functional areas** of the MODE platform Library, grouped into **five Parts** (ADR-012):\n\n| **Part** | **Functional areas** |\n|----------|----------------------|\n| 1. Legal mandate and governance | 1 Governance, Organization & Institutional Arrangements |\n| 2. SOPs by alert level | 3 Preparedness, Planning & Capacity Development; 4 EOC Activation, Incident Management & Coordination; 7 Resource, Logistics & Emergency Support Management; 8 Responder Safety, Welfare & Inclusive Emergency Operations |\n| 3. Information management and public communication | 2 Risk Intelligence, Prevention & Mitigation; 5 Early Warning, Emergency Communications & Public Information; 6 Situational Awareness, Information Management & GIS |\n| 4. Continuity and infrastructure safeguards | 9 Digital Continuity, Cybersecurity & Systems Resilience |\n| 5. Recovery and continuous improvement | 10 Recovery, Learning, Performance & Continuous Improvement |\n\nEach functional area belongs to exactly one of the **four DRRM thematic pillars**, which color-code the Library and the rest of the platform:\n\n- **Prevention and mitigation** — areas 2 (Risk Intelligence, Prevention & Mitigation) and 9 (Digital Continuity, Cybersecurity & Systems Resilience)\n- **Preparedness** — areas 1 (Governance, Organization & Institutional Arrangements) and 3 (Preparedness, Planning & Capacity Development)\n- **Response** — areas 4 (EOC Activation, Incident Management & Coordination), 5 (Early Warning, Emergency Communications & Public Information), 6 (Situational Awareness, Information Management & GIS), 7 (Resource, Logistics & Emergency Support Management) and 8 (Responder Safety, Welfare & Inclusive Emergency Operations)\n- **Recovery** — area 10 (Recovery, Learning, Performance & Continuous Improvement)\n\nEvery chapter, SOP, protocol, workflow, form and annex names the functional area it serves, so a reader can always move between the chapter view (how the manual reads) and the area view (how the institution works).\n\n# 3. Document Control and Citation Policy\n\n- The **approved manual is the legal document**. The MODE platform makes it easy to read, search and use; where the platform and the approved manual differ, the approved manual governs.\n- Versions move through **draft → in review → approved**, and only the **CDRRMO Head** approves a version for use. Approved versions are immutable; changes come as new versions.\n- Every SOP cites its authority in a control block. Where a rule is read from a **scanned or unofficial copy** of an issuance (for example, NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018), the reading is **provisional** until verified with OCD Region VIII and the City Legal Office, and the manual says so on the page. Parameters the law leaves open are stored as adjustable rules, not hard-coded guesses.\n- Citations on the platform render with the note: *the official text governs.*\n\n# 4. How We Assess Readiness: the Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT)\n\n**This section is stated once, here. No chapter repeats it; chapters apply it.**\n\n## 4.1 What the IAT measures\n\nThe **Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT) v1.7** measures the capacity of the CDRRMO (Part A), the EOC (Part B), and the CDRRMO–EOC interface (Part C):\n\n- **140 indicators** in **22 domains** — Part A: 82 indicators in 10 domains; Part B: 43 indicators in 7 domains; Part C: 15 interface indicators in 5 domains.\n- Each indicator is typed **M (mandatory)** or **GP (good practice)** and weighted 1–3 by criticality. Part C indicators have no M/GP type: they score the quality of the hand-off between the office and the center.\n- Every indicator maps to at least one **master requirement** (MR-01 to MR-18) in the Traceability and Data Dictionary, and to the manual sections, SOPs and forms that close its gap.\n\n## 4.2 Scoring rules (evidence-gated)\n\nEach indicator scores **0–4**: 0 = not present; 1 = initial; 2 = developing; 3 = established; 4 = advanced.\n\n- A score of **3 or 4 requires documentary proof** — the plan, EO, ordinance, MOA, log, report or form must exist and be shown.\n- **Interviews and workshops alone can never score above 2.** What people say is a lead, not evidence.\n- Blank barangay counts stay blank; a count that is not reported is not zero.\n\n## 4.3 From scores to action\n\nAssessment results feed, in one pass:\n\n- the **maturity index** per domain and part;\n- the **Institutional Maturity Index (IMI)** for the CDRRMO and EOC;\n- the **Interface Index** (Part C) for the CDRRMO–EOC hand-off;\n- **compliance rates** for M indicators (what the law requires) and GP indicators (what good practice expects);\n- the **gap priority** ranking; and\n- the **action plan** — each gap becomes an action with an owner office, a target quarter and a status, reviewed every cycle.\n\n## 4.4 The priority set (Pareto)\n\nA small set of indicators carries most of the readiness gain. The platform''s **Today dashboard** tracks this set live:\n\n- **Tier 1:** A1.1, A1.2, A2.2, A2.3, A4.1, A4.2 — legal mandate, council reconstitution, staffing and fund-access basics.\n- **Tier 2:** B1.1, B1.2, B1.3, A3.3, A6.1, A6.2, A6.3, B4.1, B4.2, A8.6, A8.8 — EOC establishment, alert levels, contingency planning, warning thresholds and anticipatory action.\n- **Tier 3:** A4.6, A10.4, A10.6, A10.8, A7.3, A1.8 — audit, M&E, recovery procedures and the State of Imminent Disaster workflow.\n\nA Tier 1 indicator is never left at 2 if the City can raise it to 3 with one ordinance, one executive order or one funded staffing item.\n\n# 5. The Manual and the MODE Platform\n\n**MODE** — the *Manual of Operations for the DRRMO and EOC* — is the mobile-first, offline-capable platform that hosts this manual and runs the institution around it:\n\n- **Assess** (IAT) — score the 140 indicators with the evidence gates of §4.2; results roll up to maturity indices and the action plan.\n- **Library** — this manual: chapters, annexes, SOPs, workflows and forms, with citations and the RefText legal catalogue.\n- **Protocols and Workflows** (DRRM) — the point-of-action checklists and multi-office cycles that carry out the SOPs during events and duty shifts.\n- **EOC** — activation levels, the EOC board, situational reports and anticipatory action under RA 12287.\n- **Tools** — the ICS simulator, the continuity (PSCP) workbench and the Gawad KALASAG self-check; all keep their data in the browser.\n\nThe platform follows **privacy by schema**: it never stores the names of evacuees, affected persons or informants — barangay reports are counts, consistent with the Data Privacy Act (RA 10173). Ten platform roles, enforced in the database, mirror the accountabilities in this manual.\n\n# 6. Reading Guide\n\n- **New to the manual?** Read this Introduction, then Chapter 1 (what the law requires) and Chapter 4 (what happens on event day).\n- **Looking for a procedure?** Search the Library by functional area or pillar color, or open the SOP registry — every protocol and form names the SOP it carries out and the indicators it closes.\n- **Validating a chapter?** Use the revision record at its head, check each SOP''s control block against its cited authority, and record the validation outcome in the platform''s Library workflow.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch0_Introduction_v1.0.md (Introduction, v1.0 introduction)','draft'),
('1fc69b3a-095b-54f9-9c9d-ea638e9a2f33'::uuid,'91559d3e-3452-546c-ad4f-8b66c64cd08a'::uuid,'v1.1 official working draft (assessment-informed method and systems diagnosis)',E'**MANUAL OF OPERATIONS**\n\nINTRODUCTION TO THE MANUAL\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)**\n\n**AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Purpose, legal basis, method, design principles and conventions for all ten chapters*\n\n\n**Draft 1.1 \\| 12 November 2026**\n\n\n# 1. Purpose of the Manual\n\nThis Manual of Operations (MOO) is one integrated manual for the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC) of the City Government of Ormoc. It sets out who decides, who acts, in what order, with which records and under which law, across normal operations, preparedness, early warning, anticipatory action, emergency response, recovery and continuous improvement.\n\nThe Manual is produced through a fourteen-week co-creation between the City Government of Ormoc and its consultant, Alvin M. Silva (ASilva Innovations). It is assessment-informed: each chapter answers gaps found by the Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT) and names the indicators it addresses. It is also operational: each standard operating procedure (SOP) is written so that it can be run as a checklist in the MODE platform and tested in an exercise.\n\nThree limits apply to the whole Manual:\n\n-   **It is not a source of legal power.** The Manual implements authority that already exists in law, ordinance and valid executive issuance. Where an authority is missing, the Manual proposes the instrument (for example, an EOC establishment EO) and labels it as a proposal.\n\n-   **Writing a procedure is not evidence of using it.** A procedure counts as institutionalised only when it is approved, used, recorded and reviewed. The IAT scores reflect this rule.\n\n-   **Every chapter remains a controlled working draft** until the owning office validates it and the CDRRMO Head approves it. Seeded content in MODE is never marked approved by the drafting team.\n\n# 2. The City and Its Risk Context\n\nOrmoc is an independent component city in the Province of Leyte, Eastern Visayas (Region VIII). Under the Philippine Standard Geographic Code (PSGC) the City (code 0803738000) has **85 barangays as of 31 July 2025**. The count follows City Ordinance No. 052, s. 2021 and the plebiscite of 8 October 2022, which merged the 28 numbered Poblacion districts into Barangays South (14), East (7) and West (7) and renamed District 29 as North (110 − 28 + 3 = 85). Plans prepared before the merger, including the CLUP 2017–2027 and parts of the CDRA, still use 110 barangays; chapters apply a 110-to-85 crosswalk to those sources. The CPDO, the City Secretary and the DILG City LGOO have still to sign off the City master list, so 85 is the working figure in controlled records.\n\nThe City is exposed to tropical cyclones and storm surge, riverine floods and flash floods (notably along the Anilao and Malbasag rivers), rain-induced and earthquake-induced landslides, earthquakes and liquefaction, tsunami, drought and El Niño, epidemics (including dengue), and industrial and geothermal incidents. Short-lead hazards such as flash floods and landslides usually fall below the lead time needed for a State of Imminent Disaster and are handled through city-level early action (Chapter 4). The 1991 flash flood remains the deadliest event on record; Super Typhoon Yolanda (2013), the magnitude 6.5 Leyte earthquake (2017) and Typhoon Odette (2021) shaped the current arrangements (Chapter 5, Table 5.3).\n\n**Table I.3**\n\n*Principal Offices for DRRM Governance in Ormoc (2025–2028 term)*\n\n| **Office**                                                                                                 | **Role in the Manual**                                                                                                                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **City Mayor (Lucy Torres-Gomez, 2025–2028)**                                                              | Local chief executive; chair of the CDRRMC; issues executive orders, including any State of Imminent Disaster EO.                                          |\n| **Vice Mayor and Sangguniang Panlungsod (presiding officer: Vice Mayor Leo Carmelo "Toto" L. Locsin Jr.)** | Enacts ordinances and appropriations; declares a State of Calamity on CDRRMC recommendation; approves the LDRRMF investment plan and the recovery program. |\n| **City DRRM Council (CDRRMC)**                                                                             | Statutory policy, coordination and oversight body, reorganised by EO No. 2024-032A.                                                                        |\n| **CDRRMO (Head: Ciriaco C. Tolibao)**                                                                      | Permanent technical and operational office, created by Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 as amended by Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015; secretariat of the CDRRMC.     |\n| **Emergency Operations Center**                                                                            | Activated coordination mechanism, housed with the CDRRMO OpCen; not a fourth CDRRMO division and not a field command.                                      |\n\n*Note.* Names are given for orientation only; the Manual assigns duties to positions, never to persons. The CDRRMO Head''s name is to be confirmed with HRMO before printing, as City sources show different middle initials.\n\n# 3. Legal and Policy Basis\n\nChapter 1, section 4, sets out the complete legal and policy framework, including the register of local issuances. Table I.4 lists the instruments that every chapter relies on and the facts about them that the Manual uses consistently. Each citation in the Manual and in MODE ends with the rule that the official text governs.\n\n**Table I.4**\n\n*Core Instruments and the Facts the Manual Uses Consistently*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                                 | **What the Manual relies on**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **RA No. 10121 (2010) and IRR**                                                                | CDRRMC (Sec. 11), CDRRMO (Sec. 12), State of Calamity (Sec. 16), LDRRMF of at least 5% of estimated revenue from regular sources, split 30% Quick Response Fund and 70% for prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response, rehabilitation and recovery (Sec. 21).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **RA No. 12287 (2025), Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act, and its IRR**            | Signed into law on 12 September 2025. The IRR was signed on 14 September 2026; its effectivity depends on publication and filing with the UP Office of the National Administrative Register. The lead-time rule is **three (3) days, extendable to five (5)**. AA funds may use up to 10% of the 70% LDRRMF component. Disbursement tranches (up to 70% within a three-day lead time; at most 30% beyond it) apply to hydrometeorological and geological hazards only. A declaration lifts automatically when the hazard occurs. Rule IV has ten sections and gives no count of actions; the MODE checklist derived from it is labelled as MODE-derived. |\n| **NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 (25 October 2018)**                                       | EOC establishment, activation triggers, five management functions, the rule that the EOC and the incident command post are not co-located, and LGU EOC funding chargeable to the LDRRMF. It lets each EOC set its own alert codes. It is a Memorandum, not a Memorandum Circular.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| **National Disaster Response Plan, as restated in RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006, s. 2023** | Source of the White, Blue and Red alert levels: White, normal operations; Blue, at least 50% of resources on standby; Red, all resources available.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| **DILG VIII Memorandum R08-LGCDD-2025-02-26-008 (26 February 2025)**                           | Harmonised disaster response reporting template and SOP: pre-disaster, during, post-disaster and terminal reports; LGU-to-Province cut-offs 6:00 AM and 6:00 PM at White, 9:00 AM and 7:00 PM at Blue and Red.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| **JMC No. 2014-1; JMC No. 2013-1; DBM LBC 124**                                                | LDRRMO structure and plantilla; LDRRMF programming and use.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| **COA Circular No. 2012-002**                                                                  | LDRRMF and Special Trust Fund accounting. The section on trust-fund transfers is cited inconsistently in secondary sources (4.4 or 5.1.10) and must be verified against the circular before it is quoted.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| **RA No. 10173; NPC Circular 16-03; NPC Circular 2023-06**                                     | Privacy by design; 72-hour breach notification (16-03, still in force); security measures (2023-06).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| **NDRRMC MC No. 3, s. 2025; OCD Memorandum No. 763, s. 2026 (corrected)**                      | Gawad KALASAG CY 2026: Gold 2.50 and above, Silver 2.00–2.49, Bronze 1.50–1.99.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 and No. 011, s. 2015; EO No. 2024-032A**                          | Local creation of the CDRRMO and current CDRRMC composition (Chapter 1).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. Legal readings taken from scanned copies remain at medium confidence until checked against certified copies by the City Legal Office and OCD Region VIII. Where a reading is contested, MODE keeps the value as a rule parameter with its status, and an ADR records whose reading it is.\n\n# 4. Structure of the Manual\n\nThe Manual has ten chapters, adopted on 12 November 2026. Chapters 1 to 3 lay the foundations: law and governance, the organisation and its people, and the risk knowledge and plans that preparedness rests on. Chapters 4 to 6 follow the operations cycle: warning and response, recovery, and the money and resources that pay for both. Chapters 7 to 9 make the system learn and reach outward: Chapter 8 deals with external relationships and partnerships, and Chapter 9 with building resilience in people, communities and institutions. Chapter 10 draws on all of them for long-term sustainability and excellence (Figure I.1).\n\n**Figure I.1**\n\n*Architecture of the Ten-Chapter Manual of Operations*\n\n![Figure 1: 4. Structure of the Manual](/library/introv11/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this Introduction from the chapter sequence adopted on 12 November 2026.\n\n**Table I.5**\n\n*Chapters, Assessment Anchors, Platform Home and Status*\n\n| **Ch.** | **Title**                                                                 | **Main IAT anchors**               | **MODE home**                                 | **Status (12 Nov 2026)**                      |\n|---------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability           | A1, A10.5, B1.1, Part C governance | Library, Vault                                | Draft 1.3 (this revision)                     |\n| 2       | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management   | A2, B4, B2 (facility)              | EOC module, roster, settings and roles        | Draft 1.3 (this revision)                     |\n| 3       | Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness                | A3, A5                             | Library, workflows WF-SOP3-01 to 09           | Draft 1.2 (this revision)                     |\n| 4       | Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response | A6, A7, A8, B1.2–B1.5, B5          | EOC and AA engine, SitRep forms, protocols    | Draft 1.2 (this revision)                     |\n| 5       | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                            | A9, A8.7, A10.6                    | Workflows and protocols (proposed)            | Draft 1.0 (12 Nov 2026)                       |\n| 6       | DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability                    | A4, A7.5                           | WF-BUDGET-CYCLE, AA fund calculator, Vault    | Drafted as "Chapter 5" v1.0; to be renumbered |\n| 7       | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement   | A10.3–A10.9, A5.4                  | IAT and analytics, action-plan feed           | To draft                                      |\n| 8       | Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships                     | A10.1–A10.2, Part C                | Part C crosswalk, liaison pages, EOC clusters | To draft                                      |\n| 9       | Public Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development           | A6.4–A6.6, A7, A2.6                | Training records, tools                       | To draft                                      |\n| 10      | Institutional Sustainability, Deep Resilience and Recognition             | A10.7, B3.3, B3.5                  | Public pages, GK self-check, Vault            | To draft                                      |\n\n*Note.* Anchors follow Part III of the Coherence Analysis (12 November 2026). Contractual functional modules, the ten functional areas (MR-02 to MR-11) and the chapter sequence are different levels of the same Manual, not competing structures.\n\n# 5. Assessment-informed Method and Systems Diagnosis\n\n## 5.1 The Institutional Assessment Tool\n\nThe Manual rests on the Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT), version 1.7 (workbook v1-7, 11 November 2026). It has 140 indicators in 22 domains: Part A, CDRRMO, 82 indicators in 10 domains; Part B, EOC, 43 indicators in 7 domains; and Part C, interface with other City offices and partners, 15 indicators in 5 domains. Parts A and B have 79 Mandatory (M) and 46 Good Practice (GP) indicators and form the Institutional Maturity Index (IMI). Part C has no Mandatory items, names a lead office other than the CDRRMO, and is reported as a separate Interface Index that is never added to the IMI.\n\n**Table I.6**\n\n*Scoring Rubric, Evidence Rule and Index Bands*\n\n| **Element**                         | **Rule**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n|-------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Rubric**                          | 0 Absent; 1 Initial (verbal only); 2 Developing (draft or partial); 3 Compliant (approved document and proof of use); 4 Advanced (level 3 plus a test, exercise or after-action review showing improvement); NA with written justification. |\n| **Evidence rule (Methodology 5.2)** | A score resting only on focus group discussion or interviews is capped at 2. Until validation, the lower score stands.                                                                                                                      |\n| **Index**                           | Sum of score × weight divided by the sum of weights of scored items. Weights: 3 life-safety or legal; 2 effectiveness; 1 supportive.                                                                                                        |\n| **Bands**                           | Below 0.5 Absent; 0.5–1.49 Initial; 1.5–2.49 Developing; 2.5–3.49 Compliant; 3.5 and above Advanced. The unrounded value is banded.                                                                                                         |\n| **Mandatory compliance**            | Scored Mandatory items at 3 or 4, divided by scored Mandatory items. A domain is "Compliant" only at 100%.                                                                                                                                  |\n| **Gap priority**                    | (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 for Mandatory items, × 1.0 for Good Practice and all Part C items. High 9 and above; Medium 4 to 8.9; Low below 4.                                                                                               |\n\n## 5.2 Methods and Evidence\n\nThe October–November 2026 assessment used four methods. Informants are identified by office only, per RA 10173.\n\n**Table I.7**\n\n*Assessment Methods Used in October–November 2026*\n\n| **Method**                      | **Coverage**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | **Main use**                                                                         |\n|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Focus group discussion**      | CDRRMO Head and staff at the CDRRM Office, which houses the OpCen and the EOC; indicators scored together on the IAT workbook.                                                                                                                     | Baseline scores, later capped by the evidence rule where no document supported them. |\n| **Key informant interviews**    | CDRRMO Chiefs of Operations, Research and Planning, and Administration; CPDO; CSWDO; City Administrator''s Office.                                                                                                                                  | Roles, interfaces and decision rights.                                               |\n| **Observation and site visits** | EOC; Operations and Rescue Office; Barangay Liloan (evacuation site, protocol and camp management).                                                                                                                                                | EOC status, duty system, evacuation and camp practice.                               |\n| **Document review**             | DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032, CDP 2024–2030, LCCAP 2019–2030, CDRA, CLUP 2017–2027; City issuances 2016–2025 (ordinances, executive orders, CDRRMC and SP resolutions, registered in the MODE Vault); CDRRMO organisational chart and staff papers. | Legal basis, Council practice, declarations and funding decisions.                   |\n\n## 5.3 Baseline Results\n\nWith the evidence rule applied, the baseline Institutional Maturity Index is **1.98 (Developing)**: 2.06 for the CDRRMO (Part A) and 1.85 for the EOC (Part B). Mandatory compliance is **32.9%** (26 of 79 Mandatory items at 3 or 4). The Interface Index for Part C is 1.41 (Initial). Figure I.2 shows the domain indices. All rows remain "Pending validation" until the 1–4 December 2026 workshop.\n\n**Figure I.2**\n\n*Domain Maturity Indices, IAT v1.7 Baseline*\n\n![Figure 2: 5.3 Baseline Results](/library/introv11/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Computed from the A_CDRRMO and B_EOC sheets of IAT v1.7 with the workbook formula. Dashed lines are the band thresholds. A9 (recovery), B4 (EOC organisation and staffing), B5 (information management) and B6 (coordination and resources) are the weakest domains.\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The City''s statement of design principles describes "108 indicators in 17 domains", while the controlled workbook (v1-7) has 140 indicators in 22 domains. Until the City decides which instrument is controlled, the Manual uses the workbook in the repository (140/22). The 108-indicator Google Sheet is IAT v1.0, which the Vault records as superseded.\n\n## 5.4 Systems Diagnosis of the CDRRMO and EOC\n\nThe baseline scores describe what is present or missing. A systems reading of the same evidence explains why the gaps persist and where a change in the Manual will have the largest effect. This section applies the iceberg model, causal loop diagrams and system archetypes to the IAT v1.7 results. It uses only the controlled figures in Table I.8; it is an interpretation for validation, not an additional score.\n\n**Table I.8**\n\n*Baseline Figures Used in the Systems Diagnosis*\n\n| **Measure (IAT v1.7)**                           | **Value**                      | **Reading**                                                |\n|--------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Institutional Maturity Index (Parts A and B)** | 1.98                           | Developing                                                 |\n| **Part A, CDRRMO / Part B, EOC**                 | 2.06 / 1.85                    | Both Developing; the EOC trails the office                 |\n| **Mandatory compliance**                         | 32.9% (26 of 79)               | Two in three legal or life-safety items not yet at level 3 |\n| **A1 Legal Mandate and Governance**              | 2.47; 4 of 6 M items compliant | Strongest governance base                                  |\n| **B1 EOC Legal Basis and ConOps**                | 2.23; 1 of 5 M items compliant | EOC rests on practice, not on an issuance                  |\n| **Weakest domains**                              | B4 1.31; A9 1.38; B6 1.38      | EOC staffing, recovery, coordination and resources         |\n| **Interface Index (Part C)**                     | 1.41                           | Initial; reported separately, never added to the IMI       |\n| **High-priority gaps**                           | 18 Part A, 12 Part B, 2 Part C | Gap priority ≥ 9                                           |\n\n*Note.* Computed from IAT v1.7 with the workbook formulas and the evidence rule (FGD-only scores capped at 2). Earlier figures (IMI 2.13; Mandatory compliance 41.8%) came from v1-5 and v1-6 before the evidence rule was applied and are superseded.\n\n### The Iceberg Model\n\nThe iceberg model separates what people see during an event from the patterns, structures and mental models below it (Figure I.3). The visible events are familiar: activation decided case by case, Council resolutions adopted for each event, staff stretched across OpCen, field and EOC duties, and late or uneven situation reports. Below the waterline, the evidence points to four structures.\n\n-   The CDRRMO is an office under the Office of the City Mayor, created by Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (amended by Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015) with a Head (SG-23) and three LDRRMO I posts. Most of its workforce (about 47 staff, of whom four are permanent, as reported at the FGD) is on job-order or casual terms (A1.1 and A2.3, each scored 2).\n\n-   No executive order establishes the EOC, its levels, its activation authority or its staffing (B1.1, scored 2).\n\n-   The boundary between incident command (ICS) and the EOC is not written down, so the same people play both roles (B6.1, scored 1).\n\n-   Standing rules exist, such as Ordinance No. 003, s. 2013 on forced evacuation and Ordinance No. 135, s. 2023, yet decisions are still taken through event-by-event resolutions rather than through procedures that cite them.\n\nThe mental model underneath is that disaster management is response: capability is shown by how hard the office works during an event, not by the rules, rosters and records it keeps between events.\n\n**Figure I.3**\n\n*Iceberg Model of the CDRRMO and EOC Baseline*\n\n![Figure 3: The Iceberg Model](/library/introv11/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this Introduction from the IAT v1.7 baseline, the focus group discussion and the register of City issuances. Indicator codes refer to IAT v1.7.\n\n### Causal Loop: Shifting the Burden\n\nFigure I.4 shows the dominant dynamic. Operational pressure from each event is relieved quickly by ad-hoc fixes: borrowed staff, a new resolution, an improvised EOC set-up (balancing loop B1). Fundamental solutions, namely plantilla positions, an EOC executive order and approved SOPs, also relieve the pressure but only after a delay (balancing loop B2). Each successful ad-hoc fix increases reliance on ad-hoc fixes and reduces the attention and resources given to the fundamental solutions (reinforcing loop R1). Over time the office becomes better at improvising and no better at institutionalising.\n\n**Figure I.4**\n\n*Shifting the Burden: Ad-hoc Fixes Versus Fundamental Solutions*\n\n![Figure 4: Causal Loop: Shifting the Burden](/library/introv11/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Causal loop diagram. "+" means the variables move in the same direction; "−" means opposite directions; the double bar marks a delay. B = balancing loop; R = reinforcing loop.\n\n### Causal Loop: ICS–EOC Mandate Conflation\n\nFigure I.5 shows a second, reinforcing loop (R2). Without a codified boundary, the EOC is drawn into field command; the more it commands, the less it coordinates information and resources; incidents then need more direct intervention, which pulls the EOC further into command. The loop is broken by writing the boundary down: the Incident Commander keeps incident-level command, and the EOC supports coordination, information, resources and executive decisions (SOP 4-05; indicator B6.1).\n\n**Figure I.5**\n\n*ICS–EOC Mandate Conflation and the Codified Boundary*\n\n![Figure 5: Causal Loop: ICS–EOC Mandate Conflation](/library/introv11/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Causal loop diagram. The codified boundary (SOP 4-05, B6.1) is the intervention that weakens loop R2.\n\n### System Archetypes\n\n**Table I.9**\n\n*System Archetypes Observed in the Baseline*\n\n| **Archetype**                  | **Evidence in the baseline**                                                                                                                                                                      | **Response in the Manual**                                                                                                                                                          |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Shifting the Burden**        | Event-by-event resolutions and borrowed staff relieve pressure; plantilla, the EOC executive order and approved SOPs remain drafts (A1.1, A2.3, B1.1).                                            | Amend Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (Annex 1-A); EOC executive order (Annex 1-C); SOPs that cite standing ordinances (Chapter 4).                                                      |\n| **Growth and Underinvestment** | Mandates and expectations grow (RA 12287, Gawad KALASAG, new ordinances on CESU, volunteers and BHERTs) while staffing, the EOC facility and information systems lag (B4 1.31, B5 1.47, B6 1.38). | Staffing plan and three-deep EOC roster (Chapter 2); EOC information management and reporting (Chapter 4); financing linked to the gaps (Chapter 6).                                |\n| **Drifting Goals**             | With 32.9% Mandatory compliance, the working standard risks settling at "we responded" rather than "we met the standard".                                                                         | Fixed standards: level 3 needs an approved document and proof of use; Mandatory gaps tracked to closure through the corrective action register (Chapter 1, section 9.5; Chapter 7). |\n\n*Note.* Archetypes after Senge (1990) and Meadows (2008), applied to IAT v1.7 evidence. These are interpretations for the validation workshop.\n\n## 5.5 Leverage Points and Master Requirements\n\nMeadows (1999) ranks places to intervene in a system from weak (parameters) to strong (paradigms). The Manual works at the stronger levels: it changes rules, boundaries, information flows and feedback, not only budgets and numbers. Table I.10 links each level to the Manual, the master requirements (MR-01 to MR-18 in the Traceability Data Dictionary v1.2) and the MODE mechanism that keeps the change in place. The 18 master requirements trace to the 140 IAT indicators; they are not separate indicators.\n\n**Table I.10**\n\n*Leverage Points, Manual Actions and Master Requirements*\n\n| **Leverage level**           | **System element**                                                                                            | **Manual action**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | **Master req.**                         | **MODE mechanism**                                                      |\n|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Paradigm and goals**       | From "disaster management is response" to "risk is governed through approved, tested and improved procedures" | One integrated Manual; Build Back Better (Chapter 5); TQM and PDCA (Chapter 1, section 9; Chapter 7)                                                                                                                                                  | MR-01, MR-11, MR-18                     | Manual versioning; Sendai and Ten Essentials crosswalk                  |\n| **Rules**                    | Legal basis of the office and the EOC                                                                         | Amendment of Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (Annex 1-A); EOC executive order (Annex 1-C); Part C lead-office rules                                                                                                                                        | MR-02, MR-15, MR-17                     | Vault register of issuances; role-based access enforced in the database |\n| **Boundaries and structure** | ICS–EOC boundary; resource authority                                                                          | SOP 4-05; resource requests tracked as requested, approved, mobilised, delivered and demobilised                                                                                                                                                      | MR-05, MR-08                            | EOC resource request workflow; locked decisions                         |\n| **Information flows**        | Warning, decision and anticipatory-action information                                                         | SOP GOV-05 (Annex 1-E) for RA 12287: lead time of three days, extendable to five; tranches up to 70% within three days and up to 30% beyond, hydrometeorological and geological hazards only. Warning-action matrix citing City ordinances (SOP 4-02) | MR-03, MR-06, MR-07, MR-16              | Anticipatory-action rules table; logbook; SitRep templates              |\n| **Feedback and controls**    | Accountability, evidence and correction                                                                       | RACI in every chapter; corrective action register (Chapter 1, section 9.5; Chapter 7); evidence gate                                                                                                                                                  | MR-12, MR-13, MR-14 (with MR-09, MR-10) | Scoring engine in SQL; evidence cap; v_traceability view                |\n\n*Note.* Leverage levels after Meadows (1999). Master requirements from the Master Requirements Traceability and 140-Indicator Data Dictionary v1.2. RA 12287 parameters as stated in its IRR (signed 14 September 2026); the official text governs.\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The systems diagnosis interprets baseline evidence that is still "Pending validation". If the December 2026 workshop changes indicator scores, the figures in this section and the order of leverage points will be updated in the next version of this Introduction.\n\n# 6. Design Principles\n\nThe City set five design principles for the assessment and the Manual. Table I.11 states what each demands and how the chapters meet it.\n\n**Table I.11**\n\n*Design Principles and Their Application Across the Manual*\n\n| **Principle**        | **What it demands**                                                                            | **How the Manual applies it**                                                                                                                                             |\n|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Compliant**        | Every requirement cites its legal or policy source; Mandatory is separated from Good Practice. | Each SOP control block names its policy basis and legal decision authority; indicator tables show M or GP.                                                                |\n| **Context-specific** | Ormoc''s hazards, barangays, offices and language.                                              | 85 PSGC barangays; local issuances register (Chapter 1); short-lead hazards routed to city-level early action; plain language suitable for Cebuano and Waray translation. |\n| **Efficient**        | An instrument sized for the field; one form closes each step.                                  | Every protocol or workflow step closes on a named form; registers are reused across chapters.                                                                             |\n| **Evidence-based**   | A score of 3 or 4 needs documentary or observed proof.                                         | Validation notes mark provisional statements; records of use are listed for every SOP.                                                                                    |\n| **Actionable**       | Scores convert to prioritised gaps and an action plan.                                         | Each chapter has an implementation priorities table traced to the IAT Action Plan; the Pareto priorities in section 9 order the work.                                     |\n\n# 7. Systems Thinking, Quality Management and Coherence\n\nThe Manual treats disaster risk as the result of interconnected hazards, exposure, vulnerability, capacities and dependencies. Its offices, councils and procedures are therefore written as one system with feedback loops rather than as separate units. Total Quality Management supplies the operating discipline: every process has an owner; decisions rest on evidence; procedures are standardised and measured; and corrective action closes the Plan–Do–Check–Act cycle. Each chapter states only the systems and quality principles that are specific to its subject; the general approach is set here once.\n\n**Figure I.6**\n\n*How the Assessment, the Manual and the MODE Platform Work Together*\n\n![Figure 6: 7. Systems Thinking, Quality Management and Coherence](/library/introv11/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this Introduction from the MODE system design (docs/system-design.md, section 1).\n\nTo keep the Manual, the IAT and the platform coherent, every statement a user sees must pass five tests:\n\n**Table I.12**\n\n*The Five Coherence Tests*\n\n| **Test**      | **Meaning**                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|---------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Traceable** | It links to a master requirement (MR-01 to MR-18), IAT indicators and a legal provision.                                                                                    |\n| **Cited**     | Its source exists and says what the statement says.                                                                                                                         |\n| **Singular**  | One barangay denominator (85), one indicator set, one source for alert colours and one set of Gawad KALASAG tiers, with no parallel versions in chapters, seeds or screens. |\n| **Enforced**  | In MODE, the rule sits in the database with access controls; screens mirror it and are tested against it.                                                                   |\n| **Versioned** | Approved versions are never edited; corrections are made through a superseding version or assessment cycle.                                                                 |\n\n# 8. Institutional Invariants\n\nThe following rules hold in every chapter. A chapter may add detail but may not contradict them.\n\n1.  The CDRRMC is the statutory policy, coordination and oversight body; the CDRRMO is the permanent technical and operational office and the CDRRMC secretariat.\n\n2.  The EOC is an activated coordination mechanism. It is not a fourth CDRRMO division and not a field command. The Incident Commander and the incident management team keep incident-level command; the EOC supports coordination, information, resources and executive decisions.\n\n3.  EOC activation does not declare a State of Calamity (RA 10121, by the Sanggunian on CDRRMC recommendation) or a State of Imminent Disaster (RA 12287, by the Mayor''s EO on the prescribed recommendation). The three paths stay distinct.\n\n4.  Sector offices, agencies, utilities and barangays keep their own mandates. A RACI allocates work; it does not transfer legal authority.\n\n5.  The Manual creates no independent power to order evacuation, declare no-build zones, spend funds or procure. It follows the authority in law and valid City issuances.\n\n6.  Rainfall, wind, flood, tsunami and landslide thresholds are never invented. They come from the official products of PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and MGB and from City-approved contingency plans, with their uncertainty recorded.\n\n7.  Duties are assigned to positions, never to persons.\n\n8.  No general-purpose record holds the names of evacuees, patients or informants. Counts are aggregates by barangay, a blank means "not reported", never zero, and counts are scoped to the current event.\n\n# 9. Priority Indicators\n\nNot every gap has the same leverage. An indicator is a priority when it passes three tests: it cascades (achieving it lifts other indicators), the CDRRMO controls it, and it can move within one budget or EOC cycle. Table I.13 lists the "vital few" by IAT v1.7 identifier. At the December 2026 workshop they are presented in tier order, Mandatory compliance first.\n\n**Table I.13**\n\n*Priority Indicators by Tier*\n\n| **Tier**                     | **Indicators**                                           | **Why they come first**                                                                                                                                                                        |\n|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Legal constitution       | A1.1; A1.2; A2.2 and A2.3; A4.1 and A4.2                 | CDRRMO created as a regular office; CDRRMC complete; permanent Head and minimum JMC 2014-1 positions; LDRRMF at 5% with the 70/30 split. Without these, most other indicators cannot score.    |\n| 2\\. Operating systems        | B1.1–B1.3; A3.3; A6.1–A6.3; B4.1 and B4.2; A8.6 and A8.8 | EOC established by EO with levels and activation procedures; hazard-specific contingency plans; working early warning; EOC structure staffed two deep; PDRA and anticipatory-action protocols. |\n| 3\\. High-leverage compliance | A4.6; A10.4; A10.6; A10.8; A7.3; A1.8                    | AA funding pathway; LDRRMP M&E framework; after-action reviews; the 45-day post-lifting report; BDRRMC functionality across the 85 barangays; State of Imminent Disaster procedure.            |\n\n*Note.* From Part II, section II.3 of the Coherence Analysis. The workbook will carry a priority flag for these identifiers so that the Action Plan ranks gaps by leverage, control and immediacy rather than by deficit alone.\n\n# 10. The Manual and the MODE Platform\n\nMODE, the Manual of Operations for the DRRMO and EOC Development Platform, supports the Manual but is not the Manual. The approved Manual remains the legal document; MODE holds its current text, version history and the evidence that it is used. MODE does four jobs: it **assesses** the office and the EOC with the IAT; it **writes and traces** the Manual, linking every section to its indicators and master requirements; it **runs** SOPs as protocols and workflows with owners, timings and an audit trail; and it **operates** the EOC and the RA 12287 anticipatory-action process.\n\n**Table I.14**\n\n*MODE Design Rules That the Manual Relies On*\n\n| **Rule**                          | **What it means for users of the Manual**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **City ownership**                | The City owns the code and the data. The production database is a City Supabase project in Tokyo (ap-northeast-1); the privacy policy names the transfer.                                                                                                                     |\n| **Local-first**                   | Field staff can capture logbook entries, forms and barangay figures without a connection; they sync when the device is back online.                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Authority actions online only** | Changing the EOC level, approvals, declaration steps, disbursements and role grants need a connection and are never queued.                                                                                                                                                   |\n| **Ten roles**                     | CDRRMO Head, EOC Manager, EOC staff, duty officer, assessor, validator, lead office focal person, author, viewer and admin, enforced in the database. Only the CDRRMO Head approves, retires or reopens Manual versions and protocols; a draft protocol runs only as a drill. |\n| **Immutability**                  | Approved versions, validated responses, approved SitReps, decided resource requests, declarations and disbursements are locked. The logbook is append-only; corrections are new entries. Vault documents are superseded, never deleted.                                       |\n| **Vocabulary (ADR-019)**          | **SOP** is controlled Manual text. A **protocol** is a point-of-action checklist. A **workflow** is a multi-office management cycle. A **form** is the record that closes a step. A template is never called an SOP.                                                          |\n| **Citations**                     | Every cited law or guideline opens a short note that ends "The official text governs."                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n\n*Note.* Summarised from docs/system-design.md and docs/design-system.md in the MODE repository (version 1.16.0).\n\n# 11. Document Conventions\n\n## 11.1 Standard Chapter Template\n\nEvery chapter follows the same order so that readers find the same things in the same place: cover; document control; revision and approval record; introduction and assessment findings for the chapter; purpose and intended results; scope, application and institutional boundaries; legal, policy and operational framework (chapter-specific instruments only; the general framework is here and in Chapter 1); chapter-specific systems and quality principles; the operational sections; standard operating procedures with control blocks and step tables; RACI; performance monitoring and corrective action; implementation priorities; forms, registers and annexes; relationship with other chapters; conclusion; references; validation and source control register; annexes.\n\n## 11.2 Numbering, Captions and Notes\n\n**Table I.15**\n\n*Numbering and Presentation Conventions*\n\n| **Element**                  | **Convention**                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n|------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Tables and figures**       | Numbered by chapter (Table 4.12, Figure 5.3; Table I.4 in this Introduction). APA style: the number in bold on one line, the title in italic title case below it, and a "Note." under the table or figure giving its source. |\n| **SOPs**                     | SOP chapter-sequence with two digits (SOP 3-06, SOP 4-01, SOP 5-07). Chapters 1 and 2 keep their thematic series (GOV-01 to GOV-08; HR-01 to HR-08), which MODE already uses.                                                |\n| **SOP step tables**          | Each step states its actor, action, decision rule, inputs, outputs and records, timing, control and escalation.                                                                                                              |\n| **Forms and annexes**        | Forms carry a chapter or thematic prefix (EWR-F01, REC-F06); where MODE already had a form, its MODE code is kept and both codes are shown. Annexes are lettered by chapter (Annex 4-C).                                     |\n| **Validation notes**         | Boxed text beginning "VALIDATION NOTE." marks a statement that is provisional or needs a named reviewer. Each one is also listed in the chapter''s validation and source control register.                                    |\n| **Dates and times**          | Day, month, year ("12 November 2026"; "15 Oct 2026, 14:05" in tables), Manila time.                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Barangay counts and data** | PSGC barangays (85); aggregates only; blank means not reported.                                                                                                                                                              |\n\n## 11.3 Typography and Colour\n\nThe chapters use the three type families of the MODE design system, with Roboto added for table text: chapter titles in Montserrat Bold (the standard, "classic" cut; there is no separate font named Montserrat Classic); subtitles and section titles in Poppins Bold; subsection titles in Roboto Bold; body text in Roboto Condensed with single line spacing; table text in Roboto 10 pt. Colours follow the design-system tokens: navy (#0F3D66) for titles and figure outlines, light blue (#EEF5FB) and gray-blue (#E3EBF2) for fills, and orange (#D66812) only to mark what is active or to draw attention, never to show a status. The White, Blue and Red level colours are used only where an EOC level is meant.\n\n# 12. Pending National Issuances\n\nSeveral instruments that will bind the City have not yet been issued. They are tracked as parameters with a status, not as settled rules.\n\n**Table I.16**\n\n*National Issuances Pending as of 12 November 2026*\n\n| **Pending issuance**                                                                                                        | **Expected from**                         | **Chapters affected** |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----------------------|\n| Operational guidelines on triggers, thresholds and lead times, including drought, under RA 12287                            | NDRRMC                                    | Chapters 1, 3 and 4   |\n| Funding guidelines for anticipatory action                                                                                  | DBM and COA                               | Chapters 4 and 6      |\n| Anticipatory-action monitoring and evaluation framework (due about 13 December 2026)                                        | NDRRMC / OCD                              | Chapters 4 and 7      |\n| Issuance creating the National Anticipatory Action Technical Working Group                                                  | NDRRMC                                    | Chapters 1 and 8      |\n| Emergency procurement for anticipatory action (existing: GPPB Resolution No. 17-2026, Circular No. 08-2026, 14 August 2026) | GPPB (in force); further rules may follow | Chapter 6             |\n\n# 13. Corrections Applied Across Chapters 1 to 4\n\nThis revision applies the factual corrections of the Coherence Analysis to Chapters 1 to 4 so that all chapters state the same facts. Reviewers should check them first.\n\n**Table I.17**\n\n*Corrections Applied in Chapters 1 to 4 (12 November 2026)*\n\n| **Subject**        | **Earlier text**                                                                              | **Text now used**                                                                                                                                   |\n|--------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Chapter structure  | Nine-chapter plan; financing as Chapter 5; recovery as Chapter 8; EOC operations as Chapter 6 | Ten chapters as in Table I.5; every cross-reference updated                                                                                         |\n| Chapter titles     | Ch.3 "…DRRM and Contingency Planning…"; Ch.4 "…Activation…"                                   | Ch.3 "Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness"; Ch.4 "Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response" |\n| NDRRMC Memo 131    | "Memorandum Circular No. 131"                                                                 | "Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018"; alert colours attributed to the NDRP as restated in RDRRMC VIII Res. No. 12-006, s. 2023                             |\n| RA 12287 lead time | "72 hours"                                                                                    | "three (3) days, extendable to five (5)"                                                                                                            |\n| IRR of RA 12287    | "released on 29 September 2026"                                                               | signed 14 September 2026; effective on publication and ONAR filing                                                                                  |\n| PSGC reference     | "PSGC (fourth quarter 2022)"                                                                  | "PSA PSGC as of 31 July 2025"                                                                                                                       |\n| Local issuances    | Being compiled; 48 issuances (2021–2024)                                                      | Full register of local DRRM issuances 1993–2025 (Chapter 1, section 4.5 and Annex 1-I)                                                              |\n| SOP numbering      | Ch.3 "SOP 3-1"                                                                                | "SOP 3-01"                                                                                                                                          |\n| Introductions      | General method and approach repeated in each chapter                                          | Stated once in this Introduction; chapters open with their own findings                                                                             |\n\n# 14. Abbreviations\n\n**Table I.18**\n\n*Abbreviations Used Across the Manual*\n\n| **Abbreviation**    | **Meaning**                                                                 |\n|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **AA**              | Anticipatory action                                                         |\n| **AAR**             | After-action review                                                         |\n| **ACDV**            | Accredited community disaster volunteer                                     |\n| **ADR**             | Architecture decision record (MODE)                                         |\n| **BDRRMC**          | Barangay Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Committee                   |\n| **CCCM**            | Camp coordination and camp management                                       |\n| **CDRA**            | Climate and disaster risk assessment                                        |\n| **CDRRMC / CDRRMO** | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council / Office                |\n| **CLUP**            | Comprehensive Land Use Plan                                                 |\n| **CPDO**            | City Planning and Development Office                                        |\n| **CSWDO**           | City Social Welfare and Development Office                                  |\n| **DEPDev**          | Department of Economy, Planning, and Development (formerly NEDA)            |\n| **EO**              | Executive order                                                             |\n| **EOC**             | Emergency Operations Center                                                 |\n| **GP / M**          | Good Practice / Mandatory indicator                                         |\n| **IAT**             | Institutional Assessment Tool                                               |\n| **ICS / IMT**       | Incident Command System / Incident management team                          |\n| **IMI**             | Institutional Maturity Index                                                |\n| **LDRRMF**          | Local DRRM Fund                                                             |\n| **MODE**            | Manual of Operations for the DRRMO and EOC Development Platform             |\n| **MOO**             | Manual of Operations                                                        |\n| **MR**              | Master requirement (MR-01 to MR-18)                                         |\n| **NDRP**            | National Disaster Response Plan                                             |\n| **OpCen**           | CDRRMO Operations Center                                                    |\n| **PDNA / RDANA**    | Post-disaster needs assessment / Rapid damage assessment and needs analysis |\n| **PDRA**            | Pre-disaster risk assessment                                                |\n| **PSCP**            | Public service continuity plan                                              |\n| **PSGC**            | Philippine Standard Geographic Code                                         |\n| **QRF**             | Quick Response Fund                                                         |\n| **RACI**            | Responsible, accountable, consulted, informed                               |\n| **SitRep**          | Situation report                                                            |\n| **SOP**             | Standard operating procedure                                                |\n| **SP**              | Sangguniang Panlungsod                                                      |\n\n# References\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2023). *Ormoc City disaster risk reduction and management ten-year plan (2023–2032)*.\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026a). *Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC institutional assessment tool* (Version 1.7) \\[Unpublished assessment workbook\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026b). *Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC master requirements traceability and 140-indicator data dictionary* (Version 1.2) \\[Unpublished project reference workbook\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026c). *MODE platform coherence analysis, Pareto priority indicators, and final 10-chapter MOO/EOC manual structure* \\[Unpublished project document\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026d). *MODE system design* and *MODE design system* \\[Repository documents, version 1.16.0\\]. https://github.com/asilvainnovations/ormoc-drrmo\n\nDepartment of the Interior and Local Government Region VIII. (2025, February 26). *Memorandum R08-LGCDD-2025-02-26-008: Adoption of the disaster response reporting template and SOP (RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006, s. 2023)*.\n\nMeadows, D. H. (1999). *Leverage points: Places to intervene in a system*. The Sustainability Institute.\n\nMeadows, D. H. (2008). *Thinking in systems: A primer* (D. Wright, Ed.). Chelsea Green.\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2018). *Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018: Guidelines on the establishment, operationalization and management of emergency operations centers*.\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2026). *Implementing rules and regulations of Republic Act No. 12287*.\n\nOffice of Civil Defense. (2026). *Memorandum No. 763, s. 2026: Gawad KALASAG CY 2026 guidelines (corrected)*.\n\nSenge, P. M. (1990). *The fifth discipline: The art and practice of the learning organization*. Doubleday.\n\nRepublic Act No. 10121. (2010). *Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010*.\n\nRepublic Act No. 10173. (2012). *Data Privacy Act of 2012*.\n\nRepublic Act No. 12287. (2025). *Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act*.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch0_Introduction_v1.1.md (Introduction, v1.1 official working draft (assessment-informed method and systems diagnosis))','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('e7b57d96-7f02-5c8f-83b7-f805c1f49cca'::uuid,'c906fb73-66a3-5477-8732-349b15f6e5c4'::uuid,'v1.0 revised working draft',E'**CHAPTER 1**\n\n**LEGAL MANDATE, DRRM GOVERNANCE AND INSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY**\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO) AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Revised working draft. Assessment-based. Subject to institutional and legal validation.*\n\n> **Reviewer note (platform, 2 Nov 2026).** Section 1 still quotes the workbook Dashboard as last saved: combined index 2.18, mandatory compliance 39.4%, 30 High gaps (A1 2.59 and 50%; B1 1.85 and 0%). Those cached values were not recalculated after the 30 Oct edits. Recalculating from the workbook''s own scores gives Part A 2.35, Part B 1.47, Part C 1.26, combined 2.04, mandatory compliance 34.2% and 37 High gaps. IAT v1.3 (Drive copy) changes only the Profile entry "Sites inspected: Done"; if inspections were done, re-score the OBS/TST items, recalculate, and then update these figures. The Assess module always shows the live result.\n\n# 1. Introduction\n\nDisaster risk reduction and management (DRRM) in Ormoc City requires an integrated institutional system that connects executive leadership, policy direction, technical management, operational coordination and community-level action. Its effectiveness depends not only on the existence of the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council (CDRRMC), the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC), but also on the clarity of their respective mandates, decision-making authority, functional relationships, accountability arrangements and operational interfaces.\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, governance and institutional accountability framework for the City''s CDRRMO and EOC. It defines how the City Government organizes, directs, coordinates, monitors and continuously improves its DRRM functions across normal operations, preparedness, anticipatory action, emergency response and recovery.\n\nThe chapter adopts a systems-thinking perspective: disaster risk is understood as the consequence of interconnected hazards, exposure, vulnerability, institutional capacities and dependencies across social, economic, environmental and physical systems. Accordingly, the City''s DRRM governance arrangements must operate as an integrated system rather than as a collection of independent offices, committees and emergency procedures.\n\nThe chapter also applies Total Quality Management (TQM) principles to institutional operations. These include process ownership, evidence-based decision-making, stakeholder participation, standardization, performance measurement, corrective action and continuous improvement.\n\nThe institutional provisions are informed by the Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.3 (the **Assess** module of this platform), particularly its nine assessment sheets.\n\nThe assessment is treated as a diagnostic baseline, not as a substitute for legal compliance verification or formal institutional approval.\n\n## 1.1 Assessment-Informed Governance Priorities\n\nThe October 2026 assessment identifies a significant distinction between the maturity of the CDRRMO, the EOC and their institutional interface.\n\nInstitutional Maturity Index\n\nThe results demonstrate that the CDRRMO has a stronger institutional foundation than the EOC, while the interface between the two remains the weakest component. This is a critical systems issue: the existence of functioning CDRRMO activities does not automatically translate into a coordinated, documented and reliable EOC operating system.\n\nThe dashboard identifies 30 high-priority gaps: 19 in the CDRRMO assessment and 11 in the EOC assessment. The separate Part C assessment also identifies institutional dependencies that require action by other City Government offices and external partners.\n\nFor this chapter, the most consequential findings are:\n\n| Assessment reference | Finding | Governance implication |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| A1.1 – Score 2 | No ordinance establishing the CDRRMO as a regular department with the prescribed organizational divisions. | The legal and organizational foundation requires institutional strengthening. |\n| A1.2 – Score 2 | CDRRMC membership requires updating, including at least four accredited CSO representatives and private-sector representation. | Governance must be more inclusive and representative. |\n| A1.3 – Score 4 | Quarterly CDRRMC meetings, minutes and resolutions are established. | Preserve and strengthen an existing institutional good practice. |\n| A1.4 – Score 4 | CDRRMO performs the CDRRMC Secretariat function. | Formalize the existing practice through documented procedures. |\n| A1.5 – Score 3 | Local DRRM-related ordinances exist, but evacuation procedures and hazard-sensitive zoning require improvement. | Strengthen the connection between policy, risk information and implementation. |\n| A1.6 – Score 3 | State of Calamity arrangements have been used, but a comprehensive written procedure is needed. | Standardize declaration and post-declaration governance. |\n| A1.7 – Score 4 | DRRM representation in the City Development Council and Local Finance Committee is established. | Use existing planning and financial governance mechanisms more systematically. |\n| A1.8 – Score 1 | No localized State of Imminent Disaster procedure, request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template was identified. | Establish an anticipatory governance process under RA 12287. |\n| A2.1 – Score 2 | Existing CDRRMO sections are not fully aligned with JMC 2014-1. | Clarify the permanent institutional structure and functional responsibilities. |\n| B1.1 – Score 2 | No specific EOC issuance defining its mandate and relationship with the CDRRMC and ICS was sighted. | Establish the EOC''s legal and operational authority. |\n| B6.1 – Score 1 | The EOC–ICS interface and chain of command are unclear. | Define the distinction between incident command and EOC coordination. |\n\nThe assessment also identifies broader system dependencies involving City Planning, Engineering, Budget, Environment, Social Welfare, Health, Education, Utilities and other institutions. These are addressed through the interface arrangements established in this chapter and the succeeding operational modules.\n\nManagement implication: The priority is not simply to create more committees or issue additional policies. It is to establish a coherent governance system in which each mandate has an accountable owner, each process has a defined workflow, each decision has a legitimate authority, and each institutional output can be verified.\n\nAssessment interpretation: The workbook''s Dashboard reports 2.18/4.00 as the combined Institutional Maturity Index. Part C is separately reported and is not included in that index. Unscored indicators and provisional desk assessments must not be interpreted as proof of compliance.\n\n## 1.2 Interpretation of institutional Maturity and Compliance\n\nThe assessment methodology distinguishes institutional maturity from mandatory compliance. A domain may achieve a relatively high maturity index while remaining legally non-compliant because one or more mandatory requirements have not attained the required rating.\n\nThis distinction is particularly important for Ormoc:\n\nCDRRMO Legal Mandate and DRRM Governance (A1) has a maturity index of 2.59/4.00, but only 50% mandatory compliance.\n\nEOC Legal Basis and Concept of Operations (B1) has a maturity index of 1.85/4.00, but 0% mandatory compliance.\n\nThe overall mandatory compliance rate is 39.4%, notwithstanding the combined maturity index of 2.18.\n\nThe implication is that institutional improvements must address both capability and compliance. A procedure that is practiced informally cannot automatically be treated as an established institutional control. Similarly, the existence of an executive order, organizational chart or operational facility does not establish compliance unless the relevant requirements are documented, approved, implemented and supported by evidence.\n\nThe assessment uses the following evidence-based maturity logic:\n\n| Rating | Maturity classification | Institutional interpretation |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| 0 | Absent | No established arrangement. |\n| 1 | Initial / Ad hoc | Practice depends on individuals and is not adequately documented. |\n| 2 | Developing | Arrangement exists but is incomplete, outdated, unapproved or inconsistently implemented. |\n| 3 | Compliant / Established | Applicable standards are met through approved documentation and demonstrated implementation. |\n| 4 | Advanced / Institutionalized | Compliance is sustained, tested, reviewed and improved through documented learning. |\n\nFor purposes of this MOO, a procedure shall not be considered fully institutionalized merely because it appears in the Manual. Its implementation must be demonstrated through records, operational outputs, verification and periodic review.\n\n# 2. Purpose\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal and institutional governance arrangements governing the CDRRMO, CDRRMC and EOC.\n\nIt aims to:\n\nEstablish a clear and legally grounded division of authority among the Local Chief Executive, CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\nDefine the institutional responsibilities, decision rights and accountability of participating City Government offices.\n\nInstitutionalize transparent, inclusive and evidence-based DRRM governance.\n\nEstablish procedures for CDRRMC meetings, resolutions, recommendations and action monitoring.\n\nStandardize the City''s procedures for State of Calamity and State of Imminent Disaster declarations.\n\nStrengthen vertical and horizontal coordination among the City Government, barangays, national government agencies, neighbouring LGUs, CSOs, private-sector partners and humanitarian organizations.\n\nEstablish process ownership, responsibility assignments and decision-making controls using the RACI framework.\n\nEmbed risk-informed governance, quality assurance, performance monitoring and continuous improvement into institutional operations.\n\n# 3. Scope and Application\n\nThis chapter applies to the following institutional actors:\n\nOffice of the City Mayor;\n\nCDRRMC and its Secretariat;\n\nCDRRMO Head and functional divisions;\n\nEOC Manager and EOC functional sections;\n\nSangguniang Panlungsod;\n\nCity Government departments, offices and units;\n\nBarangay DRRMCs in all 85 barangays;\n\nNational government agencies and regional DRRM institutions;\n\nAccredited CSOs, ACDVs, private-sector organizations and other partners.\n\nIts provisions apply under three operating conditions:\n\n| Operating condition | Institutional focus | Primary governance mechanism |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Normal operations | Risk reduction, institutional planning, preparedness, capacity development, monitoring and continuity. | CDRRMC oversight and CDRRMO management. |\n| Heightened preparedness / anticipatory action | Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment, warning-action decisions, resource readiness, anticipatory measures and possible State of Imminent Disaster declaration. | CDRRMC coordination, Mayor''s executive authority and CDRRMO technical leadership. |\n| Emergency operations | Incident coordination, situational awareness, resource mobilization, policy support and operational reporting. | Activated EOC, Incident Management Team and designated response clusters. |\n\nThe Manual applies to all hazards and emergencies within the City''s jurisdiction, including hydrometeorological, geological, climate-related, technological and human-induced hazards, subject to applicable institutional mandates.\n\nIt shall be read together with the City''s approved DRRM plans, contingency plans, Public Service Continuity Plan, climate and disaster risk assessment, development plans, land-use plans and other applicable local policies.\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Standards Framework\n\nThe institutional arrangements shall be interpreted and implemented within the following hierarchy of legal and policy instruments.\n\n## 4.1 Philippine Legal and Regulatory Framework\n\n| Instrument | Relevant Provisions | Application to this Chapter |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Republic Act No. 10121 (2010) | Sections 11–17 and 21 | Establishment and functions of CDRRMC/CDRRMO, emergency coordination, State of Calamity and LDRRMF governance. |\n| IRR of RA 10121 | Relevant provisions on local councils, offices, coordination and declarations | Operationalization of statutory institutional responsibilities. |\n| RA 7160 (1991) – Local Government Code | Sections 16, 33, 76 and relevant provisions on local legislative and executive authority | Local autonomy, inter-LGU cooperation, organizational structure and local governance. |\n| RA 12287 (2025) – Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act | Sections 3–7 and applicable implementing provisions | Anticipatory declaration, pre-disaster risk assessment and early action. |\n| IRR of RA 12287 (2026) | Rules II–VII | Local declaration procedures, anticipatory action, funding, capacity development and post-declaration reporting. |\n| RA 9729 (2009), as amended by RA 10174 | Climate change adaptation and financing provisions | Integration of climate resilience into DRRM governance and planning. |\n| RA 10173 (2012) – Data Privacy Act | Personal information processing and protection | Protection of affected-population, beneficiary, responder and institutional information. |\n| RA 9710 (2009) – Magna Carta of Women | Gender-responsive governance | Gender-responsive and inclusive institutional arrangements. |\n| RA 7277, as amended | Rights and accessibility of persons with disabilities | Accessible governance, warning and emergency services. |\n| RA 9994 (2010) | Senior citizen protection | Inclusion of older persons in DRRM decision-making and service arrangements. |\n| RA 10821 (2016) | Children''s Emergency Relief and Protection Act | Child protection and child-sensitive emergency arrangements. |\n| RA 11036 (2018) – Mental Health Act | Mental health services and protection | Institutional and responder psychosocial support. |\n| RA 12076 (2024) – Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act | Standards for permanent evacuation centers | Institutional coordination on evacuation infrastructure and facility compliance. |\n| RA 12009 (2024) – New Government Procurement Act | Government procurement and applicable emergency procurement procedures | Resource governance and procurement accountability. |\n\nThe Local Government Code and RA 10121 provide the foundation for the City''s organizational and disaster governance responsibilities. RA 12287 introduces an additional anticipatory governance mechanism that must be integrated into existing DRRM processes rather than treated as a separate emergency procedure.\n\n## 4.2 Administrative and Operational Guidelines\n\nThe following administrative and operational instruments shall guide institutional implementation:\n\n| Instrument | Application  |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| NDRRMC-DILG-DBM-CSC JMC No. 2014-1 | LDRRMO organizational structure, staffing and functions. |\n| NDRRMC-DBM-DILG JMC No. 2013-1 | LDRRMF programming and utilization. |\n| COA Circular No. 2012-002 | Accounting and reporting of the LDRRMF and Special Trust Fund. |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012 | Institutionalization and use of the Incident Command System. |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018, as identified in the assessment | EOC establishment, operationalization and management; issuance details require confirmation with OCD Region VIII. |\n| NDCC Memorandum Circular No. 12, s. 2008 | Cluster-based disaster response coordination. |\n| RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006 and DILG VIII memorandum dated 26 February 2025 | Harmonized pre-disaster, during-disaster, post-disaster and terminal reporting. |\n| OCD/NDRRMC Public Service Continuity Planning Guidebook | Continuity of essential functions, business impact analysis, alternate arrangements and succession. |\n| DILG Operation L\\!sto and applicable annual issuances | Preparedness and institutional readiness of LGUs and barangays. |\n\nJMC No. 2014-1 specifically recognizes the LDRRMO as a mandatory office and provides for the Head and three core functional areas: Administration and Training, Research and Planning, and Operations and Warning. It also permits appropriate augmentation of staffing and organizational arrangements, subject to the applicable requirements.\n\n## 4.3 International Frameworks and Management Standards\n\nThe MOO shall also be guided by the following frameworks:\n\n| Framework/Standard | Application to Institutional Governance |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030 | Strengthening disaster risk governance, understanding risk, investing in resilience and enhancing preparedness and recovery. |\n| UNDRR Making Cities Resilient 2030 – Ten Essentials | Alignment with the City''s urban resilience governance and institutional performance. |\n| UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities – Detailed Level Assessment (2017) | Diagnostic reference for institutional and city-wide resilience assessment. |\n| ISO 31000:2018 – Risk Management Guidelines | Integrating risk identification, analysis, evaluation, treatment, monitoring and communication into governance. |\n| ISO 9001:2015 – Quality Management Systems | Process ownership, documented procedures, performance evaluation, corrective action and continual improvement. |\n| ISO 22301:2019 – Business Continuity Management Systems | Continuity of essential services, organizational resilience and disruption management. |\n| Sphere Handbook (2018) | Humanitarian quality and accountability principles for emergency assistance. |\n| CREST / systems-thinking approaches | Understanding interdependencies, feedback loops, cascading impacts and system-level interventions. |\n\nThese frameworks are complementary rather than substitutes for Philippine laws and regulations. Where a standard or international framework is more demanding than the applicable statutory minimum, the City may adopt it as a good-practice benchmark, subject to its authority and resources.\n\n## 4.4 Policy Hierarchy and Conflict Resolution\n\nIn implementing this chapter, the following order shall apply:\n\nPhilippine Constitution and applicable statutes;\n\nValid implementing rules and regulations;\n\nNational administrative and operational issuances;\n\nProvincial and regional coordination requirements, where applicable;\n\nCity ordinances and valid executive issuances;\n\nApproved plans, policies and institutional procedures;\n\nMOO protocols, checklists and supporting forms.\n\nIn case of inconsistency, the higher-ranking legal or regulatory instrument shall prevail. The CDRRMO shall refer material legal ambiguities to the City Legal Office and seek technical clarification from OCD Region VIII or the appropriate national agency.\n\nThe MOO shall maintain a legal and policy register, with an identified custodian and review date for each applicable instrument.\n\n# 5. Systems-Thinking Framework for DRRM Governance\n\n## 5.1 Institutional System Architecture\n\nOrmoc City''s DRRM governance shall operate as a connected system comprising five interdependent layers:\n\nExecutive governance: Political leadership, statutory authority and executive decision-making.\n\nPolicy and oversight: CDRRMC deliberation, policy direction, recommendations and monitoring.\n\nInstitutional management: CDRRMO planning, technical management, administration and implementation.\n\nOperational coordination: EOC, ICS, response clusters and incident management arrangements.\n\nCommunity and external interface: BDRRMCs, CSOs, private-sector institutions, utilities, national agencies and neighbouring LGUs.\n\nIntegrated CDRRMO–EOC Governance System\n\nConceptual relationship. This is not a substitute for the statutory chain of authority or the approved ICS organizational chart.\n\nThe architecture recognizes that information, authority and resources move through different pathways.\n\nExecutive authority flows through legally established government structures.\n\nTechnical information flows from monitoring institutions, technical offices and communities toward decision-makers.\n\nOperational instructions flow through the approved command and coordination arrangements.\n\nResources flow through authorized mobilization and accountability mechanisms.\n\nFeedback and performance information flow back into planning, policy and institutional improvement.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall ensure that these pathways are connected but not conflated.\n\n## 5.2 The CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Interface\n\nThe assessment identifies the institutional interface as the weakest component, with a separate maturity index of 1.29/4.00.\n\nThis indicates that the problem is not limited to individual office capacity. It concerns the connections between institutional actors, their processes, information systems and decision-making arrangements.\n\nThe following operating distinctions shall therefore apply:\n\n| Dimension | CDRRMO | EOC |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Institutional status | Permanent LGU office | Operational coordination mechanism established through appropriate local issuance |\n| Operating condition | Continuous normal operations | Activated according to approved triggers and authority |\n| Primary accountability | CDRRMO Head | Designated EOC Manager |\n| Main function | DRRM governance, planning, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery support | Incident coordination, information management, resource coordination and decision support |\n| Planning horizon | Strategic, annual and multi-year | Incident-specific and operational-period based |\n| Information | Maintains institutional risk and readiness information | Consolidates and validates current incident information |\n| Relationship with ICS | Provides institutional support and coordinates preparedness arrangements | Supports the Incident Commander and Incident Management Team |\n| Institutional continuity | Maintains permanent office functions | Operates under activation, deactivation and continuity arrangements |\n\nThe EOC shall not replace the CDRRMO. Nor shall it become an alternative command structure for field response.\n\nThe Incident Commander shall retain incident-level command authority under the applicable ICS arrangements. The EOC shall facilitate coordination, provide information, mobilize support and elevate decisions requiring executive action.\n\n## 5.3 Institutional Interfaces and System Dependencies\n\nPart C of the assessment identifies 15 institutional interface indicators. These are deliberately excluded from the CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Maturity Index because the primary operational ownership rests with other offices or external institutions.\n\nThe distinction is important for accountability.\n\n| Interface Area | Primary Institutional Owner | CDRRMO’s Role |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Risk-informed CDP/CLUP priorities and scenarios | City Planning and Development Office (CPDO) | Supply risk evidence and provide DRR/CCA technical advice. |\n| DRR/CCA screening of projects and budgets | CPDO and City Budget Office | Provide hazard and resilience screening inputs. |\n| Damage, livelihood and ecosystem loss analysis | CPDO and relevant sector offices | Supply disaster risk and impact information. |\n| Hazard-sensitive zoning and building permits | CPDO and Zoning Administrator | Provide technical hazard information and participate in review. |\n| EOC, evacuation center and critical-facility structural assessments | City Engineer / Building Official | Identify critical facilities and coordinate risk prioritization. |\n| Power, water and telecommunications continuity | Utility providers and relevant City offices | Coordinate restoration priorities and continuity agreements. |\n| Transport anticipatory action | City Engineer, traffic office, DPWH and port operator | Integrate transport risk information into emergency coordination. |\n| Watershed and ecosystem protection | CENRO and DENR | Integrate ecosystem-related hazards and protective functions into DRRM planning. |\n| Health anticipatory action | City Health Office and hospitals | Coordinate the Health Cluster and operational information. |\n| Education continuity | DepEd Schools Division | Coordinate school preparedness and learning-continuity arrangements. |\n| Agriculture and fisheries anticipatory action | City Agriculture Office and City Veterinary Office | Coordinate hazard information and response integration. |\n| Pre-impact price monitoring | DTI and City Market Office | Facilitate information sharing with the EOC. |\n| Business continuity outreach | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce | Facilitate risk communication and preparedness outreach. |\n\nThe CDRRMO shall not assume technical or statutory responsibilities assigned to these offices. Its role is to establish the coordination mechanisms, information exchange and performance monitoring necessary for integrated city-wide resilience.\n\nThis distinction is essential to avoid institutional duplication and unfunded mandates.\n\n# 6. Governance Structure and Institutional Mandates\n\n## 6.1 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council\n\nThe CDRRMC shall serve as the City''s principal DRRM policy-making, coordinating and oversight body under RA 10121.\n\nIts composition and functions shall conform to Section 11 of the Act and its IRR.\n\nThe City shall maintain an updated Executive Order identifying the membership, institutional representation, designated alternates and coordination responsibilities of the Council.\n\nThe assessment identifies the following governance requirements:\n\nUpdating the CDRRMC Executive Order;\n\nMeeting statutory CSO representation requirements, including at least four accredited CSO representatives;\n\nIncluding private-sector representation;\n\nMaintaining institutional representation rather than relying solely on individual appointments; and\n\nReviewing the membership register annually.\n\nThe CDRRMC shall ensure representation from relevant City Government offices, national agencies, public safety institutions, CSOs, the private sector and other prescribed members.\n\nIts principal functions shall include:\n\nApproving, monitoring and evaluating the Local DRRM Plan;\n\nIntegrating DRR and climate change adaptation into local development plans and budgets;\n\nProviding strategic direction for DRRM programs and policies;\n\nRecommending pre-emptive and forced evacuation where warranted;\n\nRecommending a State of Calamity declaration to the appropriate local legislative authority;\n\nCoordinating anticipatory action under RA 12287;\n\nMobilizing institutional and external resources;\n\nMonitoring the use of DRRM funds and other dedicated resources; and\n\nReviewing institutional performance, assessment findings and corrective actions.\n\n## 6.2 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office\n\nThe CDRRMO shall function as the City''s permanent institutional and technical mechanism for DRRM.\n\nIn accordance with Section 12 of RA 10121, it shall be responsible for setting the direction, development, implementation and coordination of local DRRM programs.\n\nIts responsibilities include:\n\nRisk assessment and local risk information management;\n\nLocal DRRM planning and contingency planning;\n\nMulti-hazard early warning;\n\nTraining, orientation and knowledge management;\n\nResource and capability inventory;\n\nCoordination with communities, CSOs and private-sector partners;\n\nEmergency monitoring and mobilization;\n\nOrganization and supervision of emergency response teams and ACDVs;\n\nRecovery coordination;\n\nCDRRMC Secretariat functions; and\n\nPreparation and reporting of DRRM plans, budgets and fund utilization.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall perform these responsibilities through its approved organizational structure and in coordination with other City Government offices.\n\n## 6.3 Emergency Operations Center\n\nThe EOC shall provide the City''s centralized mechanism for operational coordination during emergencies.\n\nThe EOC shall:\n\nMaintain situational awareness;\n\nConsolidate, validate and disseminate incident information;\n\nSupport CDRRMC and executive decision-making;\n\nFacilitate coordination among the Incident Management Team and response clusters;\n\nCoordinate resource requests and mobilization;\n\nMaintain incident and decision records;\n\nFacilitate vertical coordination with provincial and regional DRRM institutions, as applicable; and\n\nSupport transition from response to recovery.\n\nThe EOC shall operate within the authority established by the Mayor and the approved institutional arrangements.\n\n## 6.4 Office of the City Mayor\n\nThe City Mayor shall exercise the powers and responsibilities vested in the Local Chief Executive under RA 7160, RA 10121, RA 12287 and other applicable laws.\n\nThe Mayor shall:\n\nProvide executive leadership and direction;\n\nChair the CDRRMC;\n\nAuthorize actions within statutory authority;\n\nDirect the mobilization of City Government offices and resources;\n\nIssue valid executive orders and administrative directives;\n\nExercise the relevant declaration authority under RA 12287;\n\nApprove institutional arrangements within the scope of executive authority; and\n\nElevate matters requiring legislative or higher-level government action.\n\nThe Mayor''s executive authority shall remain distinct from the legislative authority of the Sangguniang Panlungsod.\n\n## 6.5 Sangguniang Panlungsod\n\nThe Sangguniang Panlungsod shall exercise its legislative and oversight responsibilities in accordance with RA 7160 and applicable DRRM legislation.\n\nIts relevant functions include:\n\nEnacting the ordinance establishing or strengthening the CDRRMO;\n\nApproving the necessary organizational and budgetary measures;\n\nConsidering CDRRMC recommendations for a State of Calamity declaration;\n\nEnacting relevant local DRRM ordinances;\n\nExercising oversight over local DRRM policies and resource allocation; and\n\nSupporting the institutionalization of the City''s resilience priorities.\n\nLegal distinction: The declaration of a State of Calamity under Section 16 of RA 10121 is issued by the local sanggunian upon the recommendation of the LDRRMC, based on damage assessment and needs analysis. It is not simply a Mayor''s Executive Order.\n\n# 7. Institutional Governance Processes and Workflows\n\nThe MOO shall define governance as a series of connected, controlled and measurable processes. Each process shall have:\n\nA defined trigger or initiating condition;\n\nA designated process owner;\n\nResponsible actors;\n\nA legally authorized decision-maker;\n\nRequired inputs and evidence;\n\nDefined outputs and records;\n\nEscalation and exception procedures; and\n\nPerformance and compliance indicators.\n\nThis approach ensures that governance decisions can be traced from initiation to approval, implementation, reporting and review.\n\n## 7.1 Process 1: CDRRMC Governance and Policy Decision-making\n\nPurpose: Ensure that DRRM policies, plans, priorities and institutional decisions are deliberated, approved or recommended by the appropriate authority and translated into accountable actions.\n\nWorkflow GOV-01: CDRRMC Governance\n\nInitiation and agenda development\nCDRRMO identifies policy issues, assessment findings, risk developments and matters requiring Council action.\n\nTechnical preparation\nConcerned offices submit technical papers, evidence, financial implications and recommendations.\n\nSecretariat processing\nCDRRMO consolidates agenda items, issues meeting notices and circulates supporting documents.\n\nCouncil deliberation\nCDRRMC members review evidence, assess implications and deliberate on proposed actions.\n\nDecision and documentation\nThe Council approves matters within its authority or adopts recommendations for submission to the appropriate approving authority.\n\nImplementation and assignment\nThe Secretariat records decisions, assigns action owners and establishes target dates.\n\nMonitoring and feedback\nCDRRMO tracks implementation and reports unresolved actions at succeeding meetings.\n\nRequired Output\n\nApproved minutes, resolutions or recommendations, decision register and action-monitoring report.\n\nOperating Requirements\n\n| Control point | Required standard |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| Meeting frequency | At least once every three months and as necessary. |\n| Agenda | Include standing items on risk developments, LDRRMP implementation, funding, assessment gaps and institutional readiness. |\n| Supporting documents | Technical papers, risk information, financial implications and draft resolutions, as applicable. |\n| Documentation | Minutes, attendance, decisions, dissenting views where material, assigned owners and deadlines. |\n| Follow-through | Action register reviewed at every succeeding meeting. |\n| Review | Annual assessment of Council functionality and effectiveness. |\n\nThe assessment records a score of 4 for A1.3. This established practice should be retained and strengthened through systematic action tracking, particularly for the 30 high-priority institutional gaps.\n\n## 7.2 Process 2: CDRRMC Secretariat Management\n\nPurpose: Ensure that Council proceedings, decisions and institutional records are complete, traceable and accessible to authorized stakeholders.\n\nWorkflow GOV-02: Secretariat Operations\n\nReceive agenda requests and supporting documents.\n\nPrepare agenda, meeting notice and document package.\n\nCoordinate with the Chairperson on scheduling and meeting requirements.\n\nRecord attendance, proceedings, resolutions and decisions.\n\nPrepare draft minutes and resolutions for review and approval.\n\nDistribute approved records to authorized members.\n\nEnter decisions into the action register and assign responsible offices.\n\nTrack implementation and preserve the official records.\n\nRequired records: Meeting calendar, agenda, attendance, minutes, resolutions, action register and document distribution log.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall develop a Secretariat SOP and use standardized templates. This responds to A1.4, which is already rated 4, while addressing the need to document the existing practice more systematically.\n\n## 7.3 Process 3: Institutional Policy and Ordinance Development\n\nPurpose: Ensure that local DRRM policies are legally sound, risk-informed, coordinated and implementable.\n\nThe process shall be used for the proposed CDRRMO enabling ordinance, updated CDRRMC Executive Order, EOC establishment issuance, ICS institutionalization and amendments to existing DRRM ordinances.\n\nWorkflow GOV-03: Policy Development and Approval\n\nIdentify the policy gap. CDRRMO documents the legal, institutional or operational deficiency.\n\nDevelop the technical proposal. Prepare the proposed policy, institutional implications and implementation requirements.\n\nConduct consultation. Engage affected City offices, CDRRMC members, CSOs and relevant partners.\n\nLegal review. The City Legal Office reviews legality, authority, consistency and enforceability.\n\nResource review. HRMO and Budget Office assess staffing, funding and administrative implications.\n\nApproval. Submit the proposal to the Mayor or Sangguniang Panlungsod, depending on the nature of the instrument.\n\nPublication and dissemination. Circulate the approved policy to responsible offices and affected stakeholders.\n\nImplementation and evaluation. Monitor compliance, assess effectiveness and recommend revisions.\n\nDecision rule: The appropriate approving authority shall be determined by the legal nature of the proposed instrument. An ordinance shall not be substituted by an Executive Order where legislative action is required.\n\n## 7.4 Process 4: State of Calamity Declaration\n\nThe City shall maintain a documented protocol consistent with Section 16 of RA 10121 and its IRR.\n\nA State of Calamity declaration is based on the results of damage assessment and needs analysis. It shall be distinguished from the anticipatory declaration mechanism under RA 12287.\n\nWorkflow GOV-04: State of Calamity\n\nIncident assessment\nBDRRMCs, response agencies and the CDRRMO report casualties, affected populations, infrastructure damage, livelihood disruption and service interruptions.\n\nRDANA consolidation\nCDRRMO coordinates the validation and consolidation of damage and needs information.\n\nTechnical recommendation\nCDRRMO prepares the assessment and submits it to the CDRRMC.\n\nCDRRMC deliberation\nThe Council evaluates the evidence and adopts a recommendation where the legal criteria are met.\n\nLegislative declaration\nThe Sangguniang Panlungsod considers and issues the declaration or lifting through the appropriate resolution.\n\nImplementation\nThe City Government implements applicable remedial measures, resource arrangements, assistance and reporting requirements.\n\nMonitoring and lifting\nThe CDRRMO monitors continuing impacts and prepares the technical basis for recommending lifting when appropriate.\n\nRequired Outputs\n\nRDANA, CDRRMC recommendation, Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution, implementation records and lifting documentation.\n\nThe City shall maintain the following supporting records:\n\nTechnical assessment and recommendation;\n\nRDANA and other supporting reports;\n\nCDRRMC resolution;\n\nSangguniang Panlungsod resolution;\n\nPost-declaration action register;\n\nResource utilization and accountability records; and\n\nLifting recommendation and corresponding resolution.\n\nThe protocol shall also address coordination where national or regional declarations, infrastructure closures or emergency restrictions affect Ormoc City without automatically constituting a local State of Calamity declaration.\n\n## 7.5 Process 5: State of Imminent Disaster Declaration and Anticipatory Action\n\nNew statutory Governance Process\n\nRA 12287 establishes a mechanism for declaring a State of Imminent Disaster before a disaster occurs, based on the applicable forecast, pre-disaster risk assessment and legal criteria.\n\nThe IRR released on 29 September 2026 provides the implementing framework for this mechanism.\n\nThe assessment identifies A1.8 as a mandatory requirement with a score of 1. The City has no localized procedure, request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template documented in the evidence reviewed.\n\nThe following workflow shall therefore be institutionalized.\n\nWorkflow GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster\n\nHazard forecast and trigger monitoring\nCDRRMO Operations and Warning monitors official forecasts, advisories and hazard-specific triggers from PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and other competent agencies.\n\nPre-Disaster Risk Assessment\nCDRRMO coordinates the assessment of exposure, vulnerability, projected severity, likely impacts, affected sectors, lead time and available response capacity.\n\nTechnical recommendation\nCDRRMO prepares the PDRA findings and proposed anticipatory measures for CDRRMC consideration.\n\nRegional coordination and recommendation\nThe City coordinates with RDRRMC VIII through OCD Region VIII and submits the prescribed request and supporting documentation.\n\nRegional decision and response\nRDRRMC VIII undertakes the action required under the Act and IRR. The request, acknowledgment and prescribed response timeline are documented.\n\nLocal declaration\nUpon the applicable recommendation, the City Mayor may declare a State of Imminent Disaster through an Executive Order covering the jurisdiction forecast to be affected.\n\nActivation of anticipatory measures\nAuthorized offices implement approved early actions, including pre-positioning, evacuation, protection of critical infrastructure and targeted social assistance.\n\nFunding and accountability\nBudget, Accounting, Procurement and implementing offices document commitments, disbursements, utilization and outputs in accordance with the IRR and applicable DBM-COA rules.\n\nLifting and post-declaration reporting\nThe City observes the applicable lifting procedures and submits the required post-lifting report to OCD within the prescribed 45-day period.\n\nThe process shall observe the following safeguards:\n\nA forecast alone shall not automatically trigger a declaration. The applicable legal criteria and PDRA requirements must be met.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall provide technical recommendations, not independently exercise the declaration authority.\n\nThe local declaration shall follow the recommendation and coordination mechanism prescribed by RA 12287 and its IRR.\n\nAnticipatory actions shall be proportionate to the projected impacts, hazard lead time, available resources and applicable legal authority.\n\nMeasures shall observe inclusion, protection, do-no-harm and no-regrets principles.\n\nThe use of funds shall follow the statutory funding limits, tranche arrangements and applicable financial regulations.\n\nDecisions, approvals, actions and expenditures shall be documented for audit and evaluation.\n\nFunding control: As reflected in the assessment workbook, the IRR provides for an anticipatory-action allocation of up to 10% of the 70% LDRRMF component and a 30%/70% tranche arrangement associated with the prescribed three-day lead-time rule. The precise timing, eligibility, disbursement and documentation requirements must be implemented using the certified IRR and applicable DBM-COA guidance. The MOO shall not treat this allocation as an additional fund outside the statutory LDRRMF framework.\n\nIndependent Component City clarification: Ormoc''s status as an Independent Component City must be considered when establishing its vertical coordination arrangements. The City shall obtain written confirmation from OCD Region VIII on the application of the IRR''s regional and provincial coordination provisions, particularly whether any provincial declaration has legal applicability to Ormoc. The City''s ICC status shall not be used as a reason to bypass the RDRRMC recommendation mechanism expressly established by RA 12287.\n\n## 7.6 Process 6: Institutional Escalation and Inter-agency Coordination\n\nInstitutional escalation shall occur when:\n\nThe scale of an incident exceeds the capacity of a barangay;\n\nMultiple barangays require coordinated intervention;\n\nCity resources are insufficient;\n\nSpecialized national or regional assistance is required;\n\nCritical infrastructure or essential services are disrupted; or\n\nA statutory declaration or executive decision is necessary.\n\nRA 10121, Section 15, provides the general coordination framework: barangay-level leadership where a barangay is affected, city/municipal leadership where two or more barangays are affected, and progressively higher coordination levels when impacts exceed the relevant territorial scale. Higher councils and institutions support the LGU''s primary response responsibility.\n\nThe operational workflow shall be:\n\nIncident or risk information is reported to the appropriate local authority.\n\nThe affected BDRRMC undertakes initial action and reports to the City.\n\nCDRRMO validates the information and assesses the level of support required.\n\nThe CDRRMC and EOC coordinate City-wide institutional action, as appropriate.\n\nThe City requests external assistance through the prescribed channels when required.\n\nThe receiving institution acknowledges and assigns the request.\n\nThe CDRRMO monitors the response and maintains the institutional record.\n\nUnresolved requirements are escalated through the appropriate higher-level coordination mechanism.\n\n## 7.7 Process 7: Governance Review and Corrective Action\n\nAll governance processes shall be subject to periodic review.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall:\n\nConsolidate compliance and performance information;\n\nCompare actual results against approved targets;\n\nIdentify gaps, recurring problems and systemic causes;\n\nAssign corrective actions to accountable offices;\n\nEstablish completion dates and required resources;\n\nVerify implementation;\n\nReport unresolved issues to the CDRRMC; and\n\nUpdate policies and procedures when necessary.\n\nThis process shall link the Institutional Assessment Tool, Action Plan, CDRRMC action register, annual LDRRMP monitoring and the MOO revision mechanism.\n\n# 8. Responsibility Assignment and RACI Framework\n\n## 8.1 Application\n\nThe RACI framework shall be used to clarify institutional responsibility for governance processes and prevent overlapping mandates, unassigned tasks and ambiguous decision authority.\n\nThe following definitions apply:\n\n| Code | Responsibility | Application |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| R | Responsible | Performs the work and produces the required output. |\n| A | Accountable | Owns the result and approves the output or decision. Only one A per activity. |\n| C | Consulted | Provides technical, legal or operational advice before the decision. |\n| I | Informed | Receives relevant information on the decision or outcome. |\n\nRACI assignments shall be interpreted subject to statutory powers, formal delegations and approved organizational arrangements. A RACI designation does not create authority that does not otherwise exist under law.\n\n## 8.2 Institutional Roles Used in the RACI Matrix\n\n| Role | Abbreviation | Principal Responsibility |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Local Chief Executive | Mayor | Executive authority and statutory decisions. |\n| Sangguniang Panlungsod | SP | Legislative approval and local declaration authority. |\n| CDRRMC | Council | Collective policy direction, deliberation and recommendation. |\n| CDRRMO Head | CDRRMO | Permanent office leadership and technical accountability. |\n| CDRRMO Secretariat | Secretariat | Council administration, documentation and action tracking. |\n| City Legal Office | CLO | Legal review and compliance advice. |\n| City Planning and Development Office | CPDO | Integration of DRR/CCA into development planning. |\n| HRMO | HRMO | Staffing, qualification and personnel administration. |\n| City Budget / Accounting / BAC | Finance | Budgeting, accounting, procurement and financial control. |\n| City Information Office | CIO | Public information and risk communication support. |\n| BDRRMCs | Barangays | Community-level governance, reporting and local action. |\n| City departments and agencies | Departments | Sectoral implementation and technical support. |\n| OCD / RDRRMC VIII | Regional | Regional coordination, technical assistance and statutory recommendation functions. |\n| EOC Manager / IMT | EOC/IMT | Emergency coordination and incident-management interface. |\n| CSOs / Private Sector | Partners | Community participation, technical support and resource commitments. |\n\n## 8.3 Master RACI Matrix – institutional Governance\n\nThe following matrix establishes the proposed responsibility assignments for Chapter 1.\n\n| Governance activity | Mayor | SP | CDRRMC | CDRRMO | Secretariat | CLO | HRMO / Finance | OCD VIII |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Establish CDRRMO through ordinance | I | A | C | R | I | C | C | I |\n| Prepare CDRRMC composition EO | A | I | C | R | R | C | I | I |\n| Maintain CDRRMC membership register | I | I | C | A | R | C | I | I |\n| Prepare CDRRMC agenda and meetings | I | I | A | C | R | I | I | I |\n| Document Council resolutions | I | I | A | C | R | C | I | I |\n| Monitor Council decisions | I | I | A | R | R | I | C | I |\n| Develop local DRRM policy | A | C | C | R | C | C | C | I |\n| Review proposed DRRM ordinances | C | I | C | R | I | A | C | C |\n| Prepare State of Calamity technical assessment | I | I | C | A | R | C | C | I |\n| Recommend State of Calamity | I | I | A | R | R | C | I | I |\n| Issue local State of Calamity declaration | I | A | R | C | I | C | I | I |\n| Prepare State of Imminent Disaster PDRA | I | I | C | A | I | C | C | C |\n| Submit request to RDRRMC VIII | I | I | A | R | C | C | I | C |\n| Recommend regional State of Imminent Disaster Declaration | I | I | C | C | I | I | I | A/R\\* |\n| Issue local State of Imminent Disaster EO | A | I | C | R | I | C | C | C |\n| Coordinate anticipatory action | A | I | C | R | I | C | R | C |\n| Prepare 45-day post-lifting report | I | I | C | A/R | C | C | R | I |\n| Maintain legal and policy register | I | I | I | A | R | C | I | I |\n| Conduct annual governance review | I | I | A | R | R | C | C | C |\n\n\\*The RACI assignment for OCD/RDRRMC VIII reflects the regional recommendation function described in RA 12287. The exact responsible regional body and administrative sequence shall be finalized against the certified IRR and applicable regional procedures. The RACI matrix does not replace the statutory decision authority of the NDRRMC, regional councils, Mayor or Sanggunian.\n\n## 8.4 RACI Matrix – institutional interfaces\n\nPart C of the assessment shall be operationalized through the following responsibility arrangements.\n\n| Interface activity | Accountable owner | Responsible actors | CDRRMO role |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| CDRA and CLUP integration | CPDO | CPDO technical team | C / technical input |\n| DRR/CCA project screening | CPDO | CPDO, City Budget Office | C / risk validation |\n| Critical facility structural assessment | City Engineer / Building Official | Engineering and inspection teams | C / risk prioritization |\n| Utility continuity agreements | Relevant utility / designated City authority | Utility providers and City offices | R for coordination, subject to approved mandate |\n| Watershed and ecosystem monitoring | CENRO | CENRO, DENR and partners | C / DRRM integration |\n| Health anticipatory action | City Health Officer | CHO, hospitals and health partners | C / coordination |\n| Education anticipatory action | DepEd Schools Division | School DRRM coordinators | C / coordination |\n| Agriculture and fisheries early action | City Agriculture / Veterinary Office | CAO, CVO and relevant agencies | C / coordination |\n| Price monitoring | DTI / City Market Office | Market monitoring personnel | C / EOC information integration |\n| Business continuity outreach | CDRRMO Head | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce | A / R for DRRM outreach |\n\nThis matrix ensures that the CDRRMO coordinates the overall resilience system without appropriating the mandates of other City offices.\n\n## 8.5 RACI Compliance Rules\n\nThe following rules shall govern all RACI matrices in the MOO:\n\nEach activity shall have one clearly designated Accountable role.\n\nMultiple Responsible roles may be assigned where work is genuinely shared.\n\nConsulted roles shall be limited to those whose technical or institutional input is necessary.\n\nInformed roles shall receive the relevant outputs without becoming unnecessary approval points.\n\nRACI assignments shall be reviewed whenever the organizational structure, legal mandate or delegation changes.\n\nWhere a statutory body exercises collective authority, the Council or Sanggunian shall be identified as the accountable decision-making body, with the designated Chairperson responsible for convening and presiding over proceedings.\n\nEach RACI matrix shall identify the approving authority for the final output.\n\nThe detailed job action sheets and position-level RACI matrices shall be developed under the organizational and EOC modules.\n\n# 9. Total Quality Management and Institutional Performance\n\n## 9.1 Quality Management Approach\n\nThe CDRRMO shall apply Total Quality Management principles to the design, implementation, monitoring and improvement of its governance processes.\n\nTQM shall be treated as an institutional management approach rather than a separate administrative activity. Its purpose is to ensure that DRRM services are reliable, timely, accountable, evidence-based and responsive to the needs of affected communities.\n\nThe following principles shall guide implementation:\n\n| TQM principle | Application to CDRRMO–EOC operations |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| Customer and stakeholder focus | Decisions and services shall respond to the needs of communities, affected populations, responders and institutional partners. |\n| Leadership and accountability | Each process shall have a designated owner and authorized decision-maker. |\n| Process orientation | Activities shall be standardized through workflows, SOPs, checklists and defined outputs. |\n| Systems approach | Institutional processes shall be managed as interconnected functions rather than isolated activities. |\n| Evidence-based decisions | Decisions shall be supported by verified risk information, assessment results, operational reports and documented evidence. |\n| People engagement | Personnel, barangays, CSOs and partners shall participate in process design, implementation and improvement. |\n| Continuous improvement | Findings from exercises, incidents, audits and assessments shall result in corrective and preventive action. |\n| Relationship management | Partnerships and institutional interfaces shall be actively maintained and evaluated. |\n\n## 9.2 Plan–Do–Check–Act (PDCA) cycle\n\nThe City shall apply the PDCA cycle as the primary mechanism for institutional quality improvement.\n\nInstitutional Quality Management Cycle\n\nThe PDCA cycle shall operate continuously across all CDRRMO and EOC functional modules.\n\n## 9.3 Application of PDCA to institutional governance\n\n| PDCA stage | Key activities | Responsible institutional actors | Required evidence |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Plan | Annual governance calendar, policy review, risk-informed priorities, budget and staffing requirements | CDRRMO Head, CDRRMC, City Planning, Budget Office | Approved plans, meeting calendar, LDRRMFIP |\n| Do | Meetings, policy implementation, coordination, declaration procedures and institutional operations | CDRRMO, Secretariat, City offices and partners | Minutes, resolutions, SOP records, reports |\n| Check | Compliance assessment, performance monitoring, document verification and review of decisions | CDRRMO, Internal Audit, CDRRMC | Dashboard, audit reports, compliance register |\n| Act | Corrective action, institutional restructuring, policy amendment and procedure revision | Mayor, CDRRMC, SP and responsible offices | Corrective action register, revised policies, updated MOO |\n\n## 9.4 Process Performance Indicators\n\nThe following indicators are proposed for Chapter 1. They shall be integrated into the broader MOO Monitoring and Evaluation Framework.\n\n| Performance indicator | Proposed target | Frequency | Process owner |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| CDRRMC regular meetings conducted | At least 4 annually | Quarterly | CDRRMC Secretariat |\n| CDRRMC membership register updated | 100% of prescribed seats documented | Annual | CDRRMO |\n| CDRRMC resolutions with assigned owners and deadlines | 100% | Every meeting | Secretariat |\n| CDRRMC action items with documented status | 100% tracked | Quarterly | CDRRMO |\n| Critical DRRM policies subjected to legal review | 100% before approval | Per policy | City Legal Office |\n| Critical governance procedures with approved SOPs | 100% of identified mandatory procedures | Annual review | CDRRMO |\n| State of Calamity recommendations with supporting RDANA | 100% | Per occurrence | CDRRMO |\n| State of Imminent Disaster requests with complete PDRA documentation | 100% | Per applicable event | CDRRMO |\n| State of Imminent Disaster post-lifting reports submitted within prescribed period | 100% | Per declaration | CDRRMO |\n| CDRRMC decisions with documented follow-through | 100% recorded | Quarterly | CDRRMO Secretariat |\n| High-priority institutional corrective actions completed by approved deadlines | At least 90%, with overdue items escalated | Quarterly | CDRRMO Head |\n\nThese are proposed management targets, not existing performance claims. They shall be calibrated during the validation workshop and approved through the City''s institutional performance arrangements.\n\n## 9.5 Corrective and Preventive Action\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a Corrective and Preventive Action Register (CAPA) linked to:\n\nInstitutional Assessment Tool findings;\n\nCDRRMC resolutions;\n\nInternal and external audit findings;\n\nAfter-action reviews;\n\nExercise evaluation reports;\n\nIncident and response reports;\n\nStakeholder feedback; and\n\nApplicable changes in laws and policies.\n\nEach corrective action shall identify:\n\n| CAPA field | Required information |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| Reference | Source of the finding or non-conformity |\n| Problem statement | Specific deficiency or process failure |\n| Root cause | Underlying organizational, procedural, technical or resource-related cause |\n| Risk consequence | Potential effect on life safety, service delivery, compliance or institutional performance |\n| Corrective action | Specific intervention to address the deficiency |\n| Accountable owner | Single office or official responsible for completion |\n| Responsible actors | Personnel or offices undertaking the work |\n| Target date | Approved completion deadline |\n| Required resources | Budget, personnel, equipment or technical assistance |\n| Verification | Evidence that the action was implemented |\n| Effectiveness review | Evidence that the original problem has been resolved and has not recurred |\n\nA finding shall not be closed merely because an action has been initiated. Closure requires evidence of completion and verification of effectiveness.\n\n# 10. Alignment with the MCR2030 Ten Essentials\n\nThis chapter primarily contributes to the Governance and Financial Capacity cluster of the MCR2030 Ten Essentials while establishing the institutional foundation for the other two clusters.\n\n| MCR2030 Essential | Chapter 1 contribution | Institutional mechanism |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| 1. Organize for Disaster Resilience | Primary | CDRRMC, CDRRMO, EOC mandates, authorities and coordination architecture. |\n| 2. Identify, Understand and Use Current and Future Risk Scenarios | Supporting | Institutional responsibility for risk intelligence and evidence-based policy decisions. |\n| 3. Strengthen Financial Capability for Resilience | Supporting | Governance of DRRM investment, funding, accountability and resource mobilization. |\n| 4. Pursue Resilient Urban Development and Design | Supporting | Institutional interface with CPDO, Zoning Administrator and City Engineer. |\n| 5. Safeguard Natural Buffers to Enhance Protective Functions | Supporting | Institutional interface with CENRO, DENR and watershed partners. |\n| 6. Strengthen Institutional Capacity for Resilience | Primary | Institutional roles, accountability, staffing, competence and continuity. |\n| 7. Understand and Strengthen Societal Capacity for Resilience | Primary | CDRRMC representation, barangay participation, CSO and private-sector partnerships. |\n| 8. Increase Infrastructure Resilience | Supporting | Cross-office coordination on critical facilities and lifeline continuity. |\n| 9. Ensure Effective Disaster Response | Primary | EOC mandate, coordination arrangements and institutional decision pathways. |\n| 10. Expedite Recovery and Build Back Better | Supporting | Governance continuity and accountability for recovery and reconstruction. |\n\nThe City shall use this alignment to ensure that institutional improvements contribute to the wider urban resilience agenda, not merely to the internal functionality of the CDRRMO and EOC.\n\n# 11. Institutional Improvement Priorities Arising from the Assessment\n\nThe following actions are directly linked to the assessment findings and shall be included in the Institutional Improvement Action Plan.\n\n| Priority | Assessment reference | Required action | Accountable owner | Supporting actors | Target indicated  |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| High | A1.1 | Enact an ordinance establishing the CDRRMO as a regular department, with prescribed divisions and functions. | Mayor / SP, according to legislative process | CDRRMO, CLO, HRMO | 2027 |\n| Medium | A1.2 | Update CDRRMC composition to meet statutory membership requirements. | Mayor | CDRRMO, CLO, accredited CSOs, private sector | 2027 |\n| Medium | A1.6 | Develop a documented State of Calamity procedure and supporting templates. | CDRRMO Head | CDRRMC, CLO, RDANA team | January 2027 |\n| High | A1.8 | Establish the State of Imminent Disaster procedure, including regional coordination, request and EO templates, lifting and reporting. | Mayor for declaration; CDRRMO Head for process development | CDRRMC, OCD VIII, CLO, Budget Office | January 2027 |\n| High | B1.1 | Issue an Executive Order establishing the City EOC and defining its relationship with CDRRMC and ICS. | Mayor | CDRRMO, CLO, CDRRMC | January 2027 |\n| High | A1.7 / A4.6 | Use existing City Development Council and Local Finance Committee representation to secure anticipatory-action financing arrangements. | Mayor / appropriate finance approving authorities | CDRRMO, Budget Office, Accounting, BAC | FY2028 budget |\n| Medium | A1.3 / A1.4 | Institutionalize CDRRMC Secretariat procedures and action tracking while maintaining quarterly meetings. | CDRRMO Head | Secretariat, CDRRMC members | January 2027 |\n| Medium | A1.5 | Review and amend evacuation and other DRRM-related local ordinances, including hazard-based provisions. | Mayor / SP, according to instrument | CDRRMO, CLO, CPDO, barangays | 2027 |\n\nThe action plan should retain the detailed status, funding, target dates and evidence requirements in the workbook. This chapter establishes the governance requirements and ownership; it does not replace the Action Plan.\n\n## 11.1 Priority Sequencing\n\nThe improvement actions shall be implemented in the following sequence:\n\nPhase 1 – Legal and institutional foundation\n\nCDRRMO enabling ordinance;\n\nUpdated CDRRMC Executive Order;\n\nEOC establishment issuance;\n\nState of Imminent Disaster SOP;\n\nState of Calamity SOP.\n\nPhase 2 – Operational institutionalization\n\nSecretariat SOP and action register;\n\nFormal inter-agency coordination and delegation arrangements;\n\nApproved CDRRMO and EOC role descriptions;\n\nInstitutional policy and records register.\n\nPhase 3 – Performance and continuous improvement\n\nCDRRMC governance performance monitoring;\n\nCAPA implementation;\n\nPeriodic legal and policy review;\n\nValidation and testing of institutional procedures.\n\n# 12. Required SOPs, Forms and Institutional Records\n\nThe following instruments shall be maintained under this chapter.\n\n| Code | SOP / Instrument | Required output |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| GOV-01 | CDRRMC governance and meeting procedure | Annual calendar, notices, agendas and minutes |\n| GOV-02 | CDRRMC Secretariat management | Secretariat register and document-control system |\n| GOV-03 | Institutional policy and ordinance development | Policy development and legal review checklist |\n| GOV-04 | State of Calamity declaration and lifting | RDANA-based recommendation and declaration package |\n| GOV-05 | State of Imminent Disaster declaration and lifting | PDRA, regional request, Mayor''s EO and reporting package |\n| GOV-06 | Inter-agency and vertical coordination | Coordination protocol and contact directory |\n| GOV-07 | RACI and delegation management | Responsibility and authority register |\n| GOV-08 | Institutional records management | Controlled document and records register |\n| GOV-09 | Governance performance monitoring | KPI dashboard and action register |\n| GOV-10 | Corrective and preventive action management | CAPA register and verification reports |\n\nThe following templates shall be included in the MOO annexes:\n\nGOV-F01 – CDRRMC Membership and Designation Register;\n\nGOV-F02 – CDRRMC Meeting Notice and Agenda;\n\nGOV-F03 – CDRRMC Attendance and Minutes;\n\nGOV-F04 – CDRRMC Resolution Template;\n\nGOV-F05 – Resolution and Action Monitoring Register;\n\nGOV-F06 – Institutional Policy Review Checklist;\n\nGOV-F07 – State of Calamity Technical Assessment;\n\nGOV-F08 – State of Calamity Recommendation and Resolution;\n\nGOV-F09 – State of Imminent Disaster PDRA Summary;\n\nGOV-F10 – Request to RDRRMC VIII;\n\nGOV-F11 – Mayor''s State of Imminent Disaster Executive Order Template;\n\nGOV-F12 – State of Imminent Disaster Lifting Documentation;\n\nGOV-F13 – Post-Lifting 45-Day Report;\n\nGOV-F14 – Institutional RACI Register;\n\nGOV-F15 – Corrective and Preventive Action Register;\n\nGOV-F16 – Legal and Policy Register.\n\nAll templates shall have document identifiers, version numbers, approval status, responsible custodian and review dates.\n\n# 13. Governance Controls, Documentation and Records Management\n\nThe CDRRMO shall establish a controlled documentation system to ensure that institutional decisions and procedures remain accessible, authentic, traceable and current.\n\n## 13.1 Document Control\n\nEach controlled document shall contain:\n\nDocument title and code;\n\nVersion and revision number;\n\nDate of preparation;\n\nPrepared-by and reviewed-by designations;\n\nApproving authority;\n\nEffective date;\n\nRevision history; and\n\nDistribution and access classification.\n\nOnly approved versions shall be used as official operational references.\n\n## 13.2 Records Management\n\nThe CDRRMO Secretariat shall maintain the official governance records in coordination with the City Records Officer.\n\nRecords shall be classified into:\n\nLegal and policy records;\n\nCDRRMC governance records;\n\nInstitutional organizational records;\n\nDeclaration and executive decision records;\n\nFinancial and resource accountability records;\n\nCoordination and partnership records;\n\nPerformance and audit records; and\n\nCorrective action and improvement records.\n\nRetention and disposition shall follow applicable government records management and archival requirements.\n\n## 13.3 Data Protection and Information Sharing\n\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate with the City''s Data Protection Officer and information technology office to establish appropriate safeguards for DRRM information.\n\nThe system shall distinguish:\n\nPublicly available risk and preparedness information;\n\nInternal institutional information;\n\nRestricted operational information;\n\nPersonal information of affected populations;\n\nSensitive personal information; and\n\nConfidential personnel and security records.\n\nThe EOC shall use aggregated and appropriately anonymized information for operational dashboards whenever personal identifiers are not required.\n\nData sharing with CSWDO, CPDO, national agencies, barangays and partners shall be governed by appropriate data-sharing agreements and privacy safeguards under RA 10173.\n\n# 14. Implementation, Review and Institutional Assurance\n\n## 14.1 Institutional Ownership\n\nThe CDRRMO Head shall be the overall process owner for the implementation of this chapter, under the administrative authority of the Local Chief Executive and the policy oversight of the CDRRMC.\n\nThe City Legal Office, HRMO, Budget Office, Accounting Office, Secretariat and other concerned institutions shall discharge their assigned responsibilities.\n\n## 14.2 Review Schedule\n\n| Review activity | Frequency | Responsible owner |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| CDRRMC membership review | Annually and after material organizational changes | CDRRMO |\n| Governance SOP review | Annually and after significant policy changes | CDRRMO Head |\n| Legal and policy register review | At least annually | CDRRMO / City Legal Office |\n| RACI and delegation review | Annually and after structural changes | CDRRMO / HRMO |\n| CDRRMC performance review | Quarterly, with annual consolidation | CDRRMC Secretariat |\n| Institutional assessment | Annually, or after major institutional changes | CDRRMO |\n| CAPA review | Quarterly | CDRRMO Head |\n| MOO revision | Annually or when necessary | CDRRMO Head |\n\n## 14.3 Institutional Assurance\n\nThe implementation of this chapter shall be evaluated through three complementary tests:\n\nCompliance test: Are statutory and regulatory requirements met?\n\nEffectiveness test: Do institutional arrangements produce timely, coordinated and reliable decisions and outputs?\n\nSustainability test: Are the arrangements institutionalized, documented, resourced, tested and capable of surviving leadership and personnel changes?\n\nA governance process shall be considered effective only when it satisfies all three dimensions appropriate to its function.\n\n# 15. Technical Validation Notes and Outstanding Decisions\n\nThe following matters should be resolved before Chapter 1 is submitted for formal approval.\n\n| Validation issue | Required action | Responsible institution |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| CDRRMO legal status | Confirm existing EO and prepare enabling ordinance. | City Legal Office / SP |\n| CDRRMC composition | Verify all statutory seats and CSO accreditation. | CDRRMO / Mayor''s Office |\n| State of Calamity | Validate local declaration and lifting templates against RA 10121 Section 16. | City Legal Office |\n| State of Imminent Disaster | Confirm certified IRR provisions, RDRRMC VIII request route, response timelines and local EO procedure. | OCD VIII / City Legal Office |\n| ICC coordination | Confirm applicability of provincial coordination provisions to Ormoc. | OCD VIII |\n| EOC establishment | Verify NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 and the exact issuance title and provisions. | OCD VIII |\n| EOC–ICS interface | Confirm the relationship between EOC, IMT, Incident Commander and response clusters. | CDRRMO / OCD VIII |\n| Organizational structure | Reconcile JMC 2014-1, approved plantilla and proposed CDRRMO department structure. | HRMO / CDRRMO |\n| Institutional maturity | Validate provisional scores and resolve contradictory evidence. | Assessment team / CDRRMO |\n| Version control | The uploaded filename identifies v1.3, while the Guide sheet still identifies the instrument as v1.1. Reconcile the workbook version and revision history. | Assessment team |\n\nTwo important corrections incorporated into this revised chapter:\n\nA State of Calamity is declared by the local sanggunian upon CDRRMC recommendation, not simply through a Mayor''s Executive Order.\n\nA State of Imminent Disaster is a separate anticipatory mechanism under RA 12287. The Local Chief Executive may declare it through an Executive Order upon the prescribed Regional DRRM Council recommendation.\n\nThese distinctions are essential to legal compliance.\n\n# 16. Relationship with the Succeeding Chapters\n\nTo avoid duplication and maintain a coherent Manual, the following division of responsibilities is recommended:\n\n| Chapter | Subject | Principal assessment references |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| 1 | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability | A1, B1.1, C1–C5 interfaces |\n| 2 | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management | A2, B4 |\n| 3 | Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness | A3, A5, relevant Part C indicators |\n| 4 | Early Warning, Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response | A6, A7, A8, B1.2–B1.5 |\n| 5 | DRRM Financing, Procurement and Resource Accountability | A4 |\n| 6 | EOC Operations, Information Management, Logistics and Coordination | B2–B7, relevant A indicators |\n| 7 | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement | A5.4–A5.9, A10 |\n| 8 | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better | A9 |\n| 9 | Partnerships, Local Ownership and Institutional Sustainability | A10, relevant Part C indicators |\n\nThis structure should be reconciled with the ten functional areas in Annex A and the eight contractual functional modules. The chapter numbering is a proposed detailed document architecture, not a change to the contractual scope.\n\n# 17. Concluding Notes\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, institutional and governance foundation for the integrated operation of Ormoc City''s CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\nIt recognizes that urban resilience is not produced by a single office or emergency facility. It emerges from the effective interaction of executive leadership, institutional capacity, policy coordination, risk information, financial governance, community participation and operational systems.\n\nThe institutional assessment demonstrates that Ormoc has established important DRRM governance practices, particularly regular CDRRMC meetings, Secretariat functions, DRRM representation in development and financial bodies, and existing planning and partnership mechanisms. However, gaps in legal institutionalization, EOC authority, anticipatory governance and cross-office coordination continue to constrain the effectiveness of the wider system.\n\nThe provisions of this chapter respond to these issues by establishing:\n\nClear institutional mandates and decision rights;\n\nDefined process ownership and responsibility assignments;\n\nStandardized governance workflows;\n\nLegally differentiated declaration procedures;\n\nFormalized institutional and inter-agency interfaces;\n\nRACI-based accountability;\n\nTQM and PDCA-based quality assurance; and\n\nEvidence-based monitoring and continuous improvement.\n\nThe ultimate objective is to move Ormoc City''s DRRM governance from arrangements that are partly dependent on individual initiative toward a legally grounded, process-driven, integrated and continuously improving institutional system.\n\nThis foundation shall support the implementation of the succeeding modules, particularly organizational management, preparedness, EOC operations, information management, resource mobilization, recovery and performance improvement.\n\n# References\n\nThe following references provide the legal and technical foundation for this chapter. The final bibliography should be consolidated with the complete MOO reference register.\n\nDepartment of Budget and Management, Department of the Interior and Local Government, Civil Service Commission, & National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2014). Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2014-1: Implementing guidelines for the establishment of Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Offices or Barangay Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Committees in Local Government Units.[ ](https://www.dbm.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/Issuances/2014/Joint%20Memorandum%20Circular/JMC2014-1%20_LDRRMOs.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[Official DBM issuance](https://www.dbm.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/Issuances/2014/Joint%20Memorandum%20Circular/JMC2014-1%20_LDRRMOs.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2012). Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012: Implementing guidelines on the use of the Incident Command System.\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2026). Implementing Rules and Regulations of Republic Act No. 12287. Signed September 14, 2026, as identified in the assessment workbook.\n\nRepublic of the Philippines. (1991). Republic Act No. 7160: Local Government Code of 1991.\n\nRepublic of the Philippines. (2009). Republic Act No. 9729: Climate Change Act of 2009.\n\nRepublic of the Philippines. (2010). Republic Act No. 10121: Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010.[ ](https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2010/ra_10121_2010.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[Full text of RA 10121](https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2010/ra_10121_2010.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\nRepublic of the Philippines. (2012). Republic Act No. 10173: Data Privacy Act of 2012.\n\nRepublic of the Philippines. (2018). Republic Act No. 11036: Mental Health Act.\n\nRepublic of the Philippines. (2024). Republic Act No. 12076: Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act.\n\nRepublic of the Philippines. (2024). Republic Act No. 12009: New Government Procurement Act.\n\nRepublic of the Philippines. (2025). Republic Act No. 12287: Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act.[ ](https://ldr.senate.gov.ph/legislative-issuance/republic-act-no-12287?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[Official legislative reference](https://ldr.senate.gov.ph/legislative-issuance/republic-act-no-12287?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\nUnited Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2015). Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030.\n\nUnited Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2017). Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities: Detailed level assessment.\n\nUnited Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2018). Making cities resilient 2030: Ten Essentials for Making Cities Resilient.\n\nInternational Organization for Standardization. (2015). ISO 9001:2015: Quality management systems—Requirements.\n\nInternational Organization for Standardization. (2018). ISO 31000:2018: Risk management—Guidelines.\n\nInternational Organization for Standardization. (2019). ISO 22301:2019: Security and resilience—Business continuity management systems—Requirements.\n\n# INSTITUTIONAL IMPLEMENTATION PACKAGE\n\nThis package gives operational effect to Chapter 1 through a coordinated set of proposed legislative and executive instruments, standard operating procedures, responsibility matrices and governance templates. It is an integrated annex set and must be read with Chapter 1, particularly Sections 6–8, 11–14.\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL AND APPROVAL\n\n| Control field | Designation / entry | Control field | Designation / entry |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Document owner | CDRRMO Head | Document custodian | CDRRMO Secretariat |\n| Technical review | CDRRMO / EOC / concerned offices | Legal review | City Legal Office |\n| Administrative review | HRMO / Budget / Accounting / BAC | External technical review | OCD Region VIII, as applicable |\n| Approval | Mayor / CDRRMC / Sanggunian, as legally applicable | Effective date | Upon valid approval / issuance |\n| Review cycle | Annual and upon material legal or institutional change | Supersedes | None – initial consolidated draft |\n\n## Revision History\n\n| Version | Date | Description | Prepared / reviewed by |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| 0.9 | 31 Oct 2026 | Integrated working draft based on uploaded Chapter 1 and assessment-informed implementation package | Prepared for institutional validation |\n\nApproval status: This document is a controlled working draft. No provision becomes an official City policy, ordinance, executive order or operational directive until approved and issued by the authority legally competent to do so.\n\n# Annex Register and Cross-Reference Guide\n\n| Annex | Instrument | Approval / control route |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| 1-A | [Proposed CDRRMO Institutionalization Ordinance](/library/doc/07c8c36f-3092-5f21-8bb0-5df842023f5c) | Sangguniang Panlungsod |\n| 1-B | [Proposed CDRRMC Reconstitution Executive Order](/library/doc/eee45c0c-b84d-5f47-ad35-0d4fab001f7c) | City Mayor |\n| 1-C | [Proposed EOC Establishment Executive Order](/library/doc/ea2a9893-b34d-56d4-b144-106afe26ed06) | City Mayor |\n| 1-D | [SOP GOV-04: State of Calamity Declaration and Lifting](/library/doc/e715b34c-ec5f-5f4c-b34c-378225a2156c) | Approval under City delegation |\n| 1-E | [SOP GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster / Anticipatory Action](/library/doc/f66e432b-487f-5434-a8eb-de42ef848a67) | Approval after RA 12287 IRR validation |\n| 1-F | [Master RACI and Institutional Interface Matrix](/library/doc/2ad29b21-b115-57cb-8bc8-cc638387a41e) | CDRRMC / City approval |\n| 1-G | [Governance Forms GOV-F01 to GOV-F16](/library/doc/1a2135aa-84f2-5ac2-97ff-f5ba295fcf32) | CDRRMO Secretariat custodian |\n| 1-H | [Implementation Roadmap](/library/doc/a0e2909e-16d5-5e0b-b5ca-09486535a866) | City Mayor |\n\nConsistent definitions. CDRRMC means City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council; CDRRMO means permanent City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office;\n\nCONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT • SUBJECT TO LEGAL AND TECHNICAL VALIDATION\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch1_v1.0.md (Chapter 1, v1.0 revised working draft)','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('f63a7512-48ed-5609-a090-fb55b5b77551'::uuid,'07c8c36f-3092-5f21-8bb0-5df842023f5c'::uuid,'v1.0 revised working draft',E'# ANNEX 1-A \\| PROPOSED ORDINANCE – CDRRMO INSTITUTIONALIZATION\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\nDocument status: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n## Republic of the Philippines\n## City of Ormoc\n## Office of the Sangguniang Panlungsod\nORDINANCE NO. \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\n\n**AN ORDINANCE INSTITUTIONALIZING THE CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO) AS A REGULAR DEPARTMENT OF THE CITY GOVERNMENT OF ORMOC, DEFINING ITS ORGANIZATIONAL STRUCTURE, FUNCTIONS, STAFFING, COORDINATION MECHANISMS AND ACCOUNTABILITY SYSTEM, PROVIDING APPROPRIATIONS THEREFOR, AND FOR OTHER PURPOSES**\n\n### Be it ordained by the Sangguniang Panlungsod of Ormoc City, in session assembled:\n### Section 1. Title\nThis Ordinance shall be known as the “Ormoc City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office Institutionalization Ordinance.”\n\n### Section 2. Declaration of Policy\nIt is hereby declared the policy of the City Government of Ormoc to institutionalize an integrated, risk-informed, inclusive and accountable local disaster risk reduction and management system that protects lives, livelihoods, assets, ecosystems and essential services; reduces disaster risks; strengthens climate resilience; and supports preparedness, response, recovery and sustainable development.\n\nThe City shall pursue a whole-of-government and whole-of-society approach, recognizing that disaster risk is shaped by the interaction of hazards, exposure, vulnerability, capacity, land use, infrastructure, ecosystems, governance and socioeconomic conditions.\n\nThe City shall mainstream DRRM and climate change adaptation into local development planning, investment programming, budgeting, land use management, infrastructure development, social protection and public service delivery.\n\n### Section 3. Legal Basis\nThis Ordinance is enacted pursuant to, and shall be interpreted consistently with, the following:\n\n1. Republic Act No. 10121, otherwise known as the Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010, and its Implementing Rules and Regulations;\n2. Republic Act No. 7160, otherwise known as the Local Government Code of 1991;\n3. Republic Act No. 9729, or the Climate Change Act of 2009, as amended by Republic Act No. 10174;\n4. Republic Act No. 11038, or the Expanded National Integrated Protected Areas System Act, where applicable to local ecosystem and protected-area concerns;\n5. Republic Act No. 10173, or the Data Privacy Act of 2012;\n6. Republic Act No. 12287, or the law providing for the declaration of a State of Imminent Disaster and the implementation of anticipatory actions, together with its applicable IRR and subsequent issuances;\n7. The applicable Department of Budget and Management, Civil Service Commission, Commission on Audit, Office of Civil Defense and other national government rules; and\n8. Other relevant national laws, administrative issuances and local ordinances.\n\nLegal drafting note: The City Legal Office shall verify the complete and current titles, provisions, implementing rules and effectivity of the cited laws and issuances before enactment. References to specific funding percentages, lead times, eligibility conditions or documentary requirements under Republic Act No. 12287 and its IRR must be checked against certified official texts and applicable DBM and COA rules.\n\n### Section 4. Establishment and Institutional Status\nThereby institutionalized the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) as a regular department of the City Government of Ormoc, subject to the applicable requirements on local government organizational structures, staffing patterns, position classification, compensation, budgeting and personnel administration.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall serve as the City''s principal technical and administrative office for the coordination, integration, implementation, monitoring and continuous improvement of local DRRM and climate change adaptation programs.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall operate under the supervision of the City Mayor, consistent with the Local Government Code, Republic Act No. 10121 and other applicable laws.\n\nIts institutionalization shall not diminish the statutory authority of the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council (CDRRMC), the Sangguniang Panlungsod, the City Mayor, national government agencies or other city departments and offices.\n\n### Section 5. Mandate and Objectives\nThe CDRRMO shall:\n\na. 	Develop, implement, coordinate and monitor the City''s comprehensive DRRM programs and plans;\nb. 	Support the integration of DRRM and climate change adaptation into local development, land use, investment and budget processes;\nc. 	Maintain and periodically update risk information, hazard and exposure datasets, vulnerability assessments and risk-informed planning inputs in coordination with competent offices and agencies;\nd. 	Strengthen disaster preparedness, early warning, emergency communications, response readiness and recovery planning;\ne. 	Provide technical and Secretariat support to the CDRRMC;\nf. 	Maintain the City''s disaster operations and coordination capability, including the EOC, in accordance with approved activation and operating protocols;\ng. 	Facilitate inter-office, inter-barangay, inter-agency, civil society, private sector and community coordination;\nh. 	Support the organization, training, equipping and readiness of local response teams and volunteer groups;\ni. 	Coordinate the conduct of post-disaster needs assessments, damage and loss assessments, and recovery planning with the responsible offices and agencies;\nj. 	Monitor the implementation and performance of local DRRM programs, projects and activities;\nk. 	Maintain institutional records, operational reports, risk information and lessons learned; and\nl. 	Perform other functions assigned by law or validly delegated by the City Mayor, consistent with the mandate of the Office.\n\n### Section 6. Core Functional Divisions\nSubject to the approved organizational structure and staffing pattern, the CDRRMO shall be organized around the following functional areas:\n\n1. Administration and Finance – personnel administration, procurement coordination, financial and budget management, property and logistics records, administrative support and office continuity.\n2. Research, Planning and Risk Assessment – risk assessment, hazard and exposure information, vulnerability analysis, DRRM and CCA planning, policy development, and mainstreaming into local plans and investments.\n3. Operations and Early Warning – preparedness, early warning coordination, EOC operations, incident information management, response coordination, drills and exercises.\n4. Training, Public Information and Community Resilience – capacity development, public education, risk communication, community-based DRRM, volunteer coordination and inclusive preparedness.\n\nThe functional areas may be implemented through divisions, sections or units, as authorized by the approved organizational structure and staffing pattern. The City shall not create plantilla positions or confer statutory powers solely by virtue of this Ordinance where separate approval or compliance is required.\n\n### Section 7. Head of Office\nThe CDRRMO shall be headed by a City DRRM Officer, appointed in accordance with applicable civil service laws and regulations, subject to the prescribed qualification standards, salary grade, plantilla authorization and other requirements.\n\nThe City DRRM Officer shall:\n\na. 	Lead the administration and technical direction of the CDRRMO;\nb. 	Ensure implementation of approved DRRM policies, plans, programs and projects;\nc. 	Advise the City Mayor and CDRRMC on risk, preparedness, response and recovery matters;\nd. 	Serve as the designated CDRRMC Secretariat head or principal Secretariat coordinator, as provided in the applicable CDRRMC issuance;\ne. 	Ensure that the EOC is maintained and operated in accordance with approved protocols;\nf. 	Coordinate with national government agencies, neighboring local government units, barangays and partner institutions;\ng. 	Ensure proper use, documentation and reporting of DRRM resources; and\nh. 	Submit periodic performance, risk and readiness reports to the City Mayor and CDRRMC.\n\n### Section 8. Staffing and Competency Requirements\nThe City Government shall develop and maintain a staffing pattern appropriate to the City''s risk profile, population, geographic conditions, service demands and operational requirements.\n\nThe staffing pattern shall be consistent with applicable national guidelines, including the relevant joint issuances on LDRRMO organization and staffing, and shall be submitted for the required review and approval.\n\nThe City shall progressively establish competency-based staffing and development arrangements for the following functions:\n\n* Risk assessment and geospatial information management;\n* DRRM planning and policy integration;\n* EOC operations and information management;\n* Early warning and risk communication;\n* Emergency logistics and resource tracking;\n* Training, exercises and community preparedness;\n* Recovery planning and monitoring; and\n* Administration, finance and records management.\n\nThe City shall ensure that position descriptions, qualifications, competency requirements, reporting relationships and performance indicators are documented and periodically reviewed.\n\n### Section 9. Continuous Operations and Personnel Welfare\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain arrangements for continuous operational readiness, including an approved duty roster, contact directory, succession and relief arrangements, office continuity procedures and EOC activation support.\n\nThe City shall provide appropriate personnel safety and welfare measures, subject to applicable laws and available appropriations. These shall include risk-appropriate personal protective equipment, occupational safety measures, responder insurance or other lawful protection mechanisms, medical support, rest and rotation arrangements, and psychosocial support.\n\n### Section 10. Coordination with the CDRRMC\nThe CDRRMO shall provide technical and Secretariat support to the CDRRMC, including:\n\na. 	Preparation of meeting agendas and technical briefs;\nb. 	Consolidation of office and barangay DRRM reports;\nc. 	Documentation of resolutions, decisions and action points;\nd. 	Monitoring of CDRRMC decisions and commitments;\ne. 	Preparation of policy and investment recommendations; and\nf. 	Maintenance of the official CDRRMC records.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall not substitute for the collective deliberative and decision-making authority of the CDRRMC.\n\n### Section 11. Relationship with the Emergency Operations Center\nThe City EOC shall operate as the City''s designated facility and coordination mechanism for monitoring, information consolidation, resource coordination, decision support and operational reporting during emergencies and other authorized activation periods.\n\nThe EOC shall be managed and supported by the CDRRMO under the separate EOC Establishment Executive Order and the EOC Manual of Operations.\n\nThe EOC shall not be construed as a separate local government department or as an independent incident command authority.\n\nWhen an Incident Command System is activated, the EOC shall support coordination, information management, resource support and policy-level decision requirements while the Incident Commander retains incident-level command and control within the scope of the activated ICS organization.\n\n### Section 12. Inter-Office and External Coordination\nAll City Government departments, offices and instrumentalities shall designate DRRM focal persons and provide relevant information and technical support within their respective mandates.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall facilitate coordination but shall not assume the statutory, regulatory or technical responsibilities of other offices.\n\nExamples include:\n\n* CPDO for land use, development planning and investment programming;\n* City Engineering Office and Office of the Building Official for infrastructure and building safety matters;\n* City Health Office and health facilities for public health and medical response;\n* CENRO and relevant DENR offices for environmental and ecosystem management;\n* City Agriculture and Veterinary Office for agriculture, livestock and fisheries concerns;\n* DepEd and other education authorities for school safety and education continuity;\n* utilities and service providers for essential service continuity; and\n* private sector, business organizations and civil society partners for continuity planning, logistics, volunteer support and community resilience.\n\n### Section 13. Planning, Budgeting and Resource Mobilization\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate the preparation, updating and monitoring of the Local DRRM Plan and related investment and preparedness programs.\n\nThe City shall ensure that DRRM and CCA priorities are reflected in the appropriate local development plans, annual investment programs, annual budgets and other planning and financing instruments.\n\nThe Local DRRM Fund and other legally available resources shall be programmed, obligated, disbursed and accounted for in accordance with Republic Act No. 10121, Republic Act No. 12287 where applicable, and the relevant DBM, COA and other government rules.\n\nNothing in this Ordinance shall be construed as authorizing expenditure, fund transfer or procurement outside the applicable budget, accounting, auditing and procurement requirements.\n\n### Section 14. Performance Monitoring and Institutional Accountability\nThe CDRRMO shall establish a performance monitoring and evaluation system based on measurable indicators, verifiable evidence and periodic management review.\n\nAt a minimum, the system shall monitor:\n\na. 	Institutional readiness and compliance;\nb.	Implementation of the Local DRRM Plan;\nc. 	EOC operational readiness and activation performance;\nd. 	Early warning coverage, reach and timeliness;\ne. 	Training, drills and response capability;\nf. 	Resource availability, deployment and accountability;\ng. 	Implementation of corrective actions and lessons learned; and\nh. 	Recovery planning and implementation progress.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall submit an annual institutional performance report to the City Mayor and CDRRMC, with appropriate public disclosure consistent with applicable transparency and data privacy rules.\n\n### Section 15. Records and Information Management\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain an organized records management system covering policies, plans, risk assessments, meeting records, EOC logs, warning records, resource inventories, incident reports, assessments, financial and procurement documents, after-action reviews and performance reports.\n\nSensitive personal information and operationally restricted information shall be handled in accordance with Republic Act No. 10173 and applicable records management and disclosure rules.\n\n### Section 16. Transition and Institutional Strengthening\nWithin one hundred twenty (120) days from the effectivity of this Ordinance, the City Mayor, through the CDRRMO and concerned offices, shall submit to the Sangguniang Panlungsod, as applicable:\n\n1. The proposed organizational structure and staffing pattern;\n2. A position description and competency framework;\n3. A transition and capacity development plan;\n4. A proposed annual work and financial plan;\n5. A CDRRMO–EOC operating and coordination framework; and\n6. A proposed implementation schedule for the MOO and supporting SOPs.\n\nThe City shall prioritize the correction of high-priority institutional gaps identified in the institutional assessment, subject to lawful authority, technical validation and available resources.\n\n### Section 17. Appropriations\nThe amount necessary for the initial implementation of this Ordinance shall be charged against available appropriations of the City Government, subject to applicable budgeting, accounting and auditing rules.\n\nThereafter, such sums as may be necessary shall be included in the annual city budget, subject to the usual budgetary process.\n\n### Section 18. Implementing Rules and Regulations\nWithin ninety (90) days from the effectivity of this Ordinance, the City Mayor, upon recommendation of the CDRRMO and in consultation with the concerned offices, shall issue the necessary implementing rules and administrative guidelines, consistent with this Ordinance and applicable national laws.\n\n### Section 19. Separability Clause\nIf any provision of this Ordinance is declared invalid or unconstitutional, the remaining provisions not affected thereby shall continue in full force and effect.\n\n### Section 20. Repealing and Amending Clause\nAll local ordinances, resolutions, executive orders, rules and regulations inconsistent with this Ordinance are hereby repealed, amended or modified accordingly, to the extent of such inconsistency.\n\n### Section 21. Effectivity\nThis Ordinance shall take effect in accordance with the applicable provisions of the Local Government Code and other laws governing the enactment and effectivity of local ordinances.\n\nENACTED this \\_\\_\\_\\_ day of \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ 2026, in the City of Ormoc, Philippines.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\nPRESIDING OFFICER / VICE MAYOR\n\n**CERTIFIED CORRECT**: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ Secretary to the Sanggunian\n\nAPPROVED, if required by law: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ City Mayor\n\n**LEGAL REVIEW**: Confirm whether an existing ordinance already establishes the CDRRMO; determine new versus amendatory route; align structure and plantilla with JMC 2014-1, DBM/CSC rules; add fiscal note and identify affected prior issuances.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-A.md (Chapter 1, v1.0 revised working draft)','draft'),
('50c393c5-e996-54a6-8d60-8bb004da6f5d'::uuid,'eee45c0c-b84d-5f47-ad35-0d4fab001f7c'::uuid,'v1.0 revised working draft',E'# ANNEX 1-B | PROPOSED EXECUTIVE ORDER – CDRRMC\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\n**Document status**: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n## Republic of the Philippines\n\n## City of Ormoc\n\n## Office of the City Mayor\n\n**EXECUTIVE ORDER NO. \\_\\_\\_\\_**\n**Series of 2026**\n\n**AN EXECUTIVE ORDER RECONSTITUTING AND UPDATING THE MEMBERSHIP, COMMITTEE STRUCTURE, SECRETARIAT ARRANGEMENTS AND GOVERNANCE PROCEDURES OF THE ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT COUNCIL**\n\n### WHEREAS\n\nRepublic Act No. 10121 mandates the establishment and operation of Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Councils to coordinate, integrate, supervise and evaluate local DRRM activities;\n\nWHEREAS, the changing risk landscape of Ormoc City requires stronger institutional coordination, risk-informed planning, inclusive governance, anticipatory action, preparedness, response and recovery arrangements;\n\nWHEREAS, the City Government recognizes the need to update the composition and operational arrangements of the CDRRMC to ensure representation of relevant city offices, national government agencies, civil society organizations, private sector institutions and other stakeholders, consistent with applicable law;\n\nWHEREAS, the City seeks to clarify the relationship between the CDRRMC, CDRRMO, City Emergency Operations Center and Incident Command System;\n\nNOW, THEREFORE, I, the City Mayor of Ormoc, by virtue of the powers vested in me by law, hereby order:\n\n### Section 1. Reconstitution\n\nThe Ormoc City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council is hereby reconstituted in accordance with Republic Act No. 10121 and its Implementing Rules and Regulations.\n\nThe Council shall serve as the principal local multi-stakeholder policy and coordination body for DRRM, subject to its statutory powers and responsibilities.\n\n### Section 2. Composition\n\nThe CDRRMC shall be composed of the following, consistent with the statutory composition prescribed under Republic Act No. 10121:\n\n**Chairperson**\n\n- City Mayor\n\n**Vice-Chairpersons**\n\n- The heads of the designated city offices or departments responsible for prevention and mitigation, preparedness, response, and rehabilitation and recovery, consistent with the applicable statutory and regulatory framework.\n\n**Members**\n\n- Heads of relevant City Government departments and offices;\n- Representatives of concerned national government agencies operating in or serving Ormoc City;\n- Liga ng mga Barangay representative;\n- Philippine National Police;\n- Bureau of Fire Protection;\n- Philippine Coast Guard, where applicable;\n- Armed Forces of the Philippines, where applicable;\n- Relevant health, education, infrastructure, environment, social welfare, agriculture and utilities institutions;\n- At least four (4) accredited civil society organization representatives, subject to applicable accreditation and selection requirements;\n- Private sector representatives; and\n- Other institutions or individuals invited by the Chairperson when their technical expertise or institutional mandate is relevant.\n\nThe City Legal Office shall validate the final membership list against the exact statutory composition and applicable IRR. The membership list shall be attached as Annex A to the signed Executive Order.\n\n### Section 3. Functions of the Council\n\nThe CDRRMC shall perform the functions prescribed by Republic Act No. 10121 and its IRR, including:\n\na. Approving, coordinating, integrating, supervising and evaluating the implementation of the Local DRRM Plan;\nb. Ensuring DRRM and climate change adaptation are mainstreamed into local development plans, programs and budgets;\nc. Recommending the declaration and lifting of a State of Calamity, consistent with law;\nd. Providing recommendations and technical inputs for the declaration of a State of Imminent Disaster, where applicable;\ne. Coordinating the mobilization of resources and services of concerned offices and partner institutions;\nf. Supporting inter-LGU and inter-agency coordination;\ng. Ensuring that the needs of vulnerable and at-risk sectors are considered in policies, plans and operational decisions;\nh. Reviewing disaster preparedness, response and recovery performance; and\ni. Performing other functions mandated by law.\n\n### Section 4. Standing Governance Committees\n\nThe CDRRMC shall organize the following functional committees, subject to the statutory cluster structure and the approved Local DRRM Plan:\n\n1. Prevention and Mitigation;\n2. Preparedness;\n3. Response;\n4. Rehabilitation and Recovery;\n5. Risk Information, Planning and Mainstreaming; and\n6. Governance, Finance and Resource Mobilization.\n\nThe committees shall be chaired or co-chaired by the appropriate lead offices based on their statutory mandates and technical responsibilities.\n\nThe CDRRMC Secretariat shall maintain a committee directory identifying the lead office, members, focal persons, deliverables, reporting frequency and escalation arrangements.\n\nThe committees shall not create parallel command structures or duplicate the statutory responsibilities of existing offices.\n\n### Section 5. CDRRMC Secretariat\n\nThe CDRRMO shall serve as the permanent Secretariat of the CDRRMC, consistent with Republic Act No. 10121 and its IRR.\n\nThe Secretariat shall:\n\na. Prepare and circulate meeting notices, agendas and technical documents;\nb. Maintain the official membership and focal-person directory;\nc. Record minutes, resolutions, attendance and action points;\nd. Maintain the CDRRMC decision and action tracker;\ne. Consolidate committee reports and technical recommendations;\nf. Monitor the implementation of Council decisions;\ng. Prepare periodic governance and performance reports; and\nh. Maintain the official records and archives of the Council.\n\nThe Secretariat shall distinguish between recommendations submitted for Council deliberation and decisions formally adopted by the Council.\n\n### Section 6. Meetings and Quorum\n\nThe CDRRMC shall meet at least once every quarter and may convene special meetings when warranted by emerging risks, preparedness requirements, operational developments or other urgent matters.\n\nThe Chairperson may call a special meeting upon recommendation of the CDRRMO or upon request of a sufficient number of Council members, subject to the Council''s adopted rules.\n\nQuorum, voting and adoption of resolutions shall be governed by applicable law and the Council''s duly approved internal rules. The Secretariat shall record attendance, quorum determination, decisions, dissenting views where formally raised, and assigned actions.\n\n### Section 7. Activation for Emergencies\n\nThe CDRRMC may convene or activate its coordination arrangements when the situation requires strategic policy guidance, inter-agency coordination, resource mobilization or decisions within its statutory mandate.\n\nOperational coordination shall be supported by the EOC under its separate Establishment Executive Order.\n\nWhere an ICS structure is activated, incident-level command shall remain with the designated Incident Commander. The CDRRMC shall provide policy direction and inter-agency support within its lawful authority and shall not issue conflicting tactical instructions to field response personnel.\n\n### Section 8. Civil Society and Private Sector Participation\n\nThe Secretariat shall maintain a transparent and documented process for the participation of accredited CSOs and relevant private sector representatives.\n\nThe process shall:\n\na. Verify accreditation or eligibility in accordance with applicable rules;\nb. Ensure representation of relevant sectors and communities;\nc. Document the selection or designation process;\nd. Provide access to non-confidential Council information and meeting schedules; and\ne. Establish appropriate safeguards for conflicts of interest, confidentiality and data protection.\n\n### Section 9. Reporting and Performance Review\n\nThe CDRRMC shall conduct an annual review of the implementation of the Local DRRM Plan, institutional readiness, risk reduction investments, preparedness and response performance, and recovery progress.\n\nThe Secretariat shall maintain a consolidated action tracker and submit periodic implementation reports to the Chairperson.\n\n### Section 10. Initial Compliance Actions\n\nWithin sixty (60) days from the effectivity of this Executive Order, the Secretariat shall:\n\n1. Validate and update the CDRRMC membership;\n2. Confirm the representation of at least four accredited CSOs and the relevant private sector;\n3. Issue the updated directory of Council members and committee focal persons;\n4. Prepare the annual meeting calendar;\n5. Consolidate existing Council resolutions and outstanding action points;\n6. Submit proposed internal rules and Secretariat SOPs; and\n7. Establish the CDRRMC decision and action tracker.\n\n### Section 11. Repealing Clause\n\nAll previous executive orders or administrative issuances inconsistent with this Executive Order are hereby amended or superseded to the extent of such inconsistency.\n\n### Section 12. Effectivity\n\nThis Executive Order shall take effect immediately upon signing and shall be disseminated to all concerned offices and institutions.\n\nDONE in the City of Ormoc, Philippines, this \\_\\_\\_\\_ day of \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ 2026.\n\nCITY MAYOR\nCity Government of Ormoc\n\n## Appendix A. CDRRMC Membership and Designation Register\n\n| Position / institution | Designated representative | Alternate | Contact details | Designation reference |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Chairperson – City Mayor | To be completed | Authorized alternate, if lawful | To be completed | EO |\n| Vice-Chairperson – Prevention and Mitigation | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n| Vice-Chairperson – Preparedness | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n| Vice-Chairperson – Response | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n| Vice-Chairperson – Rehabilitation and Recovery | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n| CDRRMO / Secretariat | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n| Liga ng mga Barangay | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n| PNP / BFP / other statutory members | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n| National government agencies | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n| Accredited CSO representatives (minimum four) | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | Accreditation / designation |\n| Private sector representatives | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | Designation |\n| Other relevant members | To be completed | To be completed | To be completed | EO |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-B.md (Chapter 1, v1.0 revised working draft)','draft'),
('05b81333-d0aa-568a-8e48-f7e00f7524a1'::uuid,'ea2a9893-b34d-56d4-b144-106afe26ed06'::uuid,'v1.0 revised working draft',E'# ANNEX 1-C | PROPOSED EXECUTIVE ORDER – CITY EOC\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\n**Document status**: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n> **Review flag (platform, 2 Nov 2026):** Section 4 uses Levels 0–3. The National Disaster Response Plan and the RDRRMC VIII SOP (Res. No. 12-006, s. 2023), the EOC board in this platform and SOP GOV-05 use the White, Blue and Red alert levels; NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 lets each EOC set its own codes (para. 9.2.2). Settle one scheme before the 1–4 Dec validation workshop, or add a crosswalk (for example Level 0–1 = White, Level 2 = Blue, Level 3 = Red).\n\n## Republic of the Philippines\n\n## City of Ormoc\n\n## Office of the City Mayor\n\n**EXECUTIVE ORDER NO. \\_\\_\\_\\_**\n**Series of 2026**\n\n**AN EXECUTIVE ORDER ESTABLISHING THE ORMOC CITY EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER, PROVIDING FOR ITS ORGANIZATION, ACTIVATION LEVELS, OPERATING ARRANGEMENTS, INFORMATION MANAGEMENT, RESOURCE COORDINATION AND INTERFACE WITH THE INCIDENT COMMAND SYSTEM**\n\n### Section 1. Establishment\n\nThe Ormoc City Emergency Operations Center (EOC) is hereby established as the City''s principal facility and coordination mechanism for emergency monitoring, information management, inter-office coordination, resource support, decision support and operational reporting.\n\nThe EOC shall be maintained and managed by the CDRRMO under the supervision of the City Mayor and in accordance with the CDRRMC''s policies and the Manual of Operations.\n\n### Section 2. Purpose\n\nThe EOC shall:\n\na. Maintain situational awareness of hazards, threats, incidents and emerging risks;\nb. Receive, validate, consolidate and disseminate operational information;\nc. Support early warning and public information coordination;\nd. Facilitate coordination among City Government offices, barangays, national agencies, utilities, CSOs and private sector partners;\ne. Track resource availability, requests, allocation and deployment;\nf. Support strategic decision-making by the City Mayor and CDRRMC;\ng. Maintain a common operating picture;\nh. Support the activation and coordination of response arrangements; and\ni. Maintain operational records, reports and lessons learned.\n\n### Section 3. Institutional Relationship\n\nThe EOC shall be:\n\n1. Operationally managed by the CDRRMO;\n2. Available to support the CDRRMC''s policy and coordination functions;\n3. Activated through the procedures established in the MOO;\n4. Linked to barangay-level monitoring and reporting arrangements;\n5. Connected to relevant national and regional warning and coordination systems; and\n6. Interoperable with the Incident Command System when activated.\n\nThe EOC shall not be construed as:\n\n- A separate local government department;\n- A substitute for the CDRRMC;\n- A substitute for the CDRRMO;\n- A field command post unless expressly designated for a specific incident and appropriately organized; or\n- An authority empowered to override the Incident Commander or the lawful command structure of responding agencies.\n\n### Section 4. EOC Activation Levels\n\nThe EOC shall operate under the following activation levels. The exact criteria and triggers shall be detailed in the EOC MOO and hazard-specific SOPs.\n\n| Level | Operating condition | Minimum staffing and function |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Level 0 – Routine Monitoring | No imminent threat or major incident; routine monitoring | Duty officer, monitoring and communications personnel, scheduled reporting |\n| Level 1 – Enhanced Monitoring | Elevated threat, weather disturbance, warning advisory or developing incident | Expanded monitoring, focal-person notification, situation updates and readiness checks |\n| Level 2 – Partial Activation | Significant incident or credible threat requiring multi-office coordination | Core EOC team, operations and planning functions, resource tracking and regular situation reports |\n| Level 3 – Full Activation | Major emergency, multiple affected areas, significant resource demand or City-level coordination requirement | Full EOC staffing, executive coordination, structured information and resource management, continuous operational reporting |\n\nThe activation level shall be determined by the authorized official under the approved activation protocol, based on verified information, hazard-specific thresholds and the scale and complexity of the situation.\n\nThe EOC activation level shall not automatically determine the activation level of an ICS organization. The EOC and ICS may be activated at different scales depending on incident needs.\n\n### Section 5. Authority to Activate\n\nThe City Mayor shall have the authority to direct EOC activation, consistent with law and the City''s approved protocols.\n\nThe City DRRM Officer or designated duty officer may initiate or recommend activation based on verified hazard information, official warnings, field reports or other credible evidence.\n\nIn urgent situations, the designated duty officer may initiate immediate notification and minimum readiness measures under the approved protocol, subject to prompt confirmation by the authorized official.\n\nThe activation protocol shall specify who may approve activation, who may act in the absence of the primary authority, and how the decision shall be recorded.\n\n### Section 6. EOC Functional Organization\n\nThe EOC shall be organized around the following functions, scaled according to activation level:\n\n1. EOC Management / Coordination – oversees EOC operations, internal coordination and reporting to the authorized city leadership;\n2. Operations – consolidates field information, tracks operational activities and coordinates with response agencies and barangays;\n3. Planning and Situation Analysis – maintains the common operating picture, situation reports, projections and planning products;\n4. Logistics – coordinates resource availability, requests, procurement or deployment referrals, facilities, transport and support services;\n5. Administration and Finance – supports documentation of expenditures, personnel time, administrative requirements and financial records;\n6. Public Information and Risk Communication – coordinates validated public information, warning messages, media releases and community advisories; and\n7. Safety and Liaison – supports personnel safety coordination and liaison with partner agencies, as appropriate to the scale of operations.\n\nThe EOC functional organization may be adapted to the incident, but assigned functions and reporting relationships shall be documented.\n\n### Section 7. EOC Manager\n\nThe City DRRM Officer shall designate an EOC Manager for each activation, subject to the approval arrangements in the MOO, and corresponding Contingency Plan.\n\nThe EOC Manager shall:\n\na. Direct the internal functioning of the EOC;\nb. Ensure that assigned functional positions are staffed;\nc. Maintain coordination with the City Mayor, CDRRMC and Incident Commander, as appropriate;\nd. Ensure that information is validated and properly disseminated;\ne. Maintain the common operating picture and action tracker;\nf. Coordinate resource requests and inter-office support;\ng. Ensure the preparation and submission of situation reports; and\nh. Ensure the orderly deactivation and archiving of EOC records.\n\nThe EOC Manager is responsible for EOC coordination and administration and does not automatically become the Incident Commander.\n\n### Section 8. Incident Command System Interface\n\nWhere an incident requires an ICS structure, the designated Incident Commander shall exercise incident-level command and control in accordance with applicable ICS rules and the activated incident organization.\n\nThe EOC shall:\n\n- Receive and consolidate situation reports from the Incident Command Post;\n- Facilitate resource requests and inter-agency coordination;\n- Provide policy and strategic information to the City Mayor and CDRRMC;\n- Maintain citywide situational awareness;\n- Coordinate support from offices not directly assigned to the incident organization; and\n- Avoid issuing conflicting tactical instructions to field teams.\n\nThe Incident Commander shall communicate operational priorities, resource requirements and incident developments through the established reporting channel.\n\n### Section 9. Barangay and Community Reporting Interface\n\nThe EOC shall maintain a designated reporting and communications arrangement with all barangays.\n\nEach barangay shall identify authorized focal persons and alternates for:\n\na. Hazard monitoring and local observations;\nb. Receipt and acknowledgment of warnings;\nc. Reporting of incidents and affected populations;\nd. Evacuation and protective action updates;\ne. Requests for assistance and resources; and\nf. Confirmation of service restoration and return conditions.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall establish backup communication methods for barangays with weak or disrupted telecommunications.\n\n### Section 10. Information Management and Common Operating Picture\n\nThe EOC shall maintain a Common Operating Picture (COP) based on verified and time-stamped information.\n\nAt a minimum, the COP shall contain:\n\n- Incident and hazard status;\n- Geographic location and affected areas;\n- Official warning and advisory status;\n- Population and facilities potentially or actually affected;\n- Evacuation status and displacement information;\n- Critical infrastructure and lifeline status;\n- Response actions and responsible agencies;\n- Resource availability, requests and deployment status;\n- Access constraints and urgent needs; and\n- Key decisions, outstanding actions and next reporting deadlines.\n\nInformation shall be classified as verified, provisional or unverified. Unverified information shall not be presented as confirmed fact in public advisories or executive situation reports.\n\n### Section 11. Communications and Public Information\n\nThe EOC shall coordinate with authorized public information offices and relevant technical agencies to ensure that public advisories are accurate, timely, consistent and accessible.\n\nWarnings shall use appropriate languages and formats for affected communities, including Cebuano/Ormocanon and accessible formats where needed.\n\nA warning message shall, at minimum, identify:\n\n1. The hazard and affected location;\n2. The expected timing and level of threat;\n3. The population or sector at risk;\n4. The required protective action;\n5. The responsible source or issuing authority;\n6. The time of issuance and next update; and\n7. Available assistance or reporting channels.\n\n### Section 12. Resource Management\n\nThe EOC shall maintain a resource status and tracking system covering personnel, equipment, vehicles, supplies, facilities and other available response resources.\n\nAll requests, approvals, dispatches, transfers, receipts and returns shall be documented.\n\nThe EOC shall coordinate resource support but shall not bypass the procurement, accounting, property, budget and audit controls applicable to the City Government.\n\n### Section 13. Duty Roster and Continuous Operations\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain an approved EOC duty roster and alternate roster.\n\nThe roster shall specify:\n\n- Primary and alternate personnel;\n- Duty schedules;\n- Reporting and handover requirements;\n- Contact details and escalation channels;\n- Minimum staffing per activation level;\n- Rest and relief arrangements; and\n- Access to EOC facilities and equipment.\n\n### Section 14. Training, Exercises and Evaluation\n\nThe City shall conduct periodic EOC orientation, functional exercises, communications tests and full-scale or multi-agency exercises as appropriate.\n\nAfter each activation or exercise, the CDRRMO shall conduct an After-Action Review and prepare an Improvement Plan identifying corrective actions, owners, deadlines and verification requirements.\n\n### Section 15. Initial Implementation Requirements\n\nWithin sixty (60) days from effectivity, the CDRRMO shall submit for approval:\n\n1. The EOC organization chart and functional position descriptions;\n2. EOC activation and deactivation SOP;\n3. EOC duty roster and succession arrangements;\n4. Communications and contact directory;\n5. Common Operating Picture and situation report templates;\n6. Resource request and tracking forms;\n7. EOC–ICS interface protocol;\n8. Barangay reporting and last-mile communication protocol; and\n9. Minimum equipment, facility and continuity requirements.\n\n### Section 16. Effectivity\n\nThis Executive Order shall take effect immediately upon signing and shall be disseminated to all concerned offices and partner institutions.\n\nDONE in the City of Ormoc, Philippines, this \\_\\_\\_\\_ day of \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ 2026.\n\nCITY MAYOR\nCity Government of Ormoc\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-C.md (Chapter 1, v1.0 revised working draft)','draft'),
('6d7c0e1d-0f9b-5b5e-9b8a-e1135ee9da81'::uuid,'e715b34c-ec5f-5f4c-b34c-378225a2156c'::uuid,'v1.0 revised working draft',E'# ANNEX 1-D \\| SOP GOV-04 – STATE OF CALAMITY\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n**Document status**: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n## STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURE GOV-04: STATE OF CALAMITY DECLARATION, IMPLEMENTATION AND LIFTING\n**SOP-DRRM-GOV-04 · Draft for legal validation and adoption by the appropriate local authority**\n\n## 1. Purpose\nThis SOP establishes a consistent, evidence-based and auditable process for recommending, declaring, implementing and lifting a State of Calamity in Ormoc City.\n\nIt is intended to ensure that the decision is based on documented damage assessment, needs analysis and applicable legal criteria, and that the resulting interventions are coordinated, time-bound, properly funded and subject to monitoring.\n\n## 2. Legal Basis\nThe principal legal basis is Section 16 of Republic Act No. 10121 and its applicable IRR, together with relevant provisions of the Local Government Code and applicable national and local issuances.\n\n**Statutory decision rule**: The Sangguniang Panlungsod declares and lifts a State of Calamity upon the recommendation of the LDRRMC, based on the assessment of damage and needs. The SOP must not be interpreted as authorizing the City Mayor, CDRRMO or EOC to independently exercise the sanggunian''s statutory declaration or lifting authority.\n\n## 3. Scope\nThis SOP applies to:\n\n* The CDRRMC;\n* CDRRMO and EOC;\n* City Government departments and offices;\n* Barangays;\n* Concerned national government agencies;\n* Local response organizations; and\n* Other institutions requested to support assessment, response and recovery.\n\n## 4. Guiding Principles\n1. **Legality**: All recommendations and actions shall comply with applicable law.\n2. **Evidence**: Decisions shall be supported by verified damage, needs and impact information.\n3. **Proportionality**: Recommended measures shall be appropriate to the scale, severity and duration of the emergency.\n4. **Inclusiveness**: Assessments shall identify the differentiated needs of women, children, older persons, persons with disabilities, Indigenous Peoples where applicable, low-income households and other at-risk groups.\n5. **Accountability**: Decisions, resource commitments and actions shall be recorded and traceable.\n6. **Coordination**: All offices shall operate through agreed reporting and coordination arrangements.\n7. **Recovery orientation:** Declaration shall support timely response and recovery, not substitute for longer-term risk reduction and reconstruction planning.\n\n## 5. Triggers for Assessment and Consideration\nA State of Calamity may be considered when a disaster has occurred and the available evidence indicates that the applicable statutory criteria have been met.\n\nPotential triggers include:\n\n* Significant loss of life or injury;\n* Extensive damage to homes, public infrastructure, livelihoods or essential services;\n* Large-scale displacement or evacuation;\n* Disruption of critical lifelines or basic services;\n* Emergency needs exceeding the capacity of affected communities or local response arrangements; and\n* Other conditions prescribed by applicable law and regulations.\n\nThese are operational screening considerations, not substitutes for the exact statutory criteria. The City Legal Office shall verify the legal threshold and documentary requirements before adoption.\n\n## 6. Roles and Responsibilities\n| Actor | Primary responsibility |\n| ----- | ----- |\n| Barangay DRRM Committees | Initial incident reporting, local validation, affected-population information and urgent needs reporting |\n| CDRRMO | Coordinates assessment process, consolidates evidence and prepares technical recommendation |\n| EOC | Maintains incident log, COP, situation reports and resource status |\n| City Engineering / Building Official | Assesses damage to buildings, public facilities and infrastructure within their mandates |\n| City Health Office | Assesses health impacts, medical needs and public health risks |\n| City Social Welfare and Development Office | Validates displacement, household needs and social protection requirements |\n| City Agriculture / Veterinary Office | Assesses damage to crops, livestock, fisheries and agricultural livelihoods |\n| CPDO | Supports spatial analysis, development impact analysis and recovery planning |\n| Other technical offices | Assess impacts within their statutory and technical mandates |\n| CDRRMC | Reviews evidence and adopts the recommendation within its mandate |\n| City Mayor | Convenes and provides executive coordination; transmits the recommendation to the sanggunian |\n| Sangguniang Panlungsod | Exercises the statutory authority to declare or lift the State of Calamity |\n\n## 4.7 Procedure\n### Step 1. Incident reporting and initial validation\nThe barangay or concerned office shall report the incident to the CDRRMO or EOC through the established reporting channel.\n\nThe EOC shall record:\n\n* Date and time of incident;\n* Location and affected barangays;\n* Hazard type and available official warning information;\n* Initial reported casualties and affected households;\n* Initial damage and service disruption;\n* Immediate needs; and\n* Source and verification status of the information.\n\nThe EOC shall distinguish between initial reports and validated findings.\n\n### Step 2. Activation of assessment arrangements\nThe City DRRM Officer shall recommend the activation of the appropriate assessment teams or technical working groups.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate with relevant offices to define:\n\n* Assessment objectives;\n* Geographic coverage;\n* Sectoral responsibilities;\n* Data collection methods;\n* Reporting timelines;\n* Required technical expertise; and\n* Consolidation arrangements.\n\nThe assessment shall use applicable national tools and methodologies, including relevant damage assessment, needs analysis and post-disaster assessment protocols.\n\n### Step 3. Conduct of damage and needs assessment\nEach responsible office shall assess impacts within its mandate.\n\nThe assessment shall, as applicable, document:\n\n1. Human impacts, including deaths, injuries, missing persons and displacement;\n2. Housing damage and habitability;\n3. Damage to public buildings and infrastructure;\n4. Disruption of water, power, telecommunications, transport and other lifelines;\n5. Health, sanitation and protection needs;\n6. Agriculture, fisheries, livestock and livelihood losses;\n7. Education and social service disruption;\n8. Environmental impacts and access constraints;\n9. Priority needs of vulnerable sectors; and\n10. Estimated response and early recovery requirements.\n\nEach report shall identify the assessment date, geographic coverage, methodology, responsible assessor, source records and level of confidence.\n\n### Step 4. Consolidation and technical analysis\nThe CDRRMO shall consolidate the sectoral reports and prepare a citywide assessment summary.\n\nThe EOC shall support consolidation by maintaining:\n\n* Incident chronology;\n* Affected-area map;\n* COP;\n* Resource and assistance tracker;\n* Situation reports; and\n* Outstanding information requests.\n\nThe consolidated report shall identify information gaps and distinguish confirmed figures from estimates.\n\n### Step 5. CDRRMC deliberation and recommendation\nThe CDRRMC shall review the consolidated assessment and determine whether the evidence supports recommending the declaration of a State of Calamity.\n\nThe deliberation shall consider:\n\n* Applicable statutory criteria;\n* Scale and severity of impacts;\n* Geographic extent;\n* Capacity of affected barangays and City Government;\n* Urgency and nature of required interventions;\n* Expected duration of disruption;\n* Resource and financing implications; and\n* Risks of further deterioration.\n\nThe Council''s recommendation shall be documented in a resolution or other legally appropriate instrument, supported by the assessment report.\n\n### Step 6. Submission to the Sangguniang Panlungsod\nThe CDRRMC Secretariat shall transmit the recommendation, assessment report and supporting documents to the City Mayor and Sangguniang Panlungsod through the appropriate official channels.\n\nThe submission shall include:\n\n* CDRRMC resolution or recommendation;\n* Consolidated damage and needs assessment;\n* Maps and sectoral reports;\n* Summary of affected population and facilities;\n* Proposed geographic coverage;\n* Recommended scope and duration of emergency measures; and\n* Other documents required by law or the sanggunian.\n\n### Step 7. Sangguniang Panlungsod action\nThe Sangguniang Panlungsod shall deliberate and act on the recommendation in accordance with applicable law and its rules of procedure.\n\nIf approved, the declaration shall be embodied in the appropriate sanggunian resolution or other legally required instrument.\n\nThe resolution shall clearly state:\n\n* The legal basis;\n* The affected geographic areas;\n* The factual basis and assessment references;\n* The date of effectivity;\n* The authorized measures and relevant limitations; and\n* Any reporting or review requirements.\n\n### Step 8. Dissemination and implementation\nUpon declaration, the CDRRMO and EOC shall coordinate dissemination to:\n\n* All City Government offices;\n* Barangays;\n* Concerned national agencies;\n* Utilities and service providers;\n* CSOs and private sector partners; and\n* Affected communities through appropriate communication channels.\n\nEach responsible office shall implement measures within its legal mandate and approved authority.\n\nThe declaration shall not be treated as an automatic exemption from procurement, accounting, auditing, environmental, building safety, data protection or other applicable requirements.\n\n### Step 9. Monitoring and reporting\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a consolidated monitoring record covering:\n\n* Response and early recovery actions;\n* Resource mobilization and utilization;\n* Assistance delivered;\n* Unmet needs;\n* Service restoration;\n* Population movement and displacement;\n* Emerging risks; and\n* Progress toward recovery.\n\nThe EOC shall maintain operational logs and situation reports for the duration of activation.\n\n### Step 10. Review and recommendation for lifting\nThe CDRRMO shall periodically review whether the conditions that justified the declaration continue to exist.\n\nThe review shall consider:\n\n* Restoration of essential services;\n* Reduction of immediate life-safety threats;\n* Evacuation and displacement status;\n* Access to affected communities;\n* Remaining urgent humanitarian needs;\n* Status of critical infrastructure;\n* Capacity of local systems to manage residual impacts; and\n* Transition from emergency response to recovery arrangements.\n\nWhen conditions warrant, the CDRRMC shall deliberate and recommend lifting the State of Calamity to the Sangguniang Panlungsod.\n\n### Step 11. Lifting by the Sangguniang Panlungsod\nThe Sangguniang Panlungsod shall exercise the statutory authority to lift the declaration, upon recommendation of the CDRRMC and in accordance with applicable law.\n\nThe lifting instrument shall specify the geographic scope, effective date and any continuing administrative or recovery arrangements.\n\nLifting the declaration shall not automatically terminate ongoing recovery, rehabilitation, social protection or reconstruction programs.\n\n### Step 12. Post-incident review and records closure\nWithin the period prescribed by the MOO or applicable policy, the CDRRMO shall convene an After-Action Review with the participating offices and partners.\n\nThe review shall identify:\n\n* What worked;\n* What did not work;\n* Delays and coordination failures;\n* Resource and capability gaps;\n* Data and communication issues;\n* Good practices;\n* Corrective actions; and\n* Responsible offices and completion dates.\n\nThe Secretariat shall archive the declaration, assessment, resolutions, reports, operational logs and post-incident review.\n\n## 4.8 Process Flowchart\n![Annex 1-D process flow](/library/ch1/annex-1-D-fig-01.webp)\n\n## 4.9 Mandatory Records\nThe following records shall be maintained:\n\n* Initial Incident Report;\n* EOC Incident Log;\n* Assessment Activation Order or Memorandum;\n* Sectoral Assessment Reports;\n* Consolidated Damage and Needs Assessment;\n* Maps and affected-area information;\n* CDRRMC meeting minutes and resolution;\n* Submission to the Sangguniang Panlungsod;\n* Declaration or non-approval record;\n* Resource and assistance tracker;\n* Situation reports;\n* Review and lifting recommendation;\n* Lifting instrument; and\n* After-Action Review and Improvement Plan.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-D.md (Chapter 1, v1.0 revised working draft)','draft'),
('4f071563-388d-55b4-906a-844bad3c42fd'::uuid,'f66e432b-487f-5434-a8eb-de42ef848a67'::uuid,'v1.0 revised working draft',E'# SOP 1 \\- GOV-05: Standard Operating Procedure for Declaration of a State of Imminent Disaster and Implementation of Anticipatory Actions\nSOP-DRRM-GOV-05 · Draft for validation against Republic Act No. 12287, its certified IRR and applicable funding rules\n\n## 5.1 Purpose\nThis SOP establishes the City''s procedures for identifying an imminent disaster threat, evaluating credible forecasts and impact information, recommending a State of Imminent Disaster (SID), supporting the City Mayor''s declaration by Executive Order, implementing anticipatory actions, managing resources and documenting results.\n\nThe purpose is to enable timely action before disaster impacts occur, where credible forecasts, warnings and risk information indicate that a hazard may cause serious impacts within a defined period.\n\n## 5.2 Legal Basis and Legal Safeguards\nThis SOP shall be implemented in accordance with Republic Act No. 12287, its Implementing Rules and Regulations, and other applicable laws and issuances.\n\nThe SOP shall preserve the following distinctions:\n\n1. A State of Imminent Disaster is a legal declaration relating to an impending disaster threat under the statutory framework.\n2. A Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment (PDRA) is a technical process for assessing the potential impacts of a forecast or anticipated hazard.\n3. Anticipatory actions are time-bound actions implemented before the anticipated impacts to reduce expected loss and harm.\n4. The declaration and anticipatory actions shall be based on the applicable statutory recommendation, evidence and procedural requirements.\n5. The City Mayor''s authority to declare by Executive Order shall be exercised only upon the recommendation required by law. The exact role and sequence of the Regional DRRM Council and other competent authorities must be followed.\n6. The declaration does not dispense with applicable budget, procurement, accounting, auditing, data privacy or other legal controls.\n\nImportant validation requirement: Before this SOP is adopted, the City Legal Office and CDRRMO must verify the certified text of Republic Act No. 12287 and its IRR, particularly the provisions on the recommending authority, declaration requirements, anticipatory action protocols, fund eligibility, lead times, social amelioration, data sharing and post-lifting reporting. The assessment workbook records specific requirements, including a 45-day post-lifting report and certain fund-use and tranche provisions. Those details must be checked against the certified IRR and applicable DBM and COA rules before they are incorporated into binding city procedures.\n\n## 5.3 Scope\nThis SOP applies to the City Mayor, CDRRMC, CDRRMO, EOC, City Government departments, barangays, relevant national government agencies, utilities, CSOs and other partners involved in anticipatory action.\n\nIt applies to hazards for which credible forecasts, warnings or other recognized technical information can provide sufficient lead time for protective action.\n\n## 5.4 Operational Principles\n* Forecast-informed: Use authoritative forecasts and warnings from competent technical agencies.\n* Impact-based: Assess who and what may be affected, where, when and how severely.\n* Action-oriented: Link every trigger to a defined protective action, responsible office, resource requirement and deadline.\n* Time-critical: Establish decision and implementation deadlines based on the available lead time.\n* Inclusive: Address the needs of at-risk households and sectors, including people who may face barriers to receiving warnings or evacuating.\n* Accountable: Record the evidence, recommendation, declaration, funding, implementation and results.\n* Proportionate: Match actions and resource commitments to the forecast risk and applicable legal authority.\n\n## 5.5 Hazard-specific anticipatory action protocols\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate the development and maintenance of hazard-specific protocols. These shall be based on the City''s risk profile and the official warning and forecasting systems of competent agencies.\n\nAt a minimum, protocols should cover:\n\n| Hazard | Technical information to monitor | Potential anticipatory actions |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| Tropical cyclone and extreme rainfall | PAGASA bulletins, rainfall forecasts, wind signals, track and impact forecasts | Prepositioning, evacuation readiness, suspension recommendations, protection of facilities and lifelines |\n| Riverine flooding, including relevant river systems such as Anilao and Malbasag | Rainfall forecasts, river levels, upstream conditions, local gauges and flood models where available | Targeted warnings, pre-emptive evacuation where justified, deployment of rescue assets, protection of critical facilities |\n| Rain-induced landslide | PAGASA rainfall information, MGB susceptibility information, field observations and local rainfall thresholds | Targeted warnings, access restrictions, evacuation and relocation of people in threatened areas |\n| Tsunami affecting Ormoc Bay and coastal areas | PHIVOLCS tsunami bulletins, earthquake information, sea-level observations and official advisories | Immediate warning dissemination, evacuation to designated safe areas, closure of exposed coastal access points |\n| Other locally relevant hazards | Authoritative agency warnings and validated local monitoring | Actions defined in the relevant hazard-specific protocol |\n\nThe City shall not invent or independently substitute technical warning thresholds for those issued by competent national agencies. Locally developed thresholds may supplement official warnings only when technically validated, documented and approved through the appropriate process.\n\n## 5.6 Roles and responsibilities\n| Actor | Responsibilities |\n| ----- | ----- |\n| City Mayor | Exercises the statutory declaration authority by EO upon the recommendation required by law; provides executive direction and authorizes actions within lawful authority |\n| Regional DRRM Council / competent recommending authority | Performs the recommendation or coordination role prescribed by Republic Act No. 12287 and its IRR |\n| CDRRMC | Provides local coordination, reviews the PDRA and anticipatory action recommendations, and mobilizes institutional support within its mandate |\n| CDRRMO | Coordinates PDRA, prepares technical recommendations, maintains hazard protocols and monitors implementation |\n| EOC | Monitors forecasts, maintains COP, disseminates authorized instructions, tracks actions and resources, and prepares reports |\n| Barangays | Validate local conditions, receive and relay warnings, identify at-risk households, implement assigned local actions and report completion |\n| City Social Welfare and Development Office | Validates social protection needs and coordinates anticipatory social amelioration with relevant offices |\n| City Health Office | Coordinates health-related protective actions and continuity of essential health services |\n| City Engineering / Building Official | Advises on infrastructure safety, access, structural risks and protective measures within mandate |\n| City Agriculture / Veterinary Office | Assesses agricultural, livestock and fisheries exposure and coordinates sector-specific protective measures |\n| CPDO | Supports spatial analysis, exposure assessment and integration with local development information |\n| City Budget Office / Treasurer / Accounting / BAC / relevant offices | Ensure that funding, procurement, disbursement and accountability arrangements comply with applicable rules |\n| Utilities and private sector partners | Implement agreed continuity and protective measures within their respective responsibilities |\n\n## 5.7 Procedure\n### Step 1. Continuous monitoring and threat identification\nThe EOC shall monitor official forecasts, warnings, bulletins and relevant field information.\n\nThe duty officer shall record:\n\n* Source agency;\n* Bulletin or advisory number;\n* Date and time of issuance;\n* Hazard type;\n* Forecast location and affected areas;\n* Expected timing;\n* Severity or intensity information;\n* Forecast confidence or uncertainty, where provided;\n* Potentially exposed population and assets; and\n* Recommended monitoring or readiness actions.\n\n### Step 2. Initial threat screening\nThe CDRRMO shall conduct an initial screening to determine whether the threat requires:\n\n* Routine monitoring;\n* Enhanced monitoring;\n* Activation of the PDRA process;\n* Pre-positioning or readiness actions under existing authority; or\n* Consideration of a State of Imminent Disaster.\n\nThe City shall not delay urgent protective actions that are already authorized and warranted merely because the SID declaration process is being considered.\n\n### Step 3. Convening the PDRA process\nWhere the threat warrants formal assessment, the CDRRMO shall convene the relevant technical offices and agencies.\n\nThe PDRA shall identify:\n\n1. Hazard characteristics and forecast scenario;\n2. Geographic areas potentially affected;\n3. Exposed population and vulnerable groups;\n4. Potential impacts on housing, infrastructure, lifelines and essential services;\n5. Potential livelihood and economic impacts;\n6. Evacuation and access constraints;\n7. Critical facilities and essential services at risk;\n8. Potential secondary or cascading impacts;\n9. Existing response and coping capacities;\n10. Priority anticipatory actions; and\n11. Required resources, lead times and responsible offices.\n\nThe PDRA shall clearly identify assumptions, uncertainties, source information and the time at which the assessment was prepared.\n\n### Step 4. Development of the Anticipatory Action Matrix\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate the preparation of an action matrix that links each identified risk to a concrete action.\n\nThe matrix shall include:\n\n| Required field | Description |\n| ----- | ----- |\n| Hazard / scenario | The forecast hazard and relevant scenario |\n| Trigger | Official warning, forecast threshold or validated local trigger |\n| Geographic coverage | Barangays, communities, facilities or sectors concerned |\n| Anticipatory action | Specific action to be implemented before impact |\n| Responsible office | Office with operational responsibility |\n| Supporting offices | Offices or partners providing support |\n| Target completion | Deadline based on lead time |\n| Resource requirement | Personnel, equipment, supplies, transport and funding |\n| Approval authority | Official or body authorized to approve the action |\n| Evidence of completion | Log, acknowledgment, dispatch record, photograph or other proof |\n| Status | Not started, ongoing, completed, delayed or cancelled |\n\nActions may include:\n\n* Pre-positioning of food, water, medicines, rescue assets and other essential supplies;\n* Activation of evacuation centers and transport arrangements;\n* Pre-emptive evacuation where justified by the risk and authorized procedure;\n* Protection of critical infrastructure and essential services;\n* Public warnings and targeted advisories;\n* Assistance to people requiring mobility or medical support;\n* Protection of livestock, agricultural assets and livelihoods where feasible;\n* Continuity measures for health, education, water and other essential services; and\n* Social amelioration or other assistance in accordance with applicable law and eligibility rules.\n\n### Step 5. Review and recommendation\nThe CDRRMO shall submit the PDRA and proposed Anticipatory Action Matrix to the CDRRMC or the appropriate authority for review and recommendation, following the statutory process.\n\nThe review shall establish:\n\n* Whether the threat meets the legal basis for consideration of an SID;\n* Whether the forecast and impact evidence is sufficiently credible;\n* Whether the anticipated impacts justify the proposed declaration;\n* Whether proposed actions can be completed within the available lead time;\n* Whether funding and resources are legally available;\n* Whether affected populations can be reached through warning and assistance systems; and\n* Whether the recommended actions are within the authority of the responsible offices.\n\nThe recommendation shall be formally documented and transmitted through the legally required channel.\n\n### Step 6. Declaration by the City Mayor\nUpon receipt of the recommendation required by law, the City Mayor may issue an Executive Order declaring a State of Imminent Disaster, consistent with Republic Act No. 12287 and its IRR.\n\nThe EO shall identify:\n\n* Legal basis;\n* Hazard and forecast scenario;\n* Geographic coverage;\n* Date and time of effectivity;\n* Technical basis and recommendation reference;\n* Authorized anticipatory action arrangements;\n* Responsible offices and coordination mechanisms;\n* Resource and reporting arrangements; and\n* Review, extension or lifting arrangements, as permitted by law.\n\nThe declaration shall not be issued solely on the basis of an unsupported forecast or an unverified social media report.\n\n### Step 7. Activation and dissemination\nUpon issuance of the EO:\n\n1. The EOC shall record the declaration and disseminate it to concerned offices and barangays.\n2. The CDRRMO shall activate the relevant hazard-specific protocol.\n3. Responsible offices shall implement their assigned actions.\n4. Barangays shall acknowledge receipt of warnings and instructions.\n5. The EOC shall maintain a live action tracker and COP.\n6. Public information shall be issued through authorized channels.\n\n### Step 8. Implementation of anticipatory actions\nEach responsible office shall implement the actions assigned in the approved matrix.\n\nThe EOC shall track:\n\n* Action;\n* Responsible office;\n* Target deadline;\n* Actual completion time;\n* Geographic coverage;\n* Number and type of people reached;\n* Resources deployed;\n* Outstanding constraints; and\n* Evidence of completion.\n\nIf a planned action cannot be completed, the responsible office shall report the reason, risk implications and alternative measures.\n\n### Step 9. Anticipatory social amelioration\nWhere social amelioration is included in the approved action package, the responsible social welfare office shall coordinate beneficiary identification, eligibility validation, assistance delivery and reporting.\n\nThe process shall:\n\n* Use the applicable beneficiary registry or authorized data source;\n* Verify eligibility and avoid duplication;\n* Prioritize households facing heightened exposure and barriers to self-protection;\n* Maintain clear records of assistance;\n* Apply data minimization and access controls; and\n* Ensure lawful data sharing in accordance with Republic Act No. 10173 and applicable rules.\n\nThe CDRRMO and EOC shall coordinate information and operational requirements but shall not independently determine social welfare eligibility outside the authority of the responsible office.\n\n### Step 10. Monitoring and adjustment\nThe EOC shall monitor changes in the forecast, official warnings and actual field conditions.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall recommend adjustments to the action matrix when:\n\n* The forecast changes materially;\n* The hazard''s timing or geographic coverage changes;\n* New areas become exposed;\n* A protective action becomes unsafe or impracticable;\n* Additional resources become necessary; or\n* The threat diminishes and planned actions should be modified or stood down.\n\nChanges shall be recorded with the time, decision-maker, rationale and offices notified.\n\n### Step 11. Lifting or termination of the SID\nThe SID shall be lifted or otherwise terminated in accordance with the applicable statutory requirements and the authority prescribed by Republic Act No. 12287 and its IRR.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall prepare a technical recommendation based on:\n\n* Updated official forecasts and warnings;\n* Actual hazard development;\n* Residual risk;\n* Completion or cancellation of anticipatory actions;\n* Continuing safety requirements; and\n* The legal requirements for lifting.\n\nThe City Mayor shall issue the appropriate instrument where authorized and required by law.\n\n### Step 12. Post-lifting report and review\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate the preparation and submission of the required post-lifting report within the period prescribed by law and the IRR. The assessment workbook identifies a 45-day post-lifting reporting requirement; the City Legal Office must confirm the precise trigger, recipient, required contents and computation of the reporting period against the certified IRR before this is adopted as a binding deadline.\n\nThe report should document:\n\n* Forecast and warning chronology;\n* Basis for the PDRA and declaration;\n* Anticipatory actions planned and implemented;\n* Offices and partners involved;\n* Population and sectors reached;\n* Resources and funds used;\n* Actions not completed and reasons;\n* Actual hazard impacts compared with the forecast scenario;\n* Lessons learned;\n* Recommendations; and\n* Corrective actions.\n\n## 5.8 Process Flow\n![Annex 1-E process flow](/library/ch1/annex-1-E-fig-01.webp)\n## 5.9 Minimum Records\n* Official warning and forecast bulletins;\n* EOC monitoring log;\n* PDRA report;\n* Exposure and impact maps;\n* Anticipatory Action Matrix;\n* CDRRMC or competent authority recommendation;\n* City Mayor''s Executive Order;\n* Public advisories and barangay acknowledgments;\n* Resource prepositioning and deployment records;\n* Evacuation and assistance records;\n* Social amelioration records, subject to privacy safeguards;\n* EOC situation reports and action tracker;\n* Lifting or termination instrument;\n* Post-lifting report; and\n* After-Action Review and Improvement Plan.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-E.md (Chapter 1, v1.0 revised working draft)','draft'),
('05e5e9aa-977f-5e54-b317-bfccd1a357e5'::uuid,'2ad29b21-b115-57cb-8bc8-cc638387a41e'::uuid,'v1.0 revised working draft',E'# ANNEX 1-F | INTEGRATED RACI ACCOUNTABILITY FRAMEWORK\n\nThe following matrices translate the governance instruments into assigned responsibilities. They should be adopted as controlled annexes to the MOO and reviewed whenever the City''s organizational structure, statutory mandates or operating arrangements change.\n\n**RACI definitions**\n\n- **R – Responsible:** Performs or coordinates the work.\n- **A – Accountable:** Holds final responsibility for the task or decision. There should normally be one accountable role for each activity.\n- **C – Consulted:** Provides technical advice or is consulted before action.\n- **I – Informed:** Receives information on the action or decision.\n\nThe RACI matrix does not create legal authority. Where law assigns a decision to a specific official or body, that statutory authority prevails over any administrative assignment in the matrix.\n\n## 6.1 CDRRMC Governance RACI\n\n| Governance activity | City Mayor | CDRRMC | CDRRMO Secretariat | City offices | CSO / private sector |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Convene regular CDRRMC meeting | A | C | R | I | I |\n| Prepare agenda and technical briefs | I | C | A/R | C | C |\n| Review Local DRRM Plan | I | A | R | R | C |\n| Integrate DRRM into local plans and investment programming | I | C | R | A/R\\* | C |\n| Adopt CDRRMC resolutions | I | A/R | R (documentation) | C | C |\n| Monitor Council decisions | I | A | R | R | I |\n| Update membership and focal persons | A | C | R | C | C |\n| Conduct annual governance review | A | R | R | R | C |\n| Recommend State of Calamity | I | A/R | R (technical consolidation) | C | I |\n| Recommend SID through legally required channel | A** | R/C** | R (technical support) | C | I |\n\n\\* Each office remains accountable for its own statutory planning and technical functions. CPDO retains its role in local development and land use planning; CDRRMO provides DRRM risk information and technical integration support.\n\n\\*\\* The exact SID recommendation route must be aligned with Republic Act No. 12287 and its IRR. The matrix must be revised if the certified legal text assigns the recommendation to a regional or other competent authority. The City Mayor''s declaration authority is not a substitute for that prerequisite.\n\n## 6.2 CDRRMO–EOC Operational RACI\n\n| Operational activity | City Mayor | CDRRMO | EOC Manager / team | Barangays | Technical offices / response agencies |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Routine risk monitoring | I | A | R | R | C |\n| EOC duty roster | I | A | R | I | C |\n| Recommend EOC activation | I | A/R | R | C | C |\n| Approve EOC activation under protocol | A | R | I | I | I |\n| Maintain common operating picture | I | A | R | R | R |\n| Validate sectoral technical information | I | C | R (consolidation) | C | A/R within mandate |\n| Issue authorized public warnings | A\\* | R | R (dissemination) | R (local relay) | C |\n| Resource request consolidation | I | A | R | R | R |\n| Approve expenditure / procurement | A** | C | I | I | R** |\n| Field incident command | I | C | I/C | C | A/R – Incident Commander |\n| Situation reporting | I | A | R | R | R |\n| EOC deactivation recommendation | I | A/R | R | C | C |\n| EOC records closure | I | A | R | C | C |\n\n\\* The authorized technical agency or designated official retains responsibility for the content and issuance of warnings according to the relevant warning system. The City coordinates local dissemination and protective instructions.\n\n\\*\\* Approval and execution depend on the specific budget, procurement, accounting, property and delegated-authority rules. The City Mayor is not automatically the approving authority for every transaction.\n\n## 6.3 State of Calamity RACI\n\n| Activity | CDRRMC | CDRRMO | EOC | Technical offices | Mayor | SP |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Initial incident reporting | I | C | R | R | I | I |\n| Activate assessment coordination | I | A/R | C | R | I | I |\n| Conduct sectoral assessments | I | C | C | A/R within mandate | I | I |\n| Consolidate damage and needs assessment | C | A/R | R | R | I | I |\n| Deliberate on recommendation | A/R | R (technical support) | C | C | I | I |\n| Transmit recommendation | C | R | I | I | A/R for executive transmittal | I |\n| Declare State of Calamity | R (recommendation) | C | I | C | I | A |\n| Implement authorized measures | C | R (coordination) | R (tracking) | A/R within mandate | A (executive coordination) | I |\n| Monitor and report | C | A | R | R | I | I |\n| Recommend lifting | A/R | R | C | C | I | I |\n| Lift State of Calamity | R (recommendation) | C | I | C | I | A |\n\n## 6.4 State of Imminent Disaster RACI\n\n| Activity | Recommending authority | Mayor | CDRRMC | CDRRMO | EOC | Technical offices / barangays |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Monitor official forecasts | I | I | I | A | R | R |\n| Initial threat screening | I | I | C | A | R | C |\n| Convene PDRA | I | I | C | A/R | R | R |\n| Conduct sectoral impact assessment | I | I | C | C | C | A/R within mandate |\n| Develop Anticipatory Action Matrix | C | I | C | A/R | R | R |\n| Review local recommendation | As prescribed by law | I | R/C | R | C | C |\n| Make statutory recommendation | A/R, if prescribed by law | I | C | R (technical support) | I | C |\n| Declare SID by EO | C | A | I | C | I | I |\n| Implement assigned anticipatory actions | I | A (executive coordination) | C | R (coordination) | R (tracking) | A/R within mandate |\n| Track actions and resources | I | I | C | A | R | R |\n| Recommend lifting / termination | As prescribed by law | I | C | R | C | C |\n| Issue lifting instrument | C | A, where legally authorized | I | R (technical support) | I | I |\n| Prepare post-lifting report | As prescribed by law | I | C | A/R | R | R |\n\n## 6.5 Cross-office institutional interface RACI\n\nThis matrix is designed to preserve the mandates of technical and statutory offices while ensuring that DRRM information and coordination are integrated across the City Government.\n\n| Function / deliverable | Accountable lead | CDRRMO role | Supporting offices / partners |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| CLUP and CDP risk integration | CPDO / authorized planning authority | Provides risk data and technical review | City offices, barangays, CDRRMC |\n| Hazard, exposure and vulnerability information | Competent technical data owners, coordinated through CDRRMO | Coordinates consolidation and use in DRRM planning | MGB, PAGASA, PHIVOLCS, CPDO, barangays, other agencies |\n| Critical facility safety assessment | Relevant engineering / building authority | Coordinates prioritization based on risk | City Engineering, OBO, facility owners |\n| Watershed and ecosystem management | CENRO / competent environmental authority | Integrates ecosystem risk into DRRM plans | DENR, barangays, community organizations |\n| Public health emergency preparedness | CHO / competent health authority | Coordinates EOC and multi-sector support | Hospitals, DOH, health facilities |\n| School safety and continuity | DepEd / competent education authority | Coordinates risk information and emergency interface | School heads, barangays, CHO |\n| Agriculture and fisheries risk management | City Agriculture / Veterinary Office | Integrates sectoral risk and response information | BFAR, DA, farmers'' and fishers'' organizations |\n| Business continuity | Relevant business owners / competent economic office | Facilitates coordination and continuity planning | Chamber of Commerce, DTI, utilities |\n| Lifeline continuity | Respective utility or service provider | Coordinates status reporting and resource support | Water, power, telecom and transport providers |\n| Social protection and evacuation assistance | CSWDO / competent social welfare authority | Coordinates risk information and EOC tracking | Barangays, health offices, DSWD, CSOs |\n| Local infrastructure recovery | Relevant implementing office | Coordinates risk-informed recovery monitoring | CPDO, City Engineering, utilities, national agencies |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-F.md (Chapter 1, v1.0 revised working draft)','draft')
