                                                                                                  | CDRRMO; cluster lead offices              | Q2 2027                             | Ongoing     |\n| A8.6   | Maintain; record anticipatory actions in each PDRA.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | CDRRMO                                    | Each forecast event                 | Ongoing     |\n| A8.7   | Designate MDM and RDANA teams by EO and train them.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | CDRRMO; CHO; PNP                          | 2027                                | Not started |\n| A8.8   | Write AA protocols by hazard and lead time with office-order templates; walk them through in a PDRA tabletop to reach 3.                                                                                                                                                         | CDRRMO                                    | Jan 2027 (with MOP)                 | Ongoing     |\n| A8.9   | Issue local guidelines with CSWDO for anticipatory cash/vouchers using CBMS, with a DSA.                                                                                                                                                                                         | CSWDO; CDRRMO; City DPO                   | 2027                                | Not started |\n| B1.2   | Write the activation matrix using NDRRMC Memo 131 s.2018 triggers (9.2.1) and standard alert codes, with the SID posture (IRR Rule IV Sec. 1). Use the RDRRMOC SOPG 2022 alert model (White/Blue/Red; Standby/Alpha/Bravo/Charlie protocols) as the template.                    | CDRRMO Operations & Warning               | Jan 2027 (with MOP)                 | Ongoing     |\n| B1.3   | Add escalation/de-escalation/deactivation steps and authority (NDRRMC Memo 131 s.2018 deactivation triggers; RDRRMOC SOPG 2022 raising and downgrading rules) to EOC Manual Ch.2.                                                                                                | CDRRMO                                    | Jan 2027 (with MOP)                 | Ongoing     |\n| B1.4   | Write the ConOps in EOC Manual Ch.2 around the five NDRRMC Memo 131 s.2018 functions: Management, Operations, Planning, Logistics, Finance/Admin.                                                                                                                                | CDRRMO                                    | Jan 2027 (with MOP)                 | Ongoing     |\n| B1.5   | Write the vertical coordination protocol (reporting times by alert level, channels, RDRRMC 24-h reply for SID requests).                                                                                                                                                         | CDRRMO                                    | Jan 2027 (with MOP)                 | Ongoing     |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch4v1_Annex_4-A.md (Chapter 4, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('8b022bcd-ab5f-511a-96f4-8fbf2a7d0d09'::uuid,'65b84ccd-4200-5e2b-8ada-e6d670cbc855'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# ANNEX 4-B \\| EOC AND INCIDENT COORDINATION RACI WITH LEGAL DECISION AUTHORITY\n\nOperational accountability (A) and legal decision authority are different things. The second column names who decides in law or under a City issuance; the other columns repeat the proposed RACI of section 15.\n\n**Table 4.42**\n\n*Legal Decision Authority and Operational Accountability*\n\n| **Activity**                             | **Legal or formal decision authority**                                                    | **Process-accountable (A)**           | **Responsible (R)**                                        |\n|------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Warning receipt and verification         | None (no decision)                                                                        | CDRRMO Head                           | Operations and Warning (OpCen)                             |\n| Warning-action recommendation            | Official or body named in the approved CP or EOC EO                                       | CDRRMO Head (technical process)       | Operations and Warning                                     |\n| Evacuation and public protective action  | Official or body named by law and City issuance                                           | Competent authority                   | Barangays (implementation); CDRRMO (recommendation)        |\n| Raising or lowering the EOC alert status | Official designated in the EOC EO; LGU by EO on LDRRMC recommendation (DILG VIII s. IV.3) | Designated authority                  | EOC Manager (implementation); CDRRMO Head (recommendation) |\n| EOC coordination and reporting           | Mayor/CDRRMC for policy decisions                                                         | CDRRMO Head (institutional oversight) | EOC Manager and sections                                   |\n| Field incident command                   | Incident Commander under the ICS EO                                                       | Incident Commander / IMT              | Agencies within their command                              |\n| Resource requests with expenditure       | Authorised officials under Chapter 5                                                      | CDRRMO Head                           | EOC sections; asset owners                                 |\n| SitRep submission                        | Approving official in the reporting route                                                 | CDRRMO Head (oversight)               | EOC Planning / situation unit                              |\n| Deactivation and transition              | Official designated in the EOC EO, on the IC recommendation                               | Designated authority                  | EOC Manager; sector offices                                |\n| State of Imminent Disaster               | Mayor by EO after the RA 12287 route (SOP GOV-05)                                         | CDRRMO Head as CDRRMC secretariat     | See Chapter 1                                              |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch4v1_Annex_4-B.md (Chapter 4, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('907b7f59-18d6-5b5c-855c-f8d9e9990db4'::uuid,'35d5f0ee-1c6a-52de-adec-5234a23a0022'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# ANNEX 4-C \\| HAZARD-SPECIFIC WARNING AND ACTIVATION MATRIX REGISTER\n\nThe City matrix is kept as a controlled register, one entry per hazard and scenario. Table 4.43 is the entry template. Table 4.44 summarises the regional reference triggers; they are not City thresholds until adopted.\n\n**Table 4.43**\n\n*Matrix Register Entry*\n\n| **Field**                        | **Content**                                                                               |\n|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Hazard and scenario**          | Hazard, scenario ID and contingency plan (with version)                                   |\n| **Authoritative source product** | Agency, product name and the exact category or wording that triggers the entry            |\n| **Adopted by**                   | EOC EO or contingency plan, with number and date; "regional reference only" until adopted |\n| **Alert status and actions**     | White, Blue or Red; actions with lead office for each                                     |\n| **Decision authority**           | Official or body who decides each action                                                  |\n| **Target groups and channels**   | Barangays, facilities and groups; primary and backup channels; accessible formats         |\n| **Acknowledgement**              | Who must confirm receipt and by when                                                      |\n| **Review**                       | Last review date, exercise and corrective actions                                         |\n\n**Table 4.44**\n\n*Regional Conditions and Triggers for Raising the Alert Status (Summary)*\n\n| **Hazard**                                                                    | **White**                                                                                                      | **Blue**                                                                                         | **Red**                                                                                                              |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Tropical cyclone (flooding, storm surge, severe wind)                         | Weather advisories from PAGASA                                                                                 | PDRA result; Severe Weather Bulletin; tropical depression expected to enter the PAR (TCWS No. 1) | Severe Weather Bulletin; within the PAR; tropical storm (TCWS No. 2) or stronger                                     |\n| Other weather disturbances (monsoon, ITCZ, LPA, tail-end of a frontal system) | Yellow rainfall advisory: 7.5–15 mm observed in 1 hour and expected to continue for 2 hours; flooding possible | Orange rainfall advisory: 15–30 mm in 1 hour, expected to continue; flooding threatening         | Red rainfall advisory: more than 30 mm in 1 hour, expected to continue; serious flooding expected in low-lying areas |\n| El Niño and La Niña                                                           | Watch alert; climate outlook                                                                                   | Needs mobilisation of national resources                                                         | Needs extensive mobilisation of resources                                                                            |\n| Earthquake                                                                    | No damage reported; no tsunami threat                                                                          | Intensity VI expected or reported; minor sea-level disturbance                                   | Intensity VII or above; tsunami warning                                                                              |\n| Tsunami                                                                       | No tsunami threat; sea-level monitoring                                                                        | Minor sea-level disturbance: waves under 1 m above expected tides                                | Destructive tsunami: life-threatening waves over 1 m above expected tides; immediate response                        |\n\n*Note.* Summarised from Table 2, Conditions and Triggers in Raising the Alert Status, of the DILG VIII Disaster Response Reporting Template and SOP (RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006, s. 2023). Either one listed condition or the result of the PDRA or NDRRMC meetings applies. The table also covers volcanoes, tornadoes, conflict, fire, maritime and aviation incidents, CBRNE, epidemics and special events; consult the source for those. Rainfall categories are PAGASA products; the City does not set its own rainfall numbers.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch4v1_Annex_4-C.md (Chapter 4, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('926a5a4c-28bd-5bf4-b650-a3e21a3147c3'::uuid,'4a1db36a-0364-55dc-a164-3a87bbc73f09'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# ANNEX 4-D \\| GAWAD KALASAG CY 2026 EVIDENCE CROSSWALK\n\nThe Gawad KALASAG LDRRMO assessment toolkit (CY 2026) asks for evidence that Chapter 4 records already produce. Keeping the records as the procedures require produces that evidence as a by-product.\n\n**Table 4.45**\n\n*Chapter 4 Records Used as Gawad KALASAG Means of Verification*\n\n| **Toolkit criterion (CY 2026)**                | **Means of verification asked for**                                                                                                         | **Chapter 4 record or procedure**                                      | **Where it is kept**                                           |\n|------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Established local DRRM operations center (1.8) | EO or similar issuance creating a 24/7 OpCen; OpCen duty logbook; identified alternate EOC                                                  | SOP HR-05 handover (HR-F04); SOP 4-01 log; alternate EOC under SOP 3-7 | MODE: logbook, DRRM protocols; Vault: the EO                   |\n| Early warning system (A.2, 2.3)                | SOP or manual on EWS covering risk knowledge, monitoring and warning, dissemination and communication, response capability                  | SOP 4-01, 4-02; matrix register (Annex 4-C); EWR-F01, EWR-F03          | Library: Chapter 4; MODE: protocol runs                        |\n| Response under alert conditions                | Order or memorandum raising and downgrading the alert status; PDRA attendance and minutes; deployment memorandum; ICS forms; timely reports | SOP 4-03, 4-04, 4-05, 4-08, 4-09; EOC-F01, EWR-F04, EOC-F03, EOC-F05   | MODE: EOC level history, SitReps, protocol runs; Vault: orders |\n| Contingency plans                              | LDRRMC-approved, Sanggunian-adopted hazard-specific plans for at least the top two hazards                                                  | Chapter 3 SOP 3-6; activation in SOP 4-04                              | MODE: EOC → Plans and clusters; Vault                          |\n| ICS in place                                   | Updated IMT organisational structure; IMT members trained; AAR of a SIMEX based on the SOP or CP                                            | SOP 4-05; SOP 4-09 AAR (ER-F06)                                        | MODE: roster, protocol runs; Vault: AARs                       |\n\n*Note.* Criteria and means of verification paraphrased from the OCD Gawad KALASAG LDRRMO assessment toolkit, CY 2026 (checklists S, C and MS). The toolkit governs; this crosswalk only shows where the evidence is kept.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch4v1_Annex_4-D.md (Chapter 4, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('d8018c84-8624-5fde-8050-f8200fbbde90'::uuid,'5dd059f9-29d7-5059-a659-a11a61bed766'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0',E'# Annex 6-A. IAT and Master Requirements Traceability\n\n**Table 6.41**\n\n*IAT Rows, Master Requirements and Chapter 6 Procedures*\n\n| **IAT** | **Type**     | **w** | **Score** | **Gap priority** | **Master requirements**              | **SOP**          | **Recommended action (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                                              |\n|---------|--------------|-------|-----------|------------------|--------------------------------------|------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A3.5    | M            | 3     | 3         | 4.5 Medium       | MR-03; MR-04; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 6-01             | Tag each LDRRMFIP item to an LDRRMP and CDP PPA code; show the AA line and the EOC operations line; file the SP act adopting the CY 2026 plan. |\n| A4.1    | M            | 3     | 3         | 4.5 Medium       | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-01             | Show the computation on estimated regular revenue (NTA and local sources); correct the base if only the NTA was used.                          |\n| A4.2    | M            | 3     | 3         | 4.5 Medium       | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-01, 6-04       | Maintain the 70/30 split; show the AA sub-allocation inside the 70%.                                                                           |\n| A4.3    | M            | 2     | 3         | 3.0 Low          | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-03             | Keep an STF ledger by year of origin with the five-year deadline; list STF balances as an AA source.                                           |\n| A4.4    | GP           | 2     | 2         | 4.0 Medium       | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-02             | Track utilisation quarterly in the CDRRMC; flag PPAs at risk by Q3; programme aged balances first.                                             |\n| A4.5    | M            | 2     | 2         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-06, 6-07       | Update the pre-qualified supplier list each year; set framework agreements for relief goods, fuel and heavy equipment, usable for AA.          |\n| A4.6    | M            | 2     | 0         | 12.0 High        | MR-08; MR-16; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 6-01, 6-03, 6-05 | Put an AA line (up to 10% of the 70%) in the LDRRMFIP under CDP PPA 3.15; map other sources; write release steps with the tranche rule.        |\n| A4.7    | GP           | 1     | 3         | 1.0 Low          | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-09             | Check GSIS cover of the EOC, evacuation centres and City critical facilities; set priorities from the CDRA list.                               |\n| A4.8    | M            | 2     | 2         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-10             | Set an internal audit check of the LDRRMF before year-end.                                                                                     |\n| A4.9    | GP           | 2     | 1         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-08; MR-18; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 6-09             | Cost response and early recovery for a Thelma-type flash flood and a Yolanda-type typhoon; record the shortfall and fallback sources.          |\n| A7.5    | M            | 2     | 3         | 3.0 Low          | MR-04; MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 6-11             | Maintain; validate the warehouse inventory and replenishment triggers; add AA prepositioning triggers.                                         |\n| A8.3    | M            | 2     | 3         | 3.0 Low          | MR-05; MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 6-12             | Programme a preventive maintenance schedule for vehicles and equipment and keep logs.                                                          |\n| A10.5   | M            | 1     | 2         | 3.0 Low          | MR-07; MR-11; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 6-02             | Maintain; attach posting proof.                                                                                                                |\n| B6.3    | M            | 2     | 2         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-12             | Extend the inventory to barangays, partners and suppliers.                                                                                     |\n| B6.4    | GP           | 1     | 2         | 2.0 Low          | MR-08; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 6-08             | Write the donations and volunteer SOP under SP Res. No. 2024-102.                                                                              |\n| B6.6    | GP           | 2     | 1         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-08; MR-16; MR-18; MR-01; MR-12–15 | 6-06, 6-12       | Sign agreements listing item, quantity, trigger and contact, usable for AA.                                                                    |\n| B7.5    | M            | 2     | 3         | 3.0 Low          | MR-04; MR-11; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 6-01             | Keep the EOC operations line in each LDRRMFIP.                                                                                                 |\n| A10.8   | M            | 2     | 0         | 12.0 High        | MR-07; MR-11; MR-16; MR-01; MR-12–15 | 6-05 step 8      | Prepare the 45-day post-declaration report template (Chapter 7) and confirm the route with OCD VIII.                                           |\n| C1.2    | CPDO; Budget | 1     | 1         | 3.0 Low          | MR-03; MR-17; MR-18; MR-01; MR-12–15 | 6-01 step 4      | Add a one-page DRR-CCA screening form to LDC/LFC project review, owned by the CPDO with CDRRMO sign-off.                                       |\n\n*Note.* Copied from IAT v1.7 and the Traceability and Data Dictionary v1.2. Scores and priorities are for validation (Methodology v1.1, section 6). Part C rows carry the lead office in the Type column and are not added to the IMI.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch6v1_Annex_6-A.md (Chapter 6, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0)','draft'),
('9ccebfe8-5779-5fb7-9277-396d704bd951'::uuid,'70916b63-9696-5ef7-b223-aea1ac114527'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0',E'# Annex 6-B. Pre-audit Checklist for DRRM Transactions\n\nUsed in SOP 6-10 step 1, before year-end, by CDRRMO Administration and Training with the City Accountant. Each "No" becomes a corrective action on FIN-F11. The checklist prevents the findings COA most often raises on LDRRMF use; it does not replace COA''s own requirements.\n\n**Table 6.42**\n\n*Pre-audit Checklist*\n\n| **No.** | **Check**                                                                                                           | **Evidence**                                                 | **Yes / No / NA** |\n|---------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n| 1       | The LDRRMF was computed on estimated revenue from regular sources, and the computation is filed                     | FIN-F01 signed by the Budget Officer                         |                   |\n| 2       | The 70/30 split appears in the appropriation and in the books                                                       | Appropriation ordinance; ledgers                             |                   |\n| 3       | Every charge to the 70% is in the approved LDRRMFIP and is a DRRM use                                               | LDRRMFIP line; obligation request                            |                   |\n| 4       | Every QRF charge refers to an SP declaration of a State of Calamity in force at the time                            | SP resolution; FIN-F05                                       |                   |\n| 5       | Every AA charge refers to a State of Imminent Disaster in force, is within the AA line and passed the tranche check | Mayor''s EO; FIN-F06                                          |                   |\n| 6       | Unused QRF and 70% balances were transferred to the STF at year-end                                                 | Closing entries                                              |                   |\n| 7       | The STF ledger shows each balance by year of origin and its deadline; no balance passed five years unused           | FIN-F04                                                      |                   |\n| 8       | Each emergency procurement has its written ground, HoPE approval, documents and posting                             | FIN-F07; BAC records                                         |                   |\n| 9       | Each delivery has an inspection and acceptance report                                                               | IAR                                                          |                   |\n| 10      | Relief goods and non-food items issued have issue slips and distribution summaries                                  | Issue slips; summaries (no personal data in the CDRRMO file) |                   |\n| 11      | No stock on hand is past its expiry date; rotated items have issue records                                          | LOG-F01; count report                                        |                   |\n| 12      | Cash advances for DRRM operations were liquidated within the period                                                 | Liquidation reports                                          |                   |\n| 13      | Donations have acceptance documents, receipts and use records                                                       | FIN-F09; ORs                                                 |                   |\n| 14      | Quarterly utilisation reports were submitted to the CDRRMC and posted under the Full Disclosure Policy              | FIN-F03; posting proof                                       |                   |\n| 15      | DRRM property is on the asset register, covered by PAR or ICS, and counted this year                                | Count report                                                 |                   |\n| 16      | Insurance of DRRM buildings, warehouses and equipment is current                                                    | GSIS policy schedule                                         |                   |\n| 17      | Prior-year COA observations on DRRM funds, stocks and property have a reply and status                              | FIN-F11                                                      |                   |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch6v1_Annex_6-B.md (Chapter 6, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0)','draft'),
('48ef3766-c95e-5477-88a2-737aea98ef49'::uuid,'5158987f-c383-5e1b-9579-4e44f391eac2'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0',E'# Annex 6-C. Anticipatory Action Funding Pathway and Tranche Worksheet\n\nBasis: RA 12287 and its IRR (signed 14 September 2026), Rule V Secs. 1–3; ADR-025. The official text governs. DBM–COA guidelines on AA funds and NDRRMC operational guidelines are pending; this worksheet will be revised when they are issued.\n\n1.  **Plan.** The LDRRMFIP carries an AA line of no more than 10% of the 70% component, naming the AA measures (by hazard and sector), lead offices and procurement mode (SOP 6-01).\n\n2.  **Pre-arrange.** Framework agreements, supplier lists and stock plans cover AA items (SOPs 6-06, 6-11).\n\n3.  **Declare.** The City Mayor declares a State of Imminent Disaster after the PDRA, CDRRMC recommendation and RDRRMC process (Chapter 1, SOP GOV-05).\n\n4.  **Release.** Budget, Accounting and Treasury release funds immediately, within the tranche rule (SOP 6-05).\n\n5.  **Deliver.** Lead offices carry out AA measures; the No Regrets Principle applies.\n\n6.  **Lift and report.** The declaration lifts on hazard occurrence or on the Mayor''s lifting; unused AA funds accrue to the STF; the 45-day report includes AA fund use (Chapter 7).\n\n**Table 6.43**\n\n*Tranche Worksheet for Hydrometeorological and Geological Hazards*\n\n| **Line** | **Item**                                                      | **Formula**             | **Example (CY 2026 base)** |\n|----------|---------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------|----------------------------|\n| a        | LDRRMF for the year                                           | From FIN-F01            | PHP 139,955,741.40         |\n| b        | 70% component                                                 | a × 0.70                | PHP 97,969,018.98          |\n| c        | AA line in the LDRRMFIP                                       | not more than b × 0.10  | up to PHP 9,796,901.90     |\n| d        | Cap for interventions more than 3 days before forecast impact | c × 0.30                | PHP 2,939,070.57           |\n| e        | Allowance for interventions within 3 days of forecast impact  | c × 0.70                | PHP 6,857,831.33           |\n| f        | Already disbursed this year from the AA line                  | From FIN-F06            | —                          |\n| g        | Available for this declaration                                | c − f, split by d and e | —                          |\n\n*Note.* Illustration only: the CY 2026 plan has no AA line, and the base may change once the Budget Office confirms the regular-revenue computation (A4.1). Sources not subject to graduation: STF balances within their five-year period; partner, private and international donors; ODA; other LGUs; other lawful sources (IRR Rule V Secs. 1(b) and 2). Slow-onset hazards are not graduated until the NDRRMC guidelines issue, but never exceed the year''s AA line (ADR-025 item 4).\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch6v1_Annex_6-C.md (Chapter 6, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0)','draft'),
('684d6320-7f9c-5e43-8f81-c7569ae06c64'::uuid,'041b6980-a73c-572a-abd6-65df749fb4f9'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0',E'# Annex 6-D. Prepositioned Stock Planning Worksheet\n\nUsed in SOP 6-11 step 1. One worksheet per priority hazard scenario; the highest result per item sets the level. Figures are entered by the CSWDO and the CDRRMO from the approved contingency plans; none is set in this Manual.\n\n**Table 6.44**\n\n*Stock Planning Worksheet*\n\n| **Line** | **Item**                                                     | **Formula or source**                            | **Entry** |\n|----------|--------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|-----------|\n| a        | Hazard and scenario                                          | Contingency plan (Chapter 3)                     |           |\n| b        | Families to be served, by cluster of barangays               | CP Forms; CDRA exposure                          |           |\n| c        | Days of self-reliance before augmentation                    | CP; DSWD Field Office VIII                       |           |\n| d        | Packs per family per day (food) / kits per family (non-food) | CSWDO standard                                   |           |\n| e        | Target level by item                                         | b × c × d (food); b × d (kits)                   |           |\n| f        | National minimum                                             | DPMS Vol. 2: 500 food packs and 500 hygiene kits | 500 / 500 |\n| g        | Level adopted                                                | Higher of e and f                                |           |\n| h        | Procurement or call-off lead time (days)                     | FIN-F08 agreement                                |           |\n| i        | Average daily issue in an event                              | Past events; AARs                                |           |\n| j        | Reorder point                                                | h × i, plus a safety margin set by the CSWDO     |           |\n| k        | Warehouse split                                              | By cluster and hazard exposure (LOG-F02)         |           |\n| l        | Annual cost of holding and rotation                          | Into the stock replenishment line (SOP 6-01)     |           |\n\n# Validation and Source Control Register\n\n**Table 6.45**\n\n*Validation and Source Control Register*\n\n| **Item**                                                          | **Source used**                                                             | **Status**                                                                         |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| CY 2026 LDRRMF figures                                            | CDRRMC Res. No. 05, s. 2025                                                 | Verify SP adoption of the CY 2026 plan                                             |\n| LDRRMF base                                                       | Res. No. 05, s. 2025 (NTA only)                                             | Budget Office to confirm                                                           |\n| AA rules                                                          | RA 12287 IRR Rule V, certified true copy read 11–12 Nov 2026                | Medium confidence until the City Legal Office confirms; DBM–COA guidelines pending |\n| Emergency procurement ground and documents                        | RA 12009 Sec. 35(b); GPPB Circular No. 06-2026                              | Confirm IRR details with the BAC Secretariat                                       |\n| Framework agreements                                              | RA 12009 Sec. 16; GPPB Circular No. 07-2026 (23 Jul 2026)                   | Confirm templates with the BAC Secretariat                                         |\n| "GPPB Circular 08-2026" on emergency and anticipatory procurement | Named in the Coherence Analysis (Part III.1)                                | Not found on the GPPB site on 12 Nov 2026; not cited until a copy is obtained      |\n| JMC 2013-1 provisions                                             | Secondary sources (COA 2014 DRRM audit; DBM LBC 130)                        | Check against a certified copy                                                     |\n| COA Circular No. 2012-002 section on trust-fund transfers         | Planning guide cites section 4 (4.4); a 2021 COA audit cites section 5.1.10 | Verify against the COA original before citing a section number                     |\n| DPMS Vol. 2 stock minimum                                         | NDRRMC Disaster Preparedness Minimum Standards Vol. 2, pp. 7 and 13         | Confirm the current edition with OCD VIII                                          |\n| Donations                                                         | SP Res. No. 2024-102                                                        | Confirm the acceptance delegation                                                  |\n| Platform template codes                                           | Seed of 12 Nov 2026 (WF-SOP5-xx)                                            | Re-seed as WF-SOP6-xx with aliases                                                 |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch6v1_Annex_6-D.md (Chapter 6, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0)','draft'),
('58404cb3-4a0c-5c65-ac4f-dfafc1b70622'::uuid,'a77a3f29-f160-5f96-ac25-e8bbb357e6e8'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 5-A. IAT and Master Requirements Traceability Register\n\n**Table 5.39**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Rows Traced to Chapter 5*\n\n| **ID** | **M/GP** | **Score** | **Priority** | **MR links** | **Recommended action (workbook)**                                                              | **Responsible office (Action Plan)**         | **Target**             | **Status**  |\n|--------|----------|-----------|--------------|--------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------|-------------|\n| A9.1   | GP       | 1         | Medium       | MR-11        | Form and train a PDNA team using OCD templates.                                                | CDRRMO Research & Planning                   | 2027                   | Not started |\n| A9.2   | M        | 2         | Medium       | MR-11        | Maintain; state assumptions in projections.                                                    | CDRRMO                                       | After each major event | Ongoing     |\n| A9.3   | GP       | 1         | Low          | MR-11        | Add a recovery monitoring matrix to the event database (A5.4).                                 | CDRRMO Research & Planning                   | Q3 2027                | Not started |\n| A9.4   | GP       | 2         | Low          | MR-11        | Write referral pathways with CSWDO, CHO and PESO.                                              | CSWDO; CHO; PESO                             | Q3 2027                | Not started |\n| A9.5   | GP       | 1         | Medium       | MR-11; MR-18 | Use the OCD/World Bank Ready to Rebuild workbook to prepare a pre-disaster recovery framework. | CDRRMO Research & Planning                   | 2027                   | Not started |\n| A8.7   | M        | 2         | Medium       | MR-05        | Designate MDM and RDANA teams by EO and train them.                                            | CDRRMO; CHO; PNP                             | 2027                   | Not started |\n| A10.6  | GP       | 2         | Medium       | MR-07; MR-11 | Maintain.                                                                                      | CDRRMO                                       | After each event       | Ongoing     |\n| A3.4   | M        | 2         | Medium       | MR-10        | Complete the PSCP using the PSCP Guidebook.                                                    | CDRRMO Research & Planning; all city offices | Q2 2027                | Ongoing     |\n\n*Note.* Copied from the A_CDRRMO and Action_Plan sheets of IAT v1.7 and the Indicator_Data_Dictionary sheet of the Traceability workbook v1.2 without change, except that recommended actions are shortened where marked by the workbook text. Priority bands computed with the workbook formula. Evidence validation status: "Pending validation" for every row. Resources and budget source for each row are in the Action Plan sheet.\n\n**Table 5.40**\n\n*Part C Interface Rows Used by Chapter 5*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (summarised)**                                                    | **Lead office**                           | **Score** | **Priority** | **Chapter 5 use**                    |\n|--------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|--------------------------------------|\n| C1.3   | CDRA estimates losses beyond physical assets                                  | CPDO                                      | 2         | Low          | Loss estimation in PDNA and baseline |\n| C1.4   | Risk-sensitive development guidelines in zoning and permits                   | CPDO; Zoning Administrator                | 2         | Medium       | Siting check in SOP 5-05             |\n| C2.1   | Structural safety of EOC, evacuation centres and critical facilities assessed | City Engineer / Building Official         | 1         | High         | Design check in SOP 5-05             |\n| C2.2   | Restoration-priority arrangements with power, water and telecoms              | CDRRMO with utility providers             | 2         | Medium       | Lifeline restoration in section 10   |\n| C2.3   | Transport anticipatory actions and alternate routes                           | City Engineer; traffic office; DPWH; port | 1         | Medium       | Road and bridge BBB criteria         |\n| C3.3   | Environmental anticipatory actions incl. safe debris disposal                 | CENRO; City Engineer; GSO                 | 1         | Medium       | Debris and waste in section 10       |\n\n*Note.* Part C rows have no Mandatory items and feed the separate Interface Index, never the Institutional Maturity Index. Priority uses the factor 1.0 for all Part C rows.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch5v1_Annex_5-A.md (Chapter 5, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('f03bf2a9-ef41-5803-b83c-445f3acaa437'::uuid,'4e4e65a0-6865-52e2-a314-05fab33c5395'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 5-B. Recovery RACI With Legal Decision Authority\n\n**Table 5.41**\n\n*Legal Decision Authority Kept Apart From Operational Accountability*\n\n| **Decision**                                            | **Legal or approving authority**                          | **Operational accountable owner (A)**          |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------|\n| **Adoption of the pre-disaster recovery framework**     | CDRRMC                                                    | CDRRMO Head                                    |\n| **Conduct of a City PDNA**                              | CDRRMC                                                    | CDRRMO Head                                    |\n| **Approval of the PDNA**                                | CDRRMC (RDRRMC VIII review when NDRRMF funding is sought) | PDNA coordinator                               |\n| **Approval of the rehabilitation and recovery program** | Sangguniang Panlungsod, on CDRRMC endorsement             | CPDO (technical lead)                          |\n| **Appropriations and supplemental budgets**             | Sangguniang Panlungsod                                    | City Budget Officer                            |\n| **Building permits and structural acceptance**          | Building Official                                         | Proponent office                               |\n| **Land use and zoning changes**                         | Sangguniang Panlungsod (ordinance)                        | CPDO / Zoning Administrator                    |\n| **Relocation and resettlement**                         | Authorities under RA 7279 and DHSUD rules                 | Housing office with CSWDO                      |\n| **Eligibility for social services**                     | Each service office under its rules                       | CSWDO, CHO, PESO, City Agriculture as relevant |\n| **Procurement awards**                                  | Head of procuring entity on BAC recommendation            | BAC secretariat (Chapter 6)                    |\n\n*Note.* Authorities follow the NEDA (2020) guide and the general legal framework; the City Legal Office shall confirm each row before adoption.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch5v1_Annex_5-B.md (Chapter 5, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('727c8f79-41fe-5581-beb8-108a7e526cdb'::uuid,'4744143d-bf31-58ae-a850-aa662ce66fd7'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 5-C. Pre-disaster Recovery Framework Outline\n\n1.  Purpose, scope and adoption (CDRRMC resolution, version, review date).\n\n2.  Recovery principles: Build Back Better, inclusion, local ownership, privacy.\n\n3.  Recovery baseline summary by barangay (Annex 5-E) and known data gaps.\n\n4.  Recovery scenarios per priority hazard (most probable and most severe) and the recovery needs they imply.\n\n5.  Critical areas, assets and population groups, in order of recovery priority.\n\n6.  Recovery organisation: Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee, sub-committees, lead offices and focal persons.\n\n7.  PDNA activation: triggers, decision route, team designations, templates and OCD VIII support arrangements.\n\n8.  Temporary shelter and evacuation centre closure arrangements (with CSWDO and the housing office).\n\n9.  Debris and post-disaster waste management (with ENRO and City Engineering).\n\n10. Scalable and reprogrammable PPAs by office.\n\n11. Recovery financing map: fund sources, insurance status of City assets, farmer enrolment, partner commitments.\n\n12. Pre-agreed partnership arrangements (MOAs with private sector, CSOs, academe and suppliers; Chapter 8).\n\n13. Monitoring unit, matrix and reporting arrangements.\n\n14. Communication strategy template and feedback channels.\n\n15. Annual review record and lessons from recent events.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch5v1_Annex_5-C.md (Chapter 5, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('f3f1a168-1048-5e58-801a-0d61581a8f54'::uuid,'331670a4-51ad-58d6-b959-6e99b51b8d48'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 5-D. Recovery Monitoring Matrix Fields (REC-F06)\n\n**Table 5.42**\n\n*Minimum Fields of the Recovery Monitoring Matrix*\n\n| **Field group**    | **Fields**                                                                                           | **Source**               |\n|--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n| **Identification** | Event; PPA ID; title; sector; sub-committee; phase (early recovery, short term, medium term)         | REC-F04                  |\n| **Location**       | PSGC barangay code(s); site description (no personal data)                                           | REC-F04                  |\n| **Link to need**   | PDNA reference; ER-F05 line (if from transition); BBB screen reference                               | PDNA; REC-F02; REC-F07   |\n| **Targets**        | Output indicator; target by year; unit; beneficiaries as aggregates by sex, age group and disability | Program                  |\n| **Finance**        | Approved cost; fund source; obligations; disbursements                                               | Chapter 6 records        |\n| **Implementation** | Implementing office; partner; start and planned completion; percentage physical accomplishment       | Office reports           |\n| **Issues**         | Issue; owner; decision; due date                                                                     | Problem-solving meetings |\n| **Verification**   | Field validation date; BBB conditions met (yes/no); evidence link                                    | SOP 5-05, 5-07           |\n| **Closeout**       | Completion date; closeout evidence; outcome note; lesson reference (Chapter 7)                       | REC-F09                  |\n\n*Note.* Blank means "not reported", never zero. Figures are scoped to one event. The matrix is the event-database extension recommended for A9.3 and links to the A5.4 event database of Chapter 3.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch5v1_Annex_5-D.md (Chapter 5, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('5b6bdb86-42c2-5d85-9c1e-eeedc554b7f4'::uuid,'497fd21a-3aee-5932-9466-f989cdd0d8b6'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 5-E. Minimum Data for the Recovery Baseline\n\n**Table 5.43**\n\n*Minimum Recovery Baseline Data, Disaggregation and Source*\n\n| **Element**                          | **Data and disaggregation**                                                                        | **Likely source**                  |\n|--------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|\n| **General information**              | Land area; topography; barangay boundaries (PSGC)                                                  | CPDO; CLUP; NAMRIA; PSA            |\n| **Income and poverty**               | Income class; poverty incidence by barangay                                                        | BLGF; PSA; CBMS                    |\n| **Population**                       | Total population by barangay, urban/rural, sex, age group and disability                           | PSA; CBMS                          |\n| **Settlements and housing**          | Settlement areas and population; housing materials; informal settlers; land use and zoning map     | CLUP; Shelter Plan; CDRA           |\n| **Economic activity and livelihood** | Labour force; MSMEs; farmers and fisherfolk; agricultural areas and facilities; informal sector    | PESO; BPLO; City Agriculture; CDRA |\n| **Infrastructure and lifelines**     | Roads and bridges; power, water and telecom facilities; port; public buildings; evacuation centres | City Engineering; utilities; CDRA  |\n| **Social services**                  | Schools; health facilities; social welfare facilities and caseloads (aggregates)                   | DepEd; CHO; CSWDO                  |\n| **Environment**                      | Protected areas; watersheds; forest and mangrove cover                                             | CENRO; DENR; LCCAP                 |\n| **Hazards and past events**          | Hazard maps; past damage and losses by event                                                       | CDRA; RDANA and PDNA reports       |\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Annex A of the NEDA (2020) guide. Household-level data stays with the source office; the baseline holds aggregates by barangay.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch5v1_Annex_5-E.md (Chapter 5, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('8d0e05eb-d7dd-594c-9845-ba0e2c256116'::uuid,'dc1c4086-d8bb-592d-b44e-2cbe3f900597'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 7-A. IAT and Master Requirements Traceability\n\n**Table 7.39**\n\n*IAT Rows, Master Requirements and Chapter 7 Procedures*\n\n| **IAT** | **Type** | **w** | **Score** | **Gap priority** | **Master requirements**              | **SOP**           | **Recommended action (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                                                           |\n|---------|----------|-------|-----------|------------------|--------------------------------------|-------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A10.3   | M        | 2     | 3         | 3.0 Low          | MR-02; MR-07; MR-01; MR-12–15        | Section 10        | Adopt RDRRMC VIII Res. No. 12-006 locally by CDRRMC resolution; confirm the harmonized template is used.                                                    |\n| A10.4   | M        | 2     | 2         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-07; MR-11; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 7-01              | Produce the first annual RBME report (Form 5D) and baseline database; present it to the CDRRMC and LDC; include IAT results and CDRA monitoring indicators. |\n| A10.6   | GP       | 2     | 2         | 4.0 Medium       | MR-07; MR-11; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 7-04, 7-05        | Validate AAR reports and the plan revisions they produced.                                                                                                  |\n| A10.8   | M        | 2     | 0         | 12.0 High        | MR-07; MR-11; MR-16; MR-01; MR-12–15 | 7-06              | Prepare the 45-day post-declaration report template and confirm the route with OCD VIII.                                                                    |\n| A10.9   | GP       | 1     | 2         | 2.0 Low          | MR-11; MR-18; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 7-08              | Document changes adopted from exchanges.                                                                                                                    |\n| A5.4    | GP       | 2     | 0         | 8.0 Medium       | MR-03; MR-07; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 7-07              | Set up a disaster event and loss database (Sendai and DesInventar fields) and back-load past RDANA and PDNA data.                                           |\n| A5.5    | M        | 2     | 2         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-03; MR-07; MR-10; MR-01; MR-12–15 | 7-09              | Confirm the City DPO; sign data-sharing agreements with CSWDO and CPDO; add privacy notices to registration forms.                                          |\n| B7.3    | M        | 2     | 2         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-04; MR-11; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 7-04 (evaluation) | Run an annual exercise program testing EOC activation (Chapter 9); evaluate each exercise here.                                                             |\n| B7.4    | GP       | 2     | 2         | 4.0 Medium       | MR-04; MR-11; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 7-04, 7-05        | Track AAR actions in an improvement plan.                                                                                                                   |\n\n*Note.* Copied from IAT v1.7 and the Traceability and Data Dictionary v1.2. Scores and priorities are for validation (Methodology v1.1, section 6). SOPs 7-02 and 7-03 serve all 140 indicators and MR-12 to MR-15.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch7v1_Annex_7-A.md (Chapter 7, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('7dee14ee-e033-5481-828f-90f394167242'::uuid,'7d4d0440-bc6c-5e93-94dc-62c804432e55'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 7-B. LDRRMP M&E Matrix Template\n\nOne row per indicator. Aligned with Form 5D of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan and the M&E matrix of the NDRRMP 2020–2030 RBME Plan.\n\n**Table 7.40**\n\n*M&E Matrix Columns (ME-F01)*\n\n| **Column**                   | **Content**                                                               |\n|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Code and result level**    | Unique code; impact, outcome, output or input and process                 |\n| **Indicator and definition** | What is counted, how, and what is excluded                                |\n| **Unit and disaggregation**  | Unit; sex, age, disability and barangay where relevant                    |\n| **Baseline**                 | Value and year, with source                                               |\n| **Targets**                  | By year to 2032, as approved by the CDRRMC                                |\n| **Data source and method**   | Office records, surveys, SitReps, IAT, database                           |\n| **Frequency**                | Quarterly, annual or per event                                            |\n| **Responsible office**       | Thematic focal office and data provider                                   |\n| **Framework codes**          | Ten Essentials; NDRRMP outcome; DILG pillar; Sendai target; IAT indicator |\n| **Current value and status** | Value, date, on-track or off-track, explanation                           |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch7v1_Annex_7-B.md (Chapter 7, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('00921f31-3f01-5734-9b24-70939b0dcd5b'::uuid,'b81dbeb4-0ca3-5539-ad44-fbd6fe409922'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 7-C. After-Action Review Report Outline\n\n**Table 7.41**\n\n*Contents of an AAR Report (ME-F03)*\n\n| **No.** | **Section**                 | **Content**                                                                                |\n|---------|-----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Event and scope             | Event or exercise, dates, hazard, area, EOC level, declarations; what the AAR covers       |\n| 2       | Method and participants     | Facilitator; offices and positions taking part; sources used                               |\n| 3       | Timeline                    | Official warning products with IDs; decisions; activations; key SitReps; resource requests |\n| 4       | What was supposed to happen | Plans, SOPs and protocols that applied                                                     |\n| 5       | What happened               | Facts, with evidence                                                                       |\n| 6       | What worked                 | Practices to keep, with evidence                                                           |\n| 7       | What did not work and why   | Findings with root or contributing causes; single- or double-loop                          |\n| 8       | Actions                     | Recommended actions with owner, due date and resources, entered in the CAPA register       |\n| 9       | Inclusion and protection    | How women, children, older persons and persons with disabilities were reached; gaps        |\n| 10      | Approval                    | CDRRMO Head approval; items referred to the CDRRMC                                         |\n\n*Note.* Records name positions, not individuals. Personal data of affected people is not included.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch7v1_Annex_7-C.md (Chapter 7, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('4b4ea8e9-bbbd-550e-a440-3abb3df8c91d'::uuid,'e61be13d-6512-5352-9b3b-5b8178fee3cc'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 7-D. Post-Declaration (45-day) Report Outline\n\nBasis: RA 12287 IRR Rule VI Sec. 1. The required content (declaration status, AA fund utilisation, estimated economic and social costs minimised) is followed; the remaining sections are proposed to make the report usable for learning. The outline will be revised when the NDRRMC issues the AA monitoring and evaluation framework.\n\n**Table 7.42**\n\n*Contents of the 45-day Report*\n\n| **No.** | **Section**                         | **Content**                                                                                                                     | **Basis**                     |\n|---------|-------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| 1       | Declaration status                  | Hazard; area; PDRA; CDRRMC recommendation; RDRRMC VIII action; Mayor''s EO; dates of declaration and lifting; ground for lifting | Rule VI Sec. 1                |\n| 2       | Anticipatory actions                | Measures carried out by lead office and barangay; people and assets covered (aggregate)                                         | Proposed                      |\n| 3       | AA fund utilisation                 | Spending by source and measure; tranche compliance; unused funds transferred to the STF                                         | Rule VI Sec. 1; Rule V Sec. 3 |\n| 4       | Economic and social costs minimised | Estimate with method and assumptions                                                                                            | Rule VI Sec. 1                |\n| 5       | Lessons                             | Summary of the AAR findings and actions                                                                                         | Proposed                      |\n| 6       | Annexes                             | EO; lifting record (GOV-F12); FIN-F06 closure                                                                                   | Proposed                      |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch7v1_Annex_7-D.md (Chapter 7, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('06bfc02d-7532-50d0-8821-3307aa5d772b'::uuid,'b83a91de-80b0-53ca-b792-f9e4baf07a63'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 7-E. Disaster Event and Loss Database Fields\n\n**Table 7.43**\n\n*Minimum Fields of the Event and Loss Record (ME-F07)*\n\n| **Field group**                      | **Fields**                                                                                                                                     | **Sendai link** |\n|--------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------|\n| Event                                | Event ID; name; hazard type; start and end dates; source agency product IDs; declarations in force                                             | —               |\n| Location                             | Barangays affected (PSGC codes); area description                                                                                              | —               |\n| Human effects                        | Dead; missing; injured; affected persons and families; displaced in and outside evacuation centres (aggregate, by sex and age where available) | A, B            |\n| Housing                              | Houses partially and totally damaged                                                                                                           | B, C            |\n| Economic loss                        | Direct loss by sector (agriculture, infrastructure, housing, business), PHP, with valuation method                                             | C               |\n| Critical infrastructure and services | Damaged health and education facilities, roads, bridges, power and water; duration of service disruption                                       | D               |\n| Response and AA                      | Whether an EOC activation, State of Calamity or State of Imminent Disaster applied; AA carried out                                             | G               |\n| Sources and quality                  | Source document for each figure; date; quality flag; reviewer                                                                                  | —               |\n\n*Note.* Aggregate counts only. Blank means "not reported". Small counts are reviewed by the DPO before publication.\n\n# Validation and Source Control Register\n\n**Table 7.44**\n\n*Validation and Source Control Register*\n\n| **Item**                         | **Source used**                                                      | **Status**                                                    |\n|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|\n| IAT rows and scores              | IAT v1.7 workbook in the repository (12 Nov 2026)                    | Pending validation, 1–4 December 2026                         |\n| M&E design and reporting outputs | Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032, Chapter 5 and Table 33       | Confirm Form 5D and reporting dates with the CPDO             |\n| RA 10121 Sec. 12(c) item numbers | Cited in IAT v1.7 (A5.4, A10.4)                                      | Check item numbers against the official text                  |\n| 45-day report content            | RA 12287 IRR Rule VI Sec. 1; GOV-F13                                 | Route to be confirmed with OCD VIII; AA M&E framework pending |\n| Local EOs on M&E                 | EO 2022-00020; EO 2023-0026; EO 2024-032A, as summarised in IAT v1.7 | Attach copies in the Vault                                    |\n| NPC circulars                    | NPC Circulars No. 16-03 and No. 2023-06                              | DPO to confirm current text                                   |\n| Validation controls              | ADR-029 (MODE v1.15.0)                                               | Proposed; for the CDRRMO Head to accept before the workshop   |\n| AAR and exercise timings         | Proposed by this chapter                                             | For CDRRMC adoption                                           |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch7v1_Annex_7-E.md (Chapter 7, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('178f631a-2675-57e5-92fe-3f34a52b2418'::uuid,'19accd5e-3619-5fb2-8a61-2e9ad4691fdf'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 8-A. IAT and Master Requirements Traceability\n\n**Table 8.33**\n\n*IAT Rows, Master Requirements and Chapter 8 Procedures*\n\n| **IAT** | **Type / lead**   | **w** | **Score** | **Gap priority** | **Master requirements**              | **SOP**    | **Recommended action (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                                                |\n|---------|-------------------|-------|-----------|------------------|--------------------------------------|------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A10.1   | GP                | 2     | 1         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-02; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 8-02, 8-08 | Reframe as technical mutual aid MOAs with Kananga, Albuera, Merida, Isabel and the Province; join the Leyte DRRMCs Telecom and Response Network. |\n| A10.2   | GP                | 1     | 3         | 1.0 Low          | MR-02; MR-18; MR-01; MR-12–15        | 8-01, 8-07 | Keep a partner register; review MOAs yearly at a CDRRMC meeting; file signed copies; add AA roles and resource commitments.                      |\n| A1.2    | M                 | 2     | 3         | 3.0 Low          | MR-02; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 8-06       | Amend EO 2024-032A: City College of Ormoc and an academe seat; third private seat; SP accreditation of each CSO.                                 |\n| A8.5    | M                 | 2     | 3         | 3.0 Low          | MR-05; MR-01; MR-12–15               | 8-03       | Adopt a cluster EO aligned to the national clusters, naming lead and member offices (Chapter 4).                                                 |\n| B6.6    | GP                | 2     | 1         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-08; MR-16; MR-18; MR-01; MR-12–15 | 8-01, 8-05 | Sign agreements listing item, quantity, trigger and contact, usable for AA (Chapter 6).                                                          |\n| C1.1    | CPDO              | 2     | 2         | 4.0 Medium       | MR-03; MR-17; MR-18                  | 8-04       | Define most-probable and most-severe scenarios; use them in CDP and CLUP priorities.                                                             |\n| C1.2    | CPDO; Budget      | 1     | 1         | 3.0 Low          | MR-03; MR-17; MR-18                  | 8-04       | Add a DRR-CCA screening form to LDC and LFC review.                                                                                              |\n| C1.3    | CPDO              | 1     | 2         | 2.0 Low          | MR-03; MR-17; MR-18                  | 8-04       | Estimate jobs, income, displacement, ecosystem and heritage losses in the CDRA update.                                                           |\n| C1.4    | CPDO; Zoning      | 2     | 2         | 4.0 Medium       | MR-03; MR-17; MR-18                  | 8-04       | Add hazard overlay zones and permit conditions in the ZO update.                                                                                 |\n| C2.1    | Bldg Official     | 3     | 1         | 9.0 High         | MR-03; MR-16; MR-17; MR-18           | 8-04, 8-05 | Assess the EOC, evacuation centres and critical facilities; write a rapid pre-impact inspection checklist.                                       |\n| C2.2    | CDRRMO; utilities | 2     | 2         | 4.0 Medium       | MR-03; MR-16; MR-17; MR-18           | 8-05       | Sign restoration-priority arrangements with power, water and telecom providers.                                                                  |\n| C2.3    | Engg; DPWH; port  | 2     | 1         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-03; MR-16; MR-17; MR-18           | 8-04       | Write transport anticipatory actions with DPWH, the City Engineer, traffic office and port.                                                      |\n| C3.1    | CENRO             | 2     | 2         | 4.0 Medium       | MR-03; MR-17; MR-18                  | 8-04       | Monitor watershed forest cover and mangroves yearly; report to the CDRRMC.                                                                       |\n| C3.2    | CENRO             | 1     | 1         | 3.0 Low          | MR-03; MR-17; MR-18                  | 8-02       | Formalise watershed arrangements with DENR, EDC and neighbouring LGUs.                                                                           |\n| C3.3    | CENRO; Engg; GSO  | 2     | 1         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-03; MR-16; MR-17                  | 8-04       | Write the declogging and debris protocol.                                                                                                        |\n| C4.1    | CHO; hospitals    | 3     | 1         | 9.0 High         | MR-05; MR-16; MR-17                  | 8-04       | Prepare the health anticipatory action plan with the CHO and hospitals.                                                                          |\n| C4.2    | DepEd SDO         | 2     | 1         | 6.0 Medium       | MR-05; MR-16; MR-17                  | 8-04       | Obtain school AA plans and learning-continuity modalities; agree class suspension and school-use triggers.                                       |\n| C4.3    | Agri; Vet         | 2     | 2         | 4.0 Medium       | MR-05; MR-16; MR-17                  | 8-04       | Draft agri-fisheries early action guidelines using RSBSA and FishR; file the El Niño Task Force plan.                                            |\n| C4.4    | DTI; market       | 1     | 1         | 3.0 Low          | MR-05; MR-16; MR-17                  | 8-04       | Agree pre-impact price monitoring with DTI Leyte and the market office.                                                                          |\n| C5.1    | CDRRMO; Chamber   | 1     | 1         | 3.0 Low          | MR-04; MR-17; MR-18                  | 8-04       | Run business continuity outreach with the Chamber using CDRA scenarios.                                                                          |\n\n*Note.* Copied from IAT v1.7 and the Traceability and Data Dictionary v1.2. For Part C rows the master requirements shown are those specific to the row; every row also carries MR-01 and MR-12 to MR-15. Part C rows carry the lead office in the Type column and are scored into the Interface Index, not the IMI.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch8v1_Annex_8-A.md (Chapter 8, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('079453b4-df63-5dec-8017-66ca38ca18c7'::uuid,'56d898cb-21cb-5848-9051-be21ae734dc6'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 8-B. Model Outline of a Technical Mutual Aid Agreement\n\n**Table 8.34**\n\n*Outline of a Technical Mutual Aid Agreement*\n\n| **No.** | **Article**                     | **Content to be negotiated**                                                                                   |\n|---------|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Parties and authority           | LGUs; sanggunian resolutions authorising each Mayor; optional accession by the Province                        |\n| 2       | Purpose and principles          | Technical cooperation in DRRM; reciprocity; no transfer of authority; each party first protects its own people |\n| 3       | Definitions                     | Requesting party, assisting party, resources, deployment, host Incident Commander                              |\n| 4       | Resources covered               | Types of teams, equipment and services; annexed lists updated yearly                                           |\n| 5       | Activation                      | Who may request; request content; response time; right to decline                                              |\n| 6       | Command, control and safety     | Under the host IC (ICS); own supervisors; right of recall                                                      |\n| 7       | Logistics                       | Self-sufficiency period; food, lodging and fuel arrangements                                                   |\n| 8       | Costs and settlement            | What each party bears; reimbursement method; timing                                                            |\n| 9       | Liability and insurance         | Personnel and equipment insurance; claims; indemnity                                                           |\n| 10      | Information and data            | Directory, warnings and SitRep exchange; data protection                                                       |\n| 11      | Exercises and review            | Annual joint exercise or call-down; annual review                                                              |\n| 12      | Term, amendment and termination | Duration; renewal; notice period                                                                               |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch8v1_Annex_8-B.md (Chapter 8, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('03fdffa3-f1eb-552d-9ca6-2f49ede33ad2'::uuid,'8611a789-4246-5172-90b9-a283ff80c42f'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 8-C. Model Outline of a Part C Interface Agreement\n\n**Table 8.35**\n\n*Outline of an Interface Agreement Between the CDRRMO and a Lead Office*\n\n| **No.** | **Section**           | **Content**                                                                                                        |\n|---------|-----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Parties and approval  | CDRRMO and the lead office; approved by the City Administrator (or included in a Mayor''s EO)                       |\n| 2       | Indicators owned      | Part C indicator codes and statements from IAT v1.7                                                                |\n| 3       | Focal persons         | Positions and alternates on both sides                                                                             |\n| 4       | Actions for the year  | From the IAT recommended actions and the CAPA register; outputs and dates                                          |\n| 5       | Information exchanged | What the CDRRMO supplies (hazard maps, scenarios, event data) and what the lead office supplies (evidence, status) |\n| 6       | Role in events        | PDRA participation; anticipatory actions; reporting to the EOC                                                     |\n| 7       | Review                | Annual interface review; self-scoring with validation by another office                                            |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch8v1_Annex_8-C.md (Chapter 8, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('afeb01a9-2658-5b55-8570-8d1658efd57d'::uuid,'c01c2a2c-ad39-5d76-8c53-cd7de830f341'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'# Annex 8-D. Agenda of the Annual Partner Forum\n\n**Table 8.36**\n\n*Annual Partner Forum Agenda*\n\n| **No.** | **Item**                         | **Output**                                                                      |\n|---------|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Season outlook and hazard update | Shared understanding of the coming season (PAGASA products presented as issued) |\n| 2       | What we learned last season      | AAR findings involving partners; agreed improvements                            |\n| 3       | Partner register review          | Confirmed roles, contacts and commitments; lapsing agreements identified        |\n| 4       | Interfaces and lifelines         | Status of Part C actions and utility arrangements                               |\n| 5       | Inclusion and accountability     | Input from sectoral, faith-based and community groups                           |\n| 6       | Exercises for the year           | Partner participation in the exercise calendar (Chapter 9)                      |\n| 7       | Actions                          | Owners and dates entered in the CAPA register                                   |\n\n# Validation and Source Control Register\n\n**Table 8.37**\n\n*Validation and Source Control Register*\n\n| **Item**                              | **Source used**                                                                                         | **Status**                                                     |\n|---------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|\n| IAT rows and scores                   | IAT v1.7 workbook in the repository (12 Nov 2026)                                                       | Pending validation, 1–4 December 2026                          |\n| Inter-LGU assistance records          | CDRRMC and SP resolutions in the City issuances register                                                | Attach copies in the Vault                                     |\n| CPR-RIMT MOA                          | SP Res. No. 2023-347; annexed copy unsigned                                                             | Obtain and file the signed copy                                |\n| CCCM Team EO number                   | Register: filed as No. 263; scan reads 243                                                              | Confirm with the City Secretary                                |\n| Cluster citation                      | IAT cites NDCC MC No. 12, s. 2008; DILG VIII SOP cites NDCC Circular No. 5, s. 2007 and No. 04, s. 2008 | Confirm with OCD VIII                                          |\n| RA 10121 Sec. 12(c) item for linkages | Cited in IAT v1.7 as item (15)                                                                          | Check against the official text                                |\n| CSO accreditation status              | Not shown in the document set                                                                           | SP Secretariat to confirm                                      |\n| Faith-based partners                  | None found                                                                                              | Invite to the first partner forum                              |\n| Augmentation route and regional SOPs  | National Disaster Response Plan; RDRRMC VIII practice                                                   | Confirm the current request route with the PDRRMO and OCD VIII |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch8v1_Annex_8-D.md (Chapter 8, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft'),
('72c76001-de05-5658-b23b-27171551e508'::uuid,'d47f0498-4189-5772-9e6c-fecd708e1cbc'::uuid,'v0.1 proposed (EOC Module note, 6 Nov 2026)',E'# SOP 6-GIS-01 | Establishment, Updating and Operational Use of the EOC Geospatial Common Operating Picture\n\n**Status:** proposed SOP for the EOC Operations, Information Management, Logistics and Coordination chapter (MOO Chapter 6, not yet drafted). Loaded as a controlled draft so the Geo-COP workflow (WF-GEO-COP) points to an existing text. Source: the 6 November 2026 note "Proposed Enhancement to the EOC Module: Geospatial Operations and Common Operating Picture", section 3, unchanged except for this header.\n\nTitle: Establishment, Updating and Operational Use of the EOC Geospatial Common Operating Picture\n\nPurpose: To ensure that the EOC maintains a consistent, validated and operationally useful geographic representation of incidents, affected areas, facilities, resources and response operations.\n\n| Step                          | Responsible actor                                      | Required action                                                                                                                 | Output / control                          |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| 1. Establish reference        | EOC Information Management function / CDRRMO           | Register the EOC point from the supplied KML and verify the coordinates through field validation.                               | EOC geospatial reference register         |\n| 2. Acquire datasets           | EOC Information Management function                    | Obtain available base maps, hazard layers, administrative boundaries and critical-facility datasets from authorized custodians. | Geospatial data inventory                 |\n| 3. Validate data              | Designated data custodian / technical office           | Check source, date, coordinate system, geometry, completeness and consistency.                                                  | Data validation record                    |\n| 4. Integrate layers           | EOC Information Management function                    | Load approved datasets into the designated GIS or mapping platform and apply agreed layer naming and symbology.                 | Geo-COP operational map                   |\n| 5. Plot incident information  | EOC Situation Unit or designated information personnel | Record incident locations and spatially reference reports received from barangays, field teams and agencies.                    | Georeferenced incident records            |\n| 6. Verify field information   | Concerned technical office / field team                | Confirm material incident locations, access constraints and reported impacts through appropriate field verification.            | Verification status and supporting report |\n| 7. Update operational picture | EOC Information Management function                    | Update incident, evacuation, resource and infrastructure layers as validated information becomes available.                     | Time-stamped Geo-COP                      |\n| 8. Disseminate                | EOC Manager / authorized information officer           | Share approved map products with decision-makers and operational partners according to access permissions.                      | Situation map / map briefing              |\n| 9. Archive and review         | EOC Information Management function                    | Preserve operational map versions and underlying records for incident review, reporting and after-action analysis.              | Archived map versions and metadata        |\n\nOperational control: Each map product shall indicate its preparation date and time, data sources, verification status and intended use. Preliminary or unverified information must be visibly distinguished from confirmed information.\n\nThe SOP should be activated as part of the EOC''s information-management function whenever the EOC is activated. During normal operations, the CDRRMO shall maintain the underlying datasets, registers and readiness arrangements.\n\n**Related records in MODE:** forms EOC-GIS-REF and EOC-GIS-01 to EOC-GIS-04; EOC → Map (ADR-015). Chapter 4 (SOP 4-05) uses the Geo-COP for the incident situation; Chapter 6 owns this procedure when drafted.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/SOP_6-GIS-01_proposed.md (Area 6, v0.1 proposed (EOC Module note, 6 Nov 2026))','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('09ceaf13-f2e8-5523-85cb-62edce828c64'::uuid,'c906fb73-66a3-5477-8732-349b15f6e5c4'::uuid,'v1.2 working draft (IAT v1.5)',E'**CHAPTER 1  \n**\n\n**LEGAL MANDATE, DRRM GOVERNANCE AND  \nINSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY**\n\n**  \n**\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)  \nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*  \nAssessment-informed working draft  \nSubject to institutional and legal validation*\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Document attribute** | **Control information**                                                                                                                                          |\n|------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document title         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 1                                                                                                           |\n| Chapter title          | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability                                                                                                  |\n| Document status        | Working draft for technical and institutional validation                                                                                                         |\n| Document owner         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                               |\n| Technical custodian    | CDRRMO Head / designated MOO focal person                                                                                                                        |\n| Reviewing offices      | City Legal Office; Office of the City Mayor; Sangguniang Panlungsod Committee on Disaster Management; CPDO; CDRRMO; OCD Region VIII; other offices as applicable |\n| Version / date         | Draft 1.2 / 6 November 2026                                                                                                                                      |\n| Approval authority     | To be determined under the City''s document-control and approval arrangements                                                                                     |\n| Review cycle           | At least annually and following material changes in law, governance arrangements or operational requirements                                                     |\n\n# REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date**   | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                          | **Prepared / reviewed by** | **Approval** |\n|-------------|------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | Oct 2026   | Revised assessment-based working draft (IAT v1.3)                                                                                                                                                        | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n| 1.1         | 6 Nov 2026 | Updated to IAT v1.5: October 2026 FGD, KIIs and site visits; 2021–2024 City issuances; revised findings, priorities and action plan. Restyled to the Chapter 2 design.                                   | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n| 1.2         | 6 Nov 2026 | Added staff perspectives on planning and budgeting (1.4), the scenario-based EOC structure (6.3), the Brgy. Liloan site visit, the EOC geospatial reference point, and APA-captioned figures and tables. | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction\n\nDisaster risk reduction and management (DRRM) in Ormoc City requires an integrated institutional system that connects executive leadership, policy direction, technical management, operational coordination and community-level action. Its effectiveness depends not only on the existence of the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council (CDRRMC), the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC), but also on the clarity of their respective mandates, decision-making authority, functional relationships, accountability arrangements and operational interfaces.\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, governance and institutional accountability framework for the City''s CDRRMO and EOC. It defines how the City Government organizes, directs, coordinates, monitors and continuously improves its DRRM functions across normal operations, preparedness, anticipatory action, emergency response and recovery.\n\nThe chapter adopts a systems-thinking perspective: disaster risk is understood as the consequence of interconnected hazards, exposure, vulnerability, institutional capacities and dependencies across social, economic, environmental and physical systems. Accordingly, the City''s DRRM governance arrangements must operate as an integrated system rather than as a collection of independent offices, committees and emergency procedures.\n\nThe chapter also applies Total Quality Management (TQM) principles to institutional operations. These include process ownership, evidence-based decision-making, stakeholder participation, standardization, performance measurement, corrective action and continuous improvement.\n\nThe institutional provisions are informed by the **Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.7 (workbook v1-7, 11 November 2026)**, particularly its nine assessment sheets.\n\nThe assessment is treated as a diagnostic baseline, not as a substitute for legal compliance verification or formal institutional approval.\n\n## 1.1 Assessment-Informed Governance Priorities\n\nThe October 2026 assessment identifies a significant distinction between the maturity of the CDRRMO, the EOC and their institutional interface.\n\n**Figure 1.1**\n\n*Institutional Maturity and Mandatory Compliance, IAT v1.7*\n\n![Figure 1: 1.1 Assessment-Informed Governance Priorities](/library/ch1v12/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Maturity index on a 0–4 scale. The Institutional Maturity Index (IMI) combines Parts A and B; Part C (institutional interface) is reported separately. Mandatory compliance is the share of scored mandatory indicators rated 3 or 4. Source: Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.5, Dashboard.\n\nThe results demonstrate that the CDRRMO has a stronger institutional foundation than the EOC, while the interface between the two remains the weakest component. This is a critical systems issue: the existence of functioning CDRRMO activities does not automatically translate into a coordinated, documented and reliable EOC operating system.\n\nThe dashboard identifies 29 high-priority gaps: 17 in the CDRRMO assessment (Part A) and 12 in the EOC assessment (Part B). The separate Part C assessment adds 2 high-priority interface gaps that need action by other City Government offices and external partners.\n\nFor this chapter, the most consequential findings are:\n\n**Table 1.1**\n\n*Most Consequential Assessment Findings for Governance (IAT v1.7)*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 52%" />\n<col style="width: 32%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Assessment reference</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Finding (IAT v1.7)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Governance implication</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td><p>A1.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>No ordinance establishes the CDRRMO as a regular department with the JMC 2014-1 divisions. An Executive Order is the only basis.</td>\n<td>The legal and organizational foundation needs an enabling ordinance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>A1.2 – Score 3</td>\n<td>EO 2024-032A names all RA 10121 Sec. 11 seats, including five CSO and two private-sector seats. Gaps: the academe seat was dropped; the City College of Ormoc, recommended by CDRRMC Res. No. 10 s.2024, has no amending EO; SP accreditation of the CSOs is not shown; the third private-sector seat is vacant.</td>\n<td>Issue an amending EO and keep a yearly membership register.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>A1.3 – Score 3</td>\n<td>Meetings and resolutions are on file for 2024–2025, but not for four consecutive quarters. The presiding officer changes from meeting to meeting without a written designation by the Chair.</td>\n<td>Keep quarterly meetings; file minutes and attendance each quarter; designate the presiding officer in writing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>A1.4 – Score 3</td>\n<td>The CDRRMO acts as Secretariat, but no written secretariat procedure exists. Records lapses: an undated resolution, a reused control number, missing certifications, and two 2022 resolutions filed under each other''s numbers.</td>\n<td>Formalize the Secretariat SOP and a resolution register (GOV-02).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>A1.5 – Score 3</td>\n<td>Evacuation and class-suspension rules are applied event by event (CDRRMC Res. Nos. 08 and 09 s.2021 for Typhoon Odette; Res. No. 06 s.2022 for TD Agaton). The evacuation ordinance lacks procedural steps, and the Zoning Ordinance has no hazard overlay zones.</td>\n<td>Link ordinances to warning levels and risk information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td><p>A1.6 – Score 3</p>\n<p>(Medium)</p></td>\n<td>The State of Calamity process has been used (SP Res. No. 2021-366, Odette; CDRRMC Res. No. 06 s.2024, dengue), but there is no stand-alone written protocol, and Quick Response Fund resolutions do not cite the declaration relied on.</td>\n<td>Standardize declaration, QRF use and lifting (GOV-04).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>A1.7 – Score 3</td>\n<td>DRRM takes part in the LFC review and the CDP. No CDC or LFC minutes show CDRRMO input changing a decision.</td>\n<td>Use the CDC and LFC seats to secure anticipatory-action and EOC funding.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td><p>A1.8 – Score 1</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>No State of Imminent Disaster procedure, RDRRMC request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template exists.</td>\n<td>Establish the RA 12287 anticipatory governance process (GOV-05).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td><p>A2.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>CDRRMO sections exist but not as an approved JMC 2014-1 structure; normal-operations and response tasks are mixed.</td>\n<td>Approve the permanent structure (Chapter 2).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td><p>B1.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>No EO creates the EOC. Site visit (October 2026): a developing EOC, co-located with the OpCen in the CDRRM Office, runs 24-hour duty for City Traffic Monitoring, Public Safety and Emergency Monitoring.</td>\n<td>Issue an EO establishing the EOC and its relationship with the CDRRMC, the OpCen and ICS.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td><p>B6.1 – Score 1</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>The ICS–EOC interface and chain of command are not defined.</td>\n<td>Separate incident command from EOC coordination.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n*Note.* Scores use the 0–4 IAT scale; priority is shown where it is High or Medium.\n\nThe assessment also identifies broader system dependencies involving City Planning, Engineering, Budget, Environment, Social Welfare, Health, Education, Utilities and other institutions. These are addressed through the interface arrangements established in this chapter and the succeeding operational modules.\n\n**Management implication**: The priority is not simply to create more committees or issue additional policies. It is to establish a coherent governance system in which each mandate has an accountable owner, each process has a defined workflow, each decision has a legitimate authority, and each institutional output can be verified.\n\n**Assessment interpretation**: The workbook''s Dashboard (IAT v1.7, 11 Nov 2026) reports 1.98/4.00 as the combined Institutional Maturity Index, after the evidence rule (Methodology 5.2) was applied to 15 indicators. Part C is reported separately and is not included in that index. Unscored indicators and provisional desk assessments must not be read as proof of compliance.\n\n## 1.2 Interpretation of institutional Maturity and Compliance\n\nThe assessment methodology distinguishes institutional maturity from mandatory compliance. A domain may achieve a relatively high maturity index while remaining legally non-compliant because one or more mandatory requirements have not attained the required rating.\n\nThis distinction is particularly important for Ormoc:\n\n- CDRRMO Legal Mandate and DRRM Governance (A1) has a maturity index of 2.47/4.00, but only 66.7% mandatory compliance.\n\n- EOC Legal Basis and Concept of Operations (B1) has a maturity index of 2.23/4.00, but only 20% mandatory compliance.\n\n- The overall mandatory compliance rate is 32.9%, against a combined maturity index of 1.98.\n\nThe implication is that institutional improvements must address both capability and compliance. A procedure that is practiced informally cannot automatically be treated as an established institutional control. Similarly, the existence of an executive order, organizational chart or operational facility does not establish compliance unless the relevant requirements are documented, approved, implemented and supported by evidence.\n\nThe assessment uses the following evidence-based maturity logic:\n\n| **Rating** | **Maturity classification**  | **Institutional interpretation**                                                             |\n|------------|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 0          | Absent                       | No established arrangement.                                                                  |\n| 1          | Initial / Ad hoc             | Practice depends on individuals and is not adequately documented.                            |\n| 2          | Developing                   | Arrangement exists but is incomplete, outdated, unapproved or inconsistently implemented.    |\n| 3          | Compliant / Established      | Applicable standards are met through approved documentation and demonstrated implementation. |\n| 4          | Advanced / Institutionalized | Compliance is sustained, tested, reviewed and improved through documented learning.          |\n\nFor purposes of this MOO, a procedure shall not be considered fully institutionalized merely because it appears in the Manual. Its implementation must be demonstrated through records, operational outputs, verification and periodic review.\n\n## 1.3 Assessment Methods and Evidence Base\n\nThe October 2026 assessment used four methods. Each indicator was scored under the evidence rule in Methodology 5.2: interviews alone support a maximum score of 2; a score of 3 needs an approved document and proof of use; a score of 4 adds a test, exercise or after-action review.\n\n**Table 1.2**\n\n*Assessment Methods Used in October–November 2026*\n\n| **Method**                  | **Coverage**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | **Use in this chapter**                                                                           |\n|-----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Focus group discussion      | CDRRMO Head and staff, held at the CDRRM Office, which houses the OpCen and the EOC. Indicators were scored together using the IAT workbook.                                                                                                                             | Baseline scores for A1, A2 and B1                                                                 |\n| Key informant interviews    | CDRRMO Chiefs of Operations, Research and Planning, and Administration; CPDO; CSWDO; City Administrator''s Office. Positions only, per RA 10173.                                                                                                                          | Roles, interfaces and decision rights                                                             |\n| Observation and site visits | EOC; Operations and Rescue Office; Brgy. Liloan, one of the flood-prone barangays, with an established evacuation site, evacuation protocol, and camp coordination and management arrangements.                                                                          | EOC status (B1.1), duty system (A2.5), evacuation and camp management practice (A1.5, A3.6, A8.5) |\n| Document review             | City plans (DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032, CDP 2024–2030, LCCAP, CDRA, CLUP); 23 City documents from 2023–2025; 48 City issuances from 2021–2024 (CDRRMC resolutions, Executive Orders, SP resolutions); the CDRRMO organizational chart and internal staff papers (2026). | Legal basis, Council practice, declarations and funding decisions                                 |\n\n*Note.* Informants are identified by office only, per RA 10173.\n\n**Stakeholder position.** All offices consulted (CDRRMO, CPDO, CSWDO and the City Administrator''s Office) share one aim: clear roles and responsibilities for each unit and accountable person, and that these are carried out. All agree that a Manual of Operations will help significantly. This chapter responds by giving each governance function an accountable owner, a workflow and a record.\n\n**Issuance review (2021–2024).** The review found the following points relevant to governance:\n\n- **Council composition.** The CDRRMC was reorganized by EO No. 2022-0006 and later by EO Nos. 2023-0041 and 2024-032A. The membership register shall carry only the latest EO and its amendments.\n\n- **Warning-driven actions are decided event by event.** Pre-emptive evacuation (CDRRMC Res. No. 09 s.2021) and suspension of work and classes (Res. No. 08 s.2021; Res. No. 06 s.2022) were each adopted by a separate resolution at the time of the event. A pre-set warning–action matrix (A6.2) shall replace this.\n\n- **Declarations.** The SP adopted a State of Calamity for Typhoon Odette (SP Res. No. 2021-366). The CDRRMC recommended a one-year State of Calamity for the 2024 dengue outbreak (Res. No. 06 s.2024). See Process 4 (GOV-04).\n\n- **Odette response decisions.** The Council approved a PHP 2 M standby fund for prepositioning food and non-food items (Res. No. 11 s.2021) and asked LEYECO V to run its 2 MW standby generator during widespread outages (Res. No. 10 s.2021). It also sent cash aid from the 2021 Quick Response Fund to other LGUs (SP Res. Nos. 2021-365, -367 and -369). No standing mutual-aid agreement exists (A10.1).\n\n- **Planning and funding.** The Council approved the DRRM 10-Year Plan (Res. No. 01 s.2023), and EO No. 2022-00020 created the DRRM Plan Technical Working Group. The flood control and drainage master plan was funded in 2023 (Res. Nos. 03, 04 and 07 s.2023; about PHP 11.0 M), but no output was sighted.\n\n- **Records control.** The files of CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03 and 04 s.2022 contain each other''s text; the CCCM Executive Order is filed as No. 263 but the scan reads 243; SP Res. No. 2021-357 still cites 110 barangays. These findings support GOV-02 and GOV-08.\n\n## 1.4 Staff Perspectives on DRRM Planning and Budgeting\n\nDuring the assessment, CDRRMO staff shared written reflections on how the office plans, budgets and coordinates, together with a proposal to strengthen the link with barangays (Chapter 2, section 10.2). The papers show a shared commitment to a risk-informed office. The assessment team read them alongside the City''s records, so that points the office already agrees on can be acted on now, and points that need more data can be settled together at the December 2026 validation workshop.\n\n**Table 1.3**\n\n*Themes Raised in Staff Reflections and What the Records Show*\n\n| **Theme**                                     | **What the records show**                                                                                                                                                                                     | **Agreed way forward**                                                        |\n|-----------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Balance of the budget across thematic areas   | The 2022 Trust Fund plan gave 20% of the 70% component to prevention and mitigation, 55% to preparedness and 25% to response (Figure 1.2). No thematic split is on file yet for 2024–2026.                    | Table the 2024–2026 LDRRMF split by thematic area at the next CDRRMC meeting. |\n| Use of risk data in decisions                 | A CDRA exists (EO No. 2023-0025) but is not yet carried into zoning, project screening or investment-plan tagging. There is no loss database.                                                                 | Score every proposal against the CDRA and the LDRRMP (A3.2, C1.2).            |\n| Monitoring budget use against results         | No annual LDRRMP monitoring report or utilization report is on file. Trust Fund balances of PHP 79.5 M (2024) and PHP 52.7 M (2025) suggest slow use.                                                         | Start a quarterly results-and-finance report (A10.4, A4.4).                   |\n| Preparedness items that need funding          | Hazard-specific contingency plans, the Public Service Continuity Plan, this Manual, partnerships and school programs are all CDP 2024–2030 PPAs (2.4, 2.5, 2.10.3, 2.16). The gap is execution, not planning. | Track each PPA in the Action Plan.                                            |\n| Alignment with national and global frameworks | The DRRM 10-Year Plan follows the NDRRMC 2022 guidebook; the CDP adopts the UNDRR Ten Essentials. No LCCAP–LDRRMP crosswalk exists.                                                                           | Prepare the crosswalk with the CPDO.                                          |\n| Link between planning and budgeting           | No written budget cycle says who formulates and who executes the investment plan.                                                                                                                             | Adopt the budget-cycle RACI (Chapter 2, 2.16.2).                              |\n\n*Note.* Records reviewed: CDRRMC resolutions 2021–2025, CDP 2024–2030, DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 and the IAT v1.7 workbook. No record reviewed raises concerns about how any section handles funds. The City''s Full Disclosure postings (A10.5) and its model-LGU partnerships (EO No. 2023-0026) are strengths to build on.\n\n**Figure 1.2**\n\n*Allocation of the 70% Component of the 2022 Trust Fund LDRRMF by Thematic Area*\n\n![Figure 2: 1.4 Staff Perspectives on DRRM Planning and Budgeting](/library/ch1v12/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Data from the 2022 Trust Fund LDRRMF budget program and investment plan (CDRRMC Res. No. 01 s.2022). The response share funded COVID-19 vaccines; preparedness was mostly vehicles; prevention and mitigation was data equipment. Comparable splits for 2024–2026 are not yet available.\n\nThe following shared commitments are proposed for the office to confirm at the December 2026 workshop:\n\n- We will show how the LDRRMF splits across the four thematic areas, starting with 2024–2026, so that decisions rest on shared numbers.\n\n- Planning and execution need different strengths. Research and Planning brings risk evidence; Administration brings costing, procurement and audit discipline. The investment plan is strongest when both sign it.\n\n- Response readiness and long-term risk reduction both need funding. Scoring every proposal against the CDRA and the LDRRMP helps the office balance them.\n\n- Research and Planning will build one monitoring tool that every division reports into each quarter.\n\n- Research and Planning leads the formulation of the investment plan; Administration leads execution; the CDRRMO Head approves; the CDRRMC endorses.\n\n# 2. Purpose\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal and institutional governance arrangements governing the CDRRMO, CDRRMC and EOC.\n\nIt aims to:\n\n1.  Establish a clear and legally grounded division of authority among the Local Chief Executive, CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\n2.  Define the institutional responsibilities, decision rights and accountability of participating City Government offices.\n\n3.  Institutionalize transparent, inclusive and evidence-based DRRM governance.\n\n4.  Establish procedures for CDRRMC meetings, resolutions, recommendations and action monitoring.\n\n5.  Standardize the City''s procedures for State of Calamity and State of Imminent Disaster declarations.\n\n6.  Strengthen vertical and horizontal coordination among the City Government, barangays, national government agencies, neighbouring LGUs, CSOs, private-sector partners and humanitarian organizations.\n\n7.  Establish process ownership, responsibility assignments and decision-making controls using the RACI framework.\n\n8.  Embed risk-informed governance, quality assurance, performance monitoring and continuous improvement into institutional operations.\n\n# 3. Scope and Application\n\nThis chapter applies to the following institutional actors:\n\n- Office of the City Mayor;\n\n- CDRRMC and its Secretariat;\n\n- CDRRMO Head and functional divisions;\n\n- EOC Manager and EOC functional sections;\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod;\n\n- City Government departments, offices and units;\n\n- Barangay DRRMCs in all 85 barangays;\n\n- National government agencies and regional DRRM institutions;\n\n- Accredited CSOs, ACDVs, private-sector organizations and other partners.\n\nIts provisions apply under three operating conditions:\n\n| **Operating condition**                       | **Institutional focus**                                                                                                                                | **Primary governance mechanism**                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Normal operations                             | Risk reduction, institutional planning, preparedness, capacity development, monitoring and continuity.                                                 | CDRRMC oversight and CDRRMO management.                                           |\n| Heightened preparedness / anticipatory action | Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment, warning-action decisions, resource readiness, anticipatory measures and possible State of Imminent Disaster declaration. | CDRRMC coordination, Mayor''s executive authority and CDRRMO technical leadership. |\n| Emergency operations                          | Incident coordination, situational awareness, resource mobilization, policy support and operational reporting.                                         | Activated EOC, Incident Management Team and designated response clusters.         |\n\nThe Manual applies to all hazards and emergencies within the City''s jurisdiction, including hydrometeorological, geological, climate-related, technological and human-induced hazards, subject to applicable institutional mandates.\n\nIt shall be read together with the City''s approved DRRM plans, contingency plans, Public Service Continuity Plan, climate and disaster risk assessment, development plans, land-use plans and other applicable local policies.\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Standards Framework\n\nThe institutional arrangements shall be interpreted and implemented within the following hierarchy of legal and policy instruments.\n\n## 4.1 Philippine Legal and Regulatory Framework\n\n| **Instrument**                                                  | **Relevant Provisions**                                                                  | **Application to this Chapter**                                                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Republic Act No. 10121 (2010)                                   | Sections 11–17 and 21                                                                    | Establishment and functions of CDRRMC/CDRRMO, emergency coordination, State of Calamity and LDRRMF governance.   |\n| IRR of RA 10121                                                 | Relevant provisions on local councils, offices, coordination and declarations            | Operationalization of statutory institutional responsibilities.                                                  |\n| RA 7160 (1991) – Local Government Code                          | Sections 16, 33, 76 and relevant provisions on local legislative and executive authority | Local autonomy, inter-LGU cooperation, organizational structure and local governance.                            |\n| RA 12287 (2025) – Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act | Sections 3–7 and applicable implementing provisions                                      | Anticipatory declaration, pre-disaster risk assessment and early action.                                         |\n| IRR of RA 12287 (2026)                                          | Rules II–VII                                                                             | Local declaration procedures, anticipatory action, funding, capacity development and post-declaration reporting. |\n| RA 9729 (2009), as amended by RA 10174                          | Climate change adaptation and financing provisions                                       | Integration of climate resilience into DRRM governance and planning.                                             |\n| RA 10173 (2012) – Data Privacy Act                              | Personal information processing and protection                                           | Protection of affected-population, beneficiary, responder and institutional information.                         |\n| RA 9710 (2009) – Magna Carta of Women                           | Gender-responsive governance                                                             | Gender-responsive and inclusive institutional arrangements.                                                      |\n| RA 7277, as amended                                             | Rights and accessibility of persons with disabilities                                    | Accessible governance, warning and emergency services.                                                           |\n| RA 9994 (2010)                                                  | Senior citizen protection                                                                | Inclusion of older persons in DRRM decision-making and service arrangements.                                     |\n| RA 10821 (2016)                                                 | Children''s Emergency Relief and Protection Act                                           | Child protection and child-sensitive emergency arrangements.                                                     |\n| RA 11036 (2018) – Mental Health Act                             | Mental health services and protection                                                    | Institutional and responder psychosocial support.                                                                |\n| RA 12076 (2024) – Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act                      | Standards for permanent evacuation centers                                               | Institutional coordination on evacuation infrastructure and facility compliance.                                 |\n| RA 12009 (2024) – New Government Procurement Act                | Government procurement and applicable emergency procurement procedures                   | Resource governance and procurement accountability.                                                              |\n\nThe Local Government Code and RA 10121 provide the foundation for the City''s organizational and disaster governance responsibilities. RA 12287 introduces an additional anticipatory governance mechanism that must be integrated into existing DRRM processes rather than treated as a separate emergency procedure.\n\n## 4.2 Administrative and Operational Guidelines\n\nThe following administrative and operational instruments shall guide institutional implementation:\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                    | **Application**                                                                                                   |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| NDRRMC-DILG-DBM-CSC JMC No. 2014-1                                                | LDRRMO organizational structure, staffing and functions.                                                          |\n| NDRRMC-DBM-DILG JMC No. 2013-1                                                    | LDRRMF programming and utilization.                                                                               |\n| COA Circular No. 2012-002                                                         | Accounting and reporting of the LDRRMF and Special Trust Fund.                                                    |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012                                         | Institutionalization and use of the Incident Command System.                                                      |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018, as identified in the assessment      | EOC establishment, operationalization and management; issuance details require confirmation with OCD Region VIII. |\n| NDCC Memorandum Circular No. 12, s. 2008                                          | Cluster-based disaster response coordination.                                                                     |\n| RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006 and DILG VIII memorandum dated 26 February 2025 | Harmonized pre-disaster, during-disaster, post-disaster and terminal reporting.                                   |\n| OCD/NDRRMC Public Service Continuity Planning Guidebook                           | Continuity of essential functions, business impact analysis, alternate arrangements and succession.               |\n| DILG Operation L!sto and applicable annual issuances                              | Preparedness and institutional readiness of LGUs and barangays.                                                   |\n\nJMC No. 2014-1 specifically recognizes the LDRRMO as a mandatory office and provides for the Head and three core functional areas: Administration and Training, Research and Planning, and Operations and Warning. It also permits appropriate augmentation of staffing and organizational arrangements, subject to the applicable requirements.\n\n## 4.3 International Frameworks and Management Standards\n\nThe MOO shall also be guided by the following frameworks:\n\n| **Framework/Standard**                                                            | **Application to Institutional Governance**                                                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030                            | Strengthening disaster risk governance, understanding risk, investing in resilience and enhancing preparedness and recovery. |\n| UNDRR Making Cities Resilient 2030 – Ten Essentials                               | Alignment with the City''s urban resilience governance and institutional performance.                                         |\n| UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities – Detailed Level Assessment (2017) | Diagnostic reference for institutional and city-wide resilience assessment.                                                  |\n| ISO 31000:2018 – Risk Management Guidelines                                       | Integrating risk identification, analysis, evaluation, treatment, monitoring and communication into governance.              |\n| ISO 9001:2015 – Quality Management Systems                                        | Process ownership, documented procedures, performance evaluation, corrective action and continual improvement.               |\n| ISO 22301:2019 – Business Continuity Management Systems                           | Continuity of essential services, organizational resilience and disruption management.                                       |\n| Sphere Handbook (2018)                                                            | Humanitarian quality and accountability principles for emergency assistance.                                                 |\n| CREST / systems-thinking approaches                                               | Understanding interdependencies, feedback loops, cascading impacts and system-level interventions.                           |\n\nThese frameworks are complementary rather than substitutes for Philippine laws and regulations. Where a standard or international framework is more demanding than the applicable statutory minimum, the City may adopt it as a good-practice benchmark, subject to its authority and resources.\n\n## 4.4 Policy Hierarchy and Conflict Resolution\n\nIn implementing this chapter, the following order shall apply:\n\n1.  Philippine Constitution and applicable statutes;\n\n2.  Valid implementing rules and regulations;\n\n3.  National administrative and operational issuances;\n\n4.  Provincial and regional coordination requirements, where applicable;\n\n5.  City ordinances and valid executive issuances;\n\n6.  Approved plans, policies and institutional procedures;\n\n7.  MOO protocols, checklists and supporting forms.\n\nIn case of inconsistency, the higher-ranking legal or regulatory instrument shall prevail. The CDRRMO shall refer material legal ambiguities to the City Legal Office and seek technical clarification from OCD Region VIII or the appropriate national agency.\n\nThe MOO shall maintain a legal and policy register, with an identified custodian and review date for each applicable instrument.\n\n# 5. Systems-Thinking Framework for DRRM Governance\n\n## 5.1 Institutional System Architecture\n\nOrmoc City''s DRRM governance shall operate as a connected system comprising five interdependent layers:\n\n1.  **Executive governance**: Political leadership, statutory authority and executive decision-making.\n\n2.  **Policy and oversigh**t: CDRRMC deliberation, policy direction, recommendations and monitoring.\n\n3.  **Institutional management**: CDRRMO planning, technical management, administration and implementation.\n\n4.  **Operational coordination**: EOC, ICS, response clusters and incident management arrangements.\n\n5.  **Community and external interface:** BDRRMCs, CSOs, private-sector institutions, utilities, national agencies and neighbouring LGUs.\n\n**Integrated CDRRMO–EOC Governance System**\n\n![Figure 3: 5.1 Institutional System Architecture](/library/ch1v12/fig-03.webp)\n\n**Conceptual relationship**. This is not a substitute for the statutory chain of authority or the approved ICS organizational chart.\n\nThe architecture recognizes that information, authority and resources move through different pathways.\n\n- Executive authority flows through legally established government structures.\n\n- Technical information flows from monitoring institutions, technical offices and communities toward decision-makers.\n\n- Operational instructions flow through the approved command and coordination arrangements.\n\n- Resources flow through authorized mobilization and accountability mechanisms.\n\n- Feedback and performance information flow back into planning, policy and institutional improvement.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall ensure that these pathways are connected but not conflated.\n\n## 5.2 The CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Interface\n\nThe assessment identifies the institutional interface as the weakest component, with a separate maturity index of 1.41/4.00.\n\nThis indicates that the problem is not limited to individual office capacity. It concerns the connections between institutional actors, their processes, information systems and decision-making arrangements.\n\nThe following operating distinctions shall therefore apply:\n\n| **Dimension**            | **CDRRMO**                                                                                     | **EOC**                                                                                   |\n|--------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Institutional status     | Permanent LGU office                                                                           | Operational coordination mechanism established through appropriate local issuance         |\n| Operating condition      | Continuous normal operations                                                                   | Activated according to approved triggers and authority                                    |\n| Primary accountability   | CDRRMO Head                                                                                    | Designated EOC Manager                                                                    |\n| Main function            | DRRM governance, planning, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery support | Incident coordination, information management, resource coordination and decision support |\n| Planning horizon         | Strategic, annual and multi-year                                                               | Incident-specific and operational-period based                                            |\n| Information              | Maintains institutional risk and readiness information                                         | Consolidates and validates current incident information                                   |\n| Relationship with ICS    | Provides institutional support and coordinates preparedness arrangements                       | Supports the Incident Commander and Incident Management Team                              |\n| Institutional continuity | Maintains permanent office functions                                                           | Operates under activation, deactivation and continuity arrangements                       |\n\nThe EOC shall not replace the CDRRMO. Nor shall it become an alternative command structure for field response.\n\nThe Incident Commander shall retain incident-level command authority under the applicable ICS arrangements. The EOC shall facilitate coordination, provide information, mobilize support and elevate decisions requiring executive action.\n\n## 5.3 Institutional Interfaces and System Dependencies\n\nPart C of the assessment identifies 15 institutional interface indicators. These are deliberately excluded from the CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Maturity Index because the primary operational ownership rests with other offices or external institutions.\n\n![Figure 4: 5.3 Institutional Interfaces and System Dependencies](/library/ch1v12/fig-04.webp)\n\nThe distinction is important for accountability.\n\n| **Interface Area**                                                  | **Primary Institutional Owner**                       | **CDRRMO’s Role**                                                                |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk-informed CDP/CLUP priorities and scenarios                     | City Planning and Development Office (CPDO)           | Supply risk evidence and provide DRR/CCA technical advice.                       |\n| DRR/CCA screening of projects and budgets                           | CPDO and City Budget Office                           | Provide hazard and resilience screening inputs.                                  |\n| Damage, livelihood and ecosystem loss analysis                      | CPDO and relevant sector offices                      | Supply disaster risk and impact information.                                     |\n| Hazard-sensitive zoning and building permits                        | CPDO and Zoning Administrator                         | Provide technical hazard information and participate in review.                  |\n| EOC, evacuation center and critical-facility structural assessments | City Engineer / Building Official                     | Identify critical facilities and coordinate risk prioritization.                 |\n| Power, water and telecommunications continuity                      | Utility providers and relevant City offices           | Coordinate restoration priorities and continuity agreements.                     |\n| Transport anticipatory action                                       | City Engineer, traffic office, DPWH and port operator | Integrate transport risk information into emergency coordination.                |\n| Watershed and ecosystem protection                                  | CENRO and DENR                                        | Integrate ecosystem-related hazards and protective functions into DRRM planning. |\n| Health anticipatory action                                          | City Health Office and hospitals                      | Coordinate the Health Cluster and operational information.                       |\n| Education continuity                                                | DepEd Schools Division                                | Coordinate school preparedness and learning-continuity arrangements.             |\n| Agriculture and fisheries anticipatory action                       | City Agriculture Office and City Veterinary Office    | Coordinate hazard information and response integration.                          |\n| Pre-impact price monitoring                                         | DTI and City Market Office                            | Facilitate information sharing with the EOC.                                     |\n| Business continuity outreach                                        | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce                        | Facilitate risk communication and preparedness outreach.                         |\n\nThe CDRRMO shall not assume technical or statutory responsibilities assigned to these offices. Its role is to establish the coordination mechanisms, information exchange and performance monitoring necessary for integrated city-wide resilience.\n\nThis distinction is essential to avoid institutional duplication and unfunded mandates.\n\n# 6. Governance Structure and Institutional Mandates\n\n![Figure 5: 6. Governance Structure and Institutional Mandates](/library/ch1v12/fig-05.webp)\n\n## 6.1 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council\n\nThe CDRRMC shall serve as the City''s principal DRRM policy-making, coordinating and oversight body under RA 10121.\n\nIts composition and functions shall conform to Section 11 of the Act and its IRR.\n\nThe City shall maintain an updated Executive Order identifying the membership, institutional representation, designated alternates and coordination responsibilities of the Council.\n\nThe assessment (IAT v1.7, A1.2) identifies the following governance requirements:\n\n- Issuing an amending EO to EO 2024-032A, the current reorganization order;\n\n- Keeping the CSO seats (five under EO 2024-032A) supported by SP accreditation of each CSO;\n\n- Filling the vacant third private-sector seat and restoring an academe seat (the City College of Ormoc, recommended by CDRRMC Res. No. 10 s.2024);\n\n- Maintaining institutional representation rather than relying solely on individual appointments; and\n\n- Reviewing the membership register annually.\n\nThe CDRRMC shall ensure representation from relevant City Government offices, national agencies, public safety institutions, CSOs, the private sector and other prescribed members.\n\nIts principal functions shall include:\n\n1.  Approving, monitoring and evaluating the Local DRRM Plan;\n\n2.  Integrating DRR and climate change adaptation into local development plans and budgets;\n\n3.  Providing strategic direction for DRRM programs and policies;\n\n4.  Recommending pre-emptive and forced evacuation where warranted;\n\n5.  Recommending a State of Calamity declaration to the appropriate local legislative authority;\n\n6.  Coordinating anticipatory action under RA 12287;\n\n7.  Mobilizing institutional and external resources;\n\n8.  Monitoring the use of DRRM funds and other dedicated resources; and\n\n9.  Reviewing institutional performance, assessment findings and corrective actions.\n\n## 6.2 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office\n\nThe CDRRMO shall function as the City''s permanent institutional and technical mechanism for DRRM.\n\nIn accordance with Section 12 of RA 10121, it shall be responsible for setting the direction, development, implementation and coordination of local DRRM programs.\n\nIts responsibilities include:\n\n- Risk assessment and local risk information management;\n\n- Local DRRM planning and contingency planning;\n\n- Multi-hazard early warning;\n\n- Training, orientation and knowledge management;\n\n- Resource and capability inventory;\n\n- Coordination with communities, CSOs and private-sector partners;\n\n- Emergency monitoring and mobilization;\n\n- Organization and supervision of emergency response teams and ACDVs;\n\n- Recovery coordination;\n\n- CDRRMC Secretariat functions; and\n\n- Preparation and reporting of DRRM plans, budgets and fund utilization.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall perform these responsibilities through its approved organizational structure and in coordination with other City Government offices.\n\n## 6.3 Emergency Operations Center\n\nThe EOC shall provide the City''s centralized mechanism for operational coordination during emergencies.\n\nThe EOC shall:\n\n1.  Maintain situational awareness;\n\n2.  Consolidate, validate and disseminate incident information;\n\n3.  Support CDRRMC and executive decision-making;\n\n4.  Facilitate coordination among the Incident Management Team and response clusters;\n\n5.  Coordinate resource requests and mobilization;\n\n6.  Maintain incident and decision records;\n\n7.  Facilitate vertical coordination with provincial and regional DRRM institutions, as applicable; and\n\n8.  Support transition from response to recovery.\n\nThe EOC shall operate within the authority established by the Mayor and the approved institutional arrangements.\n\n**Scenario-based activation.** The EOC is one fixed structure, separate from the CDRRMO office chart (Figure 1.3). Each contingency plan switches on only the modules its scenario needs (Figure 1.4). CDRRMO divisions supply the section chiefs, partner offices staff the cluster desks, and the proposed cluster coordinators form the Barangay Liaison Desk. The Incident Commander commands on site; the EOC coordinates and supplies; the EOC Manager elevates policy decisions to the CDRRMC and the Mayor.\n\n**Figure 1.3**\n\n*Proposed City EOC Structure and Its Relationship With Incident Command*\n\n![Figure 6: 6.3 Emergency Operations Center](/library/ch1v12/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Proposed for validation at the December 2026 workshop, following NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018. AA = anticipatory action; IMT = incident management team; MDM = management of the dead and missing; PDRA = pre-disaster risk assessment; QRF = Quick Response Fund; WASH = water, sanitation and hygiene.\n\n**Table 1.4**\n\n*EOC Activation Levels, Staffing and Provincial Reporting Times*\n\n| **Level**      | **Who staffs the EOC**                                                                                                                                        | **Reports to the Province (DILG VIII cut-offs)** |\n|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| White (normal) | OpCen duty team, 2 per shift; EOC Manager on call.                                                                                                            | 6 AM and 6 PM                                    |\n| Blue (alert)   | Adds the EOC Manager, Planning (situation unit, PDRA and AA desk) and the Logistics lead. Cluster focal persons and the Barangay Liaison Desk are on standby. | 9 AM and 7 PM                                    |\n| Red (full)     | All four sections, command staff, cluster desks and six barangay desks; 12-hour shifts, two deep.                                                             | 9 AM and 7 PM, and as events require             |\n\n*Note.* At Red the EOC needs about 50 people over two shifts, more than the CDRRMO can field alone. Chapter 2, section 10.1 sets out how pre-designated augmentees from City offices close the gap.\n\n**Figure 1.4**\n\n*EOC Modules Activated by Contingency Scenario*\n\n![Figure 7: 6.3 Emergency Operations Center](/library/ch1v12/fig-07.webp)\n\n*Note.* Proposed; each contingency plan sets its exact triggers. "Lead clusters" means only the clusters that lead that scenario are seated. Typhoon: Blue on forecast landfall, Red at Wind Signal No. 2 or a flood or storm-surge warning; the RA 12287 path applies when lead time is 72 hours or more. Flash flood: Red on an orange or red rainfall warning; usually below the 72-hour floor, so city early action applies. Earthquake: Red automatically on strong shaking; move to the alternate EOC if the primary is damaged. El Niño: White or Blue only, coordinated by the El Niño Task Force (EO No. 2023-0042). Disease outbreak: Blue, Red at the Health Cluster''s request. Industrial incident, fire or planned event: Blue as needed, with the IMT under CDRRMC Res. No. 09 s.2024.\n\n**Geospatial common operating picture.** The EOC shall keep a geospatial common operating picture (Geo-COP) as part of its information-management function. Its reference point is the EOC location registered from the KML supplied for this Manual (11.013896° N, 124.604287° E), which the site check under IAT B2.1 used for the hazard-map overlay. The facility entrance and footprint still need a field survey. Partner offices keep ownership of their own map layers; the CDRRMO maintains the system. The Geo-COP supports coordination and does not transfer command from the Incident Commander. The full procedure (SOP 6-GIS-01) and its forms belong to the EOC Module, Chapter 6.\n\nThese arrangements address IAT items B1.4 (concept of operations), B4.1 (EOC structure), B4.4 (liaison desks) and B6.1 (ICS–EOC interface) on paper. The scores rise once the EOC Executive Order is issued and an exercise tests the structure (B7.3).\n\n## 6.4 Office of the City Mayor\n\nThe City Mayor shall exercise the powers and responsibilities vested in the Local Chief Executive under RA 7160, RA 10121, RA 12287 and other applicable laws.\n\nThe Mayor shall:\n\n- Provide executive leadership and direction;\n\n- Chair the CDRRMC;\n\n- Authorize actions within statutory authority;\n\n- Direct the mobilization of City Government offices and resources;\n\n- Issue valid executive orders and administrative directives;\n\n- Exercise the relevant declaration authority under RA 12287;\n\n- Approve institutional arrangements within the scope of executive authority; and\n\n- Elevate matters requiring legislative or higher-level government action.\n\nThe Mayor''s executive authority shall remain distinct from the legislative authority of the Sangguniang Panlungsod.\n\n## 6.5 Sangguniang Panlungsod\n\nThe Sangguniang Panlungsod shall exercise its legislative and oversight responsibilities in accordance with RA 7160 and applicable DRRM legislation.\n\nIts relevant functions include:\n\n- Enacting the ordinance establishing or strengthening the CDRRMO;\n\n- Approving the necessary organizational and budgetary measures;\n\n- Considering CDRRMC recommendations for a State of Calamity declaration;\n\n- Enacting relevant local DRRM ordinances;\n\n- Exercising oversight over local DRRM policies and resource allocation; and\n\n- Supporting the institutionalization of the City''s resilience priorities.\n\nLegal distinction: The declaration of a State of Calamity under Section 16 of RA 10121 is issued by the local sanggunian upon the recommendation of the LDRRMC, based on damage assessment and needs analysis. It is not simply a Mayor''s Executive Order.\n\n# 7. Institutional Governance Processes and Workflows\n\nThe MOO shall define governance as a series of connected, controlled and measurable processes. Each process shall have:\n\n- A defined trigger or initiating condition;\n\n- A designated process owner;\n\n- Responsible actors;\n\n- A legally authorized decision-maker;\n\n- Required inputs and evidence;\n\n- Defined outputs and records;\n\n- Escalation and exception procedures; and\n\n- Performance and compliance indicators.\n\nThis approach ensures that governance decisions can be traced from initiation to approval, implementation, reporting and review.\n\n## 7.1 Process 1: CDRRMC Governance and Policy Decision-making\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that DRRM policies, plans, priorities and institutional decisions are deliberated, approved or recommended by the appropriate authority and translated into accountable actions.\n\n![Figure 8: 7.1 Process 1: CDRRMC Governance and Policy Decision-making](/library/ch1v12/fig-08.webp)\n\n**Workflow GOV-01: CDRRMC Governance**\n\n1.  **Initiation and agenda development**  \n    > CDRRMO identifies policy issues, assessment findings, risk developments and matters requiring Council action.\n\n2.  **Technical preparation**  \n    > Concerned offices submit technical papers, evidence, financial implications and recommendations.\n\n3.  **Secretariat processing  \n    > **CDRRMO consolidates agenda items, issues meeting notices and circulates supporting documents.\n\n4.  **Council deliberation  \n    > **CDRRMC members review evidence, assess implications and deliberate on proposed actions.\n\n5.  **Decision and documentation**  \n    > The Council approves matters within its authority or adopts recommendations for submission to the appropriate approving authority.\n\n6.  **Implementation and assignment  \n    > **The Secretariat records decisions, assigns action owners and establishes target dates.\n\n7.  **Monitoring and feedback  \n    > **CDRRMO tracks implementation and reports unresolved actions at succeeding meetings.\n\n**Required Output**\n\nApproved minutes, resolutions or recommendations, decision register and action-monitoring report.\n\n**Operating Requirements**\n\n| **Control point**    | **Required standard**                                                                                                     |\n|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Meeting frequency    | At least once every three months and as necessary.                                                                        |\n| Agenda               | Include standing items on risk developments, LDRRMP implementation, funding, assessment gaps and institutional readiness. |\n| Supporting documents | Technical papers, risk information, financial implications and draft resolutions, as applicable.                          |\n| Documentation        | Minutes, attendance, decisions, dissenting views where material, assigned owners and deadlines.                           |\n| Follow-through       | Action register reviewed at every succeeding meeting.                                                                     |\n| Review               | Annual assessment of Council functionality and effectiveness.                                                             |\n\nThe assessment records a score of 4 for A1.3. This established practice should be retained and strengthened through systematic action tracking, particularly for the 30 high-priority institutional gaps.\n\n## 7.2 Process 2: CDRRMC Secretariat Management\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that Council proceedings, decisions and institutional records are complete, traceable and accessible to authorized stakeholders.\n\n![Figure 9: 7.2 Process 2: CDRRMC Secretariat Management](/library/ch1v12/fig-09.webp)\n\n**Workflow GOV-02: Secretariat Operations**\n\n1.  Receive agenda requests and supporting documents.\n\n2.  Prepare agenda, meeting notice and document package.\n\n3.  Coordinate with the Chairperson on scheduling and meeting requirements.\n\n4.  Record attendance, proceedings, resolutions and decisions.\n\n5.  Prepare draft minutes and resolutions for review and approval.\n\n6.  Distribute approved records to authorized members.\n\n7.  Enter decisions into the action register and assign responsible offices.\n\n8.  Track implementation and preserve the official records.\n\n**Required records**: Meeting calendar, agenda, attendance, minutes, resolutions, action register and document distribution log.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall develop a Secretariat SOP and use standardized templates. This responds to A1.4, which is already rated 4, while addressing the need to document the existing practice more systematically.\n\n## 7.3 Process 3: Institutional Policy and Ordinance Development\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that local DRRM policies are legally sound, risk-informed, coordinated and implementable.\n\nThe process shall be used for the proposed CDRRMO enabling ordinance, updated CDRRMC Executive Order, EOC establishment issuance, ICS institutionalization and amendments to existing DRRM ordinances.\n\n![Figure 10: 7.3 Process 3: Institutional Policy and Ordinance Development](/library/ch1v12/fig-10.webp)\n\n**Workflow GOV-03: Policy Development and Approval**\n\n1.  **Identify the policy gap.** CDRRMO documents the legal, institutional or operational deficiency.\n\n2.  **Develop the technical proposal**. Prepare the proposed policy, institutional implications and implementation requirements.\n\n3.  **Conduct consultation.** Engage affected City offices, CDRRMC members, CSOs and relevant partners.\n\n4.  **Legal review.** The City Legal Office reviews legality, authority, consistency and enforceability.\n\n5.  **Resource review**. HRMO and Budget Office assess staffing, funding and administrative implications.\n\n6.  **Approva**l. Submit the proposal to the Mayor or Sangguniang Panlungsod, depending on the nature of the instrument.\n\n7.  **Publication and dissemination**. Circulate the approved policy to responsible offices and affected stakeholders.\n\n8.  **Implementation and evaluation**. Monitor compliance, assess effectiveness and recommend revisions.\n\n**Decision rule**: The appropriate approving authority shall be determined by the legal nature of the proposed instrument. An ordinance shall not be substituted by an Executive Order where legislative action is required.\n\n## 7.4 Process 4: State of Calamity Declaration\n\nThe City shall maintain a documented protocol consistent with Section 16 of RA 10121 and its IRR.\n\n**Practice to date (IAT v1.7, A1.6).** The SP adopted a State of Calamity for Typhoon Odette (SP Res. No. 2021-366, December 2021). The CDRRMC recommended a State of Calamity for the 2024 dengue outbreak for a period not exceeding one year, liftable earlier on the Council''s recommendation (CDRRMC Res. No. 06 s.2024). These cases show the process in use. They also show the gaps this protocol closes: no written criteria, no standard recommendation package, and Quick Response Fund resolutions that do not cite the declaration relied on.\n\nA State of Calamity declaration is based on the results of damage assessment and needs analysis. It shall be distinguished from the anticipatory declaration mechanism under RA 12287.\n\n**Workflow GOV-04: State of Calamity**\n\n1.  **Incident assessment**  \n    > BDRRMCs, response agencies and the CDRRMO report casualties, affected populations, infrastructure damage, livelihood disruption and service interruptions.\n\n2.  **RDANA consolidation**  \n    > CDRRMO coordinates the validation and consolidation of damage and needs information.\n\n3.  **Technical recommendation  \n    > **CDRRMO prepares the assessment and submits it to the CDRRMC.\n\n4.  **CDRRMC deliberation**  \n    > The Council evaluates the evidence and adopts a recommendation where the legal criteria are met.\n\n5.  **Legislative declaration  \n    > **The Sangguniang Panlungsod considers and issues the declaration or lifting through the appropriate resolution.\n\n6.  **Implementation  \n    > **The City Government implements applicable remedial measures, resource arrangements, assistance and reporting requirements.\n\n7.  **Monitoring and lifting  \n    > **The CDRRMO monitors continuing impacts and prepares the technical basis for recommending lifting when appropriate.\n\n**Required Outputs**\n\nRDANA, CDRRMC recommendation, Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution, implementation records and lifting documentation.\n\nThe City shall maintain the following supporting records:\n\n- Technical assessment and recommendation;\n\n- RDANA and other supporting reports;\n\n- CDRRMC resolution;\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution;\n\n- Post-declaration action register;\n\n- Resource utilization and accountability records; and\n\n- Lifting recommendation and corresponding resolution.\n\nThe protocol shall also address coordination where national or regional declarations, infrastructure closures or emergency restrictions affect Ormoc City without automatically constituting a local State of Calamity declaration.\n\nEvery resolution that uses the Quick Response Fund shall cite the declaration relied on: the SP resolution number and date, or the Proclamation number and date for a national or regional declaration. The reading of the QRF trigger shall be validated with the City Legal Office and COA.\n\n## 7.5 Process 5: State of Imminent Disaster Declaration and Anticipatory Action\n\n**New statutory Governance Process**\n\nRA 12287 establishes a mechanism for declaring a State of Imminent Disaster before a disaster occurs, based on the applicable forecast, pre-disaster risk assessment and legal criteria.\n\nThe IRR released on 29 September 2026 provides the implementing framework for this mechanism.\n\nThe assessment identifies A1.8 as a mandatory requirement with a score of 1. The City has no localized procedure, request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template documented in the evidence reviewed.\n\nThe following workflow shall therefore be institutionalized.\n\n![Figure 11: 7.5 Process 5: State of Imminent Disaster Declaration and Anticipatory Action](/library/ch1v12/fig-11.webp)\n\n**Workflow GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster**\n\n1.  **Hazard forecast and trigger monitoring  \n    > **CDRRMO Operations and Warning monitors official forecasts, advisories and hazard-specific triggers from PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and other competent agencies.\n\n2.  **Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment  \n    > **CDRRMO coordinates the assessment of exposure, vulnerability, projected severity, likely impacts, affected sectors, lead time and available response capacity.\n\n3.  **Technical recommendation**  \n    > CDRRMO prepares the PDRA findings and proposed anticipatory measures for CDRRMC consideration.\n\n4.  **Regional coordination and recommendation  \n    > **The City coordinates with RDRRMC VIII through OCD Region VIII and submits the prescribed request and supporting documentation.\n\n5.  **Regional decision and response**  \n    > RDRRMC VIII undertakes the action required under the Act and IRR. The request, acknowledgment and prescribed response timeline are documented.\n\n6.  **Local declaration**  \n    > Upon the applicable recommendation, the City Mayor may declare a State of Imminent Disaster through an Executive Order covering the jurisdiction forecast to be affected.\n\n7.  **Activation of anticipatory measures**  \n    > Authorized offices implement approved early actions, including pre-positioning, evacuation, protection of critical infrastructure and targeted social assistance.\n\n8.  **Funding and accountability  \n    > **Budget, Accounting, Procurement and implementing offices document commitments, disbursements, utilization and outputs in accordance with the IRR and applicable DBM-COA rules.\n\n9.  **Lifting and post-declaration reporting  \n    > **The City observes the applicable lifting procedures and submits the required post-lifting report to OCD within the prescribed 45-day period.\n\nThe process shall observe the following safeguards:\n\n1.  A forecast alone shall not automatically trigger a declaration. The applicable legal criteria and PDRA requirements must be met.\n\n2.  The CDRRMO shall provide technical recommendations, not independently exercise the declaration authority.\n\n3.  The local declaration shall follow the recommendation and coordination mechanism prescribed by RA 12287 and its IRR.\n\n4.  Anticipatory actions shall be proportionate to the projected impacts, hazard lead time, available resources and applicable legal authority.\n\n5.  Measures shall observe inclusion, protection, do-no-harm and no-regrets principles.\n\n6.  The use of funds shall follow the statutory funding limits, tranche arrangements and applicable financial regulations.\n\n7.  Decisions, approvals, actions and expenditures shall be documented for audit and evaluation.\n\n**Funding control**: As reflected in the assessment workbook, the IRR provides for an anticipatory-action allocation of up to 10% of the 70% LDRRMF component and a 30%/70% tranche arrangement associated with the prescribed three-day lead-time rule. The precise timing, eligibility, disbursement and documentation requirements must be implemented using the certified IRR and applicable DBM-COA guidance. The MOO shall not treat this allocation as an additional fund outside the statutory LDRRMF framework.\n\n**Independent Component City clarification:** Ormoc''s status as an Independent Component City must be considered when establishing its vertical coordination arrangements. The City shall obtain written confirmation from OCD Region VIII on the application of the IRR''s regional and provincial coordination provisions, particularly whether any provincial declaration has legal applicability to Ormoc. The City''s ICC status shall not be used as a reason to bypass the RDRRMC recommendation mechanism expressly established by RA 12287.\n\n## 7.6 Process 6: Institutional Escalation and Inter-agency Coordination\n\nInstitutional escalation shall occur when:\n\n- The scale of an incident exceeds the capacity of a barangay;\n\n- Multiple barangays require coordinated intervention;\n\n- City resources are insufficient;\n\n- Specialized national or regional assistance is required;\n\n- Critical infrastructure or essential services are disrupted; or\n\n- A statutory declaration or executive decision is necessary.\n\nRA 10121, Section 15, provides the general coordination framework: barangay-level leadership where a barangay is affected, city/municipal leadership where two or more barangays are affected, and progressively higher coordination levels when impacts exceed the relevant territorial scale. Higher councils and institutions support the LGU''s primary response responsibility.\n\n![Figure 12: 7.6 Process 6: Institutional Escalation and Inter-agency Coordination](/library/ch1v12/fig-12.webp)\n\nThe operational workflow shall be:\n\n1.  Incident or risk information is reported to the appropriate local authority.\n\n2.  The affected BDRRMC undertakes initial action and reports to the City.\n\n3.  CDRRMO validates the information and assesses the level of support required.\n\n4.  The CDRRMC and EOC coordinate City-wide institutional action, as appropriate.\n\n5.  The City requests external assistance through the prescribed channels when required.\n\n6.  The receiving institution acknowledges and assigns the request.\n\n7.  The CDRRMO monitors the response and maintains the institutional record.\n\n8.  Unresolved requirements are escalated through the appropriate higher-level coordination mechanism.\n\n## 7.7 Process 7: Governance Review and Corrective Action\n\nAll governance processes shall be subject to periodic review.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall:\n\n1.  Consolidate compliance and performance information;\n\n2.  Compare actual results against approved targets;\n\n3.  Identify gaps, recurring problems and systemic causes;\n\n4.  Assign corrective actions to accountable offices;\n\n5.  Establish completion dates and required resources;\n\n6.  Verify implementation;\n\n7.  Report unresolved issues to the CDRRMC; and\n\n8.  Update policies and procedures when necessary.\n\nThis process shall link the Institutional Assessment Tool, Action Plan, CDRRMC action register, annual LDRRMP monitoring and the MOO revision mechanism.\n\n![Figure 13: 7.7 Process 7: Governance Review and Corrective Action](/library/ch1v12/fig-13.webp)\n\n# 8. Responsibility Assignment and RACI Framework\n\n## 8.1 Application\n\nThe RACI framework shall be used to clarify institutional responsibility for governance processes and prevent overlapping mandates, unassigned tasks and ambiguous decision authority.\n\nThe following definitions apply:\n\n| **Code** | **Responsibility** | **Application**                                                               |\n|----------|--------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| R        | Responsible        | Performs the work and produces the required output.                           |\n| A        | Accountable        | Owns the result and approves the output or decision. Only one A per activity. |\n| **C**    | Consulted          | Provides technical, legal or operational advice before the decision.          |\n| **I**    | Informed           | Receives relevant information on the decision or outcome.                     |\n\nRACI assignments shall be interpreted subject to statutory powers, formal delegations and approved organizational arrangements. A RACI designation does not create authority that does not otherwise exist under law.\n\n## 8.2 Institutional Roles Used in the RACI Matrix\n\n| **Role**                             | **Abbreviation** | **Principal Responsibility**                                                        |\n|--------------------------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Local Chief Executive                | Mayor            | Executive authority and statutory decisions.                                        |\n| Sangguniang Panlungsod               | SP               | Legislative approval and local declaration authority.                               |\n| CDRRMC                               | Council          | Collective policy direction, deliberation and recommendation.                       |\n| CDRRMO Head                          | CDRRMO           | Permanent office leadership and technical accountability.                           |\n| CDRRMO Secretariat                   | Secretariat      | Council administration, documentation and action tracking.                          |\n| City Legal Office                    | CLO              | Legal review and compliance advice.                                                 |\n| City Planning and Development Office | CPDO             | Integration of DRR/CCA into development planning.                                   |\n| HRMO                                 | HRMO             | Staffing, qualification and personnel administration.                               |\n| City Budget / Accounting / BAC       | Finance          | Budgeting, accounting, procurement and financial control.                           |\n| City Information Office              | CIO              | Public information and risk communication support.                                  |\n| BDRRMCs                              | Barangays        | Community-level governance, reporting and local action.                             |\n| City departments and agencies        | Departments      | Sectoral implementation and technical support.                                      |\n| OCD / RDRRMC VIII                    | Regional         | Regional coordination, technical assistance and statutory recommendation functions. |\n| EOC Manager / IMT                    | EOC/IMT          | Emergency coordination and incident-management interface.                           |\n| CSOs / Private Sector                | Partners         | Community participation, technical support and resource commitments.                |\n\n## 8.3 Master RACI Matrix – institutional Governance\n\nThe following matrix establishes the proposed responsibility assignments for Chapter 1.\n\n| **Governance activity**                                   | **Mayor** | **SP** | **CDRRMC** | **CDRRMO** | **Secretariat** | **CLO** | **HRMO / Finance** | **OCD VIII** |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------|------------|------------|-----------------|---------|--------------------|--------------|\n| Establish CDRRMO through ordinance                        | I         | A      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Prepare CDRRMC composition EO                             | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Maintain CDRRMC membership register                       | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Prepare CDRRMC agenda and meetings                        | I         | I      | A          | **C**      | R               | I       | I                  | I            |\n| Document Council resolutions                              | I         | I      | A          | **C**      | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Monitor Council decisions                                 | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | I       | **C**              | I            |\n| Develop local DRRM policy                                 | A         | **C**  | **C**      | R          | **C**           | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Review proposed DRRM ordinances                           | **C**     | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | A       | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Prepare State of Calamity technical assessment            | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | R               | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Recommend State of Calamity                               | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Issue local State of Calamity declaration                 | I         | A      | R          | C          | I               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Prepare State of Imminent Disaster PDRA                   | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Submit request to RDRRMC VIII                             | I         | I      | A          | R          | **C**           | **C**   | I                  | **C**        |\n| Recommend regional State of Imminent Disaster Declaration | I         | I      | **C**      | **C**      | I               | I       | I                  | A/R\\*        |\n| Issue local State of Imminent Disaster EO                 | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Coordinate anticipatory action                            | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | R                  | **C**        |\n| Prepare 45-day post-lifting report                        | I         | I      | **C**      | A/R        | **C**           | **C**   | R                  | I            |\n| Maintain legal and policy register                        | I         | I      | I          | A          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Conduct annual governance review                          | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n\n\\*The RACI assignment for OCD/RDRRMC VIII reflects the regional recommendation function described in RA 12287. The exact responsible regional body and administrative sequence shall be finalized against the certified IRR and applicable regional procedures. The RACI matrix does not replace the statutory decision authority of the NDRRMC, regional councils, Mayor or Sanggunian.\n\n## 8.4 RACI Matrix – institutional interfaces\n\nPart C of the assessment shall be operationalized through the following responsibility arrangements.\n\n| **Interface activity**                  | **Accountable owner**                        | **Responsible actors**             | **CDRRMO role**                                 |\n|-----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRA and CLUP integration               | CPDO                                         | CPDO technical team                | C / technical input                             |\n| DRR/CCA project screening               | CPDO                                         | CPDO, City Budget Office           | C / risk validation                             |\n| Critical facility structural assessment | City Engineer / Building Official            | Engineering and inspection teams   | C / risk prioritization                         |\n| Utility continuity agreements           | Relevant utility / designated City authority | Utility providers and City offices | R for coordination, subject to approved mandate |\n| Watershed and ecosystem monitoring      | CENRO                                        | CENRO, DENR and partners           | C / DRRM integration                            |\n| Health anticipatory action              | City Health Officer                          | CHO, hospitals and health partners | C / coordination                                |\n| Education anticipatory action           | DepEd Schools Division                       | School DRRM coordinators           | C / coordination                                |\n| Agriculture and fisheries early action  | City Agriculture / Veterinary Office         | CAO, CVO and relevant agencies     | C / coordination                                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch1_v1.2.md (Chapter 1, v1.2 working draft (IAT v1.5))','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('ec083ec5-1b46-53fa-99e0-1d9a63df7dd1'::uuid,'f379ea8e-ae82-5eb6-9887-f700227215ce'::uuid,'v1.2 working draft (IAT v1.5)',E'**CHAPTER 2  \n**\n\n**CDRRMO ORGANIZATIONAL STRUCTURE,  \nSTAFFING AND HUMAN RESOURCE MANAGEMENT**\n\n**  \n**\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)  \nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*  \nAssessment-informed working draft  \nSubject to institutional, HR, budgetary and legal validation*\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Document attribute** | **Control information**                                                                                  |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document title         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 2                                                   |\n| Chapter title          | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management                                  |\n| Document status        | Working draft for technical and institutional validation                                                 |\n| Document owner         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                       |\n| Technical custodian    | CDRRMO Head / designated MOO focal person                                                                |\n| Reviewing offices      | City Legal Office; HRMO; Budget Office; CDRRMO; OCD Region VIII; other offices as applicable             |\n| Version / date         | Draft 1.2 / 6 November 2026                                                                              |\n| Approval authority     | To be determined under the City''s document-control and approval arrangements                             |\n| Review cycle           | At least annually and following material changes in law, structure, staffing or operational requirements |\n\n## REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | **Prepared / reviewed by** | **Approval** |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|--------------|\n| 0.1         | 31 Oct 2026 | Initial assessment-informed Chapter 2 draft                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n| 1.1         | 6 Nov 2026  | Updated to IAT v1.5: October 2026 FGD, KIIs and site visits; 2021–2024 City issuances; A2 and B4 findings; priority actions aligned to the workbook Action Plan.                                                                                                     | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n| 1.2         | 6 Nov 2026  | Added the structure and staffing assessment (6.3), EOC staffing at Red (10.1), the cluster-based barangay coordinator proposal (10.2), the budget-cycle RACI (2.16.2), the Brgy. Liloan site visit, the Geo-COP staffing note, and APA-captioned figures and tables. | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction and Rationale\n\nThe effectiveness of Ormoc City''s disaster risk reduction and management system depends on a permanent, competent and adequately supported institutional workforce. Laws, plans, facilities, information systems and response assets produce results only when responsibilities are assigned to qualified personnel, authority is understood, work is coordinated and continuity is maintained during periods of heightened risk and emergency operations.\n\nChapter 1 established the legal and governance relationships among the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council (CDRRMC), the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO), the Emergency Operations Center (EOC), and the Incident Command System (ICS). This chapter translates that governance framework into an organizational and human-resource operating model. It defines the CDRRMO''s functional structure, division-level responsibilities, staffing principles, position accountability, workforce development, personnel welfare and arrangements for supporting an activated EOC.\n\nThe chapter treats organizational capacity as a system property. It is not measured solely by the number of positions or the existence of an organizational chart. It depends on the alignment of mandates, staffing, competencies, supervision, work processes, information flows, equipment, funding and inter-office relationships. A position is operationally meaningful only when its mandate, authority, expected outputs, reporting relationship, required competencies and performance measures are clear.\n\n![Figure 1: 1. Introduction and Rationale](/library/ch2v12/fig-01.webp)\n\nThe October 2026 Institutional Assessment identifies the CDRRMO''s organizational structure and human-resource arrangements as a priority improvement area. The A2 domain tests alignment with Joint Memorandum Circular (JMC) No. 2014-1, the permanent status and qualifications of the CDRRMO Head, minimum staffing, position descriptions, 24/7 operational capability, competency-based training and personnel welfare. The EOC B4 domain separately tests the EOC''s incident-time organizational structure, pre-designated personnel and alternates, duty rosters, liaison desks, job action sheets and welfare arrangements. These are related but distinct workforce systems.\n\nThe assessment is diagnostic. It does not itself establish legal non-compliance, approve a staffing pattern or create positions. All staffing and organizational proposals remain subject to verification of the current legal framework, approved City organizational structure, plantilla, qualification standards, appropriations and applicable Civil Service Commission (CSC) and Department of Budget and Management (DBM) rules.\n\n## 1.1 Assessment Findings for this Chapter (IAT v1.7)\n\nThe findings below come from the Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.7 (workbook v1-7, 11 November 2026). The October 2026 assessment combined a focus group discussion with the CDRRMO Head and staff at the CDRRM Office, which houses the OpCen and the EOC; key informant interviews with the CDRRMO Chiefs of Operations, Research and Planning, and Administration, the CPDO, the CSWDO and the City Administrator''s Office; site visits to the EOC and the Operations and Rescue Office; and a visit to Brgy. Liloan, one of the flood-prone barangays, with an established evacuation site, evacuation protocol, and camp coordination and management arrangements. The team also reviewed City plans, 2021–2025 issuances, the CDRRMO organizational chart and internal staff papers.\n\n**Stakeholder position.** All offices consulted share one aim: clear roles and responsibilities for each unit and accountable person, and that these are carried out. All agree that a Manual of Operations will help significantly. This chapter turns that aim into approved structures, positions, rosters and records.\n\n**Table 2.1**\n\n*Maturity and Compliance of the Chapter 2 Domains, IAT v1.7*\n\n| **Domain**                                                    | **Maturity index (0–4)** | **Band**   | **Mandatory compliance** | **High-priority gaps** |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|------------|--------------------------|------------------------|\n| A2 Organization, Staffing and Human Resources (10 indicators) | 1.86                     | Developing | 14.3% (1 of 7)           | 4                      |\n| B4 EOC Organization and Staffing (6 indicators)               | 1.31                     | Initial    | 0% (0 of 4)              | 2                      |\n\n*Note.* Maturity index on a 0–4 scale. Mandatory compliance is the share of scored mandatory indicators rated 3 or 4.\n\nScores follow the evidence rule in Methodology 5.2: interviews alone support a maximum score of 2; a 3 needs an approved document and proof of use; a 4 adds a test, exercise or after-action review.\n\n**Table 2.2**\n\n*Chapter 2 Indicators, Findings and Responses (IAT v1.7)*\n\n| **IAT ref** | **Score** | **Priority** | **Finding (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                                                                                                                    | **Chapter response (section)** |\n|-------------|-----------|--------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| A1.4        | 3         | Low          | CDRRMO acts as CDRRMC Secretariat; no written secretariat procedure; records lapses.                                                                                                                      | 7.2; Chapter 1 GOV-02          |\n| A2.1        | 2         | High         | Sections exist, but not as an approved JMC 2014-1 structure; normal-operations and response tasks are mixed.                                                                                              | 6; 7                           |\n| A2.2        | 3         | Medium       | The head-of-office post is permanent. Qualification records and the succession order are still to be documented.                                                                                          | 9.2; 11.2                      |\n| A2.3        | 2         | High         | Filled-to-required ratio not proven. The 10-Year Plan lists 10 planned plantilla items (4 + 4 + 2).                                                                                                       | 8                              |\n| A2.4        | 2         | Medium       | No position description for each post; Admin functions bypassed in some activities.                                                                                                                       | 9; Annex 2-A                   |\n| A2.5        | 2         | High         | Site visit: 24-hour duty runs for City Traffic Monitoring, Public Safety and Emergency Monitoring. No approved OpCen SOP, roster or handover record; no written split of tasks between the three streams. | 11.1; Annex 2-E                |\n| A2.6        | 1         | Medium       | No training needs analysis or competency-based training plan.                                                                                                                                             | 12                             |\n| A2.7        | 2         | Medium       | CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03/04 s.2022 approved a basis for volunteer insurance; no policy sighted. Nothing for regular staff (insurance, hazard pay, PPE, MHPSS).                                                 | 13                             |\n| A2.8        | 3         | Low          | ACDV accreditation and a partner MOA exist and are used; no registry, deployment or insurance rules; tasking during activation unclear.                                                                   | 14.3                           |\n| A2.9        | 0         | High         | No anticipatory-action focal person designated (RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 1).                                                                                                                             | 7.1                            |\n| A2.10       | 1         | Low          | No skills inventory across the CDRRMO, partner offices and barangays.                                                                                                                                     | 12                             |\n| B4.1        | 1         | High         | EOC structure with the five NDRRMC Memo 131 functions not defined.                                                                                                                                        | 10                             |\n| B4.2        | 1         | High         | Alternates not named; a 72-hour activation cannot be staffed two deep.                                                                                                                                    | 10; 11.2; Annex 2-D            |\n| B4.3        | 2         | Medium       | Site visit: 24-hour duty observed. Roster and handover protocol not sighted.                                                                                                                              | 11.1; Annex 2-E                |\n| B4.4        | 2         | Medium       | Liaison desks not written down.                                                                                                                                                                           | 10                             |\n| B4.5        | 1         | Medium       | No job action sheets for EOC roles.                                                                                                                                                                       | 10                             |\n| B4.6        | 1         | Low          | No written rest cycles, meals or MHPSS for EOC staff.                                                                                                                                                     | 11.3; 13                       |\n\n*Note.* Priority is the workbook''s gap-priority band: (4 − score) × weight, × 1.5 for mandatory items; High is 9 or more.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Objectives\n\nThis chapter establishes a clear, legally grounded and implementable organizational and human-resource framework for the CDRRMO and its support to the EOC.\n\nSpecifically, it aims to:\n\n1.  Translate the mandates established in Chapter 1 into a functional organizational structure aligned with RA No. 10121 and JMC No. 2014-1.\n\n2.  Define the responsibilities, authority, outputs and interfaces of the CDRRMO Head and its core functional divisions.\n\n3.  Establish principles for staffing establishment, plantilla management, position descriptions, succession and workforce continuity.\n\n4.  Clarify the distinction between permanent CDRRMO positions and personnel assigned or designated to support the EOC during activation.\n\n5.  Provide a systematic approach to competency assessment, training, certification, exercises and professional development.\n\n6.  Establish duty-of-care arrangements for staff and responders, including occupational safety, insurance, protective equipment, rest, mental health and psychosocial support.\n\n7.  Strengthen performance management, supervision, records, accountability and continuous improvement.\n\n8.  Provide operational workflows, RACI assignments, forms and evidence requirements that can be implemented and audited.\n\n# 3. Scope and Application\n\nThis chapter applies to the CDRRMO as a permanent office of the City Government and to City personnel formally assigned, designated or mobilized to support its functions. It also establishes the personnel interface with the EOC, without replacing the detailed EOC activation, incident management and operational procedures in subsequent chapters.\n\n| **Institutional actor**                          | **Application under this chapter**                                                                                                                                           |\n|--------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| City Mayor / Local Chief Executive               | Executive leadership; approval of organizational and personnel actions within legal authority; designation and mobilization of personnel as permitted by law and City rules. |\n| CDRRMC                                           | Policy oversight, review of institutional capability and endorsement of DRRM capacity priorities; does not administer individual personnel matters.                          |\n| CDRRMO Head                                      | Office leadership, functional integration, workforce planning, supervision, performance and institutional accountability.                                                    |\n| Administration and Training Division             | Administrative services, personnel coordination, training administration, records and office support within approved structure.                                              |\n| Research and Planning Division                   | Risk-informed planning, policy and technical analysis, institutional planning, monitoring inputs and knowledge support.                                                      |\n| Operations and Warning Division                  | Operational readiness, warning and preparedness functions, response coordination support and duty officer arrangements.                                                      |\n| EOC Manager / designated EOC staff               | Incident-time coordination roles assigned under the EOC organizational structure and activation procedures.                                                                  |\n| HRMO / Budget / Accounting / Legal Office        | Specialist review and administration of personnel, position, budgetary, legal and records requirements.                                                                      |\n| City departments, barangays and partner agencies | Provide designated liaison personnel and technical support within their respective mandates.                                                                                 |\n\nThe chapter covers normal operations, heightened preparedness and emergency operations. It does not authorize the CDRRMO to exercise powers assigned to other City offices, national government agencies, utilities, professional regulators or incident commanders.\n\n![Figure 2: 3. Scope and Application](/library/ch2v12/fig-02.webp)\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Administrative Framework\n\nOrganizational and personnel arrangements shall be implemented in accordance with the hierarchy of laws, rules and approved City instruments described in Chapter 1. The following references are particularly relevant to this chapter:\n\n| **Instrument / framework**                                        | **Relevance to organizational and HR arrangements**                                                                                       |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA No. 10121 (2010), particularly Sections 12 and 14              | Establishes the LDRRMO and its functions, including the Head and the functional areas; provides for DRRM capacity development.            |\n| IRR of RA No. 10121                                               | Operational details for local DRRM councils and offices; exact provisions to be checked against the official current text.                |\n| NDRRMC–DILG–DBM–CSC JMC No. 2014-1                                | Guidance on LDRRMO organizational structure, functions, staffing and personnel arrangements.                                              |\n| RA No. 7160 (1991), Local Government Code                         | Local government authority, organization, personnel and administrative arrangements, subject to national law.                             |\n| CSC laws, rules and qualification standards                       | Appointment, qualification, position classification, performance management, training, conduct and personnel administration.              |\n| DBM rules and approved staffing pattern                           | Position creation, classification, compensation, plantilla and budget authorization.                                                      |\n| RA No. 11036 (2018), Mental Health Act                            | Mental health promotion, access to support and protection relevant to personnel and responders.                                           |\n| RA No. 10173 (2012), Data Privacy Act                             | Protection of personnel, health, emergency contact and other personal information.                                                        |\n| RA No. 9710, RA No. 7277 as amended, RA No. 9994 and RA No. 10821 | Gender-responsive, disability-inclusive, older-person-sensitive and child-sensitive institutional and operational arrangements.           |\n| ICS guidance and applicable NDRRMC/OCD EOC guidance               | Role-based incident organization and EOC staffing; exact current EOC guidance and issuance status shall be verified with OCD Region VIII. |\n\nWhere a local organizational chart, staffing proposal, designation or SOP conflicts with a statute, national rule or approved plantilla, the higher legal or administrative authority shall prevail. This chapter does not independently create plantilla positions, authorize compensation, confer professional licenses or amend the City''s approved organizational structure.\n\n![Figure 3: 4. Legal, Policy and Administrative Framework](/library/ch2v12/fig-03.webp)\n\n# 5. Organizational Design Principles\n\n| **Principle**                                   | **Operational application**                                                                                                         |\n|-------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Legality and mandate alignment                  | Every function and position must be traced to a lawful mandate, approved structure or authorized designation.                       |\n| Functional clarity                              | Each division and position must have defined responsibilities, deliverables, authority limits and reporting lines.                  |\n| Separation of governance and management         | CDRRMC provides statutory oversight; CDRRMO manages the permanent technical and administrative work.                                |\n| Separation of permanent and activated functions | The CDRRMO remains operational during normal periods; the EOC is configured and staffed according to activation requirements.       |\n| Scalability and interoperability                | Staffing and role assignments must expand or contract according to incident complexity and integrate with ICS and partner agencies. |\n| Continuity and redundancy                       | Critical positions require alternates, documented handover and succession arrangements.                                             |\n| Competency-based deployment                     | Personnel are assigned according to qualifications, training, demonstrated competence and role requirements.                        |\n| Inclusion and duty of care                      | Workforce arrangements must be safe, accessible, gender-responsive and attentive to fatigue and psychosocial needs.                 |\n| Evidence and performance                        | Organizational effectiveness must be demonstrated through approved records, work outputs, exercises and review.                     |\n\n![Figure 4: 5. Organizational Design Principles](/library/ch2v12/fig-04.webp)\n\nThe organizational model shall be proportionate to Ormoc City''s risk profile, geographic and sectoral diversity, service obligations and available resources. It shall not assume that a larger organizational chart automatically produces better performance. The objective is a fit-for-purpose structure with clear accountability and sufficient capability to discharge mandatory functions.\n\n# 6. CDRRMO Organizational Structure and Functional Architecture\n\nThe CDRRMO shall be headed by a qualified CDRRMO Head and organized around the three core functional areas identified in JMC No. 2014-1: (a) Administration and Training; (b) Research and Planning; and (c) Operations and Warning. The precise division names, unit configuration, staffing complement and reporting relationships shall follow the City''s approved organizational and staffing pattern and applicable national requirements.\n\nThe proposed functional architecture below is a functional reference model for MOO purposes. It is not, by itself, an approved organogram or authority to create offices or positions.\n\n| **Organizational level**           | **Core accountability**                                                                                                       | **Key interfaces**                                                        |\n|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| City Mayor / Local Chief Executive | Executive direction and lawful personnel / resource decisions                                                                 | CDRRMC; CDRRMO; HRMO; Budget; City departments                            |\n| CDRRMC                             | Policy oversight, coordination and review of institutional DRRM performance                                                   | Mayor; CDRRMO Secretariat; member offices; CSOs; private sector           |\n| CDRRMO Head                        | Overall management, technical leadership, integration and accountability for CDRRMO outputs                                   | Mayor; CDRRMC; all divisions; EOC Manager; HRMO                           |\n| Administration and Training        | Administrative support, personnel records coordination, training systems, office services and Secretariat support as assigned | HRMO; Budget; Accounting; procurement; records; CDRRMC Secretariat        |\n| Research and Planning              | Risk analysis, plans, policy integration, program development, monitoring and technical knowledge management                  | CPDO; sector offices; GIS/ICT; barangays; academic and technical partners |\n| Operations and Warning             | Operational readiness, warning coordination, preparedness, duty officer and response coordination functions                   | EOC; ICS/IMT; BDRRMCs; PAGASA; PHIVOLCS; response agencies                |\n\nThe CDRRMO Head may establish internal work teams, focal-person arrangements or task groups within the approved structure to address cross-cutting functions such as climate change adaptation, anticipatory action, inclusive DRRM, public service continuity, digital systems and partnership coordination. Such arrangements shall not be represented as permanent divisions or plantilla positions unless duly approved.\n\n## 6.1 Organizational Chart – Functional Reference\n\nCity Mayor / Local Chief Executive → CDRRMC (policy oversight and coordination) → CDRRMO Head (permanent office leadership) → three core functional divisions: Administration and Training; Research and Planning; Operations and Warning. The EOC is shown as an operational coordination mechanism supported by designated CDRRMO and inter-agency personnel when activated. It is not a fourth permanent CDRRMO division and does not replace ICS command.\n\n![Figure 5: 6.1 Organizational Chart – Functional Reference](/library/ch2v12/fig-05.webp)\n\n## 6.2 Management and Reporting Relationships\n\n- The CDRRMO Head reports through the City''s approved administrative and executive reporting arrangements.\n\n- Division chiefs report to the CDRRMO Head and are accountable for division work plans, outputs, staff supervision and records.\n\n- Personnel remain subject to the City''s personnel administration, supervision and performance management systems.\n\n- Personnel designated to an activated EOC retain their underlying employment and administrative relationships unless a lawful temporary assignment or detail provides otherwise.\n\n- Incident-specific operational coordination and tasking shall follow the approved EOC and ICS arrangements; it shall not be interpreted as a change in permanent appointment or plantilla.\n\n## 6.3 Current Structure Compared With the Recommended Model\n\nThe office chart reviewed during the assessment shows that every function RA 10121 Sec. 12(a) requires is already in place, carried by a committed workforce of about 47 people that includes a 24/7 medical and rescue team. The main structural issue is employment status: four posts are permanent, the rest are casual or job order, and two posts are vacant (Figure 2.1). Strengthening the structure is therefore mainly about securing the positions the office already relies on.\n\n**Figure 2.1**\n\n*CDRRMO Posts by Organizational Unit and Employment Status*\n\n![Figure 6: 6.3 Current Structure Compared With the Recommended Model](/library/ch2v12/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Counts from the CDRRMO organizational chart reviewed in October 2026; heads of sections are included in their units. The casual and job-order split is hard to read on the scanned chart and shall be confirmed against the Plantilla of Personnel. The chart predates recent staff movements and shall be updated.\n\n**Table 2.3**\n\n*Compliance Findings on the Current Organizational Structure*\n\n| **\\#** | **Standard**                                                                                    | **Finding**                                                                                                                                                                                                              | **IAT link (score)**         |\n|--------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------|\n| 1      | RA 10121 Sec. 12(a): administration and training; research and planning; operations and warning | All three functions are in place, arranged as four sections: training sits with operations, and warning and monitoring is a separate section. Reconcile the chart with the three-division model in section 7.            | A2.1 (2)                     |\n| 2      | JMC 2014-1 minimum plantilla                                                                    | Two of the four section-head posts are permanent, and two sections have no permanent post yet. Confirm the exact JMC 2014-1 items with the HRMO.                                                                         | A2.3 (2), A1.1 (2)           |\n| 3      | CDRRMO at department level                                                                      | No ordinance yet makes the CDRRMO a regular department. Confirm the head-of-office item and salary grade with the HRMO.                                                                                                  | A1.1 (2), A2.2 (3)           |\n| 4      | 24/7 OpCen with a documented roster                                                             | Seven OpCen staff cannot hold two per shift around the clock (Figure 2.2).                                                                                                                                               | A2.5 (2), B4.3 (2)           |\n| 5      | Research and planning capacity                                                                  | LDRRMP work, CDRA liaison, PDRA support, monitoring, GIS and data rest on a small team with no dedicated GIS, data or M&E post.                                                                                          | A5.6 (2), A10.4 (2)          |\n| 6      | Accountable functions                                                                           | Petty cash, supply and inventory control and procurement support sit in the Administrative Section. Confirm with the HRMO and the City Accountant that accountable roles are held by eligible staff (COA and CSC rules). | A4.8                         |\n| 7      | Posts the IAT expects                                                                           | No anticipatory-action focal person, public information and risk communication post, PDNA team or logistics and warehouse lead.                                                                                          | A2.9 (0), A6.5 (2), A9.1 (1) |\n| 8      | EOC structure                                                                                   | The chart shows peacetime sections only; the EOC structure, alternates and cluster desks are set out separately (Chapter 1, section 6.3).                                                                                | B4.1 (1), B4.2 (1)           |\n\n*Note.* Findings describe the structure, not the performance of any person. Medium-confidence items (3 and 6) shall be confirmed with the HRMO and the City Accountant.\n\n**Figure 2.2**\n\n*OpCen Staff in Post Compared With the Staff Needed for Two Per Shift*\n\n![Figure 7: 6.3 Current Structure Compared With the Recommended Model](/library/ch2v12/fig-07.webp)\n\n*Note.* Three shifts × two staff × seven days = 42 shifts a week. At about five shifts per person, about nine people are needed before leave and training are counted.\n\n**Recommended structure.** The assessment recommends keeping RA 10121''s three divisions (Figure 2.3):\n\n- Administration and Training takes over the training programme from Operations.\n\n- Operations and Warning holds two units: the OpCen and the rescue and medical team.\n\n- Every division has a permanent head, and the OpCen grows to at least nine staff so it can keep two per shift.\n\n- Research and Planning grows to at least four technical posts: planning and M&E, GIS and data, the anticipatory-action focal person, and a cluster-coordination lead (section 10.2). CDP 2024–2030 PPAs 1.1.1, 1.15.4 and 2.1.3 already call for regular positions, data posts and risk-communication posts.\n\n- The EOC keeps its own structure, separate from the office chart (Chapter 1, section 6.3).\n\n**Figure 2.3**\n\n*Recommended CDRRMO Structure Under RA 10121 and JMC 2014-1*\n\n![Figure 8: 6.3 Current Structure Compared With the Recommended Model](/library/ch2v12/fig-08.webp)\n\n*Note.* Proposed for validation at the December 2026 workshop. M&E = monitoring and evaluation.\n\n# 7. Functional Mandates and Division-Level Responsibilities\n\n## 7.1 CDRRMO Head\n\nThe CDRRMO Head provides overall leadership and is accountable for translating statutory mandates, CDRRMC policy direction and approved City plans into coordinated office work. Subject to the City''s approved structure and applicable rules, the Head shall:\n\n- Lead preparation and implementation of the CDRRMO annual work and financial plans, staffing proposals and performance commitments.\n\n- Direct and integrate the three core functional divisions and ensure that responsibilities are allocated without duplication.\n\n- Advise the City Mayor and CDRRMC on DRRM policy, institutional readiness, risk-informed priorities and capability gaps.\n\n- Ensure that the CDRRMO performs its statutory and assigned Secretariat functions and maintains reliable governance records.\n\n- Coordinate with CPDO and sector offices to mainstream DRR and CCA into development, land-use, investment and public-service continuity processes.\n\n- Ensure that risk information, preparedness, early warning, response coordination and recovery support are linked through documented processes.\n\n- Oversee readiness arrangements for the EOC, including pre-designated personnel, alternates, role descriptions and coordination with partner agencies.\n\n- Ensure compliance with approved personnel, procurement, financial, records, privacy, occupational safety and reporting requirements.\n\n- Lead performance review, after-action learning and corrective action within the CDRRMO''s authority.\n\n**Anticipatory-action focal person (A2.9).** The CDRRMO Head shall designate an anticipatory-action focal person by office order (RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 1; Rule VII). The focal person, BDRRMC officers, ACDVs and partner CSOs shall attend anticipatory-action training when OCD Region VIII cascades the IRR. This is a new mandatory item and currently scores 0.\n\n## 7.2 Administration and Training Division\n\nThe Administration and Training Division provides the administrative and organizational support required for a dependable CDRRMO. It shall perform the following functions, subject to the approved division mandate and coordination with HRMO and other competent offices:\n\n- Prepare and maintain administrative work plans, office records, correspondence, internal directives, schedules and document-control registers.\n\n- Coordinate with HRMO on staffing records, personnel movement, attendance, leave, training nominations, performance documentation and position-description updates.\n\n- Support CDRRMC Secretariat administration when assigned, including notices, agendas, attendance, minutes, resolutions and action monitoring.\n\n- Maintain the CDRRMO''s inventory of office equipment, supplies, vehicles, communications assets and administrative resources in coordination with property and supply offices.\n\n- Coordinate training needs assessment, annual training plans, training records, competency matrices and post-training evaluation.\n\n- Maintain staff induction materials, orientation checklists, internal administrative SOPs and handover records.\n\n- Coordinate personnel welfare and administrative support during extended operations, including rest, meals, transport and family-contact arrangements as approved.\n\n- Support budget preparation, procurement planning, records retention and audit readiness in coordination with Budget, Accounting, BAC and Records Management.\n\nThe Division shall not assume the statutory personnel authority of HRMO, the financial authority of the Budget and Accounting Offices, or procurement authority assigned to the BAC and authorized procurement officials.\n\n## 7.3 Research and Planning Division\n\nThe Research and Planning Division provides the analytical and planning functions that enable the City to understand risk, prioritize interventions and integrate DRRM and climate resilience into development decisions. It shall:\n\n- Coordinate preparation, review and updating of the Local DRRM Plan and relevant contingency, preparedness, mitigation and recovery plans.\n\n- Maintain technical linkages with the CDRA, CLUP, CDP, LCCAP, public service continuity plans and sectoral plans, ensuring that versions, assumptions and data sources are traceable.\n\n- Coordinate with CPDO, GIS/ICT and sector offices on hazard, exposure, vulnerability, capacity and critical-facility information, without displacing their technical mandates.\n\n- Develop and maintain risk-information products, planning assumptions, scenario analyses and evidence briefs for CDRRMC and executive decision-making.\n\n- Support pre-disaster risk assessment and anticipatory-action planning with Operations and Warning and relevant sector offices.\n\n- Prepare planning inputs for annual investment programming, DRRM financing proposals, project screening and resource prioritization.\n\n- Maintain planning indicators, monitoring frameworks, data dictionaries, evidence registers and knowledge products.\n\n- Coordinate technical studies, research partnerships, lessons analysis and integration of climate and disaster risk into local policy.\n\n- Ensure that data collection and sharing comply with privacy, confidentiality, records and information-security requirements.\n\nThe Division shall distinguish technical data stewardship from policy approval. CPDO remains the competent office for its statutory and assigned development-planning functions; engineering, health, environment and other technical offices retain their respective professional and regulatory mandates.\n\n## 7.4 Operations and Warning Division\n\nThe Operations and Warning Division maintains the CDRRMO''s operational readiness and coordinates warning, preparedness and response-support functions in accordance with approved plans and the separate EOC and ICS procedures. It shall:\n\n- Maintain routine hazard monitoring and warning coordination arrangements with PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and other competent sources.\n\n- Translate official warnings and risk information into operational recommendations and warning-action coordination with barangays and sector offices.\n\n- Maintain duty officer arrangements, operational contact directories, readiness checklists and escalation protocols.\n\n- Coordinate preparedness activities, drills, exercises and operational readiness reviews with BDRRMCs and response agencies.\n\n- Support pre-disaster risk assessment, anticipatory action planning, prepositioning and evacuation recommendations under the applicable legal and operational procedures.\n\n- Coordinate response information and resource requests through the activated EOC and designated incident management arrangements.\n\n- Support the CDRRMO''s coordination of response clusters and technical working arrangements without assuming the command authority of the Incident Commander or mandates of response agencies.\n\n- Maintain operational logs, warning dissemination records, deployment coordination records and after-action inputs.\n\n- Coordinate operational transition and technical inputs to recovery and rehabilitation functions.\n\nThe Division''s internal duty officer system shall operate during normal periods and heightened readiness. Its activation and shift arrangements shall be aligned with the EOC procedures in the relevant chapters and shall not be used to imply that the full EOC is continuously activated.\n\n![Figure 9: 7.4 Operations and Warning Division](/library/ch2v12/fig-09.webp)\n\n# 8. Staffing Establishment, Plantilla and Workforce Planning\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a staffing complement that is lawful, functionally sufficient, competency-based and sustainable. Staffing decisions shall be anchored in the statutory functions of the office, JMC No. 2014-1, the City''s approved organizational structure and staffing pattern, CSC qualification standards, DBM requirements and available appropriations.\n\n![Figure 10: 8. Staffing Establishment, Plantilla and Workforce Planning](/library/ch2v12/fig-10.webp)\n\n## 8.1 Staffing Establishment Principles\n\n- Mandatory office functions and minimum positions shall be identified first, before proposing additional or specialized roles.\n\n- Each proposed position shall have a documented justification linking workload, risk exposure, service requirements, outputs and required competencies.\n\n- Positions shall be created, reclassified, filled or redesigned only through the prescribed HRMO, Budget, DBM/CSC and local approval processes.\n\n- Critical functions shall have designated alternates or continuity arrangements to reduce single-person dependency.\n\n- Temporary, project-based, volunteer or partner personnel shall not be treated as substitutes for mandatory plantilla positions where law requires permanent staffing.\n\n- Staffing proposals shall account for 24/7 operational requirements, leave, training, fatigue, field deployment and emergency surge demands.\n\n## 8.2 Workforce and Plantilla Review\n\nThe CDRRMO Head, in coordination with HRMO and Budget, shall conduct an annual workforce review. The review shall compare the approved plantilla with required functions, filled and vacant positions, qualifications, workload, duty coverage, training and operational gaps.\n\n| **Review dimension**      | **Minimum information / evidence**                                                                              |\n|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Approved staffing pattern | Latest approved organizational chart, plantilla and relevant DBM/CSC approvals.                                 |\n| Position status           | Authorized, filled, vacant, temporarily assigned, detailed or otherwise unavailable.                            |\n| Qualification compliance  | Position qualification standards, appointment records and supporting credentials, subject to HRMO verification. |\n| Workload and coverage     | Work plans, task distribution, duty rosters, leave coverage, incident deployments and overtime records.         |\n| Competency profile        | Required versus demonstrated competencies, training and certification records.                                  |\n| Continuity risks          | Single-person dependencies, absence of alternates, succession gaps and critical knowledge held by individuals.  |\n| Resource implications     | Personnel services costs, training, PPE, insurance and operational support requirements.                        |\n\n## 8.3 Minimum Staffing and Gap Analysis\n\nA staffing gap analysis shall be prepared against the applicable JMC No. 2014-1 requirements and the current approved staffing pattern. The analysis shall not assume a fixed numerical complement without verifying the current standard and its applicability to Ormoc City. It shall identify: (a) required positions; (b) authorized positions; (c) filled positions; (d) vacancies; (e) qualification gaps; (f) operational coverage gaps; and (g) proposed corrective actions.\n\nThe assessment workbook''s A2.3 indicator requires evidence of minimum plantilla positions and the ratio of filled to required positions. The CDRRMO and HRMO shall reconcile the indicator against the approved plantilla and current national guidance before using it as a compliance conclusion.\n\n## 8.4 Position Creation and Approval Workflow\n\n1.  CDRRMO Head documents the functional need, workload evidence, proposed duties, competency requirements and expected outputs.\n\n2.  HRMO reviews the proposed position, qualification standards, organizational fit and personnel implications.\n\n3.  The Budget Office reviews personnel-services affordability and funding implications.\n\n4.  City Legal Office reviews legal authority and consistency with existing ordinances and organizational issuances where required.\n\n5.  The proposal is routed through the City''s authorized executive and legislative processes and to DBM/CSC or other competent authorities as applicable.\n\n6.  Only after required approvals are secured shall the position be incorporated into the approved staffing pattern and recruitment or appointment process.\n\n# 9. Position Descriptions, Authority and Accountability\n\nEvery approved CDRRMO position shall have a current position description or job description consistent with CSC and City HR requirements. The description shall define the position''s purpose, major duties, decision authority, reporting relationship, qualifications, competencies, outputs and performance measures.\n\n## 9.1 Minimum contents of position descriptions\n\n| **Field**                         | **Required content**                                                                                           |\n|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Position title and item number    | Official title, salary grade where applicable, plantilla item and employment status.                           |\n| Organizational placement          | Office, division or unit; immediate supervisor; positions supervised.                                          |\n| Position purpose                  | Concise statement of why the position exists and the institutional result it supports.                         |\n| Major duties and responsibilities | Specific functions, frequency or context, expected outputs and coordination duties.                            |\n| Authority and limits              | Decisions the position may make; approvals required; matters reserved to superior or statutory authority.      |\n| Qualifications                    | Education, experience, training, eligibility, licenses or other requirements under applicable standards.       |\n| Competencies                      | Technical, managerial, operational, digital, communication and behavioural competencies.                       |\n| Performance measures              | Output, quality, timeliness, compliance and service indicators linked to OPCR/IPCR or equivalent system.       |\n| Interfaces                        | Internal and external offices, agencies, barangays and partners with which the position routinely coordinates. |\n| Review and approval               | Preparer, HRMO review, approving authority, effective date and revision history.                               |\n\n## 9.2 Delegation and accountability\n\nDelegation shall be documented and limited to authority that may lawfully be delegated. It shall identify the delegating authority, delegate, scope, duration, conditions, reporting requirements and revocation mechanism. Delegation does not remove the delegating official''s accountability for oversight where provided by law.\n\nRACI matrices in this chapter allocate operational work but do not confer appointment authority, statutory decision rights, financial authority or professional licensing. Where a decision is reserved by law or City policy to a particular official or body, that authority prevails over a RACI designation.\n\n# 10. EOC Staffing Interface and Operational Augmentation\n\nThe EOC is an operational coordination mechanism supported by personnel designated from the CDRRMO and participating City offices, agencies and partners. It is not a permanent fourth division of the CDRRMO. Its incident-time configuration shall be determined by the EOC activation level, incident complexity, hazard profile and coordination requirements.\n\nThe EOC staffing model shall be elaborated in the dedicated EOC organization and activation chapters. For purposes of this chapter, the CDRRMO shall ensure that the permanent office maintains an EOC personnel readiness register and coordinates with HRMO and participating offices to identify trained personnel and alternates.\n\n| **EOC functional role (illustrative)** | **Staffing source / interface**                                                                        | **Chapter 2 control**                                                                 |\n|----------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| EOC Manager                            | Designated by competent authority; may be drawn from CDRRMO or another qualified official as approved. | Written designation, role profile, authority limits and alternate.                    |\n| Operations                             | CDRRMO Operations and Warning and relevant response-agency representatives.                            | Competency and role-specific job action sheet.                                        |\n| Planning / Information                 | CDRRMO Research and Planning, GIS/ICT and agency information focal persons.                            | Data access, reporting standards and information-quality requirements.                |\n| Logistics                              | CDRRMO support staff and relevant City supply, property, procurement and transport offices.            | Clear separation of operational request, procurement, custody and financial approval. |\n| Administration / Finance               | Authorized administrative, budget, accounting and HR personnel.                                        | Maintain lawful approval and transaction controls.                                    |\n| Agency / cluster liaison               | CSWD, CHO, Engineering, PNP, BFP, AFP, DepEd, utilities, Red Cross and other relevant partners.        | Written focal-person designation and alternate; agency retains its mandate.           |\n\n![Figure 11: 10. EOC Staffing Interface and Operational Augmentation](/library/ch2v12/fig-11.webp)\n\nThe Institutional Assessment B4 indicators require a defined EOC structure, pre-designated staff with alternates, duty rosters and handover protocols, agency/cluster liaison assignments, job action sheets and responder welfare. These are to be implemented through the EOC-specific chapters and annexes; Chapter 2 establishes the workforce and designation controls that support them.\n\n## 10.1 EOC Staffing at Red Activation\n\nAt Red, the EOC needs about 25 seats a shift, or about 50 people over two 12-hour shifts, before cluster desks are counted. The CDRRMO''s non-field staff and the proposed cluster coordinators cover most of this. The City shall pre-designate 15 to 20 trained augmentees from City offices to cover the rest, plus leave, fatigue and field deployment (SOP HR-06; Annex 2-D).\n\n**Figure 2.4**\n\n*People Needed to Staff the EOC at Red Compared With Available Sources*\n\n![Figure 12: 10.1 EOC Staffing at Red Activation](/library/ch2v12/fig-12.webp)\n\n*Note.* Estimates from the proposed EOC structure (Chapter 1, Figure 1.3). Field rescue staff are excluded because they deploy with the Incident Management Team.\n\n**Geospatial support.** The EOC Planning Section shall include a GIS and information-management function that keeps the geospatial common operating picture during activation. In normal operations, the GIS and data post in Research and Planning maintains the EOC reference point, map layers and offline map packages. Staff assigned to this function shall be trained in incident georeferencing and map-product control (section 12). The procedure itself is SOP 6-GIS-01 in the EOC Module.\n\n## 10.2 Cluster-Based Barangay DRRM Coordinators (Proposal for Validation)\n\nA proposal developed within the CDRRMO would assign six coordinators to clusters of barangays, about 14 barangays each, covering all 85 barangays. It answers gaps no other plan covers: there is no barangay-to-EOC reporting flow (B5.2 = 1), no drill yet shows purok welfare checks within 12 hours (A7.9 = 1), and BDRRMC functionality has not been validated across all barangays (A7.3). It builds on national tools the City already uses: Operation L!sto, the harmonized SitRep (RDRRMC VIII Res. No. 12-006) and RDANA. The assessment supports the proposal in principle, with the conditions in Table 2.4.\n\n**Figure 2.5**\n\n*Hazard Tags Recorded in the Cluster Proposal, by Cluster*\n\n![Figure 13: 10.2 Cluster-Based Barangay DRRM Coordinators (Proposal for Validation)](/library/ch2v12/fig-13.webp)\n\n*Note.* Numbers in brackets are barangays per cluster. Tag counts exceed barangay counts where a barangay carries two tags (San Juan, Linao, Naungan and Punta: flood and storm surge). Thirty-five barangays carry no tag yet and need tags from the updated CDRA.\n\n**Table 2.4**\n\n*Conditions for Adopting the Cluster-Based Coordinator Model*\n\n| **\\#** | **Area**             | **Condition**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n|--------|----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1      | Cluster balance      | Clusters range from 6 to 18 barangays. Add a cluster map and balance clusters by population, hazard and travel time, not by count. One option: move Lao and San Juan from District 2 to District 3, making one west-coast cluster of eight with shared coastal exposure. |\n| 2      | Hazard tagging       | Tag every barangay from one hazard register, the updated CDRA. Reconcile the tags with the DRRM 10-Year Plan''s 46 critical flood barangays and the LCCAP''s 73 flood-prone barangays.                                                                                     |\n| 3      | Reporting line       | During warning and response, coordinators report to the EOC Planning Section (situation unit), keeping one channel and the DILG VIII reporting cut-offs. In normal operations they report to Research and Planning.                                                      |\n| 4      | Evacuation decisions | Coordinators advise. The decision stays with the Punong Barangay or the Mayor under the evacuation ordinance (A1.5).                                                                                                                                                     |\n| 5      | Barangay DRRM funds  | Each barangay''s BDRRMF is approved by its Sangguniang Barangay; coordinators give technical help only, as the field secretariat of the BDRRMP Review Team (EO No. 2023-0085).                                                                                            |\n| 6      | Employment and cost  | State plantilla or contract of service, the annual cost and the fund source. Check with the Budget Office whether LDRRMF use for salaries is allowed (JMC 2013-1, COA rules). Contract staff cannot hold supervisory or accountable roles.                               |\n| 7      | Approval route       | The SP committee chair recommends approval; the Mayor approves; new posts go through the SP.                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| 8      | Measures             | Adopt five measures: % of BDRRMPs certified; % of barangays with a completed Operation L!sto checklist by 1 June; barangay report timeliness against SitRep cut-offs; % of puroks accounted for within 12 hours in drills; % of barangays with a trained response team.  |\n\n*Note.* Conditions proposed for validation at the December 2026 workshop. Two citations in the proposal shall be checked before it goes to the SP: RA 10121 Sec. 12(a) and the DILG circular for Operation L!sto.\n\n**Where coordinators sit.** In normal operations, coordinators sit in Research and Planning for plan review, data and L!sto checks, and work with Administration and Training on training content. On EOC activation they become the Barangay Liaison Desk under the Planning Section, one desk per cluster. The coordinator for the eastern upland cluster shall be able to report by radio or satellite, because that cluster can be cut off.\n\n**Pilot.** Run two clusters for one typhoon season, June to November 2027: District 1 (eastern uplands, isolation risk) and District 3 (west coast, storm-surge risk). Measure them against condition 8, then scale to six clusters through the FY2028 budget.\n\n# 11. Duty Systems, Availability and Continuity of Personnel\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a duty system proportionate to its legal functions, operational requirements and approved staffing. The system shall distinguish routine office hours, duty officer coverage, heightened readiness and full EOC activation. It shall not assume continuous full EOC activation.\n\n## 11.1 Duty roster and handover\n\nA controlled duty roster shall identify primary personnel, alternates, contact details, duty period, supervisor, escalation route and any special competency or authorization required. Handover shall cover current warnings, incidents, pending actions, resource requests, equipment status, communications issues, unresolved decisions and records requiring follow-up.\n\n**Current practice (site visit, October 2026).** The CDRRMO already runs 24-hour duty from the CDRRM Office for three priority areas: City Traffic Monitoring, Public Safety and Emergency Monitoring. To move A2.5 and B4.3 from 2 to 3, the CDRRMO shall: (a) have the White-level OpCen SOP approved; (b) give each monitoring stream a duty post, a task list and a reporting line, including who escalates to the CDRRMO Head and when; and (c) keep signed rosters and handover forms (Annex 2-E) for at least three months as proof of use.\n\n## 11.2 Continuity and succession\n\n- Maintain at least one trained alternate for critical CDRRMO functions and ensure EOC key positions are designated with alternates in accordance with the applicable EOC standard.\n\n- Maintain updated contact and emergency notification lists with access controls.\n\n- Document acting arrangements and succession in accordance with City rules.\n\n- Ensure essential records, credentials and operating instructions are accessible to authorized alternates.\n\n- Include staff absence, facility disruption, ICT outage and simultaneous incidents in continuity exercises.\n\n- Coordinate Public Service Continuity Planning for CDRRMO functions with the City''s designated continuity focal office.\n\n## 11.3 Fatigue management\n\nDuty schedules shall provide reasonable rest periods, relief arrangements, meals, hydration and safe transport where required. Supervisors shall monitor fatigue and fitness for duty, particularly during prolonged operations. The EOC''s shift cycle and rest arrangements shall be specified in its dedicated operational procedures.\n\n![Figure 14: 11.3 Fatigue management](/library/ch2v12/fig-14.webp)\n\n# 12. Competency Development and Professionalization\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a competency-based human-resource development system. Training shall be driven by role requirements, risk exposure, performance findings and the City''s operational priorities rather than by attendance targets alone.\n\n## 12.1 Competency framework\n\n| **Competency group**         | **Indicative competencies**                                                                                                                          |\n|------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Institutional and governance | RA 10121 and local DRRM governance; planning; policy coordination; public administration; records and accountability.                                |\n| Risk and planning            | Hazard and risk assessment; CDRA interpretation; contingency planning; climate adaptation; PDRA; recovery planning.                                  |\n| Operational                  | ICS; EOC operations; emergency coordination; warning dissemination; evacuation coordination; resource request and tracking.                          |\n| Technical and field          | First aid / basic life support; search and rescue or water safety where relevant; damage assessment; safety and incident awareness, subject to role. |\n| Information and digital      | GIS; data management; situation reporting; dashboard use; cybersecurity; privacy and information security.                                           |\n| Leadership and people        | Supervision; facilitation; negotiation; decision-making under pressure; inclusion; conflict sensitivity; staff welfare.                              |\n| Continuity and improvement   | Public service continuity; business impact analysis; exercise design; after-action review; corrective action and quality improvement.                |\n\n## 12.2 Training needs assessment and annual plan\n\n1.  Map each approved position to required competencies and proficiency levels.\n\n2.  Assess current competence using records, supervisor review, exercises, tests and demonstrated performance.\n\n3.  Identify priority gaps based on life-safety risk, legal requirements, operational criticality and current assessment findings.\n\n4.  Prepare an annual training and development plan with objectives, target personnel, provider, cost, schedule and evaluation method\n\n5.  Secure HRMO, Budget and approving-authority review and integrate the plan into the City''s personnel and financial planning cycles\n\n6.  Evaluate learning transfer through observed performance, exercises, work products and after-action reviews.\n\nTraining records shall include course title, provider, dates, participant, competency addressed, certificate or evidence, assessment result and renewal requirement where applicable. Attendance alone shall not be treated as proof of operational competence.\n\n![Figure 15: 12.2 Training needs assessment and annual plan](/library/ch2v12/fig-15.webp)\n\n# 13. Personnel Welfare, Occupational Safety and Psychosocial Support\n\nThe City shall apply a duty-of-care approach to CDRRMO personnel and responders. Personnel shall not be assigned to tasks beyond their competence, authorization or safe operating conditions. Safety and welfare arrangements shall be coordinated with HRMO, occupational safety and health focal persons, health services and other competent offices.\n\n## 13.1 Minimum welfare and safety controls\n\n- Documented hazard identification and risk assessment for office, field and emergency assignments.\n\n- Appropriate PPE, safety equipment, communications and task-specific protective measures.\n\n- Pre-deployment briefing, fitness-for-duty checks where appropriate, buddy arrangements and accountability procedures.\n\n- Access to first aid, medical referral, insurance and applicable benefits, subject to law and approved City policy.\n\n- Rest cycles, meals, hydration, sanitation and safe accommodation or transport during extended operations.\n\n- Psychological first aid, psychosocial support, referral pathways and post-incident support, consistent with RA 11036 and applicable protocols.\n\n- Protection against harassment, discrimination, retaliation and unsafe working conditions.\n\n- Family communication and emergency contact arrangements consistent with privacy safeguards.\n\n- Post-deployment check-in, incident reporting, injury/near-miss reporting and learning review.\n\n**Insurance and welfare evidence (A2.7).** CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03 and 04 s.2022 (31 March 2022) adopted NDRRMC MC No. 64 s.2021 on the accreditation, mobilization and protection of community disaster volunteers, and approved a risk categorization as the basis for buying insurance for accredited volunteers. No policy or proof of purchase was sighted, and no equivalent arrangement covers regular CDRRMO staff (insurance, hazard pay, PPE issue, MHPSS). The responder welfare policy shall close both gaps.\n\n![Figure 16: 13.1 Minimum welfare and safety controls](/library/ch2v12/fig-16.webp)\n\n## 13.2 Inclusion and reasonable accommodation\n\nPersonnel arrangements shall respect gender equality, disability inclusion, age-related needs, cultural and language diversity and reasonable accommodation. Field assignments, facilities, training and communications shall be designed to enable qualified personnel to participate safely and effectively.\n\n## 13.3 Responder welfare ownership\n\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate the welfare framework but shall not displace HRMO, CHO, occupational safety officers or other competent offices. Each deploying office remains responsible for the personnel under its administrative control, subject to incident coordination and applicable law.\n\n# 14. Human Resource Administration, Performance and Conduct\n\nAll CDRRMO personnel remain subject to the City''s approved personnel administration systems and applicable CSC rules. The CDRRMO Head and division chiefs shall align work plans and individual performance commitments with the CDRRMO mandate, approved office targets and City performance management requirements.\n\n## 14.1 Performance management\n\n- Translate annual office outputs into division and individual commitments.\n\n- Use measurable indicators covering timeliness, quality, compliance, coordination, readiness and service outcomes.\n\n- Conduct regular supervisory discussions and document performance support or corrective measures.\n\n- Use after-action review findings and exercise results to identify training and process improvements.\n\n- Ensure that institutional assessment findings are used for system improvement and not as a substitute for formal individual performance evaluation.\n\n## 14.2 Conduct, confidentiality and information security\n\nPersonnel shall comply with applicable codes of conduct, confidentiality obligations, records rules, data privacy and cybersecurity policies. Access to beneficiary information, personnel files, health information, incident intelligence and sensitive operational records shall be role-based and limited to legitimate official purposes.\n\n## 14.3 Volunteers, surge personnel and partner staff\n\nVolunteers, accredited civil society partners, auxiliary personnel and surge staff may augment capacity where legally permitted and appropriately managed. Their roles, supervision, safety, training, insurance, access to information and limits of authority shall be documented. They shall not be treated as substitutes for required permanent positions or authorized officials.\n\nUnder CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03 and 04 s.2022, the City applies NDRRMC MC No. 64 s.2021 to accredited community disaster volunteers (ACDVs). SP Res. No. 2023-347 authorizes a MOA with the CPR-RIMT Rapid Rescue Rider Ormoc Chapter for volunteer augmentation. To close A2.8, the CDRRMO shall file the signed MOA; keep an ACDV registry with accreditation, insurance and deployment rules; route volunteer tasking through the EOC during activation; and add anticipatory-action tasks for ACDVs (RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 5).\n\n# 15. Workforce Governance Workflows\n\n## 15.1 Annual workforce planning and staffing review\n\n1.  CDRRMO Head issues the annual workforce review instruction and confirms the review period.\n\n2.  Division chiefs submit workload, outputs, staffing, competency, duty coverage and continuity information.\n\n3.  Administration and Training consolidates the workforce profile and evidence register.\n\n4.  HRMO validates positions, qualification standards, appointment status and personnel implications.\n\n5.  The Budget Office assesses personnel-services and training affordability.\n\n6.  CDRRMO Head approves the office-level workforce gap analysis for submission through the City''s authorized process.\n\n7.  Corrective actions are assigned, monitored and reviewed quarterly.\n\n## 15.2 Recruitment, appointment and onboarding interface\n\n1.  CDRRMO identifies an approved vacant position and documents operational requirements.\n\n2.  HRMO confirms vacancy status, qualification standards, recruitment and selection requirements.\n\n3.  Budget confirms funding and personnel-services availability.\n\n4.  Recruitment, selection and appointment proceed through the City''s authorized process and CSC rules.\n\n5.  Upon appointment or assignment, the supervisor provides an induction briefing, position description, work plan, records and access orientation, safety briefing and training needs assessment.\n\n6.  Completion of onboarding is documented and retained in the appropriate personnel and office records.\n\n## 15.3 Training needs assessment and development cycle\n\n1.  Map position competencies and assess current proficiency.\n\n2.  Identify gaps using performance, incident, exercise and assessment evidence.\n\n3.  Prioritize training by legal requirement, risk and operational criticality.\n\n4.  Prepare and approve the annual training plan and budget.\n\n5.  Deliver training and maintain verifiable records.\n\n6.  Evaluate application in work performance or exercises and update the competency matrix.\n\n## 15.4 EOC personnel designation and readiness\n\n1.  CDRRMO Head identifies permanent office personnel who may be assigned to EOC roles, in consultation with division chiefs.\n\n2.  Participating City offices and partner agencies nominate qualified primary and alternate liaison personnel.\n\n3.  HRMO and the relevant office head confirm administrative status and availability.\n\n4.  CDRRMO maintains the controlled roster and records relevant training and competencies.\n\n5.  On activation, personnel report under the approved EOC activation and staffing procedure.\n\n6.  After deactivation, personnel return to their normal assignments and submit handover, records and welfare reports as required.\n\n## 15.5 Personnel welfare incident and corrective action\n\n1.  Personnel report injury, exposure, near miss, fatigue concern or psychosocial support need through the designated channel.\n\n2.  Supervisor ensures immediate safety, medical response or referral and notification of the competent office.\n\n3.  HRMO / occupational safety / health focal persons record and assess the incident under applicable rules.\n\n4.  CDRRMO reviews operational implications and identifies corrective actions.\n\n5.  Actions are assigned, tracked and verified for effectiveness without disclosing protected personal information.\n\n# 2.16 RACI and Coordination Matrices\n\nThe following matrices allocate operational responsibility. They are subject to the statutory authority, approved organizational structure, personnel rules and formal delegations described in Chapter 1. One accountable owner is identified for each routine process; where a decision belongs to a statutory or collective authority, that authority remains controlling.\n\n| **Process / activity**                                 | **Accountable (A)**                                               | **Responsible (R)**                                       | **Consulted (C) / Informed (I)**         |\n|--------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| Annual CDRRMO workforce plan                           | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Division chiefs; Administration and Training              | HRMO; Budget; Mayor                      |\n| Review of organizational structure                     | City Mayor / authorized approving authority                       | CDRRMO Head; HRMO                                         | CLO; Budget; DBM/CSC as applicable       |\n| Position description preparation                       | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Immediate supervisor / division chief                     | HRMO; CLO where needed                   |\n| Qualification standards and recruitment administration | HRMO / authorized appointing authority                            | HRMO                                                      | CDRRMO Head; Budget; concerned division  |\n| Annual training needs assessment                       | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Division chiefs; Administration and Training              | HRMO; OCD; training providers; Budget    |\n| Annual training plan and budget                        | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Administration and Training                               | HRMO; Budget; division chiefs            |\n| Duty roster and internal handover                      | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Operations and Warning / designated supervisor            | HRMO; division chiefs                    |\n| EOC primary and alternate nominations                  | Head of each participating office for its personnel               | Office focal persons                                      | CDRRMO; HRMO; EOC Manager                |\n| EOC roster consolidation                               | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Designated EOC readiness focal person                     | Participating offices; HRMO; OCD         |\n| Personnel safety and welfare system                    | City authority designated under City OSH / HR policy              | HRMO; CHO; occupational safety focal persons; supervisors | CDRRMO; Budget; partner agencies         |\n| Performance monitoring                                 | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Division chiefs / immediate supervisors                   | HRMO; City performance management office |\n| Workforce records and privacy controls                 | HRMO for official personnel files; CDRRMO for operational records | Authorized records custodians                             | DPO; ICT; CLO                            |\n\n## 2.16.1 Functional interface matrix\n\n| **Function**                   | **Lead division**                               | **Key supporting offices / partners**                        | **Boundary / coordination rule**                                                              |\n|--------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Personnel administration       | Administration and Training                     | HRMO; Budget; Accounting                                     | CDRRMO coordinates; HRMO retains personnel administration authority.                          |\n| DRRM and climate-risk planning | Research and Planning                           | CPDO; CENRO; Engineering; sector offices; academe            | CDRRMO supplies DRRM technical inputs; each office retains its planning or technical mandate. |\n| Hazard monitoring and warning  | Operations and Warning                          | PAGASA; PHIVOLCS; BDRRMCs; communications providers          | Use official warning sources; issuing agencies retain authority over technical advisories.    |\n| EOC readiness                  | CDRRMO Head / designated readiness focal person | HRMO; IT; Engineering; utilities; participating offices; OCD | Maintain roster and readiness; detailed activation and EOC management are in later chapters.  |\n| Training and exercises         | Administration and Training                     | OCD; HRMO; BFP; PNP; CHO; training institutions              | Training plan is competency-based; agencies retain professional standards.                    |\n| Responder welfare              | Designated City HR / OSH / health authority     | HRMO; CHO; supervisors; insurance providers                  | Each deploying office retains duty of care for its personnel.                                 |\n| Information and records        | Division owning the operational record          | ICT; Records Officer; DPO; CLO                               | Apply role-based access, retention and privacy requirements.                                  |\n\n## 2.16.2 Budget-Cycle RACI (Draft for Validation)\n\nStaff reflections asked for clearer roles in forming the LDRRMF investment plan. Planning and execution need different strengths, and the plan is strongest when both sign it. The RACI below gives Research and Planning the lead in formulation, keeps execution and audit controls with Administration and the City''s finance offices, and closes IAT gaps A4.4 and A10.4.\n\n**Table 2.5**\n\n*Budget-Cycle RACI for the LDRRMF Investment Plan*\n\n| **Step**                                                                                    | **Responsible**                           | **Accountable**        | **Consulted**                           | **Informed**          |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-----------------------|\n| 1\\. Baseline: CDRA, LDRRMP results, last year''s utilization                                 | Research and Planning                     | CDRRMO Head            | Operations and Warning; CPDO            | CDRRMC                |\n| 2\\. Division needs and proposals                                                            | Each division                             | CDRRMO Head            | Research and Planning                   | —                     |\n| 3\\. Draft LDRRMFIP: prioritized; tagged to LDRRMP/CDP PPA and thematic area; 70/30; AA line | Research and Planning                     | CDRRMO Head            | Administration (costing); Budget Office | —                     |\n| 4\\. Costing, PPMP/APP and procurement plan                                                  | Administration                            | CDRRMO Head            | Research and Planning                   | BAC                   |\n| 5\\. CDRRMC approval, then SP adoption                                                       | Secretariat                               | CDRRMC; SP             | Budget Office                           | DILG; OCD VIII        |\n| 6\\. Execution: obligation, procurement, disbursement                                        | Administration                            | CDRRMO Head            | Budget Office; Accountant; BAC          | Research and Planning |\n| 7\\. Quarterly report: results and finances                                                  | Research and Planning with Administration | CDRRMO Head            | —                                       | CDRRMC                |\n| 8\\. Realignment or reallocation                                                             | Research and Planning                     | CDRRMC; SP concurrence | Administration; Accountant              | Budget Office         |\n\n*Note.* R = does the work; A = accountable; C = consulted; I = informed. Draft for validation at the 1–4 December 2026 workshop.\n\n# 17. Performance Standards, Records and Quality Assurance\n\n![Figure 17: 17. Performance Standards, Records and Quality Assurance](/library/ch2v12/fig-17.webp)\n\nThe CDRRMO shall apply a Plan–Do–Check–Act (PDCA) approach to organizational and human-resource management. The objective is to ensure that the structure is not only approved on paper but functions reliably and improves through evidence.\n\n| **PDCA stage** | **Organizational / HR application**                                                                        | **Minimum evidence**                                                                        |\n|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Plan           | Review mandates, workload, staffing, competencies, duty coverage and welfare risks; set annual objectives. | Approved organogram; plantilla review; workforce plan; competency matrix; training plan.    |\n| Do             | Implement staffing, supervision, training, duty systems, designation and welfare controls.                 | Position descriptions; designation orders; rosters; training records; handover records.     |\n| Check          | Review outputs, vacancies, qualifications, response readiness, exercise performance and staff welfare.     | OPCR/IPCR or equivalent; attendance; audit findings; exercise evaluations; welfare reports. |\n| Act            | Correct staffing, competency, process and continuity gaps; revise position descriptions and SOPs.          | Corrective action register; revised plans; approved changes; follow-up verification.        |\n\n## 17.1 Core workforce performance indicators\n\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_v1.2.md (Chapter 2, v1.2 working draft (IAT v1.5))','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('a206dcd0-633c-55d7-9426-66c9bb53b57a'::uuid,'77080547-c4e3-52f7-8908-02ce0fc78258'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'**CHAPTER 3**\n\n**RISK INTELLIGENCE, DRRM AND CONTINGENCY PLANNING,  \nAND INSTITUTIONAL PREPAREDNESS**\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)  \nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n***Working Draft \\| 31 October 2026  \nSubject to technical, institutional, planning, legal and data validation***\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Control field** | **Designation / requirement**                                                                           | **Control field**      | **Designation / requirement**                                |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document owner    | CDRRMO Head                                                                                             | Document custodian     | CDRRMO Research and Planning function                        |\n| Technical review  | CPDO; City Engineering; CENRO; City Agriculture; City Health; CSWDO; GIS/data custodians; relevant NGAs | Legal review           | City Legal Office                                            |\n| Planning review   | CPDO / Local Development Council / relevant sector offices                                              | Data governance review | City Data Protection Officer / data custodians               |\n| Approval          | Mayor / CDRRMC / Sanggunian, as legally applicable                                                      | Effective date         | Upon valid approval and issuance                             |\n| Review cycle      | Annual; after major hazard/data/planning changes; after exercises or disasters                          | Status                 | Controlled working draft; not yet an approved City directive |\n\n## REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date**        | **Description**                                                                                                                 | **Prepared / reviewed by**                           |\n|-------------|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| 0.9         | 31 October 2026 | Rebuilt from project Chapter 1, Chapter 2, IAT v1.3, and verified official methodology sources; replaces generic workflow draft | For CDRRMO, CPDO, sector-office and legal validation |\n\n# CHAPTER 3 STRUCTURE\n\n- 3.1 Introduction and assessment-informed rationale\n\n- 3.2 Purpose and intended results\n\n- 3.3 Scope, boundaries and interfaces\n\n- 3.4 Legal, policy and methodological framework\n\n- 3.5 Systems-thinking and TQM operating principles\n\n- 3.6 Institutional roles and process ownership\n\n- 3.7 Risk information governance and authoritative hazard data\n\n- 3.8 Hazard information acquisition, validation and mapping\n\n- 3.9 Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA)\n\n- 3.10 Exposure, vulnerability, loss and impact-chain analysis\n\n- 3.11 Critical facilities, lifelines and cascading-failure analysis\n\n- 3.12 Risk-informed planning and investment mainstreaming\n\n- 3.13 Contingency planning and exercise programme\n\n- 3.14 Public Service Continuity Planning (PSCP)\n\n- 3.15 Barangay DRRM planning and quality assurance\n\n- 3.16 Inclusive DRRM and ecosystem-based risk reduction\n\n- 3.17 Process workflows and decision controls\n\n- 3.18 Responsibility assignment (RACI)\n\n- 3.19 Performance monitoring, records and corrective action\n\n- 3.20 Priority implementation and validation agenda\n\n- 3.21 Conclusion\n\n- Annexes 3-A to 3-L: SOPs, forms, registers and traceability\n\n# 3.1 Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nThis chapter establishes the City''s operating system for understanding disaster and climate risk, translating risk evidence into land-use and development decisions, preparing for priority hazard scenarios, maintaining essential public services during disruption, and strengthening preparedness from city to barangay level. It treats risk intelligence as a continuous institutional process—not a one-time mapping or report-writing exercise.\n\nThe chapter is based on the October 2026 Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT) workbook, the governance and chapter architecture established in Chapter 1, and the process ownership and documented-control approach established in Chapter 2. The IAT is a diagnostic instrument. Its scores are not legal certification and do not independently establish that a plan, map, database or process is current, approved, complete or operational.\n\n![Figure 1: 3.1 Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale](/library/ch3/fig-01.webp)\n\nThe assessment indicates that Ormoc has an existing CDRA, hazard information, a Local DRRM Plan and contingency-planning activity. The critical issue is the conversion of these assets into a coherent, maintained and decision-used system: complete multi-hazard coverage; technically validated exposure and vulnerability data; spatial integration into CLUP and zoning; risk screening of development proposals; tested hazard-specific contingency arrangements; and continuity arrangements for essential city services.\n\n# 3.2 Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThe purpose of this chapter is to standardize how Ormoc City identifies, assesses, communicates, plans for and periodically reviews disaster and climate risks. It shall produce the following institutional results:\n\n- An authoritative, version-controlled city risk information base, with source provenance, scale, date, limitations and responsible custodian for each dataset.\n\n- A technically defensible CDRA that follows the applicable DHSUD methodology and is spatially explicit, multi-hazard, multi-sectoral and usable for land-use and development decisions.\n\n- An approved and monitored LDRRMP aligned with the NDRRMP and integrated with the CLUP, CDP, LCCAP, ELA, LDIP, AIP and annual budget processes through CPDO-led planning mechanisms.\n\n- Hazard-specific contingency plans with clear scenario assumptions, objectives, roles, triggers, resource arrangements, information flows and exercise evidence.\n\n- A Public Service Continuity Plan based on an essential-functions inventory, business impact analysis, dependencies, continuity strategies, recovery priorities and tested arrangements.\n\n- A quality-assured system for reviewing barangay DRRM plans, including a verified barangay master list and documented feedback and follow-up.\n\n- Evidence that gender, age, disability, child protection, Indigenous Peoples'' rights and other contextually relevant inclusion requirements shape risk assessment, planning and service arrangements.\n\n# 3.3 Scope, Boundaries and Interfaces\n\nThis chapter applies to the CDRRMO Research and Planning function and all City offices that own, generate, validate, regulate, fund or use risk information and preparedness arrangements. It covers normal operations, heightened preparedness and anticipatory action, and readiness for response and continuity. It does not transfer the statutory or technical mandates of other offices to the CDRRMO.\n\n| **Institution / office**                                                           | **Accountability retained**                                                                                                                 | **Required interface with Chapter 3**                                                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRRMC                                                                             | Policy direction, approval/oversight functions assigned by law, monitoring and evaluation of LDRRMP                                         | Review risk evidence, endorse/recommend plans and monitor implementation                                  |\n| CDRRMO                                                                             | Facilitate risk assessment and contingency planning; consolidate local risk information; maintain local risk map; coordinate LDRRMP process | Own the risk-information and DRRM planning coordination process; maintain registers and technical records |\n| CPDO / LDC                                                                         | Local development planning, CLUP/CDP preparation and integration, investment prioritization                                                 | Lead formal mainstreaming of CDRA into CLUP/CDP and investment plans; convene planning review             |\n| Engineering / OBO / Zoning                                                         | Engineering, building-permit, locational-clearance, zoning and code decisions under their mandates                                          | Apply adopted hazard overlays, technical standards and permit conditions; retain regulatory decisions     |\n| CENRO / DENR / City Agriculture / sector offices                                   | Environmental, ecosystem, agriculture, health, social welfare, education and infrastructure mandates                                        | Provide authoritative sector data, impact analysis, service dependencies and technical validation         |\n| Budget / LFC / BAC / Accounting                                                    | Budget, financial management, procurement and accounting controls                                                                           | Apply approved risk-screening and budget controls; ensure proposals are fiscally and procedurally valid   |\n| HRMO / department heads                                                            | Personnel administration and office continuity responsibilities                                                                             | Identify critical functions, alternates, staffing, succession and employee welfare arrangements           |\n| Barangays / BDRRMCs                                                                | Barangay-level DRRM governance and plan implementation                                                                                      | Prepare and test BDRRMPs; validate local exposure, vulnerable households and evacuation information       |\n| OCD Region VIII / PAGASA / PHIVOLCS / MGB / GeoRiskPH and other competent agencies | National/regional mandates and authoritative technical products                                                                             | Provide source data, advisories, technical guidance and review as applicable                              |\n\n# 3.4 Legal, Policy and Methodological Framework\n\n| **Instrument / methodology**                                                   | **Operational relevance to this chapter**                                                                                                                                                                                                    | **Application / control**                                                                               |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA 10121 (2010), especially Sections 11–12                                     | LDRRMC oversight; LDRRMO functions include facilitating risk assessment and contingency planning, consolidating local risk information and maintaining a local risk map, and formulating an integrated LDRRMP in close coordination with LDC | Primary statutory basis; apply with its IRR and current official guidance                               |\n| RA 7160 (1991)                                                                 | Local planning, general welfare, local development and land-use planning powers                                                                                                                                                              | CPDO and competent regulatory offices retain their mandates                                             |\n| RA 9729, as amended by RA 10174                                                | Climate change adaptation and mainstreaming into local development                                                                                                                                                                           | Use climate projections and adaptation analysis within CDRA and plans                                   |\n| DHSUD MC No. 2025-004, Enhanced CDRA Policy                                    | Policy direction for standardized, updated, multi-hazard, multi-sectoral and spatially explicit CDRA                                                                                                                                         | Obtain and retain signed circular and annexes; follow current DHSUD process                             |\n| DHSUD CDRA training materials / 5-Step+ process                                | Hazard and climate information; impact scoping; exposure database; CDRA; MDA synthesis; compilation; CLUP mainstreaming; M&E; communication                                                                                                  | Methodological sequence for SOP 3-03; check technical manuals and prescribed tools                      |\n| RA 10173 and applicable NPC issuances                                          | Processing of personal and sensitive personal information                                                                                                                                                                                    | Apply purpose limitation, lawful basis, minimization, access control, retention and security            |\n| RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 9994; RA 10821; RA 8371; RA 11106              | Gender, disability, older persons, children, Indigenous Peoples and Filipino Sign Language inclusion                                                                                                                                         | Apply to data disaggregation, consultations, warnings, evacuation and continuity                        |\n| RA 12076 and its IRR                                                           | Permanent evacuation centre standards                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Coordinate facility planning and compliance; do not treat all buildings as suitable evacuation centres  |\n| DILG MC No. 2025-035 (Listo si KAP), Operation L!sto and current DILG guidance | Barangay preparedness and plan implementation                                                                                                                                                                                                | Use current issuance and quality-assurance tools; verify local implementation instructions              |\n| OCD/NDRRMC PSCP Guidebook (project reference; edition to be confirmed)         | Essential functions, business impact analysis, continuity strategies and recovery arrangements                                                                                                                                               | Use the actual guidebook copy as controlling technical reference; confirm edition and forms             |\n| NDRRMC/OCD contingency-planning guidance (exact edition to be confirmed)       | Scenario-based preparedness, response arrangements and plan testing                                                                                                                                                                          | CDRRMO shall obtain the controlling current manual and map its required steps before final SOP approval |\n| Sendai Framework 2015–2030; UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities     | Risk governance, understanding risk, resilience investment and city-level good practice                                                                                                                                                      | Benchmark only; does not replace Philippine law or national methodology                                 |\n\n**Source-control note:** The IAT References sheet lists some instruments as current but explicitly flags others for confirmation. Before approval, the CDRRMO Secretariat shall maintain certified copies or official links and record exact title, issuance number, date, version, issuing authority, amendments, custodian and review date. Where a source is named in the workbook but the actual operative text is not in the project file set (notably the current PSCP and contingency-planning manuals), this chapter identifies the dependency instead of attributing unverified step-by-step requirements to that source.\n\n# 3.5 Systems-Thinking and TQM Operating Principles\n\n| **Principle**               | **Required application**                                                                                                           | **Quality control**                                                                          |\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk as a system            | Assess hazard, exposure, vulnerability, capacity, land-use decisions, ecosystem functions and infrastructure dependencies together | Use impact chains and dependency maps; avoid isolated hazard maps as the sole decision basis |\n| Source provenance           | Every dataset has source agency, date, scale, coordinate reference system, method, limitations and custodian                       | Metadata completeness check; version register; traceable update history                      |\n| Decision-use orientation    | Risk analysis must inform a named decision, plan, investment, permit, preparedness action or continuity priority                   | Record the decision, decision owner, evidence considered and resulting action                |\n| Participation and inclusion | Engage affected communities and sector representatives in defining impacts, validating exposure and testing plans                  | Consultation records, disaggregated feedback and documented response to issues               |\n| PDCA / TQM                  | Plan data and analysis; do the assessment and planning; check quality and test arrangements; act on findings                       | Corrective-action register, audit trail, annual review and post-exercise update              |\n| Precaution and uncertainty  | Distinguish verified data, modelled estimates, assumptions and unknowns                                                            | State uncertainty and avoid presenting model outputs as parcel-level guarantees              |\n| No mandate transfer         | CDRRMO coordinates; competent offices validate and decide within their mandates                                                    | RACI reviewed by CLO and office heads; one accountable owner per decision                    |\n\n# 3.6 Institutional Roles and Process Ownership\n\n| **Process**                                       | **Accountable owner (proposed)**                                                       | **Responsible contributors / validators**                                   | **Approval or decision route**                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| City risk-information register and local risk map | CDRRMO Head                                                                            | Research and Planning; GIS/data custodians; source agencies; sector offices | CDRRMO administrative approval; technical sign-off by source/sector owners                                   |\n| CDRA preparation and update                       | CPDO for formal planning integration; CDRRMO for DRRM coordination                     | CDRA TWG; GIS; sector offices; barangays; competent NGAs                    | Technical validation by TWG; adoption/integration through applicable City planning and legislative processes |\n| CLUP/CDP mainstreaming                            | CPDO                                                                                   | CDRRMO, Engineering, Zoning, CENRO, sector offices, LDC                     | Formal CLUP/CDP and zoning approval processes                                                                |\n| LDRRMP and annual DRRM planning                   | CDRRMO Head / CDRRMC within their respective statutory roles                           | CPDO, departments, barangays, CSOs, private sector                          | CDRRMC approval/oversight as provided by RA 10121; local approval processes                                  |\n| Contingency plans                                 | CDRRMO Head as coordinating process owner; sector/incident lead per hazard             | Hazard technical agencies, IMT/response clusters, utilities, barangays      | CDRRMC/Mayor or designated authority according to applicable local issuance                                  |\n| PSCP                                              | City Administrator / Mayor-designated continuity executive (to be formally designated) | Department continuity coordinators; HRMO; ICT; Engineering; Budget; CDRRMO  | City executive approval; office heads accept function-specific responsibilities                              |\n| Barangay DRRM plan quality review                 | BDRRMC / Barangay Development Council within applicable law                            | CDRRMO and DILG review team; CSOs; vulnerable-sector representatives        | Barangay approval route under current law and DILG issuances                                                 |\n\nThe proposed accountable owners above are operational assignments for validation. They do not override legal authority, existing ordinances, approved organizational arrangements or the current DILG Listo si KAP framework. The City Legal Office shall confirm the precise barangay governance terminology and approval route before the final Manual is issued.\n\n# 3.7 Risk Information Governance and Authoritative Hazard Data\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a City Risk Information Register and a controlled geospatial repository. It shall not independently re-author or downgrade authoritative hazard products. Source agencies retain responsibility for their technical products; the City records provenance, communicates limitations and determines how approved information is used in local planning.\n\n| **Data class**                                                                     | **Preferred source / custodian**                                            | **Required metadata and validation**                                                                                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Tropical cyclone, rainfall, flood forecast and climate information                 | PAGASA; competent climate data providers; City engineering/drainage records | Product title, issue date, forecast/projection period, scenario, spatial resolution, units, baseline, uncertainty                        |\n| Ground shaking, fault, liquefaction, tsunami, volcanic hazards                     | DOST-PHIVOLCS / GeoRiskPH                                                   | Map/report title, scale, date, model assumptions, legend, hazard intensity, limitations and whether site-specific assessment is required |\n| Rain-induced landslide and geohazards                                              | DENR-MGB                                                                    | Map scale, susceptibility class, date, methodology, field validation status and limitations                                              |\n| Exposure: population, households, buildings, roads, facilities and economic assets | PSA/CBMS; CPDO; Assessor; Engineering; sector offices; barangays            | Reference date, geography, definitions, completeness, source, disaggregation, update frequency                                           |\n| Climate projections and ecosystem services                                         | DHSUD/CCC methodology; PAGASA; CENRO/DENR; City Agriculture                 | Emissions/scenario assumptions, time horizon, baseline, downscaling and uncertainty                                                      |\n| Critical infrastructure and lifelines                                              | Asset-owning departments, utilities, NGAs and facility operators            | Owner/operator, coordinates, capacity, condition, service area, dependencies, redundancy, restoration time and contact                   |\n\nNo single hazard portal or map layer should be treated as a complete site-specific engineering determination. Where a decision requires parcel-level, geotechnical, structural or other specialist assessment, the City shall obtain the assessment from the competent agency or licensed professional under the applicable regulatory process.\n\n# 3.8 Hazard Information Acquisition, Validation and Mapping\n\nThe mapping process is controlled by source provenance and fitness-for-purpose. The CDRRMO GIS/risk-information custodian shall maintain the layer catalogue; the originating agency remains the technical source. A map shall not be published as an official City planning layer until its source, date, scale, coordinate system, legend, geographic coverage and limitations have been checked and the relevant technical office has signed the validation record.\n\n1.  Register the request and define the decision purpose, hazard, geographic extent, map scale and intended users. Reject requests that do not specify whether the output is for screening, planning, public information or site-specific technical review.\n\n2.  Acquire the current official dataset from the responsible agency or its designated portal. Retain the original download, accompanying metadata, readme, legends, technical notes and correspondence; record access date and version.\n\n3.  Check completeness and integrity: file opens; geometry is valid; attributes and classes are present; units and coordinate reference system are known; coverage includes the required City/barangay extent; and metadata identifies scale and date.\n\n4.  Compare the product with other authoritative layers and local evidence without averaging or arbitrarily choosing conflicting values. Log discrepancies, identify which agency owns the disputed technical interpretation, and request clarification or a revised product.\n\n5.  Transform or reproject a working copy only when necessary, document the original and target coordinate reference systems, transformation method and software/version, and preserve the unmodified source file.\n\n6.  Overlay the hazard layer with the authoritative barangay boundary, exposure units and critical assets. Record the spatial operation, date, data versions, treatment of no-data areas and any assumptions.\n\n7.  Submit the draft map to the relevant technical office and source agency for validation where needed. The review must check scale suitability, legend, interpretation, local anomalies, uncertainty and whether the layer is suitable for the stated decision.\n\n8.  Approve the map for a defined use category: internal screening, public information, CLUP/CDP planning layer, or regulatory use. Regulatory use requires the appropriate CPDO/Zoning/Engineering/legal and formal adoption route.\n\n9.  Publish the approved version with map title, date, source, scale, legend, limitations, contact and version number. Archive superseded versions and notify users when a material revision occurs.\n\n**Decision control**: If source data conflict, are outdated, have inadequate scale, or leave an unmapped area, the map custodian shall mark the limitation and refer the issue to the source agency and relevant technical office. The CDRRMO shall not fill a gap by drawing an unverified hazard boundary. The output is a controlled map package: source files, metadata, validation record, map layout, approval/use classification, publication record and revision entry.\n\n# 3.9 Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA)\n\nOrmoc shall conduct and update its CDRA using the applicable DHSUD Enhanced CDRA Policy and current official CDRA training and technical materials. The official DHSUD learning material describes a 5-Step+ approach: (1) collect and organize climate-change and hazard information; (2) scope potential impacts; (3) develop an exposure database; (4) conduct the CDRA; and (5) summarize findings, followed by compilation, CLUP mainstreaming, monitoring and evaluation, and communication planning. The City shall use the current prescribed tools, definitions and technical parameters rather than replacing them with an internally invented scoring model.\n\n| **Method stage**                                        | **Required substantive work**                                                                                                                                                                                                 | **Required output / quality gate**                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Collect and organize hazard and climate information | Identify priority hazards and climate stressors; acquire authoritative current and future hazard/climate information; document spatial scale, temporal horizon, scenario, baseline and limitations                            | Hazard/climate information inventory; metadata; agreed assessment scope; gap register                            |\n| 2\\. Scope potential impacts                             | For each hazard/climate stressor, identify affected sectors, elements at risk, impact pathways, spatial extent, relevant time horizon and local context; consult technical offices and communities                            | Hazard-impact scoping matrix and documented impact chains                                                        |\n| 3\\. Develop exposure database                           | Build georeferenced exposure units for population, households, buildings, infrastructure, critical facilities, livelihoods, agriculture, ecosystems and relevant cultural assets; standardize identifiers and definitions     | Validated exposure geodatabase and attribute dictionary; completeness and privacy checks                         |\n| 4\\. Conduct CDRA                                        | Apply the official method for vulnerability/sensitivity, adaptive capacity and disaster risk assessment; use the prescribed hazard-specific parameters and available climate projections; retain calculations and assumptions | Hazard/sector risk assessment tables, maps, calculation files, uncertainty statement and validation record       |\n| 5\\. Summarize findings and MDAs                         | Synthesize risk levels and identify Major Decision Areas and corresponding policy interventions; connect findings to land-use, development, infrastructure, social protection and ecosystem decisions                         | MDA register with spatial reference, evidence, affected elements, policy intervention, lead office and indicator |\n| 6\\. Compile results                                     | Prepare the CDRA report, map atlas, geodatabase, technical annexes, consultation records, data dictionary and executive summary                                                                                               | Complete, quality-assured CDRA package                                                                           |\n| 7\\. Mainstream in CLUP and development planning         | Translate risk findings and MDAs into land-use policies, zoning overlays, development controls, programs, projects and investment priorities through CPDO-led processes                                                       | CDRA-to-CLUP/CDP crosswalk; draft policy and zoning amendments; investment tags                                  |\n| 8\\. Monitor and evaluate                                | Define indicators, data owners, update frequency and triggers for reassessment; monitor hazard, exposure, vulnerability and implementation changes                                                                            | CDRA M&E matrix and annual update record                                                                         |\n| 9\\. Communicate                                         | Prepare audience-specific products for decision-makers, barangays, communities, sector groups and the public; explain uncertainty and limitations                                                                             | Communication plan, accessible maps/briefs and consultation feedback                                             |\n\nOrmoc-specific completion requirements. The existing CDRA is reported in the IAT as covering flood, rain-induced landslides and storm surge. The workbook identifies earthquake, liquefaction and tsunami assessment as outstanding. The City shall confirm whether additional hazards (including tropical cyclone wind/rain impacts, drought/heat, technological/industrial and geothermal-related scenarios) are required by the updated risk profile and official methodology. It shall also correct or verify climate-projection tables carrying Naval/Biliran labels before any such table is reused. No numerical exposure or loss figure from the earlier report should be republished until reconciled against the source table, geographic coverage and current master datasets.\n\n# 3.10 Exposure, Vulnerability, Loss and Impact-chain Analysis\n\nThe exposure database shall identify what is located in hazard-affected areas; vulnerability analysis shall explain why and how exposed elements may be harmed; capacity analysis shall identify resources and adaptive capabilities that reduce impacts. The analysis must not treat exposure as synonymous with risk. It must include direct and, where data permit, indirect and cascading impacts.\n\n| **Dimension**                       | **Minimum analytical content for Ormoc**                                                                                                                                                                 | **Primary data owners / contributors**                                |\n|-------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Population and households           | Population by barangay and relevant sex, age, disability, pregnancy/lactation, child, older-person and Indigenous Peoples'' variables where lawfully collected; household location and access constraints | CPDO/CBMS custodian, PSA, CSWDO, City Health, barangays               |\n| Housing and buildings               | Building use, occupancy, construction/material, floor level where relevant, condition, tenure/informal settlement and critical building function                                                         | Assessor, Engineering, OBO, CPDO, barangays                           |\n| Livelihoods and economic activity   | Employment, income sources, market access, business interruption, agriculture/fisheries, tourism and informal livelihoods                                                                                | City Agriculture, market/economic offices, DTI, CPDO, business groups |\n| Infrastructure and services         | Roads, bridges, ports, power, water, wastewater, telecommunications, fuel, hospitals, schools, evacuation centres and EOC                                                                                | Asset-owning departments, utilities, NGAs                             |\n| Ecosystems and natural capital      | Watersheds, forests, mangroves, coastal ecosystems, wetlands, critical habitats and ecosystem services that reduce hazard impacts                                                                        | CENRO/DENR, City Agriculture, CPDO                                    |\n| Cultural heritage and social assets | Heritage overlay assets, culturally significant sites, community facilities and displacement impacts                                                                                                     | CPDO, culture/tourism offices, barangays, IP representatives          |\n| Vulnerability and capacity          | Physical fragility, access, poverty, social exclusion, service access, coping capacity, preparedness and adaptive capacity                                                                               | Sector offices, CSOs, barangays, community validation                 |\n\nPersonal and sensitive personal information shall not be placed in public-facing GIS layers or general-purpose map exports. Use aggregated or appropriately de-identified information for planning and publication. Household-level targeting data must be held by authorized custodians under a documented data-sharing and access-control arrangement consistent with RA 10173.\n\n# 3.11 Critical Facilities, Lifelines and Cascading-Failure Analysis\n\nThe existing CDRA facility and lifeline inventory is the starting point, not the completed dependency analysis. The IAT identifies that facilities and lifelines have been listed but interdependencies and failure chains have not yet been mapped or used to set protection and restoration priorities.\n\n10. Confirm the asset universe and unique asset identifier with each owner/operator. At minimum include EOC, hospitals, health centres, evacuation centres, schools used for authorized emergency functions, bridges, major roads, port, water supply, wastewater, power, telecommunications, fuel and key government facilities.\n\n11. For each asset, record location, owner/operator, service function, capacity, service population/area, operating hours, condition, hazard exposure, vulnerability, backup arrangements, access routes, critical inputs and restoration dependencies.\n\n12. Identify upstream and downstream dependencies. Examples to test—not presume—include power loss affecting water pumping, telecommunications and hospital operations; road/bridge disruption isolating evacuation centres; fuel disruption affecting generators, ambulances and water delivery; and EOC communications failure affecting coordination.\n\n13. Construct directed dependency maps and scenario-based failure chains. For each chain, identify initiating hazard, asset failure, service consequence, affected population, duration, cascading assets and alternative arrangements.\n\n14. Validate each dependency with the asset owner/operator and affected service providers. Record disputed or unknown links as data gaps; do not convert assumptions into confirmed dependencies.\n\n15. Prioritize risk treatment and continuity options using consequence, likelihood, service criticality, redundancy, restoration time and vulnerable-population impact. Use approved criteria and document any local weighting adopted.\n\n16. Integrate priority actions into asset management, capital investment, contingency plans and PSCP. Maintain a restoration-priority list with accountable owner, target, funding route and evidence of completion.\n\nThe output shall be a Critical Facilities and Lifelines Register, dependency diagram, failure-chain scenario sheets, and a protection/restoration priority register. Asset owners remain accountable for the accuracy and operation of their assets.\n\n# 3.12 Risk-Informed Planning and Investment Mainstreaming\n\nThe IAT identifies the CLUP 2017–2027 as predating the CDRA and notes that the CDRA results have not been demonstrably translated into current zoning overlays or a formal project/budget screening step. This chapter therefore establishes an interface, not a transfer of CPDO or Budget Office mandates.\n\n17. CPDO convenes a planning integration review using the approved CDRA package, MDA register, hazard maps, exposure database, critical-facility dependencies and barangay validation.\n\n18. Each MDA is mapped to the relevant CLUP land-use policy, zoning provision, CDP outcome, LCCAP action, ELA priority, LDIP/AIP proposal and responsible implementing office.\n\n19. Where a hazard or climate risk affects land-use suitability, CPDO and the Zoning Administrator lead the assessment of land-use policy and zoning options; Engineering/OBO and other regulators determine applicable technical standards and permit conditions.\n\n20. Before an investment proposal is prioritized, the originating office completes the DRR/CCA screening record. It identifies hazard exposure, climate sensitivity, affected people/assets, design and siting implications, ecosystem effects, residual risk and proposed risk treatment.\n\n21. CPDO reviews consistency with plans and spatial policy; Engineering/OBO/Zoning review technical and regulatory matters; CDRRMO provides risk evidence and DRRM advice; Budget/LFC assess fiscal and budget requirements; the competent approving body decides.\n\n22. Record screening outcomes in the project appraisal and LDC/LFC minutes. Proposals with unresolved material risk shall be returned for redesign, specialist assessment or documented decision by the competent authority; the CDRRMO does not independently veto statutory approvals.\n\n23. Monitor whether adopted measures are implemented and whether risk exposure is reduced. Feed permit, project and loss information back into the next CDRA update.\n\n| **Screening question**                                            | **Minimum evidence to attach**                                                                       | **Review lead**                         |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| Is the site or service exposed to current or future hazards?      | Official hazard overlay, map date/scale, climate scenario/time horizon                               | CPDO with CDRRMO technical input        |\n| Could the project increase exposure or vulnerability elsewhere?   | Site alternatives, drainage/downstream effects, access and displacement analysis                     | CPDO / Engineering / CENRO              |\n| Are design, siting and operational standards adequate?            | Applicable code, engineering/geotechnical assessment, service continuity and residual-risk statement | Engineering / OBO / competent regulator |\n| Are vulnerable groups disproportionately affected?                | Disaggregated impact analysis, accessible consultation and mitigation measures                       | CSWDO / GAD / sector offices            |\n| Does the project affect protective ecosystems or cultural assets? | Ecosystem service and heritage screening; permits/clearances as applicable                           | CENRO / DENR / CPDO                     |\n| Is the risk treatment funded and assigned?                        | Costed mitigation, responsible office, implementation schedule and monitoring indicator              | Originating office / Budget / LFC       |\n\n# 3.13 Contingency planning and exercise programme\n\nContingency planning is a management process for analyzing specific potential events or emerging situations and establishing arrangements in advance for timely, effective and appropriate response. RA 10121 requires the LDRRMO to facilitate and support local contingency planning; the LDRRMC must regularly review and test the LDRRMP. The detailed hazard-specific methodology must be checked against the current OCD/NDRRMC contingency-planning guide held by the City. This chapter sets the minimum City control architecture and does not claim that the sequence below reproduces an unverified manual verbatim.\n\n| **Plan component**               | **Required content**                                                                                                                                   |\n|----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Purpose and scope                | Hazard, geographic coverage, planning assumptions, plan owner, version, validity and relationship to LDRRMP/EOC/ICS                                    |\n| Risk scenario                    | Most-probable and credible severe scenario, source evidence, time/place, onset, magnitude, cascading effects and uncertainty                           |\n| Planning assumptions             | Forecast/monitoring basis, warning lead time, access, population, service and resource assumptions; assumptions requiring confirmation                 |\n| Objectives and priorities        | Life safety, evacuation, protection of critical assets, continuity of essential services, protection of vulnerable groups and environmental safeguards |\n| Concept of operations            | Preparedness, activation, response, stabilization and transition; coordination with incident command and EOC; no displacement of field command         |\n| Roles and coordination           | Lead/support agencies, incident command/IMT interface, cluster/sector responsibilities, barangay roles and escalation routes                           |\n| Triggers and decision points     | Authoritative warning/advisory thresholds and locally approved action triggers; source and authority for each trigger; avoid invented thresholds       |\n| Operational arrangements         | Warning, evacuation, shelter/CCCM, search and rescue, health, protection, logistics, public information, RDANA and service restoration                 |\n| Resources and gaps               | Personnel, equipment, supplies, facilities, transport, communications, suppliers, prepositioning, mutual aid and request process                       |\n| Information and reporting        | Situation reports, common operating picture, decision log, data validation, public information approval and reporting frequency                        |\n| Safety, inclusion and protection | Accessible warning, safe evacuation, child protection, gender and protection safeguards, PWD access, older persons, IP and MHPSS                       |\n| Exercise, review and update      | Tabletop/functional/full-scale exercise objectives, evaluation, corrective action owner, deadline and plan revision                                    |\n\nPriority hazard portfolio for validation. The IAT names flood/flash flood, landslide, earthquake/tsunami, tropical cyclone and industrial/geothermal hazards. The City shall confirm priority and scenario scope using the current CDRA, official agency products, historical impact records and sector risk. A single generic all-hazards plan is not a substitute for hazard-specific operational annexes where triggers, warning lead times, response tactics or technical agencies differ.\n\nExercise control: Each approved plan shall have an exercise schedule and documented test. At minimum, the CDRRMO shall ensure that the plan is walked through with assigned agencies and barangays, that contact and resource arrangements are checked, and that decisions and gaps are recorded. Exercise type and frequency shall be set in the approved annual programme and applicable national guidance. A plan is not “tested” merely because a meeting or orientation occurred.\n\n# 3.14 Public Service Continuity Planning (PSCP)\n\nThe IAT records the PSCP as not completed and no business impact analysis or essential-functions list sighted. The City shall therefore treat PSCP as a priority institutional deliverable. The PSCP shall address continuity of essential City functions during disruption, including the CDRRMO/EOC and services whose interruption may create cascading impacts on life safety and public welfare.\n\n| **PSCP element**               | **Required procedure and output**                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Governance and scope           | Mayor/City Administrator designates continuity executive, department continuity coordinators, plan custodian and approval route; define covered offices and essential services                    |\n| Essential-function inventory   | Each department identifies legal mandate, service users, minimum service level, process owner, key staff, systems, facilities, records, suppliers and interdependencies                           |\n| Business impact analysis (BIA) | Assess disruption consequences over time; identify maximum tolerable disruption and recovery priorities using an approved City method; distinguish statutory deadlines from service-level choices |\n| Dependency analysis            | Map staff, ICT, power, water, telecom, transport, facilities, records, suppliers, fuel and inter-office dependencies; validate with owners                                                        |\n| Continuity strategies          | Identify alternate work locations, remote/manual procedures, backup power/communications, data backup, alternate suppliers, mutual aid and staff succession                                       |\n| Plan development               | Define activation authority, notification, relocation, service prioritization, internal/external communication, resource control and deactivation                                                 |\n| Testing and maintenance        | Exercise continuity scenarios, document performance, correct gaps, review after organizational/technology changes and update contact lists                                                        |\n\nThe City shall not invent universal recovery time objectives without a BIA. Each department shall propose service-specific priorities and tolerable disruption periods based on statutory obligations, life-safety consequences, affected population and operational dependencies; the designated continuity executive shall review and approve the city-wide prioritization framework. The PSCP shall link with Chapter 6 EOC alternate-site and communications arrangements but shall not make the EOC the sole continuity solution for all City services.\n\n# 3.15 Barangay DRRM planning and quality assurance\n\nRA 10121 establishes a BDRRMC in every barangay and assigns local DRRM planning and coordination functions. The IAT refers to 85 barangays, while the CLUP and other project records refer to 110. The Manual shall not settle this discrepancy by assumption. CPDO, the City Secretary and DILG City Local Government Operations Office shall reconcile the authoritative current barangay master list and document the basis before the coverage denominator is used in official reporting.\n\n24. Maintain one master register of barangays with official name, geographic identifier, legal status, boundary reference, population reference year and responsible BDRRMC contact.\n\n25. Request the current BDRRMP and approval evidence from each barangay using the current DILG/OCD-prescribed template and review tool. Record the document version, coverage period, approval date and evidence source.\n\n26. Conduct a completeness and quality review covering risk profile, priority hazards, exposed and vulnerable groups, early warning, evacuation, response arrangements, resources, annual work programme, budget, inclusion and monitoring.\n\n27. Return each plan with specific, actionable comments linked to the applicable quality criteria; identify mandatory corrections separately from recommended improvements.\n\n28. Verify that comments have been addressed and record the disposition. A submitted plan is not counted as quality-assured merely because it has been received.\n\n29. Consolidate status by barangay: not submitted, submitted, under review, returned for revision, technically accepted, approved, tested, and due for update.\n\n30. Provide technical assistance and schedule plan-testing support. Link barangay plans with City hazard scenarios, evacuation arrangements, warning protocols and resource requests without removing barangay ownership.\n\n31. Report gaps and overdue actions to the CDRRMC and DILG through the applicable channel; maintain a follow-up register.\n\n# 3.16 Inclusive DRRM and ecosystem-based risk reduction\n\nInclusion is a design requirement across the risk-information and preparedness cycle, not a stand-alone consultation event. Data collection must be lawful and proportionate; consultation must be accessible; and the analysis must show how different groups experience exposure, sensitivity, barriers and capacities.\n\n| **Area**                               | **Minimum operating requirement**                                                                                                         | **Evidence**                                                                        |\n|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Gender and women                       | Analyze differentiated impacts, care responsibilities, safety, livelihoods and participation; integrate GAD planning and budgeting        | Disaggregated analysis, consultation records, GAD-tagged actions                    |\n| Children                               | Apply child-sensitive risk analysis, protection, education continuity and safe evacuation arrangements                                    | Child-focused impact analysis, CSWDO/DepEd validation, child protection measures    |\n| Persons with disabilities              | Identify functional/access requirements, accessible warning, transport, shelter and communication needs with consent and privacy controls | PWD organization consultation, accessible plan/map formats, facility access review  |\n| Older persons                          | Assess mobility, health, medication, support networks and accessible service arrangements                                                 | Sector validation, evacuation and continuity arrangements                           |\n| Indigenous Peoples                     | Respect IPRA rights, cultural protocols, Indigenous Knowledge Systems and Practices, and meaningful participation                         | Consultation record, appropriate consent/process, culturally relevant risk analysis |\n| Pregnant/lactating persons and infants | Include maternal, newborn and infant feeding needs in evacuation, health and continuity planning                                          | Health-office validation and service protocols                                      |\n| Ecosystems                             | Treat watershed, upland forest, mangroves, wetlands and coastal ecosystems as risk-reduction assets and sources of ecosystem services     | Ecosystem inventory, condition monitoring, restoration/protection actions           |\n\nThe IAT identifies the upper Anilao–Malbasag watershed, upland forest cover and coastal mangroves as important protective ecosystems. The assessment notes that these are identified in plans but are not yet monitored consistently as risk-reduction assets. CENRO/DENR and relevant offices shall define measurable condition indicators and a monitoring programme; the CDRRMO shall incorporate relevant findings into risk analysis and planning, not assume responsibility for environmental regulation.\n\n# 3.17 Process workflows and decision controls\n\nThe workflows below are operational control procedures derived from the legal mandate, the official CDRA sequence and the City''s documented assessment needs. They identify actors, actions, decision gates and records. Where an external manual contains a more specific mandatory step, the controlling manual shall prevail and the local SOP shall be revised to reflect it.\n\n## SOP 3-01 \\| Hazard data acquisition, validation and map release\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO Research and Planning / GIS custodian. Purpose: Obtain authoritative, current and fit-for-purpose hazard information and control its use.\n\n| **Step / control point**     | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                                                              | **Mandatory record / output**                      |\n|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n| Open and scope request       | CDRRMO records requester, purpose, hazard, geography, scale, deadline and intended decision. Return incomplete requests for clarification.                                                         | Risk-information request and tracking number       |\n| Acquire source product       | GIS custodian obtains current product from source agency/official portal; preserves original file, metadata, legend, technical note and access date.                                               | Source dataset and acquisition log                 |\n| Check metadata and integrity | Check date, scale, CRS, units, geometry, attributes, legend, geographic coverage, no-data areas, model assumptions and limitations.                                                                | Completed metadata/quality checklist               |\n| Resolve discrepancies        | Compare authoritative sources and local evidence; record differences; refer technical interpretation to source agency and competent sector office; do not average or redraw uncertain boundaries.  | Discrepancy log and source-agency response         |\n| Prepare controlled overlay   | Use approved barangay boundaries and exposure layers; document reprojection, spatial operation, data versions and processing software.                                                             | Working GIS layer and processing record            |\n| Technical validation         | Relevant technical office checks interpretation and fitness for purpose; source agency consulted for material uncertainty; CPDO/Zoning/Engineering review if intended for planning/regulatory use. | Signed technical validation                        |\n| Classify and approve use     | Assign use category (internal screening, public information, planning layer, regulatory layer); route regulatory use through formal adoption process.                                              | Approval/use classification record                 |\n| Publish and maintain         | Publish map with date, source, scale, legend, limitations and version; archive superseded version and notify users of material changes.                                                            | Released map, publication log and version register |\n\n## SOP 3-02 \\| Risk information and geospatial repository maintenance\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO Head / designated data custodian. Purpose: Maintain a controlled, auditable city risk information base.\n\n| **Step / control point**   | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                   | **Mandatory record / output**           |\n|----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| Register datasets          | Assign dataset ID, title, source owner, custodian, date, geography, scale, CRS, update cycle, sensitivity and permitted use.                            | Risk Information Register               |\n| Apply data governance      | Classify public, internal, confidential or personal/sensitive data; establish access rights, lawful purpose, retention and sharing conditions.          | Data classification and access register |\n| Validate updates           | Compare incoming version with existing data; identify changed attributes/geometries, missing records and implications for previously issued maps/plans. | Change log and validation note          |\n| Release controlled version | Assign version, approval status and effective date; preserve source file and processing history; distribute only approved products.                     | Versioned dataset and distribution log  |\n| Review and archive         | Review at the defined cycle and after major events, agency revisions or boundary/asset changes; archive superseded versions and record reason.          | Review record and archive index         |\n\n## SOP 3-03 \\| City Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment\n\nProcess owner: CPDO-led CDRA process; CDRRMO coordinates DRRM risk evidence. Purpose: Produce a method-compliant CDRA that supports CLUP and development decisions.\n\n| **Step / control point**                  | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                                | **Mandatory record / output**                              |\n|-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Approve assessment protocol               | CPDO and CDRRMO define scope, planning horizon, hazards, sectors, geography, technical team, data needs, prescribed DHSUD tools and consultation plan.               | Approved CDRA inception/protocol                           |\n| Collect and organize information (Step 1) | Acquire authoritative hazard and climate data; document baseline, scenarios, time horizons, resolution, metadata and limitations; identify data gaps.                | Hazard/climate inventory and data-gap register             |\n| Scope potential impacts (Step 2)          | For each hazard/stressor, identify potential impacts by sector and element at risk; construct impact chains; validate with sector offices and communities.           | Hazard-impact scoping matrix                               |\n| Develop exposure database (Step 3)        | Build georeferenced exposure units; standardize IDs and attributes; validate spatial joins, completeness, reference dates and privacy controls.                      | Exposure geodatabase and data dictionary                   |\n| Conduct risk assessment (Step 4)          | Apply current DHSUD prescribed vulnerability and risk methods, parameters and tools; retain calculations, assumptions and uncertainty; have sector experts validate. | Risk tables, maps, calculation files and validation record |\n| Summarize findings and MDAs (Step 5)      | Synthesize risk results into Major Decision Areas and policy interventions; identify affected places, groups, systems and responsible planning decisions.            | MDA register and policy-intervention matrix                |\n| Compile results (Step 6)                  | Prepare report, map atlas, geodatabase, technical annexes, data sources, consultation records and executive summary; correct inconsistencies before sign-off.        | Complete CDRA package                                      |\n| Mainstream into CLUP (Step 7)             | CPDO translates MDAs into spatial policies, land-use suitability, overlays, zoning and development control proposals through formal planning process.                | CDRA-to-CLUP crosswalk and proposed amendments             |\n| Monitor and evaluate (Step 8)             | Assign indicators, data owners and review frequency; update after material hazard, exposure, vulnerability, climate or land-use change.                              | CDRA M&E matrix and update log                             |\n| Communicate (Step 9)                      | Prepare accessible, audience-specific risk communication and explain uncertainty, implications and action choices.                                                   | CDRA communication products and feedback record            |\n\n## SOP 3-04 \\| Critical facilities and dependency/failure-chain analysis\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO coordinates; asset owners are accountable for asset data. Purpose: Identify critical service dependencies and inform protection and restoration priorities.\n\n| **Step / control point** | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                         | **Mandatory record / output**              |\n|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| Define asset scope       | Confirm critical services and asset categories with department heads, utilities and NGAs; assign asset owner and unique ID.                                   | Approved asset scope and owner list        |\n| Inventory assets         | Record coordinates, function, capacity, condition, hazard exposure, service population, operator and alternate arrangements.                                  | Critical Facilities and Lifelines Register |\n| Map dependencies         | Identify required inputs, upstream services, downstream users, access routes, communications, fuel, staff, ICT and utilities.                                 | Dependency matrix and network diagram      |\n| Develop failure chains   | For priority scenarios, trace initiating hazard through asset failure to service disruption and population consequence; mark unverified links as assumptions. | Failure-chain scenario sheets              |\n| Validate with operators  | Conduct structured validation with asset owners and service providers; correct errors and document unknowns.                                                  | Signed validation records                  |\n| Prioritize treatments    | Apply approved criteria to consequence, likelihood, criticality, redundancy, recovery time and vulnerable-group effects; document rationale.                  | Protection/restoration priority register   |\n| Integrate and monitor    | Transfer actions to PSCP, contingency plans, asset plans and AIP/LDIP; monitor completion and service resilience.                                             | Action register and monitoring report      |\n\n## SOP 3-05 \\| LDRRMP and DRR/CCA mainstreaming interface\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO coordinates LDRRMP; CPDO leads development-plan integration. Purpose: Ensure risk evidence is converted into plans, programmes and budget decisions.\n\n| **Step / control point**                | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                         | **Mandatory record / output**                           |\n|-----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\n| Compile planning evidence               | CDRRMO provides approved CDRA, maps, MDA register, contingency findings, losses, ecosystem and dependency analysis.                           | Planning evidence pack                                  |\n| Convene planning review                 | CPDO convenes LDC and sector offices; review existing CLUP/CDP/LCCAP/ELA/LDIP/AIP and identify policy/programme gaps.                         | Planning review minutes and gap matrix                  |\n| Set objectives and interventions        | Translate risk findings into prevention, mitigation, preparedness and adaptation outcomes; assign measurable outputs and responsible offices. | Results framework and intervention matrix               |\n| Screen spatial and investment proposals | Apply DRR/CCA screening to proposed sites, designs and service models; route technical/regulatory questions to competent offices.             | Completed screening form and technical comments         |\n| Prioritize and resource                 | CPDO/LDC and Budget/LFC use applicable planning and budget processes to prioritize viable, costed interventions.                              | Prioritized project list and budget records             |\n| Approve, implement and monitor          | Submit plans and proposals through statutory approval routes; track outputs, outcomes, risk reduction and corrective action.                  | Approved plans, implementation reports and M&E register |\n\n## SOP 3-06 \\| Hazard-specific contingency plan development and control\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO as coordinating process owner; hazard/sector leads as technical owners. Purpose: Prepare and maintain tested plans for priority hazard scenarios.\n\n| **Step / control point**                       | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                          | **Mandatory record / output**                          |\n|------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|\n| Select priority scenario                       | Use current CDRA, authoritative hazard information, historical impacts and sector risk; record why the hazard/scenario is prioritized.                         | Scenario selection note                                |\n| Confirm planning team and source guidance      | Designate lead/support offices, barangay and community participants; obtain current OCD/NDRRMC contingency-planning manual and applicable sector protocols.    | Team designation and source register                   |\n| Build scenario and assumptions                 | Define plausible scenario, location, magnitude, timing, warning lead time, exposed population, cascading impacts and uncertainty using authoritative evidence. | Scenario and assumptions sheet                         |\n| Set objectives and concept of operations       | Define operational priorities, phases, coordination, field command/EOC interface, escalation and transition to recovery.                                       | Concept of operations                                  |\n| Assign tasks and resources                     | Specify lead/support agency, task, trigger, resource, communication, reporting and request route; validate contacts and capacity.                              | Task matrix, resource gap and contact registers        |\n| Complete protection and inclusion arrangements | Set warning accessibility, evacuation, shelter/CCCM, child protection, health, MHPSS, disability access and environmental safeguards.                          | Protection/inclusion checklist and operational annexes |\n| Validate and approve                           | Conduct technical workshop/tabletop; resolve contradictions; obtain approval through the City route established by current issuance.                           | Validation minutes and approved plan                   |\n| Exercise and correct                           | Conduct scheduled exercise; evaluate objectives and decision points; assign corrective action owner and deadline; update plan version.                         | Exercise report and corrective-action register         |\n| Review and maintain                            | Review after exercise, event, material change in hazard data, staffing, resource or contact arrangements and at the approved periodic interval.                | Annual review and revision history                     |\n\n## SOP 3-07 \\| Public Service Continuity Planning\n\nProcess owner: City continuity executive to be designated; department heads own service continuity. Purpose: Protect essential City functions during disruption.\n\n| **Step / control point**         | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                               | **Mandatory record / output**                 |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| Establish governance             | Mayor/City Administrator designates continuity executive, coordinators, plan custodian, activation authority and approval route.                                    | Designation/authority record                  |\n| Inventory essential functions    | Each department identifies services, legal basis, service users, minimum service level, process owner, staff, records, facilities, ICT and suppliers.               | Essential Functions Inventory                 |\n| Conduct BIA                      | Assess disruption impacts over time, statutory deadlines, life-safety and welfare consequences, dependencies and tolerable disruption; justify recovery priorities. | BIA worksheet and validated priority schedule |\n| Analyze dependencies and risks   | Map staff, power, water, telecom, ICT, facilities, transport, records, suppliers, fuel and inter-office dependencies; test single points of failure.                | Dependency and continuity risk register       |\n| Select continuity strategies     | Specify alternate site/work arrangements, manual workarounds, backup systems, staff succession, alternate suppliers, mutual aid and communication methods.          | Continuity strategy matrix                    |\n| Prepare departmental plans       | Set activation, notification, relocation, service prioritization, resource allocation, external communication, record protection and deactivation procedures.       | Department continuity plans                   |\n| Integrate city-wide arrangements | Resolve cross-department dependencies; coordinate with EOC alternate-site arrangements and emergency response plans; define city-wide priorities.                   | Consolidated PSCP                             |\n| Approve and exercise             | Approve through designated executive route; test realistic disruption scenarios and evaluate service restoration.                                                   | Approved PSCP and exercise report             |\n| Review and update                | Update after exercise, incident, staff/technology/site/supplier changes and periodic review; close corrective actions.                                              | Revision log and corrective-action register   |\n\n## SOP 3-08 \\| Barangay DRRM plan review and quality assurance\n\nProcess owner: Barangay plan owner: BDRRMC/Barangay Development Council; City reviewer: CDRRMO. Purpose: Maintain a verified and quality-assured city-wide BDRRMP status system.\n\n| **Step / control point**       | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                               | **Mandatory record / output**           |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| Reconcile barangay master list | CPDO, City Secretary and DILG City LGOO validate official barangay count, names, identifiers and boundaries; record authoritative source.                           | Signed barangay master list             |\n| Call for current plans         | Issue formal request with current DILG/OCD template, quality tool, required approval evidence and submission deadline.                                              | Submission notice and receipt log       |\n| Screen completeness            | Check plan period, approval, risk profile, priority hazards, objectives, projects, budgets, roles, warning, evacuation, inclusion and monitoring.                   | Completeness checklist                  |\n| Conduct quality assessment     | Use the current official Quality Assessment System/Quality Assessment Tool and Listo si KAP guidance where applicable; do not substitute an unapproved local score. | Completed official review tool          |\n| Return and support revision    | Provide itemized comments, technical assistance and a revision due date; separate mandatory criteria from recommendations.                                          | Review letter and assistance record     |\n| Verify correction and status   | Recheck evidence and record status; do not count submission as approval or quality acceptance.                                                                      | Barangay plan status matrix             |\n| Consolidate and report         | Identify coverage gaps, recurring deficiencies, resource needs and plans requiring exercise; report to CDRRMC/DILG as applicable.                                   | Consolidated report and action register |\n| Test and update                | Coordinate scenario-based plan testing and update after material changes or exercises.                                                                              | Exercise record and plan revision log   |\n\n## SOP 3-09 \\| Inclusive risk assessment and ecosystem monitoring interface\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO coordinates; sector offices and CENRO/DENR retain technical ownership. Purpose: Embed inclusion and protective ecosystem evidence in risk intelligence and preparedness.\n\n| **Step / control point**                      | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                        | **Mandatory record / output**            |\n|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| Set participation and data protocol           | Identify relevant sector groups and ecosystem custodians; define accessible consultation, lawful data use and consent where applicable.      | Stakeholder and data protocol            |\n| Collect disaggregated evidence                | Use official and lawfully held datasets; minimize personal data; document missing groups and coverage limitations.                           | Disaggregated dataset and privacy review |\n| Validate lived experience and access barriers | Consult women, PWD organizations, older persons, youth/children through appropriate safeguards, IP representatives and affected barangays.   | Consultation record and issue log        |\n| Assess ecosystem services                     | CENRO/DENR and relevant offices identify watershed, upland forest, mangrove and coastal assets, condition indicators and monitoring method.  | Ecosystem asset and indicator register   |\n| Integrate findings                            | Translate barriers and ecosystem findings into CDRA impact chains, MDA interventions, contingency plans and service continuity arrangements. | Updated CDRA/plan crosswalk              |\n| Monitor and report                            | Review inclusion and ecosystem indicators at approved intervals; record trend, uncertainty and corrective action.                            | Annual monitoring report                 |\n\n# 3.18 Responsibility assignment (RACI)\n\nRACI clarifies work allocation; it does not transfer statutory or regulatory authority. The matrix below is a proposed operational assignment and must be validated by office heads and the City Legal Office.\n\n| **Activity**                                  | **CDRRMO**      | **CPDO/LDC**                 | **Engineering/OBO/Zoning** | **Sector offices / utilities**     | **Barangays**               | **CLO / Mayor / CDRRMC**          |\n|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------|------------------------------|----------------------------|------------------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| Maintain risk information register            | A/R             | C                            | C                          | R/C                                | C                           | I                                 |\n| Acquire and validate hazard source data       | R               | C                            | C                          | A/R for own technical data         | C                           | I                                 |\n| Prepare CDRA                                  | R/C             | A                            | C                          | R                                  | C                           | I                                 |\n| Approve/use CDRA in CLUP/CDP                  | C               | A/R                          | C                          | C                                  | C                           | Statutory approval route          |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_v0.9.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('f299153b-3799-5366-994b-8b2ad3a31799'::uuid,'77080547-c4e3-52f7-8908-02ce0fc78258'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.6)',E'**CHAPTER 3**\n\n**RISK INTELLIGENCE, DRRM AND CONTINGENCY PLANNING,  \nAND INSTITUTIONAL PREPAREDNESS**\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)  \nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n***Working Draft 1.0 \\| 8 November 2026  \nSubject to technical, institutional, planning, legal and data validation***\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Control field** | **Designation / requirement**                                                                           | **Control field**      | **Designation / Requirement**                                |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document owner    | CDRRMO Head                                                                                             | Document custodian     | CDRRMO Research and Planning function                        |\n| Technical review  | CPDO; City Engineering; CENRO; City Agriculture; City Health; CSWDO; GIS/data custodians; relevant NGAs | Legal review           | City Legal Office                                            |\n| Planning review   | CPDO / Local Development Council / relevant sector offices                                              | Data governance review | City Data Protection Officer / data custodians               |\n| Approval          | Mayor / CDRRMC / Sanggunian, as legally applicable                                                      | Effective date         | Upon valid approval and issuance                             |\n| Review cycle      | Annual; after major hazard/data/planning changes; after exercises or disasters                          | Status                 | Controlled working draft; not yet an approved City directive |\n\n## REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date**        | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | **Prepared / reviewed by**                           |\n|-------------|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| 0.9         | 31 October 2026 | Rebuilt fromChapter 1, Chapter 2, IAT v1.3, and verified official methodology sources; replaces generic workflow draft                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | For CDRRMO, CPDO, sector-office and legal validation |\n| 1.0         | 8 November 2026 | Aligned with the Chapter 1 and 2 v1.2 house style (APA figure and table captions); source findings verified against the CDRA, CLUP 2017–2027 and DRRM 10-Year Plan; SOP control block per SOP (MODE workflow, trigger, forms, IAT indicators, policy references, records); barangay denominator resolved (85, PSA PSGC); form numbering made consistent; Annex 3-N glossary; IAT v1.6 (7 Nov 2026) references | For CDRRMO, CPDO, sector-office and legal validation |\n\n# 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nThis chapter establishes the City''s operating system for understanding disaster and climate risk, translating risk evidence into land-use and development decisions, preparing for priority hazard scenarios, maintaining essential public services during disruption, and strengthening preparedness from city to barangay level. It treats risk intelligence as a continuous institutional process—not a one-time mapping or report-writing exercise.\n\n**Figure 3.1**\n\n*From Static Risk Assets to a Continuous Risk-Intelligence System*\n\n![Figure 1: 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale](/library/ch3v10/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Conceptual illustration prepared for this chapter from the IAT findings (A5.1, A5.2, A5.7).\n\nThe chapter is based on the October 2026 Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT) workbook, the governance and chapter architecture established in Chapter 1, and the process ownership and documented-control approach established in Chapter 2. The IAT is a diagnostic instrument. Its scores are not legal certification and do not independently establish that a plan, map, database or process is current, approved, complete or operational.\n\nThe assessment indicates that Ormoc has an existing CDRA, hazard information, a Local DRRM Plan and contingency-planning activity. The critical issue is the conversion of these assets into a coherent, maintained and decision-used system: complete multi-hazard coverage; technically validated exposure and vulnerability data; spatial integration into CLUP and zoning; risk screening of development proposals; tested hazard-specific contingency arrangements; and continuity arrangements for essential city services.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThe purpose of this chapter is to standardize how Ormoc City identifies, assesses, communicates, plans for and periodically reviews disaster and climate risks. It shall produce the following institutional results:\n\n- An authoritative, version-controlled city risk information base, with source provenance, scale, date, limitations and responsible custodian for each dataset.\n\n- A technically defensible CDRA that follows the applicable DHSUD methodology and is spatially explicit, multi-hazard, multi-sectoral and usable for land-use and development decisions.\n\n- An approved and monitored LDRRMP aligned with the NDRRMP and integrated with the CLUP, CDP, LCCAP, ELA, LDIP, AIP and annual budget processes through CPDO-led planning mechanisms.\n\n**Figure 3.2**\n\n*The Risk-Intelligence System of Chapter 3*\n\n![Figure 2: 2. Purpose and Intended Results](/library/ch3v10/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Inputs (risk information base), engine (assessment of hazards, exposure and lifelines), outputs (mainstreaming and preparedness) and governance.\n\n- Hazard-specific contingency plans with clear scenario assumptions, objectives, roles, triggers, resource arrangements, information flows and exercise evidence.\n\n- A Public Service Continuity Plan based on an essential-functions inventory, business impact analysis, dependencies, continuity strategies, recovery priorities and tested arrangements.\n\n- A quality-assured system for reviewing barangay DRRM plans, including a verified barangay master list and documented feedback and follow-up.\n\n- Evidence that gender, age, disability, child protection, Indigenous Peoples'' rights and other contextually relevant inclusion requirements shape risk assessment, planning and service arrangements.\n\n# 3. Scope, Boundaries and Interfaces\n\nThis chapter applies to the CDRRMO Research and Planning function and all City offices that own, generate, validate, regulate, fund or use risk information and preparedness arrangements. It covers normal operations, heightened preparedness and anticipatory action, and readiness for response and continuity. It does not transfer the statutory or technical mandates of other offices to the CDRRMO.\n\n| **Institution / office**                                                           | **Accountability retained**                                                                                                                 | **Required interface with Chapter 3**                                                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRRMC                                                                             | Policy direction, approval/oversight functions assigned by law, monitoring and evaluation of LDRRMP                                         | Review risk evidence, endorse/recommend plans and monitor implementation                                  |\n| CDRRMO                                                                             | Facilitate risk assessment and contingency planning; consolidate local risk information; maintain local risk map; coordinate LDRRMP process | Own the risk-information and DRRM planning coordination process; maintain registers and technical records |\n| CPDO / LDC                                                                         | Local development planning, CLUP/CDP preparation and integration, investment prioritization                                                 | Lead formal mainstreaming of CDRA into CLUP/CDP and investment plans; convene planning review             |\n| Engineering / OBO / Zoning                                                         | Engineering, building-permit, locational-clearance, zoning and code decisions under their mandates                                          | Apply adopted hazard overlays, technical standards and permit conditions; retain regulatory decisions     |\n| CENRO / DENR / City Agriculture / sector offices                                   | Environmental, ecosystem, agriculture, health, social welfare, education and infrastructure mandates                                        | Provide authoritative sector data, impact analysis, service dependencies and technical validation         |\n| Budget / LFC / BAC / Accounting                                                    | Budget, financial management, procurement and accounting controls                                                                           | Apply approved risk-screening and budget controls; ensure proposals are fiscally and procedurally valid   |\n| HRMO / department heads                                                            | Personnel administration and office continuity responsibilities                                                                             | Identify critical functions, alternates, staffing, succession and employee welfare arrangements           |\n| Barangays / BDRRMCs                                                                | Barangay-level DRRM governance and plan implementation                                                                                      | Prepare and test BDRRMPs; validate local exposure, vulnerable households and evacuation information       |\n| OCD Region VIII / PAGASA / PHIVOLCS / MGB / GeoRiskPH and other competent agencies | National/regional mandates and authoritative technical products                                                                             | Provide source data, advisories, technical guidance and review as applicable                              |\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Methodological Framework\n\n| **Instrument / methodology**                                                   | **Operational relevance to this chapter**                                                                                                                                                                                                    | **Application / control**                                                                               |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA 10121 (2010), especially Sections 11–12                                     | LDRRMC oversight; LDRRMO functions include facilitating risk assessment and contingency planning, consolidating local risk information and maintaining a local risk map, and formulating an integrated LDRRMP in close coordination with LDC | Primary statutory basis; apply with its IRR and current official guidance                               |\n| RA 7160 (1991)                                                                 | Local planning, general welfare, local development and land-use planning powers                                                                                                                                                              | CPDO and competent regulatory offices retain their mandates                                             |\n| RA 9729, as amended by RA 10174                                                | Climate change adaptation and mainstreaming into local development                                                                                                                                                                           | Use climate projections and adaptation analysis within CDRA and plans                                   |\n| DHSUD MC No. 2025-004, Enhanced CDRA Policy                                    | Policy direction for standardized, updated, multi-hazard, multi-sectoral and spatially explicit CDRA                                                                                                                                         | Obtain and retain signed circular and annexes; follow current DHSUD process                             |\n| DHSUD CDRA training materials / 5-Step+ process                                | Hazard and climate information; impact scoping; exposure database; CDRA; MDA synthesis; compilation; CLUP mainstreaming; M&E; communication                                                                                                  | Methodological sequence for SOP 3-03; check technical manuals and prescribed tools                      |\n| RA 10173 and applicable NPC issuances                                          | Processing of personal and sensitive personal information                                                                                                                                                                                    | Apply purpose limitation, lawful basis, minimization, access control, retention and security            |\n| RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 9994; RA 10821; RA 8371; RA 11106              | Gender, disability, older persons, children, Indigenous Peoples and Filipino Sign Language inclusion                                                                                                                                         | Apply to data disaggregation, consultations, warnings, evacuation and continuity                        |\n| RA 12076 and its IRR                                                           | Permanent evacuation centre standards                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Coordinate facility planning and compliance; do not treat all buildings as suitable evacuation centres  |\n| DILG MC No. 2025-035 (Listo si KAP), Operation L!sto and current DILG guidance | Barangay preparedness and plan implementation                                                                                                                                                                                                | Use current issuance and quality-assurance tools; verify local implementation instructions              |\n| OCD/NDRRMC PSCP Guidebook (project reference; edition to be confirmed)         | Essential functions, business impact analysis, continuity strategies and recovery arrangements                                                                                                                                               | Use the actual guidebook copy as controlling technical reference; confirm edition and forms             |\n| NDRRMC/OCD contingency-planning guidance (exact edition to be confirmed)       | Scenario-based preparedness, response arrangements and plan testing                                                                                                                                                                          | CDRRMO shall obtain the controlling current manual and map its required steps before final SOP approval |\n| Sendai Framework 2015–2030; UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities     | Risk governance, understanding risk, resilience investment and city-level good practice                                                                                                                                                      | Benchmark only; does not replace Philippine law or national methodology                                 |\n\n**Source-control note (verified 8 November 2026).** The City supplied the Ormoc CDRA report and the Comprehensive Land Use Plan 2017–2027 on 8 November 2026. They were reviewed with the DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 (the LDRRMP on file), the PSCP Guidebook in the project library and IAT v1.6. Table 3.1 records what each source establishes and what remains open. Where a source is still missing (the current OCD/NDRRMC contingency-planning manual), this chapter states the dependency instead of attributing step-by-step requirements to it.\n\n**Table 3.1**\n\n*Source Verification Status, 8 November 2026*\n\n| **Source**                                                   | **Status in the project file set**                                                                                                                                                              | **What it establishes for this chapter**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | **Open point / action**                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Ormoc City CDRA report (CDRA_Ormoc.pdf, 194 pp.)             | Received 8 Nov 2026. Report pages i–96 (Chapters 1–4) are the City''s CDRA text; PDF pages 137–194 are a separate draft reconstruction of Chapters 5–6 for TWG review, not adopted City results. | Hazard information, climate projections, impact chains (CCICA) and climate risk (CCRA) for forest, agriculture, urban and coastal ecosystems; exposure-database findings for flood and rain-induced landslide by population, urban use, critical point facilities and lifelines.                                              | The detailed barangay risk tables of original Chapters 5–6 are not in the copy. Obtain the complete report and calculation files from the CDRA TWG. Projection tables are labelled "Municipality of Naval, Province of Biliran": verify before reuse. |\n| Comprehensive Land Use Plan 2017–2027 (Volumes 1–3, 609 pp.) | Received 8 Nov 2026.                                                                                                                                                                            | Hazards recognized: flood and flash flood, rain-induced landslide, typhoon and storm surge, ground rupture, ground shaking, liquefaction and tsunami. Hazard maps are from the READY Project (2010). Coastal barangays are the most at risk; growth is directed inland. Barangays grouped into 12 clusters by shared hazards. | Table 2-5 lists a Risk Overlay Zone with no area delineated, and the plan uses the former 110-barangay configuration. Both are inputs to SOP 3-5 and to the next CLUP update (IAT A3.2, C1.4).                                                        |\n| DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 (LDRRMP on file)                 | In the platform reference folder. Signed/adopted copy to be filed in the Vault.                                                                                                                 | GeoRiskPH modelled potential damage: flood ₱222.4 billion, rain-induced landslide ₱205.5 billion, liquefaction ₱189.3 billion. 46 barangays with critical flood risk (total population 59,545), 19 of them with potential damage above ₱1 billion. PPAs for contingency plans for other hazards and a PSCP within 2023–2028.  | Modelled exposure values, not observed or annual expected losses. Reconcile with the CDRA exposure database before use in the risk register.                                                                                                          |\n| NDRRMC–OCD PSCP Guidebook                                    | In the project library (PSCP_Guidebook_Final_Version.pdf).                                                                                                                                      | Method for essential functions, business impact analysis, continuity strategies and testing (Section 14; SOP 3-7).                                                                                                                                                                                                            | Record the edition and issuance date in Annex 3-M before sign-off.                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| Current OCD/NDRRMC contingency-planning manual               | Not in the project file set.                                                                                                                                                                    | —                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | Obtain from OCD Region VIII and reconcile SOP 3-6 before approval.                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| IAT v1.6 (workbook updated 7 Nov 2026)                       | Platform reference; adds an evidence-validation status for every indicator (all "Pending validation").                                                                                          | Indicator scores, evidence notes and gaps cited in this chapter.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | Indicator status changes after the 1–4 December 2026 validation workshop.                                                                                                                                                                             |\n\n*Note.* Page references are to the PDF files supplied by the City. "Modelled" values come from the DRRM 10-Year Plan''s GeoRiskPH analysis and are not interchangeable with CDRA results.\n\n# 5. Systems-Thinking and TQM Operating Principles\n\n| **Principle**               | **Required application**                                                                                                           | **Quality control**                                                                          |\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk as a system            | Assess hazard, exposure, vulnerability, capacity, land-use decisions, ecosystem functions and infrastructure dependencies together | Use impact chains and dependency maps; avoid isolated hazard maps as the sole decision basis |\n| Source provenance           | Every dataset has source agency, date, scale, coordinate reference system, method, limitations and custodian                       | Metadata completeness check; version register; traceable update history                      |\n| Decision-use orientation    | Risk analysis must inform a named decision, plan, investment, permit, preparedness action or continuity priority                   | Record the decision, decision owner, evidence considered and resulting action                |\n| Participation and inclusion | Engage affected communities and sector representatives in defining impacts, validating exposure and testing plans                  | Consultation records, disaggregated feedback and documented response to issues               |\n| PDCA / TQM                  | Plan data and analysis; do the assessment and planning; check quality and test arrangements; act on findings                       | Corrective-action register, audit trail, annual review and post-exercise update              |\n| Precaution and uncertainty  | Distinguish verified data, modelled estimates, assumptions and unknowns                                                            | State uncertainty and avoid presenting model outputs as parcel-level guarantees              |\n| No mandate transfer         | CDRRMO coordinates; competent offices validate and decide within their mandates                                                    | RACI reviewed by CLO and office heads; one accountable owner per decision                    |\n\n# 6. Institutional Roles and Process Ownership\n\n| **Process**                                       | **Accountable owner (proposed)**                                                       | **Responsible contributors / validators**                                   | **Approval or decision route**                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| City risk-information register and local risk map | CDRRMO Head                                                                            | Research and Planning; GIS/data custodians; source agencies; sector offices | CDRRMO administrative approval; technical sign-off by source/sector owners                                   |\n| CDRA preparation and update                       | CPDO for formal planning integration; CDRRMO for DRRM coordination                     | CDRA TWG; GIS; sector offices; barangays; competent NGAs                    | Technical validation by TWG; adoption/integration through applicable City planning and legislative processes |\n| CLUP/CDP mainstreaming                            | CPDO                                                                                   | CDRRMO, Engineering, Zoning, CENRO, sector offices, LDC                     | Formal CLUP/CDP and zoning approval processes                                                                |\n| LDRRMP and annual DRRM planning                   | CDRRMO Head / CDRRMC within their respective statutory roles                           | CPDO, departments, barangays, CSOs, private sector                          | CDRRMC approval/oversight as provided by RA 10121; local approval processes                                  |\n| Contingency plans                                 | CDRRMO Head as coordinating process owner; sector/incident lead per hazard             | Hazard technical agencies, IMT/response clusters, utilities, barangays      | CDRRMC/Mayor or designated authority according to applicable local issuance                                  |\n| PSCP                                              | City Administrator / Mayor-designated continuity executive (to be formally designated) | Department continuity coordinators; HRMO; ICT; Engineering; Budget; CDRRMO  | City executive approval; office heads accept function-specific responsibilities                              |\n| Barangay DRRM plan quality review                 | BDRRMC / Barangay Development Council within applicable law                            | CDRRMO and DILG review team; CSOs; vulnerable-sector representatives        | Barangay approval route under current law and DILG issuances                                                 |\n\nThe proposed accountable owners above are operational assignments for validation. They do not override legal authority, existing ordinances, approved organizational arrangements or the current DILG Listo si KAP framework. The City Legal Office shall confirm the precise barangay governance terminology and approval route before the final Manual is issued.\n\n**Figure 3.3**\n\n*Institutional Roles and Mandate Boundaries*\n\n![Figure 3: 6. Institutional Roles and Process Ownership](/library/ch3v10/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* A = accountable; R = responsible; C = consulted. Proposed assignments for validation by office heads and the City Legal Office; see Section 18.\n\n# 7. Risk Information Governance and Authoritative Hazard Data\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a City Risk Information Register and a controlled geospatial repository. It shall not independently re-author or downgrade authoritative hazard products.\n\nSource agencies retain responsibility for their technical products; the City records provenance, communicates limitations and determines how approved information is used in local planning.\n\n| **Data class**                                                                     | **Preferred source / custodian**                                            | **Required metadata and validation**                                                                                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Tropical cyclone, rainfall, flood forecast and climate information                 | PAGASA; competent climate data providers; City engineering/drainage records | Product title, issue date, forecast/projection period, scenario, spatial resolution, units, baseline, uncertainty                        |\n| Ground shaking, fault, liquefaction, tsunami, volcanic hazards                     | DOST-PHIVOLCS / GeoRiskPH                                                   | Map/report title, scale, date, model assumptions, legend, hazard intensity, limitations and whether site-specific assessment is required |\n| Rain-induced landslide and geohazards                                              | DENR-MGB                                                                    | Map scale, susceptibility class, date, methodology, field validation status and limitations                                              |\n| Exposure: population, households, buildings, roads, facilities and economic assets | PSA/CBMS; CPDO; Assessor; Engineering; sector offices; barangays            | Reference date, geography, definitions, completeness, source, disaggregation, update frequency                                           |\n| Climate projections and ecosystem services                                         | DHSUD/CCC methodology; PAGASA; CENRO/DENR; City Agriculture                 | Emissions/scenario assumptions, time horizon, baseline, downscaling and uncertainty                                                      |\n| Critical infrastructure and lifelines                                              | Asset-owning departments, utilities, NGAs and facility operators            | Owner/operator, coordinates, capacity, condition, service area, dependencies, redundancy, restoration time and contact                   |\n\nNo single hazard portal or map layer should be treated as a complete site-specific engineering determination. Where a decision requires parcel-level, geotechnical, structural or other specialist assessment, the City shall obtain the assessment from the competent agency or licensed professional under the applicable regulatory process.\n\n# 8. Hazard Information Acquisition, Validation and Mapping\n\nThe mapping process is controlled by source provenance and fitness-for-purpose. The CDRRMO GIS/risk-information custodian shall maintain the layer catalogue; the originating agency remains the technical source. A map shall not be published as an official City planning layer until its source, date, scale, coordinate system, legend, geographic coverage and limitations have been checked and the relevant technical office has signed the validation record.\n\n**Figure 3.4**\n\n*Authoritative Hazard Data and Provenance*\n\n![Figure 4: 8. Hazard Information Acquisition, Validation and Mapping](/library/ch3v10/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Source agencies remain the technical owners of their products (Section 7).\n\n1.  Register the request and define the decision purpose, hazard, geographic extent, map scale and intended users. Reject requests that do not specify whether the output is for screening, planning, public information or site-specific technical review.\n\n2.  Acquire the current official dataset from the responsible agency or its designated portal. Retain the original download, accompanying metadata, readme, legends, technical notes and correspondence; record access date and version.\n\n3.  Check completeness and integrity: file opens; geometry is valid; attributes and classes are present; units and coordinate reference system are known; coverage includes the required City/barangay extent; and metadata identifies scale and date.\n\n4.  Compare the product with other authoritative layers and local evidence without averaging or arbitrarily choosing conflicting values. Log discrepancies, identify which agency owns the disputed technical interpretation, and request clarification or a revised product.\n\n5.  Transform or reproject a working copy only when necessary, document the original and target coordinate reference systems, transformation method and software/version, and preserve the unmodified source file.\n\n6.  Overlay the hazard layer with the authoritative barangay boundary, exposure units and critical assets. Record the spatial operation, date, data versions, treatment of no-data areas and any assumptions.\n\n7.  Submit the draft map to the relevant technical office and source agency for validation where needed. The review must check scale suitability, legend, interpretation, local anomalies, uncertainty and whether the layer is suitable for the stated decision.\n\n8.  Approve the map for a defined use category: internal screening, public information, CLUP/CDP planning layer, or regulatory use. Regulatory use requires the appropriate CPDO/Zoning/Engineering/legal and formal adoption route.\n\n9.  Publish the approved version with map title, date, source, scale, legend, limitations, contact and version number. Archive superseded versions and notify users when a material revision occurs.\n\n**Decision control**: If source data conflict, are outdated, have inadequate scale, or leave an unmapped area, the map custodian shall mark the limitation and refer the issue to the source agency and relevant technical office. The CDRRMO shall not fill a gap by drawing an unverified hazard boundary. The output is a controlled map package: source files, metadata, validation record, map layout, approval/use classification, publication record and revision entry.\n\n# 9. Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA)\n\nOrmoc shall conduct and update its CDRA using the applicable DHSUD Enhanced CDRA Policy and current official CDRA training and technical materials. The official DHSUD learning material describes a 5-Step+ approach: (1) collect and organize climate-change and hazard information; (2) scope potential impacts; (3) develop an exposure database; (4) conduct the CDRA; and (5) summarize findings, followed by compilation, CLUP mainstreaming, monitoring and evaluation, and communication planning.\n\n**Figure 3.5**\n\n*The CDRA Process Applied in Ormoc*\n\n![Figure 5: 9. Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA)](/library/ch3v10/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Follows the DHSUD 5-Step+ sequence; Ormoc-specific completion requirements are listed in Section 9.\n\nThe City shall use the current prescribed tools, definitions and technical parameters rather than replacing them with an internally invented scoring model.\n\n| **Method stage**                                        | **Required substantive work**                                                                                                                                                                                                 | **Required output / quality gate**                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Collect and organize hazard and climate information | Identify priority hazards and climate stressors; acquire authoritative current and future hazard/climate information; document spatial scale, temporal horizon, scenario, baseline and limitations                            | Hazard/climate information inventory; metadata; agreed assessment scope; gap register                            |\n| 2\\. Scope potential impacts                             | For each hazard/climate stressor, identify affected sectors, elements at risk, impact pathways, spatial extent, relevant time horizon and local context; consult technical offices and communities                            | Hazard-impact scoping matrix and documented impact chains                                                        |\n| 3\\. Develop exposure database                           | Build georeferenced exposure units for population, households, buildings, infrastructure, critical facilities, livelihoods, agriculture, ecosystems and relevant cultural assets; standardize identifiers and definitions     | Validated exposure geodatabase and attribute dictionary; completeness and privacy checks                         |\n| 4\\. Conduct CDRA                                        | Apply the official method for vulnerability/sensitivity, adaptive capacity and disaster risk assessment; use the prescribed hazard-specific parameters and available climate projections; retain calculations and assumptions | Hazard/sector risk assessment tables, maps, calculation files, uncertainty statement and validation record       |\n| 5\\. Summarize findings and MDAs                         | Synthesize risk levels and identify Major Decision Areas and corresponding policy interventions; connect findings to land-use, development, infrastructure, social protection and ecosystem decisions                         | MDA register with spatial reference, evidence, affected elements, policy intervention, lead office and indicator |\n| 6\\. Compile results                                     | Prepare the CDRA report, map atlas, geodatabase, technical annexes, consultation records, data dictionary and executive summary                                                                                               | Complete, quality-assured CDRA package                                                                           |\n| 7\\. Mainstream in CLUP and development planning         | Translate risk findings and MDAs into land-use policies, zoning overlays, development controls, programs, projects and investment priorities through CPDO-led processes                                                       | CDRA-to-CLUP/CDP crosswalk; draft policy and zoning amendments; investment tags                                  |\n| 8\\. Monitor and evaluate                                | Define indicators, data owners, update frequency and triggers for reassessment; monitor hazard, exposure, vulnerability and implementation changes                                                                            | CDRA M&E matrix and annual update record                                                                         |\n| 9\\. Communicate                                         | Prepare audience-specific products for decision-makers, barangays, communities, sector groups and the public; explain uncertainty and limitations                                                                             | Communication plan, accessible maps/briefs and consultation feedback                                             |\n\n**Ormoc-specific completion requirements.** The CDRA text on file (Chapters 1–4) covers hazard information, climate projections, impact chains and climate risk for four ecosystems, and its executive summary reports exposure findings for flood and rain-induced landslide. The IAT records earthquake, liquefaction and tsunami assessment as outstanding, although the CLUP maps these hazards and the DRRM 10-Year Plan models their potential damage. The City shall confirm which further hazards the updated risk profile requires (tropical cyclone wind, drought and heat, technological and geothermal-related scenarios), verify the climate tables labelled for Naval, Biliran, and reconcile every exposure or loss figure with its source table, geographic coverage and current master datasets before it is republished. Table 3.2 summarizes the verified evidence.\n\n**Table 3.2**\n\n*Verified Local Risk Evidence for Ormoc City*\n\n| **Finding**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | **Source (verified)**                                              | **Use in this chapter**                                                                                                                         |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Flood carries the largest modelled potential damage (₱222.4 billion), followed by rain-induced landslide (₱205.5 billion) and liquefaction (₱189.3 billion); built-up areas account for most of the value exposed.                                 | DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 (GeoRiskPH results)                    | Hazard priority for SOP 3-3 and 3-6; investment screening in SOP 3-5                                                                            |\n| 105 barangays exposed to flood; 90 with high or very high exposure; 46 with critical flood risk (≥50% of population in exposed areas), total population 59,545; 19 of the 46 with potential damage above ₱1 billion.                               | DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032                                        | Barangay priority list for contingency planning and early warning; counts use the former 110-barangay base and need the crosswalk in Section 15 |\n| Flood exposure findings for critical point facilities and roads (vulnerability scores, share of high-risk road segments) and rain-induced landslide exposure for population, urban use areas and critical point facilities.                        | CDRA executive summary                                             | Starting point for the Critical Facilities and Lifelines Register (SOP 3-4); counts to be validated                                             |\n| Hazards recognized for land-use planning: flood and flash flood, rain-induced landslide, typhoon and storm surge, ground rupture, ground shaking, liquefaction and tsunami; coastal barangays and the dense city centre are in the high-risk zone. | CLUP 2017–2027, Vol. 1 sec. D.2; hazard maps (READY Project, 2010) | Hazard scope of SOP 3-1 and 3-3                                                                                                                 |\n| A Risk Overlay Zone is listed in the land-use plan with no area delineated.                                                                                                                                                                        | CLUP 2017–2027, Vol. 1 Table 2-5                                   | Evidence for IAT C1.4 and A3.2; priority input to SOP 3-5                                                                                       |\n| Climate projections (2036–2065, relative to 1971–2000) appear under a heading for Naval, Biliran.                                                                                                                                                  | CDRA, projection tables                                            | Do not reuse until PAGASA source tables are checked (Section 20, P1)                                                                            |\n\n*Note.* Modelled values describe potential damage to exposed assets under the plan''s assumptions; they are not historical or annual expected losses. Barangay counts in the DRRM Plan and CLUP use the 110-barangay configuration.\n\n**10. Exposure, Vulnerability, Loss and Impact-chain Analysis**\n\nThe exposure database shall identify what is located in hazard-affected areas; vulnerability analysis shall explain why and how exposed elements may be harmed; capacity analysis shall identify resources and adaptive capabilities that reduce impacts.\n\nThe analysis must not treat exposure as synonymous with risk. It must include direct and, where data permit, indirect and cascading impacts.\n\nPersonal and sensitive personal information shall not be placed in public-facing GIS layers or general-purpose map exports. Use aggregated or appropriately de-identified information for planning and publication. Household-level targeting data must be held by authorized custodians under a documented data-sharing and access-control arrangement consistent with RA 10173.\n\n**Figure 3.6**\n\n*Exposure, Vulnerability and Inclusion Layers*\n\n![Figure 6: 9. Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA)](/library/ch3v10/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Household-level data stay with authorized custodians; public maps use aggregated data (RA 10173).\n\n| **Dimension**                       | **Minimum analytical content for Ormoc**                                                                                                                                                                 | **Primary data owners / contributors**                                |\n|-------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Population and households           | Population by barangay and relevant sex, age, disability, pregnancy/lactation, child, older-person and Indigenous Peoples'' variables where lawfully collected; household location and access constraints | CPDO/CBMS custodian, PSA, CSWDO, City Health, barangays               |\n| Housing and buildings               | Building use, occupancy, construction/material, floor level where relevant, condition, tenure/informal settlement and critical building function                                                         | Assessor, Engineering, OBO, CPDO, barangays                           |\n| Livelihoods and economic activity   | Employment, income sources, market access, business interruption, agriculture/fisheries, tourism and informal livelihoods                                                                                | City Agriculture, market/economic offices, DTI, CPDO, business groups |\n| Infrastructure and services         | Roads, bridges, ports, power, water, wastewater, telecommunications, fuel, hospitals, schools, evacuation centres and EOC                                                                                | Asset-owning departments, utilities, NGAs                             |\n| Ecosystems and natural capital      | Watersheds, forests, mangroves, coastal ecosystems, wetlands, critical habitats and ecosystem services that reduce hazard impacts                                                                        | CENRO/DENR, City Agriculture, CPDO                                    |\n| Cultural heritage and social assets | Heritage overlay assets, culturally significant sites, community facilities and displacement impacts                                                                                                     | CPDO, culture/tourism offices, barangays, IP representatives          |\n| Vulnerability and capacity          | Physical fragility, access, poverty, social exclusion, service access, coping capacity, preparedness and adaptive capacity                                                                               | Sector offices, CSOs, barangays, community validation                 |\n\n# 11. Critical Facilities, Lifelines and Cascading-Failure Analysis\n\nThe existing CDRA facility and lifeline inventory is the starting point, not the completed dependency analysis. The IAT identifies that facilities and lifelines have been listed but interdependencies and failure chains have not yet been mapped or used to set protection and restoration priorities.\n\n1.  Confirm the asset universe and unique asset identifier with each owner/operator. At minimum include EOC, hospitals, health centres, evacuation centres, schools used for authorized emergency functions, bridges, major roads, port, water supply, wastewater, power, telecommunications, fuel and key government facilities.\n\n2.  For each asset, record location, owner/operator, service function, capacity, service population/area, operating hours, condition, hazard exposure, vulnerability, backup arrangements, access routes, critical inputs and restoration dependencies.\n\n3.  Identify upstream and downstream dependencies. Examples to test—not presume—include power loss affecting water pumping, telecommunications and hospital operations; road/bridge disruption isolating evacuation centres; fuel disruption affecting generators, ambulances and water delivery; and EOC communications failure affecting coordination.\n\n4.  Construct directed dependency maps and scenario-based failure chains. For each chain, identify initiating hazard, asset failure, service consequence, affected population, duration, cascading assets and alternative arrangements.\n\n5.  Validate each dependency with the asset owner/operator and affected service providers. Record disputed or unknown links as data gaps; do not convert assumptions into confirmed dependencies.\n\n6.  Prioritize risk treatment and continuity options using consequence, likelihood, service criticality, redundancy, restoration time and vulnerable-population impact. Use approved criteria and document any local weighting adopted.\n\n7.  Integrate priority actions into asset management, capital investment, contingency plans and PSCP. Maintain a restoration-priority list with accountable owner, target, funding route and evidence of completion.\n\n**Figure 3.7**\n\n*Illustrative Cascading-Failure Chain*\n\n![Figure 7: 11. Critical Facilities, Lifelines and Cascading-Failure Analysis](/library/ch3v10/fig-07.webp)\n\n*Note.* Illustrative only. Dependencies are confirmed with asset owners before they are recorded (SOP 3-4).\n\nThe output shall be a Critical Facilities and Lifelines Register, dependency diagram, failure-chain scenario sheets, and a protection/restoration priority register. Asset owners remain accountable for the accuracy and operation of their assets.\n\n# 12. Risk-Informed Planning and Investment Mainstreaming\n\nThe IAT identifies the CLUP 2017–2027 as predating the CDRA and notes that the CDRA results have not been demonstrably translated into current zoning overlays or a formal project/budget screening step. This chapter therefore establishes an interface, not a transfer of CPDO or Budget Office mandates.\n\n1.  CPDO convenes a planning integration review using the approved CDRA package, MDA register, hazard maps, exposure database, critical-facility dependencies and barangay validation.\n\n2.  Each MDA is mapped to the relevant CLUP land-use policy, zoning provision, CDP outcome, LCCAP action, ELA priority, LDIP/AIP proposal and responsible implementing office.\n\n3.  Where a hazard or climate risk affects land-use suitability, CPDO and the Zoning Administrator lead the assessment of land-use policy and zoning options; Engineering/OBO and other regulators determine applicable technical standards and permit conditions.\n\n**Figure 3.8**\n\n*Mainstreaming Funnel and Project Screening*\n\n![Figure 8: 12. Risk-Informed Planning and Investment Mainstreaming](/library/ch3v10/fig-08.webp)\n\n*Note.* Screening questions correspond to Form 3-09 (Annex 3-J).\n\n4.  Before an investment proposal is prioritized, the originating office completes the DRR/CCA screening record. It identifies hazard exposure, climate sensitivity, affected people/assets, design and siting implications, ecosystem effects, residual risk and proposed risk treatment.\n\n5.  CPDO reviews consistency with plans and spatial policy; Engineering/OBO/Zoning review technical and regulatory matters; CDRRMO provides risk evidence and DRRM advice; Budget/LFC assess fiscal and budget requirements; the competent approving body decides.\n\n6.  Record screening outcomes in the project appraisal and LDC/LFC minutes. Proposals with unresolved material risk shall be returned for redesign, specialist assessment or documented decision by the competent authority; the CDRRMO does not independently veto statutory approvals.\n\n7.  Monitor whether adopted measures are implemented and whether risk exposure is reduced. Feed permit, project and loss information back into the next CDRA update.\n\n| **Screening question**                                            | **Minimum evidence to attach**                                                                       | **Review lead**                         |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| Is the site or service exposed to current or future hazards?      | Official hazard overlay, map date/scale, climate scenario/time horizon                               | CPDO with CDRRMO technical input        |\n| Could the project increase exposure or vulnerability elsewhere?   | Site alternatives, drainage/downstream effects, access and displacement analysis                     | CPDO / Engineering / CENRO              |\n| Are design, siting and operational standards adequate?            | Applicable code, engineering/geotechnical assessment, service continuity and residual-risk statement | Engineering / OBO / competent regulator |\n| Are vulnerable groups disproportionately affected?                | Disaggregated impact analysis, accessible consultation and mitigation measures                       | CSWDO / GAD / sector offices            |\n| Does the project affect protective ecosystems or cultural assets? | Ecosystem service and heritage screening; permits/clearances as applicable                           | CENRO / DENR / CPDO                     |\n| Is the risk treatment funded and assigned?                        | Costed mitigation, responsible office, implementation schedule and monitoring indicator              | Originating office / Budget / LFC       |\n\n# 13. Contingency Planning and Exercise Programme\n\nContingency planning is a management process for analyzing specific potential events or emerging situations and establishing arrangements in advance for timely, effective and appropriate response. RA 10121 requires the LDRRMO to facilitate and support local contingency planning; the LDRRMC must regularly review and test the LDRRMP. The detailed hazard-specific methodology must be checked against the current OCD/NDRRMC contingency-planning guide held by the City.\n\n**Figure 3.9**\n\n*Hazard-Specific Contingency Planning Cycle*\n\n![Figure 9: 13. Contingency Planning and Exercise Programme](/library/ch3v10/fig-09.webp)\n\n*Note.* Plan content requirements are listed in the table in Section 13.\n\nThis chapter sets the minimum City control architecture and does not claim that the sequence below reproduces an unverified manual verbatim.\n\n| **Plan component**               | **Required content**                                                                                                                                   |\n|----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Purpose and scope                | Hazard, geographic coverage, planning assumptions, plan owner, version, validity and relationship to LDRRMP/EOC/ICS                                    |\n| Risk scenario                    | Most-probable and credible severe scenario, source evidence, time/place, onset, magnitude, cascading effects and uncertainty                           |\n| Planning assumptions             | Forecast/monitoring basis, warning lead time, access, population, service and resource assumptions; assumptions requiring confirmation                 |\n| Objectives and priorities        | Life safety, evacuation, protection of critical assets, continuity of essential services, protection of vulnerable groups and environmental safeguards |\n| Concept of operations            | Preparedness, activation, response, stabilization and transition; coordination with incident command and EOC; no displacement of field command         |\n| Roles and coordination           | Lead/support agencies, incident command/IMT interface, cluster/sector responsibilities, barangay roles and escalation routes                           |\n| Triggers and decision points     | Authoritative warning/advisory thresholds and locally approved action triggers; source and authority for each trigger; avoid invented thresholds       |\n| Operational arrangements         | Warning, evacuation, shelter/CCCM, search and rescue, health, protection, logistics, public information, RDANA and service restoration                 |\n| Resources and gaps               | Personnel, equipment, supplies, facilities, transport, communications, suppliers, prepositioning, mutual aid and request process                       |\n| Information and reporting        | Situation reports, common operating picture, decision log, data validation, public information approval and reporting frequency                        |\n| Safety, inclusion and protection | Accessible warning, safe evacuation, child protection, gender and protection safeguards, PWD access, older persons, IP and MHPSS                       |\n| Exercise, review and update      | Tabletop/functional/full-scale exercise objectives, evaluation, corrective action owner, deadline and plan revision                                    |\n\nPriority hazard portfolio for validation. The IAT names flood/flash flood, landslide, earthquake/tsunami, tropical cyclone and industrial/geothermal hazards. The City shall confirm priority and scenario scope using the current CDRA, official agency products, historical impact records and sector risk. A single generic all-hazards plan is not a substitute for hazard-specific operational annexes where triggers, warning lead times, response tactics or technical agencies differ.\n\nExercise control: Each approved plan shall have an exercise schedule and documented test. At minimum, the CDRRMO shall ensure that the plan is walked through with assigned agencies and barangays, that contact and resource arrangements are checked, and that decisions and gaps are recorded. Exercise type and frequency shall be set in the approved annual programme and applicable national guidance. A plan is not “tested” merely because a meeting or orientation occurred.\n\n# 14. Public Service Continuity Planning (PSCP)\n\nThe IAT records the PSCP as not completed and no business impact analysis or essential-functions list sighted. The City shall therefore treat PSCP as a priority institutional deliverable.\n\n**Figure 3.10**\n\n*Public Service Continuity Planning Steps*\n\n![Figure 10: 14. Public Service Continuity Planning (PSCP)](/library/ch3v10/fig-10.webp)\n\n*Note.* Recovery priorities follow a business impact analysis; no generic recovery times are set (Section 14).\n\nThe PSCP shall address continuity of essential City functions during disruption, including the CDRRMO/EOC and services whose interruption may create cascading impacts on life safety and public welfare.\n\n| **PSCP element**               | **Required procedure and output**                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Governance and scope           | Mayor/City Administrator designates continuity executive, department continuity coordinators, plan custodian and approval route; define covered offices and essential services                    |\n| Essential-function inventory   | Each department identifies legal mandate, service users, minimum service level, process owner, key staff, systems, facilities, records, suppliers and interdependencies                           |\n| Business impact analysis (BIA) | Assess disruption consequences over time; identify maximum tolerable disruption and recovery priorities using an approved City method; distinguish statutory deadlines from service-level choices |\n| Dependency analysis            | Map staff, ICT, power, water, telecom, transport, facilities, records, suppliers, fuel and inter-office dependencies; validate with owners                                                        |\n| Continuity strategies          | Identify alternate work locations, remote/manual procedures, backup power/communications, data backup, alternate suppliers, mutual aid and staff succession                                       |\n| Plan development               | Define activation authority, notification, relocation, service prioritization, internal/external communication, resource control and deactivation                                                 |\n| Testing and maintenance        | Exercise continuity scenarios, document performance, correct gaps, review after organizational/technology changes and update contact lists                                                        |\n\nThe City shall not invent universal recovery time objectives without a BIA. Each department shall propose service-specific priorities and tolerable disruption periods based on statutory obligations, life-safety consequences, affected population and operational dependencies; the designated continuity executive shall review and approve the city-wide prioritization framework. The PSCP shall link with Chapter 6 EOC alternate-site and communications arrangements but shall not make the EOC the sole continuity solution for all City services.\n\n# 15. Barangay DRRM Planning and Quality Assurance\n\nRA 10121 establishes a BDRRMC in every barangay and assigns local DRRM planning and coordination functions. **The current denominator is 85 barangays** (PSA Philippine Standard Geographic Code, fourth quarter 2022): the 28 numbered Poblacion barangays were merged into South, East and West, and Barangay 29 became North. The CLUP 2017–2027 (110 barangays: 69 rural, 41 urban, 16 coastal), the DRRM 10-Year Plan and parts of the CDRA still use the former configuration. CPDO, the City Secretary and the DILG City Local Government Operations Office shall file the merger instrument and plebiscite record in the Vault and keep a crosswalk from the 110 former barangays to the 85 current ones, so that older counts (for example the 46 critical flood barangays) can be restated.\n\n1.  Maintain one master register of barangays with official name, geographic identifier, legal status, boundary reference, population reference year and responsible BDRRMC contact.\n\n2.  Request the current BDRRMP and approval evidence from each barangay using the current DILG/OCD-prescribed template and review tool. Record the document version, coverage period, approval date and evidence source.\n\n3.  Conduct a completeness and quality review covering risk profile, priority hazards, exposed and vulnerable groups, early warning, evacuation, response arrangements, resources, annual work programme, budget, inclusion and monitoring.\n\n4.  Return each plan with specific, actionable comments linked to the applicable quality criteria; identify mandatory corrections separately from recommended improvements.\n\n5.  Verify that comments have been addressed and record the disposition. A submitted plan is not counted as quality-assured merely because it has been received.\n\n6.  Consolidate status by barangay: not submitted, submitted, under review, returned for revision, technically accepted, approved, tested, and due for update.\n\n7.  Provide technical assistance and schedule plan-testing support. Link barangay plans with City hazard scenarios, evacuation arrangements, warning protocols and resource requests without removing barangay ownership.\n\n8.  Report gaps and overdue actions to the CDRRMC and DILG through the applicable channel; maintain a follow-up register.\n\n# 16. Inclusive DRRM and Ecosystem-Based Risk Reduction\n\nInclusion is a design requirement across the risk-information and preparedness cycle, not a stand-alone consultation event. Data collection must be lawful and proportionate; consultation must be accessible; and the analysis must show how different groups experience exposure, sensitivity, barriers and capacities.\n\n| **Area**                               | **Minimum operating requirement**                                                                                                         | **Evidence**                                                                        |\n|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Gender and women                       | Analyze differentiated impacts, care responsibilities, safety, livelihoods and participation; integrate GAD planning and budgeting        | Disaggregated analysis, consultation records, GAD-tagged actions                    |\n| Children                               | Apply child-sensitive risk analysis, protection, education continuity and safe evacuation arrangements                                    | Child-focused impact analysis, CSWDO/DepEd validation, child protection measures    |\n| Persons with disabilities              | Identify functional/access requirements, accessible warning, transport, shelter and communication needs with consent and privacy controls | PWD organization consultation, accessible plan/map formats, facility access review  |\n| Older persons                          | Assess mobility, health, medication, support networks and accessible service arrangements                                                 | Sector validation, evacuation and continuity arrangements                           |\n| Indigenous Peoples                     | Respect IPRA rights, cultural protocols, Indigenous Knowledge Systems and Practices, and meaningful participation                         | Consultation record, appropriate consent/process, culturally relevant risk analysis |\n| Pregnant/lactating persons and infants | Include maternal, newborn and infant feeding needs in evacuation, health and continuity planning                                          | Health-office validation and service protocols                                      |\n| Ecosystems                             | Treat watershed, upland forest, mangroves, wetlands and coastal ecosystems as risk-reduction assets and sources of ecosystem services     | Ecosystem inventory, condition monitoring, restoration/protection actions           |\n\nThe IAT identifies the upper Anilao–Malbasag watershed, upland forest cover and coastal mangroves as important protective ecosystems. The assessment notes that these are identified in plans but are not yet monitored consistently as risk-reduction assets. CENRO/DENR and relevant offices shall define measurable condition indicators and a monitoring programme; the CDRRMO shall incorporate relevant findings into risk analysis and planning, not assume responsibility for environmental regulation.\n\n# 17. Process Workflows and Decision Controls\n\nThe workflows below are operational control procedures derived from the legal mandate, the official CDRA sequence and the City''s documented assessment needs. They identify actors, actions, decision gates and records. Where an external manual contains a more specific mandatory step, the controlling manual shall prevail and the local SOP shall be revised to reflect it.\n\n## SOP 3-1 \\| Hazard Data Acquisition, Validation and Map Release\n\n**Figure 3.11**\n\n*SOP 3-1 Process Flow: Hazard Data Acquisition, Validation and Map Release*\n\n![Figure 11: SOP 3-1 | Hazard Data Acquisition, Validation and Map Release](/library/ch3v10/fig-11.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\n**Process owner**: CDRRMO Research and Planning / GIS custodian. Purpose: Obtain authoritative, current and fit-for-purpose hazard information and control its use.\n\n**Table 3.3**\n\n*SOP 3-1 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                               |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow             |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-01 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                                         |\n| Trigger           | A request for risk information or a map; a new or revised product from a source agency                  |\n| Forms             | Form 3-01 Risk Information and Map Request; Form 3-02 Dataset Metadata and Quality Checklist; Form 3-11 |\n| IAT indicators    | A5.1, A5.6, C1.4                                                                                        |\n| Policy references | RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(3); PHIVOLCS, MGB and PAGASA product terms of use; DHSUD MC 2025-004                |\n| Records kept in   | Map package and version register (Vault, Geo-COP layer with validation status)                          |\n\n| **Step / control point**     | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                                                              | **Mandatory record / output**                      |\n|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n| Open and scope request       | CDRRMO records requester, purpose, hazard, geography, scale, deadline and intended decision. Return incomplete requests for clarification.                                                         | Risk-information request and tracking number       |\n| Acquire source product       | GIS custodian obtains current product from source agency/official portal; preserves original file, metadata, legend, technical note and access date.                                               | Source dataset and acquisition log                 |\n| Check metadata and integrity | Check date, scale, CRS, units, geometry, attributes, legend, geographic coverage, no-data areas, model assumptions and limitations.                                                                | Completed metadata/quality checklist               |\n| Resolve discrepancies        | Compare authoritative sources and local evidence; record differences; refer technical interpretation to source agency and competent sector office; do not average or redraw uncertain boundaries.  | Discrepancy log and source-agency response         |\n| Prepare controlled overlay   | Use approved barangay boundaries and exposure layers; document reprojection, spatial operation, data versions and processing software.                                                             | Working GIS layer and processing record            |\n| Technical validation         | Relevant technical office checks interpretation and fitness for purpose; source agency consulted for material uncertainty; CPDO/Zoning/Engineering review if intended for planning/regulatory use. | Signed technical validation                        |\n| Classify and approve use     | Assign use category (internal screening, public information, planning layer, regulatory layer); route regulatory use through formal adoption process.                                              | Approval/use classification record                 |\n| Publish and maintain         | Publish map with date, source, scale, legend, limitations and version; archive superseded version and notify users of material changes.                                                            | Released map, publication log and version register |\n\n## SOP 3-2 \\| Risk Information and Geospatial Repository Maintenance\n\n**Figure 3.12**\n\n*SOP 3-2 Process Flow: Risk Information and Geospatial Repository Maintenance*\n\n![Figure 12: SOP 3-2 | Risk Information and Geospatial Repository Maintenance](/library/ch3v10/fig-12.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head / designated data custodian. Purpose: Maintain a controlled, auditable city risk information base.\n\n**Table 3.4**\n\n*SOP 3-2 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                                    |\n|-------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow                  |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-02 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                                              |\n| Trigger           | A new dataset; quarterly review; after a major event or a source-agency revision                             |\n| Forms             | Form 3-02 Dataset Metadata and Quality Checklist; Form 3-11 Corrective Action and Document Revision Register |\n| IAT indicators    | A5.1, A5.4, A5.5, A5.6, A5.7                                                                                 |\n| Policy references | RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(3) and (5); RA 10173 and NPC issuances                                                   |\n| Records kept in   | Risk Information Register; data classification and access register                                           |\n\n| **Step / control point**   | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                   | **Mandatory record / output**           |\n|----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| Register datasets          | Assign dataset ID, title, source owner, custodian, date, geography, scale, CRS, update cycle, sensitivity and permitted use.                            | Risk Information Register               |\n| Apply data governance      | Classify public, internal, confidential or personal/sensitive data; establish access rights, lawful purpose, retention and sharing conditions.          | Data classification and access register |\n| Validate updates           | Compare incoming version with existing data; identify changed attributes/geometries, missing records and implications for previously issued maps/plans. | Change log and validation note          |\n| Release controlled version | Assign version, approval status and effective date; preserve source file and processing history; distribute only approved products.                     | Versioned dataset and distribution log  |\n| Review and archive         | Review at the defined cycle and after major events, agency revisions or boundary/asset changes; archive superseded versions and record reason.          | Review record and archive index         |\n\n## SOP 3-3 \\| City Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment\n\n**Figure 3.13**\n\n*SOP 3-3 Process Flow: City Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment*\n\n![Figure 13: SOP 3-3 | City Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment](/library/ch3v10/fig-13.webp)\n\n*Note.* The step table below is the controlling text; the figure summarizes it. In MODE the SOP runs as the workflow named in its SOP control block.\n\n**Process** **owner**: CPDO-led CDRA process; CDRRMO coordinates DRRM risk evidence. Purpose: Produce a method-compliant CDRA that supports CLUP and development decisions.\n\n**Table 3.5**\n\n*SOP 3-3 Control Block*\n\n| **SOP control**   | **Entry**                                                                                                                              |\n|-------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type              | Standard operating procedure (controlling text in this chapter); runs in MODE as a workflow                                            |\n| MODE workflow     | WF-SOP3-03 (DRRM → Workflows; every step is closed on its form)                                                                        |\n| Trigger           | CDRA update with the CLUP/CDP revision; a material change in hazard, exposure or climate information; a new DHSUD issuance             |\n| Forms             | Form 3-03 Hazard-Impact Scoping Matrix; Form 3-04 Major Decision Area Register; Form 3-10 Consultation and Inclusion Validation Record |\n| IAT indicators    | A5.2, A5.3, C1.1, C1.3                                                                                                                 |\n| Policy references | DHSUD MC 2025-004 (Enhanced CDRA) and the 5-Step+ process; RA 9729 as amended by RA 10174; RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(3)                      |\n| Records kept in   | Complete CDRA package; MDA register                                                                                                    |\n\n| **Step / control point**                  | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                                | **Mandatory record / output**                              |\n|-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Approve assessment protocol               | CPDO and CDRRMO define scope, planning horizon, hazards, sectors, geography, technical team, data needs, prescribed DHSUD tools and consultation plan.               | Approved CDRA inception/protocol                           |\n| Collect and organize information (Step 1) | Acquire authoritative hazard and climate data; document baseline, scenarios, time horizons, resolution, metadata and limitations; identify data gaps.                | Hazard/climate inventory and data-gap register             |\n| Scope potential impacts (Step 2)          | For each hazard/stressor, identify potential impacts by sector and element at risk; construct impact chains; validate with sector offices and communities.           | Hazard-impact scoping matrix                               |\n| Develop exposure database (Step 3)        | Build georeferenced exposure units; standardize IDs and attributes; validate spatial joins, completeness, reference dates and privacy controls.                      | Exposure geodatabase and data dictionary                   |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3v1_v1.0.md (Chapter 3, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.6))','draft')
