mergency cases; Sec. 16 framework agreements.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | SOPs 6-06, 6-07                                                                                                                            |\n| GPPB Circular No. 06-2026 (Res. No. 10-2026, 3 Feb 2026)                                        | Documentary requirements for other modes, including negotiated procurement – emergency cases.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | SOP 6-07 step 4                                                                                                                            |\n| GPPB Circular No. 07-2026 (Res. No. 12-2026, 23 Jul 2026)                                       | Guidelines on the use of framework agreements, applicable to LGUs, with templates for the call-off, the framework agreement and the framework agreement list.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | SOP 6-06; FIN-F08                                                                                                                          |\n| DILG MC No. 2012-141 and later Full Disclosure Policy issuances                                 | LDRRMF utilisation among the documents posted under the Full Disclosure Policy.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | SOP 6-02 step 6                                                                                                                            |\n| NDRRMC Disaster Preparedness Minimum Standards, Vol. 2                                          | Relief warehouse in a low-risk area; minimum standby stock of 500 food packs and 500 hygiene kits per LGU, each good for a family of five for three days; tagging and inventory.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Section 13; SOP 6-11; Annex 6-D. The national minimum is a floor; Ormoc''s levels come from its own scenarios.                              |\n| COA property rules (PAR, ICS, annual physical count, relief from accountability)                | Accountability for government property and supplies.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Section 14; SOPs 6-10, 6-11, 6-12                                                                                                          |\n| RA 656 (Property Insurance Law); GSIS indemnity                                                 | Public property insured with the GSIS.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | SOP 6-09                                                                                                                                   |\n| RA 9729 as amended by RA 10174 (People''s Survival Fund)                                         | Access to the PSF for adaptation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | SOP 6-09                                                                                                                                   |\n| NDRRMC Memo No. 147, s. 2017                                                                    | LDRRMP review; judicious use of the LDRRMF; AIP consistency.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | SOP 6-01 step 2                                                                                                                            |\n| RA 10173 (Data Privacy Act)                                                                     | Personal data of beneficiaries limited to the offices that need it.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | SOPs 6-08 and 6-11 keep distribution lists with the CSWDO; the CDRRMO records aggregates                                                   |\n| Local: SP Res. Nos. 2024-088 and 2024-102; CDRRMC Res. Nos. 01–08, s. 2025; EO 2024-032A Sec. 2 | Current approvals, donations rules and CDRRMC functions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 | Baseline and approvals (Chapter 1, section 4.5)                                                                                            |\n\n> PENDING NATIONAL RULES. NDRRMC operational guidelines on AA triggers and slow-onset timelines; DBM–COA guidelines on AA funds; the NDRRMC AA monitoring and evaluation framework. Until they are issued, this chapter (a) applies the IRR text, (b) treats slow-onset hazards as not graduated but never above the year''s AA allocation (ADR-025 item 4), and (c) asks the City Legal Office and OCD VIII to confirm the readings listed in section 18.\n\n# 5. Systems Thinking and TQM Principles for DRRM Finance and Resources\n\nThe Introduction (section 5.4) found that ad-hoc fixes during events (borrowed supplies, event-by-event resolutions) relieve pressure faster than fundamental solutions and so keep the office improvising. In finance and logistics the fundamental solutions are known: a plan line before the event, a supplier agreement before the season, a stock level before the forecast. This chapter puts them in place and keeps them visible.\n\n-   **One chain from plan to proof** (Figure 6.2). LDRRMP target → LDRRMFIP line → AIP and budget → PPMP and APP → obligation → procurement → delivery → use → report → audit. A break anywhere shows up as unspent money or unsupported spending.\n\n-   **Speed through preparation.** The fastest emergency purchase is the one whose supplier, specification and price were agreed before the event; the fastest relief is the stock already in a safe warehouse.\n\n-   **Separation of duties.** The office that requests is not the office that certifies, pays, awards or holds the property. MODE records each step by position.\n\n-   **Feedback loops.** Quarterly utilisation, the STF ageing ledger, stock counts and COA observations feed the next LDRRMFIP and the next stock plan.\n\n-   **Prevention over correction.** The pre-audit checklist (Annex 6-B) prevents the most common COA findings: non-DRRM charges, QRF use without a declaration, missing utilisation reports, unliquidated advances, expired stocks and incomplete supporting documents.\n\n**Figure 6.2**\n\n*The Chain From Plan to Proof for Every DRRM Peso*\n\n![Figure 2: 5. Systems Thinking and TQM Principles for DRRM Finance and Resources](/library/chp6v11/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. Dark boxes are the ends of the chain: the LDRRMP target that justifies the spending and the COA audit that tests it.\n\n**Figure 6.3**\n\n*Three Funding Windows and Their Legal Triggers*\n\n![Figure 3: 5. Systems Thinking and TQM Principles for DRRM Finance and Resources](/library/chp6v11/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from RA 10121 Sec. 21, JMC 2013-1 and RA 12287 IRR Rule V. A State of Imminent Disaster does not open the QRF, and a State of Calamity does not open the AA line.\n\n# 6. LDRRMF Planning and Allocation\n\n## 6.1 Computing the Fund\n\nThe LDRRMF is not less than 5% of **estimated revenue from regular sources** (RA 10121 Sec. 21). For a city this means the National Tax Allotment share plus regular local revenue (local taxes, fees and charges, and other recurring income), as defined by the Budget Office under DBM rules. It excludes one-time receipts, grants and borrowings.\n\nThe CY 2026 computation used the National Tax Allotment alone (Res. No. 05, s. 2025). The City Budget Office confirms the base each year on form FIN-F01 and keeps the computation with the budget. If the base was understated, the difference is appropriated through a supplemental budget or corrected in the next budget, as the Budget Office and the SP decide.\n\n## 6.2 The 70/30 Split and the AA Line\n\n**Table 6.7**\n\n*Components of the LDRRMF*\n\n| **Component**                                                                  | **Share**                   | **Use**                              | **Trigger**                                       |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Quick Response Fund                                                            | 30% of the LDRRMF           | Standby fund for relief and recovery | State of Calamity declared by the SP (JMC 2013-1) |\n| Prevention and mitigation, preparedness, response, rehabilitation and recovery | 70% of the LDRRMF           | PPAs in the LDRRMFIP                 | Normal budget execution                           |\n| – of which the anticipatory action line                                        | No more than 10% of the 70% | AA measures under RA 12287           | State of Imminent Disaster in force               |\n\n**Worked example (illustration only, CY 2026 figures).** The 70% component is PHP 97,969,018.98, so the maximum AA line is PHP 9,796,901.90. For a hydrometeorological or geological hazard, at most PHP 2,939,070.57 (30%) may be disbursed for interventions more than 3 days before forecast impact, and up to PHP 6,857,831.33 (70%) for interventions within 3 days (Figure 6.4). The CY 2026 plan has no AA line, so these figures apply only once a plan includes one.\n\n## 6.3 The LDRRMF Investment Plan\n\nThe LDRRMFIP is the annual plan that turns the LDRRMP into funded lines. Each line carries the LDRRMP and CDP PPA code; the thematic area (prevention and mitigation, preparedness, response, rehabilitation and recovery); the fund source (70%, QRF, STF by year of origin, other); the amount; the implementing office; the output and target; the procurement mode; and the quarter of implementation. Four lines are always shown on their own: the **AA line** (A4.6), the **EOC operations and maintenance line** (B7.5), **stock replenishment** for prepositioned relief items (A7.5), and **insurance premiums** for DRRM assets (A4.7).\n\n## 6.4 Placement in the City Budget Calendar\n\n**Table 6.8**\n\n*Chapter 6 Actions in the City Budget Calendar*\n\n| **Period**       | **Budget calendar event**                                                        | **Chapter 6 action**                                                                                                                                                                          |\n|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| January–February | Prior-year closing; COA audit fieldwork                                          | STF ageing update (SOP 6-03); Q4 utilisation report (SOP 6-02); annual physical count of DRRM property and stocks (SOPs 6-10, 6-11)                                                           |\n| March–May        | COA Annual Audit Report                                                          | Audit response plan (SOP 6-10); supplier list, specifications and stock levels refreshed before the typhoon season (SOPs 6-06, 6-11); inventory update with barangays and partners (SOP 6-12) |\n| By mid-year      | Budget call; Local Finance Committee and Local Development Council deliberations | LDRRMFIP drafting (SOP 6-01); adequacy test (SOP 6-09)                                                                                                                                        |\n| July–September   | CDRRMC approval; AIP finalised                                                   | LDRRMFIP approval; PPMP and APP                                                                                                                                                               |\n| October–December | SP enacts the annual budget and adopts the LDRRMFIP                              | Insurance renewal check; framework agreements for the next year; pre-audit check (SOP 6-10)                                                                                                   |\n| Every quarter    | CDRRMC quarterly meeting                                                         | Utilisation, stock and results report; Full Disclosure Policy posting                                                                                                                         |\n\n*Note.* Exact dates follow the Local Budget Calendar and the City Budget Office''s budget call each year.\n\n# 7. Budget Execution, Utilisation and Disclosure\n\nExecution follows the City''s normal controls: allotment, obligation request certified by the Budget Officer, procurement, inspection and acceptance, disbursement voucher certified by the Accountant, and payment by the Treasurer. The CDRRMO keeps each LDRRMFIP line visible through every step.\n\n**Table 6.9**\n\n*Utilisation Measures Computed Each Quarter*\n\n| **Measure**                      | **Formula**                                        | **Use**                                   |\n|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|\n| Obligation rate                  | Obligations ÷ allotment for the year, by component | Shows whether plans are moving            |\n| Disbursement rate                | Disbursements ÷ obligations, by component          | Shows whether deliveries are being paid   |\n| PPA completion                   | Completed PPAs ÷ PPAs due by the quarter           | Shows results, not only spending          |\n| Utilisation of the 70% component | Disbursements ÷ current-year 70% allotment         | IAT A4.4 target: at least 80% by year-end |\n\nPPAs below plan by the end of the third quarter are flagged for realignment, with CDRRMC approval and SP concurrence where the law requires it. The quarterly report (FIN-F03, carried in MODE as BUD-F02) goes to the CDRRMC, is posted under the Full Disclosure Policy and feeds the Chapter 7 reporting calendar.\n\n# 8. Special Trust Fund Management\n\nUnexpended LDRRMF accrues to the STF and may be used only for DRRM within five years from the year it accrued; after that it reverts to the general fund (RA 10121 Sec. 21). The STF ledger (FIN-F04) keeps each balance by **year of origin** and shows its deadline, so the oldest money is programmed first. STF balances are also a lawful AA source under RA 12287 IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b), and they are not subject to the 30%/70% graduation.\n\nProgramming STF balances follows the same approvals as the 2024 and 2025 Trust Fund plans: CDRRMC approval, then SP adoption or concurrence on the City Accountant''s advice (Res. No. 08, s. 2025). A bill to shorten the five-year period has been filed in Congress but is not law and is not applied.\n\n# 9. Quick Response Fund\n\nThe QRF is a standby fund for relief and recovery in areas struck by a disaster. Under JMC 2013-1 it is used when the City is under a **State of Calamity** declared by the SP (Chapter 1, SOP GOV-04). COA has found LGUs using the QRF without that declaration; this chapter does not allow it. A State of Imminent Disaster under RA 12287 does not open the QRF: anticipatory action is funded from the AA line and other lawful sources (A4.6).\n\nUnused QRF at year-end accrues to the STF. The QRF balance is reported in every quarterly report and after each use (FIN-F05). When QRF purchases replenish relief stocks, as in Res. No. 06, s. 2025, the items enter the stock card under SOP 6-11 like any other receipt.\n\n# 10. Anticipatory Action Financing under RA 12287\n\n## 10.1 Sources and Their Rules\n\n**Table 6.10**\n\n*Sources of Anticipatory Action Funds*\n\n| **Source**                                            | **Legal basis**          | **30%/70% graduation**                              | **Approval route**                                                  |\n|-------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| AA line in the LDRRMFIP (no more than 10% of the 70%) | IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b)     | Yes, for hydrometeorological and geological hazards | Released under the State of Imminent Disaster per the LDRRMFIP line |\n| STF balances within their five-year period            | IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b)(i)  | No                                                  | CDRRMC programming; SP concurrence where required                   |\n| Donations, partners, ODA, other LGUs                  | IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b)(ii) | No                                                  | Per donor terms and SP Res. No. 2024-102                            |\n| NDRRM Fund augmentation                               | IRR Rule V Sec. 1(c)     | Per national rules                                  | Request through OCD; approval on NDRRMC recommendation              |\n\n## 10.2 Release Rules\n\n1.  Funds are released only while a State of Imminent Disaster declared by the City Mayor is in force (Chapter 1, SOP GOV-05), and only for AA measures in the approved PDRA. MODE''s AA checklist is derived from the ten sections of IRR Rule IV; it is a working aid, not a list fixed by law.\n\n2.  Disbursement is based on the anticipated extent of damage and disruption in the PDRA (Rule V Sec. 2).\n\n3.  **Tranche rule** (hydrometeorological and geological hazards): disbursements for interventions to be carried out more than 3 days before forecast impact may not exceed 30% of the AA allocation; up to 70% may be disbursed for interventions within 3 days of impact (Figure 6.4).\n\n4.  **Slow-onset hazards** (for example, drought): timelines and rates await the NDRRMC operational guidelines. Until then they are not graduated, but disbursements never exceed the year''s AA allocation (ADR-025 item 4).\n\n5.  **No Regrets Principle.** Goods bought for AA continue to reach the intended beneficiaries even if the hazard does not materialise (Rule V Sec. 3). Their distribution is recorded as for any other relief.\n\n6.  Procurement, budget, accounting and audit rules still apply (Rule V Sec. 2). Where the procurement ground is "time is of the essence", the BAC records it under RA 12009 Sec. 35(b) (SOP 6-07).\n\n7.  Unused AA funds accrue to the STF (Rule V Sec. 3).\n\n8.  Third parties engaged for AA are paid on a *quantum meruit* basis from the AA allocation (Rule V Sec. 3).\n\n**Figure 6.4**\n\n*Anticipatory Action Allocation and the Tranche Rule, CY 2026 Illustration*\n\n![Figure 4: 10.2 Release Rules](/library/chp6v11/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Computed from CDRRMC Res. No. 05, s. 2025 and RA 12287 IRR Rule V Sec. 2. Illustration only: the CY 2026 LDRRMFIP has no AA line, and the base may change once the Budget Office confirms the regular-revenue computation (A4.1). MODE applies the 30% and 70% limits from the aa_rules table (ADR-025).\n\nMODE applies the tranche and slow-onset rules from the aa_rules table, so a change in national guidelines is a parameter change, not a code change (ADR-025). Each disbursement is recorded against the declaration with its source, amount, purpose, intervention time and obligation reference, and MODE computes the lead time and tranche.\n\n## 10.3 Getting the AA Line into the Plan\n\nThe CY 2026 plan predates the IRR. Two routes are open, for the City to choose:\n\n-   **Amend the current or next-year LDRRMFIP** by CDRRMC resolution and SP action, realigning within the 70% component to create the AA line (precedent: Res. No. 08, s. 2025); or\n\n-   **Include it in the FY 2028 LDRRMFIP** at the next budget call, under CDP PPA 3.15.\n\nEither way, the line names the AA measures it funds (by hazard and sector, from the contingency plans and the PDRA practice in Chapter 4), the implementing offices and the procurement mode for each item. Stocks to be prepositioned on an imminent threat (CDP PPA 1.1.7) are planned under section 13 so that the AA line buys what the warehouse cannot already supply.\n\n# 11. Procurement for DRRM\n\n## 11.1 Normal Procurement\n\nDRRM goods, works and services follow the PPMP, the APP and competitive bidding or the appropriate alternative mode under RA 12009 and its IRR. The CDRRMO prepares the PPMP from the LDRRMFIP and writes the technical specifications; the BAC runs the procurement.\n\n## 11.2 Pre-arranged Supply\n\nBefore each typhoon season and at least once a year, the CDRRMO and the CGSO, with the BAC Secretariat, keep the following ready (SOP 6-06):\n\n-   a list of suppliers for relief goods, fuel, water, heavy equipment rental, transport, medicines and medical supplies, and generator service, with capacity and a contact position (FIN-F08);\n\n-   framework agreements under RA 12009 Sec. 16 and GPPB Circular No. 07-2026 where the BAC finds them suitable, stating item, maximum quantity, unit price or price basis, delivery time, call-off trigger and validity;\n\n-   standard specifications and price references for recurrent emergency items;\n\n-   agreements usable for anticipatory action, as RA 12287 IRR Rule IV contemplates (B6.6).\n\n## 11.3 Emergency Procurement\n\nNegotiated procurement – emergency cases (RA 12009 Sec. 35(b)) is used only when its ground exists: imminent danger to life or property during a State of Calamity, or time is of the essence because of a calamity and immediate action is needed to prevent loss of life or property or to restore vital public services, infrastructure or utilities. The requesting office writes the ground on FIN-F07 before the purchase, and the BAC and the HoPE confirm it under the RA 12009 IRR. Items are limited to what the emergency requires. Documentary requirements follow GPPB Circular No. 06-2026; posting and reporting follow the IRR and GPPB rules.\n\n# 12. Donations and External Assistance\n\nDonations in cash or in kind and external financial assistance are accepted, recorded and used as follows (SOP 6-08):\n\n1.  One intake point per event, staffed by the CSWDO and the CGSO with the EOC Logistics or Finance and Administration desk.\n\n2.  Acceptance follows SP Res. No. 2024-102 and COA rules; a deed of donation or acknowledgement receipt is issued (FIN-F09).\n\n3.  Cash goes to the City Treasurer and is recorded by the City Accountant under COA Circular No. 2012-002 as a receipt from other sources, kept to its stated purpose.\n\n4.  Goods are inspected, valued, recorded and stored by the CGSO (or the CSWDO for relief items), enter the stock card (SOP 6-11), and are issued against distribution records.\n\n5.  Conditions set by the donor are respected; unsuitable goods are declined politely at intake.\n\n6.  Donations used for AA are not subject to the 30%/70% graduation (IRR Rule V Sec. 2).\n\n7.  Receipts, use and balances are disclosed with the quarterly report.\n\nNo personal data of beneficiaries is copied into the donations log; distribution lists stay with the CSWDO (RA 10173).\n\n# 13. Resource Management and Prepositioned Stocks\n\nChapter 4 coordinates resources during an event: it receives requests, sets priorities and tracks each request from "requested" to "demobilized" (SOP 4-07). It can do so only if the resources exist, are known and can be released lawfully. This section sets how the City keeps relief stocks, warehouses and the resource inventory ready between events (Figure 6.5).\n\n## 13.1 Stock Levels From Scenarios\n\nStock levels are set from the contingency plan scenarios (Chapter 3, SOP 3-06), not from habit. For each priority hazard the CSWDO and the CDRRMO take the number of families expected to be displaced in the most probable scenario, the number of days before national or provincial augmentation is expected, and the ration per family, and compute the level for each item (Annex 6-D). The NDRRMC Disaster Preparedness Minimum Standards set a floor of 500 food packs and 500 hygiene kits per LGU, each good for a family of five for three days; for a city of 85 barangays the scenario figure is likely to be higher, and the higher figure applies.\n\n**Table 6.11**\n\n*Parameters That Set Prepositioned Stock Levels*\n\n| **Parameter**                | **Meaning**                                                                                                  | **Source**                                            |\n|------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|\n| Families to be served        | Families expected in evacuation centres or home-based in the most probable scenario, by cluster of barangays | Contingency plans (CP Forms); CDRA exposure data      |\n| Days of self-reliance        | Days the City must cover before augmentation arrives                                                         | Contingency plans; DSWD Field Office VIII agreements  |\n| Ration per family per day    | Content of the food pack and non-food item kits                                                              | CSWDO standard pack; DPMS Vol. 2 minimum              |\n| Target level                 | Families × days × ration, by item and by warehouse                                                           | Annex 6-D; approved by the CDRRMO Head with the CSWDO |\n| Reorder point                | Level at which replenishment starts, allowing for procurement or call-off lead time                          | SOP 6-06 framework agreement delivery time            |\n| Shelf life and rotation date | Expiry or best-before date and the date the item must be issued or replaced                                  | Supplier documents; stock card                        |\n\n*Note.* No fixed target is set in this chapter. The levels are computed and approved each year under SOP 6-11 and recorded in Annex 6-D.\n\n## 13.2 Warehousing, Rotation and Replenishment\n\n-   **Location.** Warehouses stand in low-risk areas (DPMS Vol. 2), checked against the hazard maps in Chapter 3, and are spread so that one flooded road does not cut off all stocks. The 10-Year Plan''s seven planned storage facilities are sited using the same check.\n\n-   **Condition.** Dry, secure, ventilated and pest-controlled; stock on pallets; a fire extinguisher and a posted layout; access controlled by the custodian.\n\n-   **Rotation.** First expiry, first out. Items within the rotation window are issued to regular CSWDO programmes, feeding programmes or drills before they expire, so nothing is wasted.\n\n-   **Replenishment.** When the stock of an item reaches its reorder point, the custodian raises a replenishment request against the stock replenishment line, the AA line (on an imminent threat) or the QRF (under a State of Calamity), whichever window is open.\n\n-   **Count.** A monthly spot count by the custodian and an annual physical count with the CGSO and the COA schedule; variances are explained in writing.\n\n**Figure 6.5**\n\n*Prepositioned Stock Cycle*\n\n![Figure 5: 13.2 Warehousing, Rotation and Replenishment](/library/chp6v11/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. The two shaded boxes are the decisions that keep stocks adequate. The same item carries one record across the stock card and the MODE resource list.\n\n## 13.3 The DRRM Resource Inventory\n\nThe EOC needs one view of what can be mobilised, whoever owns it (B6.3). The DRRM resource inventory therefore covers four holders: City offices; barangays (BDRRMC equipment and stocks); partners (national agencies, neighbouring LGUs under mutual aid, CSOs); and suppliers and private-sector providers under signed commitments (B6.6). City-owned items are copied from the property records; others are recorded with the agreement that commits them, the contact position, the activation trigger and the date last confirmed. An entry that has not been confirmed in the last twelve months is shown as unconfirmed.\n\nIn MODE, the resources table holds each item with category, unit, quantity, owner office, location and serviceability, and resource requests in Chapter 4 link to it. The current schema has no fields for holder type, commitment reference, reorder point or expiry date. Until they are added, these are kept on the LOG forms (section 20) and noted in the resource record; the gap is listed in section 19 for the platform custodian.\n\n## 13.4 Equipment Readiness\n\nResponse equipment and vehicles are inventoried, serviceable and on a preventive maintenance schedule (A8.3). The CDRRMO keeps the schedule and the maintenance log for DRRM equipment; serviceability is updated in the inventory after each check, so the EOC does not count equipment that cannot be used. Equipment out of service for more than one operational period is reported to the CDRRMO Head with the repair plan.\n\n# 14. Property and Asset Accountability\n\nDRRM property bought with the LDRRMF, STF, QRF, AA funds or donations is government property. The CGSO records it, issues it to the end user on a Property Acknowledgement Receipt or Inventory Custodian Slip, and includes it in the annual physical count. The CDRRMO:\n\n-   keeps a DRRM asset register linked to the MODE resource inventory, so the same item has one record;\n\n-   returns or transfers property on reassignment or separation of the accountable person;\n\n-   reports loss or damage at once, with the incident record, for relief from accountability under COA rules;\n\n-   applies the same rules to equipment lent to barangays, with a signed receipt and a return date.\n\nConsumables issued in an emergency (fuel, food packs, non-food items) are supported by issue slips, trip tickets and distribution summaries. The CDRRMO file holds aggregate distribution counts only; named lists stay with the CSWDO.\n\n# 15. Risk Financing and Insurance\n\nThe City layers its risk finance (Figure 6.6): the QRF and the 70% component for frequent, smaller events; the AA line, the STF and pre-arranged supply for imminent events; insurance for damage to public assets; and national funds, contingent credit and partners for catastrophic events. SOP 6-09 tests that layering each year against a severe credible scenario (a Thelma-type flash flood; a Yolanda-strength typhoon) and records the shortfall and the fallback sources.\n\n**Figure 6.6**\n\n*Risk Layering of DRRM Finance*\n\n![Figure 6: 15. Risk Financing and Insurance](/library/chp6v11/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter after the UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard, Essential 3, and the Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide (NEDA, 2020).\n\nThe CDRA reports that only 15% of critical point facilities are insured and no lifeline utility is insured. Under RA 656, public property is insured with the GSIS. CDP PPAs 1.3 and 1.2.3 programme insurance of buildings, equipment and early warning devices. The EOC, evacuation centres, DRRM warehouses and DRRM equipment are listed first.\n\n# 16. Standard Operating Procedures\n\nEach SOP has a control block and a step table. Every step states the actor (a position or office, never a person), the action and decision rule, inputs and outputs, and timing, control and escalation. Form codes are those of section 20. In MODE, each SOP runs as a workflow (a multi-office management cycle) or a protocol (a point-of-action checklist) that carries the same steps.\n\n> PLATFORM NOTE. SOPs 6-01 to 6-10 were seeded on 12 November 2026 under their Draft 1.0 numbers (WF-BUDGET-CYCLE for SOPs 5-01 and 5-02; WF-SOP5-03, PR-QRF-USE, PR-AA-FUNDS, WF-SOP5-06, PR-EMERGENCY-PROCURE, WF-SOP5-08, WF-SOP5-09 and WF-SOP5-10). The control blocks below give the template codes to be used after the Chapter 6 re-seed (WF-SOP6-xx); protocol codes do not change. SOPs 6-11 and 6-12 are new and their templates are proposed. Until the re-seed, the old codes remain valid aliases.\n\n## SOP 6-01 \\| LDRRMF computation and LDRRMFIP formulation, approval and adoption\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning Division, with the City Budget Office. **Purpose:** Compute the LDRRMF on the correct base and turn the LDRRMP into an approved, costed investment plan with the AA, EOC, stock replenishment and insurance lines shown.\n\n**Table 6.12**\n\n*SOP 6-01 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                                            |\n|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-BUDGET-CYCLE (with SOP 6-02)                                                                            |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMC approves the LDRRMFIP; SP appropriates and adopts                                                             |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning; City Budget Office for the computation                                                 |\n| **Trigger**                     | The City budget call each year; a supplemental budget; a realignment request                                         |\n| **Forms**                       | FIN-F01; FIN-F02 (MODE BUD-F01); GOV-F03 (CDRRMC resolution)                                                         |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A3.5 (3, Medium); A4.1 (3, Medium); A4.2 (3, Medium); A4.6 (0, High); B7.5 (3, Low); A1.7 (3, Low); C1.2 (1, Part C) |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-08; MR-03; MR-04; MR-16 (A4.6)                                                                                    |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 10121 Sec. 21; JMC 2013-1; RA 12287 IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b); NDRRMC Memo No. 147, s. 2017                            |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 6-02, 6-05, 6-09, 6-11; Chapter 3 (LDRRMP); Chapter 1 (CDRRMC)                                                  |\n\n**Table 6.13**\n\n*SOP 6-01 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Compute</th>\n<th>City Budget Officer with CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Compute 5% of estimated revenue from regular sources (NTA plus regular local revenue); split 70/30; compute the AA ceiling (10% of the 70%).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is the base complete?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Revenue estimates</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> FIN-F01</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 10 working days of the budget call</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Computation signed by the Budget Officer</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Base dispute: Treasurer and Budget Officer reconcile; unresolved to the City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Review results</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Review LDRRMP targets, last year''s utilisation (FIN-F03), STF ageing (FIN-F04), adequacy test (FIN-F10), stock plan (Annex 6-D), COA observations (FIN-F11) and IAT gaps.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is every input dated? Missing data is named, not estimated.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Prior reports</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Review note</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before drafting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Review note filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Collect proposals</th>\n<th>CDRRMO divisions; sector offices</th>\n<th><p>Submit PPAs with LDRRMP and CDP code, output, cost and procurement mode.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Complete proposal?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Office proposals</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Proposal list</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Two weeks</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> One list, dated</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Draft</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Prioritise life-safety and legal items first; tag each line; show the AA, EOC operations, stock replenishment and insurance lines; apply the DRR-CCA screening to capital items (C1.2).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Does every line trace to an LDRRMP PPA and an allowable use?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Proposal list</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> FIN-F02 draft LDRRMFIP</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Per budget calendar</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Lines without a code removed or justified in writing</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Cost and plan procurement</th>\n<th>Administration and Training with BAC Secretariat</th>\n<th><p>Cost each line; prepare PPMP entries; identify items for framework agreements.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Procurement mode fits the item and timing?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft LDRRMFIP</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> PPMP; procurement plan</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before the CDRRMC meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> BAC Secretariat review</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> BAC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Endorse</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head; LFC and LDC representatives</th>\n<th><p>Present to the Local Finance Committee and Local Development Council; record CDRRMO input.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Endorsed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft LDRRMFIP</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Minutes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Per budget calendar</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Minutes filed in the Vault</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Approve</th>\n<th>CDRRMC (CDRRMO as secretariat)</th>\n<th><p>Approve the LDRRMFIP by resolution.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Approved, returned or approved with changes? Returned goes to step 4.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Endorsed draft</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> GOV-F03 resolution</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Quarterly or special meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Resolution numbered and filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>8. Adopt</th>\n<th>SP</th>\n<th><p>Appropriate and adopt the LDRRMFIP with the annual budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Adopted?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> CDRRMC resolution</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> SP resolution or ordinance</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> With the annual budget</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> SP act filed with the LDRRMFIP</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Secretariat follows up before year-end</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>9. Publish and load</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Load approved lines into MODE (aa_fund_years for the AA line) and post as required.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Lines match the adopted plan?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Adopted plan</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Controlled LDRRMFIP in MODE</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 10 working days of adoption</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Second person checks the load</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** FIN-F01; FIN-F02; PPMP; LFC/LDC minutes; CDRRMC resolution; SP act; controlled LDRRMFIP in MODE.\n\n## SOP 6-02 \\| LDRRMF execution, utilisation monitoring, quarterly reporting and disclosure\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Administration and Training Division, with Research and Planning. **Purpose:** Keep each LDRRMFIP line moving from allotment to use, report it quarterly and disclose it.\n\n**Table 6.14**\n\n*SOP 6-02 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-BUDGET-CYCLE (with SOP 6-01)                                                               |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | Budget Officer, Accountant, Treasurer and HoPE within their powers; CDRRMC and SP for realignment       |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Administration and Training                                                                      |\n| **Trigger**                     | Start of the fiscal year; end of each quarter                                                           |\n| **Forms**                       | FIN-F03 (MODE BUD-F02)                                                                                  |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A4.4 (2, Medium); A10.5 (2, Low); A10.3 (3, Low)                                                        |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-08; MR-07; MR-11                                                                                     |\n| **Policy basis**                | JMC 2013-1; COA Circular No. 2012-002; DILG Full Disclosure Policy issuances, including MC No. 2012-141 |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 6-01, 6-03, 6-10; Chapter 7 (reporting calendar)                                                   |\n\n**Table 6.15**\n\n*SOP 6-02 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Release</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Request allotment and obligation for each line by its planned quarter.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Line due this quarter?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> LDRRMFIP</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Obligation requests</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Per quarter plan</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Request cites the LDRRMFIP line</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Track</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Record obligation, procurement stage, delivery and disbursement per line from Budget and Accounting records.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Any line stalled for more than 30 days?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Budget and Accounting records</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Line tracker</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Monthly</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Tracker dated</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head for stalled lines</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Reconcile</th>\n<th>City Accountant with Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Reconcile the CDRRMO tracker with the books (LDRRMF, QRF, STF by year).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Variance found?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Tracker; ledgers</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Reconciled figures</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 15 days after quarter-end</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Variances explained in writing</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> City Accountant</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Report</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Prepare FIN-F03: obligation, disbursement and completion rates by component; QRF and STF balances; stock levels against targets; PPAs at risk; results against the LDRRMP.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Complete?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Reconciled figures</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> FIN-F03</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> For the next CDRRMC quarterly meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Figures match the reconciliation</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Decide</th>\n<th>CDRRMC</th>\n<th><p>Note the report; direct catch-up or realignment.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is utilisation of the 70% on track for 80%? A third-quarter shortfall triggers a realignment proposal (SOP 6-01 steps 4–8).</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> FIN-F03</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Minutes; resolution</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Quarterly meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Decision recorded</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Disclose</th>\n<th>Administration and Training with the City Full Disclosure Policy focal person</th>\n<th><p>Post the LDRRMF utilisation report as the Full Disclosure Policy requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Posted within the period?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> FIN-F03</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Posting proof (link or photo)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within the FDP period</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Proof filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head for a missed posting</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. File</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>File reports and proofs in the Vault.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Filed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Reports; proofs</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Vault entries</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Same week</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Vault reference on FIN-F03</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Line tracker; reconciliation; FIN-F03; CDRRMC minutes; posting proof; Vault entries.\n\n## SOP 6-03 \\| Special Trust Fund ledger, ageing and programming\n\n**Process owner:** City Accountant (ledger) with CDRRMO Research and Planning (programming). **Purpose:** Use every unexpended LDRRMF balance for DRRM within its five-year period.\n\n**Table 6.16**\n\n*SOP 6-03 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                            |\n|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP6-03 (seeded as WF-SOP5-03)                                          |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMC programmes; SP adopts or concurs                                              |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | City Accountant; CDRRMO Research and Planning                                        |\n| **Trigger**                     | Year-end closing; LDRRMFIP preparation; any STF reallocation request                 |\n| **Forms**                       | FIN-F04                                                                              |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A4.3 (3, Low); A4.6 (0, High)                                                        |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-08; MR-16                                                                         |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 10121 Sec. 21; COA Circular No. 2012-002; RA 12287 IRR Rule V Secs. 1(b)(i) and 3 |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 6-01, 6-05                                                                      |\n\n**Table 6.17**\n\n*SOP 6-03 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Close the year</th>\n<th>City Accountant</th>\n<th><p>Determine unexpended LDRRMF (70% and QRF) and transfer it to the STF.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Balance confirmed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Year-end books</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> STF entry</p></th>\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch6v1_v1.1.md (Chapter 6, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0)','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('e4c40f42-2c02-513c-9bfa-7f3af91ec663'::uuid,'d7190e43-31f8-55d7-b646-04574073dedf'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'**CHAPTER 5**\n\nRECOVERY, REHABILITATION AND BUILD BACK BETTER\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)**\n\n**AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft*\n\n*Subject to institutional, operational, technical and legal validation*\n\n**Draft 1.0 \\| 12 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# Document Control\n\n**Table 5.1**\n\n*Document Control*\n\n| **Document attribute**     | **Control information**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Document title**         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 5                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Chapter title**          | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| **Position in the Manual** | Fifth of ten chapters in the structure adopted on 12 November 2026. It follows Chapter 4 (Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response) and precedes Chapter 6 (DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability).                                                            |\n| **Document status**        | Working draft for technical, institutional and legal validation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| **Document owner**         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| **Technical custodian**    | CDRRMO Head / Research and Planning focal person for recovery, with the City Planning and Development Office (CPDO) as proposed technical lead for recovery planning                                                                                                                                             |\n| **Reviewing offices**      | City Legal Office; CPDO; City Engineering Office and Building Official; CSWDO; City Health Office; PESO; City Agriculture Office; City Environment and Natural Resources Office; City Budget Office; City Accountant; Business Permits and Licensing Office; OCD Region VIII; DEPDev Region VIII (formerly NEDA) |\n| **Version / date**         | Draft 1.0 / 12 November 2026                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| **Approval authority**     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Review cycle**           | At least annually; after every event that triggers a PDNA or a rehabilitation and recovery program; after a material legal, policy or plan change                                                                                                                                                                |\n\n## Revision and Approval Record\n\n**Table 5.2**\n\n*Revision and Approval Record*\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | **Prepared / reviewed by** | **Approval** |\n|-------------|-------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | 12 Nov 2026 | Initial assessment-informed working draft under the adopted ten-chapter structure. Applies the Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide (NEDA, 2020) to Ormoc; reconciles the A9 rows and the related A8.7, A10.6 and Part C rows with the controlled IAT v1.7 workbook and the Traceability and Data Dictionary v1.2; continues from Chapter 4 SOP 4-09 and form ER-F05; eight SOPs with control blocks; six figures; APA captions; Annexes 5-A to 5-E. | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nChapter 4 ends when the activated EOC stands down. SOP 4-09 closes the EOC in a controlled manner, and its transition register (form ER-F05) lists what is still open: displaced families who cannot yet go home, damaged houses and lifelines, lost incomes, unresolved protection cases, and recovery recommendations. Chapter 5 receives that register. It sets out how the City turns the open items into an approved, funded and monitored program that restores living conditions and leaves Ormoc less exposed than before the event.\n\nRecovery is the longest and most expensive phase of a disaster. It is also the phase in which the City has the most room to reduce future risk: houses can be rebuilt outside hazard zones, bridges can be rebuilt to a higher standard, and livelihoods can be diversified. The Philippine DRRM system names this phase "rehabilitation and recovery", one of its four thematic areas, and sets "Build Back Better" as its guiding principle (NEDA, 2020). Build Back Better means that reconstruction restores pre-disaster conditions and also raises resilience standards and includes climate adaptation and mitigation measures.\n\nOrmoc knows the cost of recovery from experience. The 1991 flash flood (Typhoon Uring) remains the deadliest event in the City''s record. Super Typhoon Yolanda (2013), the magnitude 6.5 Leyte earthquake (2017), Typhoon Ursula (2019) and Typhoon Odette (2021) each left damage that took months or years to repair (Table 5.3). The City has carried out a PDNA before (the 2017 earthquake PDNA cited in the DRRM 10-Year Plan) and runs a cash-for-work program after disasters. What it lacks is a standing system: a pre-disaster recovery framework, a trained PDNA team, a decision route that is written down, and a way to track recovery projects to completion.\n\nThis chapter applies the same systems-thinking and Total Quality Management (TQM) approach as Chapters 1 to 4. Recovery is treated as a cycle with feedback: plan before the event; assess after it; program, fund and build; track to completion; evaluate; and feed the lessons into the land use plan, the contingency plans and the LDRRMP. A program document is not evidence of recovery. Recovery is complete only when the planned outputs are delivered, the intended outcomes are checked, and the residual risk is lower than before.\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. Draft 1.0 copies the A9 rows and the related A8.7, A10.6, A3.4 and Part C rows from the controlled IAT v1.7 workbook (reference/Ormoc_CDRRMO-EOC_Institutional_Assessment_Tool_v1-7.xlsx, 12 November 2026) without change. Priority bands were computed from the workbook formula ((4 − score) × weight × 1.5 for Mandatory, × 1.0 for Good Practice and Part C). All rows are "Pending validation" in the workbook, so the scores remain provisional until the December 2026 validation workshop. The "Feeds Manual Chapter" column of the workbook still reads "MOO Ch.8" for the A9 rows; under the adopted structure these rows feed Chapter 5 (see the Validation and Source Control Register).\n\n## 1.1 What the Records Show\n\n**Table 5.3**\n\n*Most Significant Disasters Recorded in the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan*\n\n| **Year and event**                   | **Dead** | **Missing**     | **Injured** | **Estimated cost of damage (PHP) and source**                                                                                  |\n|--------------------------------------|----------|-----------------|-------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1991: Typhoon Uring (flash flood)    | 4,922    | More than 3,000 | 3,020       | 604,000,000.00                                                                                                                 |\n| 2013: Super Typhoon Yolanda          | 38       | 0               | 5,089       | Figure printed in the plan is not plausible and must be verified before use (see note). Source: Ormoc City DRRM Plan 2016–2020 |\n| 2017: Magnitude 6.5 Leyte earthquake | 2        | 0               | 329         | 330,764,454.02. Source: PDNA report ("Paglaum")                                                                                |\n| 2017: Tropical Storm Urduja          | 3        | 0               | 0           | 187,898,674.48. Source: CDRRMO RDANA reports                                                                                   |\n| 2019: Typhoon Ursula                 | 0        | 0               | 59          | 159,198,509.59. Source: CDRRMO RDANA reports                                                                                   |\n| 2021: Typhoon Odette                 | 0        | 0               | 3           | 159,676,397.89. Source: CDRRMO RDANA reports                                                                                   |\n\n*Note.* Figures are copied from Table 3 of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 (City Government of Ormoc, 2023). The plan prints the Yolanda damage as "Php 8,000,000,000,000.00", which is not plausible for one city and is probably a typographical error; the CDRRMO should confirm the correct figure from the source plan before it is cited. Ordered by year.\n\nThe assessment records five A9 indicators for rehabilitation and recovery. Figure 5.1 shows their scores. The domain index is 1.38 (score × weight summed over the five rows, divided by the total weight of 8), which falls in the Initial band (0.5–1.49). The one Mandatory row, A9.2, scores 2, so Mandatory compliance in the domain is 0 of 1. A9.2 was lowered from 4 to 2 in v1.7 because its only evidence was FGD self-scoring; the score can be restored at validation once the rehabilitation plans and implementation reports are attached.\n\n**Figure 5.1**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Scores for Domain A9, Rehabilitation and Recovery*\n\n![Figure 1: 1.1 What the Records Show](/library/chp5v10/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Scores are copied from the A_CDRRMO sheet of IAT v1.7. M = Mandatory; GP = Good Practice; w = weight. The orange bar is the only Mandatory indicator. The domain index is (1×2 + 2×2 + 1×1 + 2×1 + 1×2) ÷ 8 = 1.375, banded on the unrounded value. All rows are pending validation.\n\n**Table 5.4**\n\n*Assessment Findings That Shape This Chapter*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                           | **Score** | **Priority** | **Evidence observed and gap (summarised from the workbook)**                                                                                                                               |\n|--------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A9.1   | PDNA capacity: trained team and templates ready (GP, w2)                                                           | 1         | Medium       | No trained PDNA team. The 10-Year Plan plans five PDNA positions (2025).                                                                                                                   |\n| A9.2   | Rehabilitation and recovery plan after major events, with build-back-better and resettlement provisions (M, w2)    | 2         | Medium       | FGD self-score of 4 lowered to 2 (Methodology §5.2). Rehabilitation plans and implementation reports are to be validated.                                                                  |\n| A9.3   | Recovery data (damage, losses, livelihood, housing) tracked to completion (GP, w1)                                 | 1         | Low          | PDNA and RDANA reports exist, but there is no recovery monitoring matrix.                                                                                                                  |\n| A9.4   | Psychosocial and livelihood recovery coordinated with CSWDO, CHO, PESO (GP, w1)                                    | 2         | Low          | A cash-for-work program exists; referral pathways and MOAs were not sighted.                                                                                                               |\n| A9.5   | Pre-disaster recovery framework: roles, PDNA activation, temporary shelter, debris, recovery fund sources (GP, w2) | 1         | Medium       | No framework. The 10-Year Plan and CDP 2024–2030 (PPA 4.1) plan a TWG for the Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Plan and PlanSmart Ready to Rebuild training; no TWG order was sighted. |\n| A8.7   | MDM and RDANA teams designated and trained (M, w2)                                                                 | 2         | Medium       | MDM protocol covers COVID-19 only (CDRRMC Res. 06 s. 2021); no all-hazards MDM or RDANA team is designated.                                                                                |\n| A10.6  | After-action reviews after significant events, lessons integrated into plans (GP, w2)                              | 2         | Medium       | FGD self-score of 4 lowered to 2; AAR reports and plan revisions are to be validated.                                                                                                      |\n| A3.4   | PSCP covering CDRRMO and essential city services (M, w2)                                                           | 2         | Medium       | PSCP formulation is planned (CDP PPA 2.5); no PSCP, BIA or essential-functions list in the document set.                                                                                   |\n\n*Note.* Priority is the workbook band of (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 (Mandatory) or × 1.0 (Good Practice): High 9 or more, Medium 4 to 8.9, Low below 4. A8.7 and A10.6 are shared with Chapters 4 and 7; A3.4 belongs to Chapter 3 and is listed because continuity of City services is the first condition of recovery. Annex 5-A gives the full row set, owners and targets.\n\nThe DRRM 10-Year Plan reaches the same conclusion in its own words. It records that the City has an approved shelter plan and an approved CLUP and Zoning Ordinance but still has to start land development for identified settlement sites; that the cash-for-work program works but there is no loan program for micro, small and medium enterprises; that farmers have limited insurance cover and there is no buffer seed storage; and that RDNA/PDNA teams still need to be organised and their protocols institutionalised (City Government of Ormoc, 2023). The plan already budgets for most of the remedies (Table 5.5).\n\n**Table 5.5**\n\n*Recovery and Rehabilitation PPAs Already Programmed in the DRRM 10-Year Plan*\n\n| **Program, project or activity**          | **Output in the plan**                                                                         | **Office responsible**                                       | **Partner agencies**             |\n|-------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| Training of PDNA personnel                | Five PDNA technical personnel hired (2023–2025); additional personnel trained later            | CDRRMO                                                       | OCD; NEDA (now DEPDev)           |\n| Disaster Recovery and Rehabilitation Plan | TWG created; PlanSmart Ready to Rebuild training; plan formulated, then revisited and updated  | CDRRMO                                                       | NEDA (now DEPDev); OCD; PHIVOLCS |\n| Buffer seed stocks and storage facilities | Program of works and feasibility study; storage facility built; seeds procured and distributed | City Agriculture Office; City Engineering Office             | Visayas State University         |\n| Lot acquisition for LGU-funded housing    | Lot acquisition through MOA/MOU and legal processes; housing for target beneficiaries          | CDRRMO; CPDO; City Legal Office; OCHO (as named in the plan) | NHA; Pag-IBIG; SHFC              |\n| Capacity building on PDNA                 | PDNA training for CDRRMC and BDRRMC members, then barangay officials                           | CDRRMO                                                       | OCD                              |\n| MSME access to finance                    | Operational LGU-managed MSME loan program                                                      | BPLO                                                         | SB Corp; financial institutions  |\n| Post-disaster waste management            | Clearing and clean-up operations; waste collection vehicle (dump truck with boom)              | ENRO                                                         | —                                |\n\n*Note.* Summarised from Tables 20 and 24 of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032. Budgets and years are in the plan and its LDRRMF Investment Plan (Table 32); Chapter 6 governs their financing.\n\n## 1.2 Design Considerations\n\n**Table 5.6**\n\n*Recovery Design Considerations and the Chapter 5 Response*\n\n| **Design consideration**         | **Existing context / evidence**                                                                                                                                                     | **Chapter 5 response**                                                                                                                   |\n|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Recovery starts before the event | A9.5 scores 1; no pre-disaster recovery framework. The NEDA guide says pre-disaster recovery planning should be done by local DRRMCs.                                               | SOP 5-01 makes the framework a standing, annually updated product of the CDRRMC, adopted as the recovery annex of each contingency plan. |\n| Seamless handover from response  | Chapter 4 SOP 4-09 produces the ER-F05 transition register and the ER-F06 AAR record.                                                                                               | SOP 5-03 receives every ER-F05 item, names its recovery owner and opens the recovery record. Nothing is closed by deactivation alone.    |\n| Assessment capacity              | A8.7 and A9.1: no designated RDANA or PDNA team; five PDNA positions planned.                                                                                                       | SOP 5-02 sets the RDANA-to-PDNA decision route, team composition by function and approval.                                               |\n| Who decides                      | RA 10121 places recovery under the local DRRMC; the NEDA guide sets the CDRRMC as the body that decides a city PDNA and the Sanggunian as the body that approves the local program. | Decision bodies are named in each SOP control block, separately from the operational owner.                                              |\n| Build Back Better                | A9.2 scores 2; C1.4 (permit review) 2; C2.1 (Building Official assessment) 1.                                                                                                       | SOP 5-05 screens every recovery project for hazard, siting, design standard and maintenance before it is funded.                         |\n| People, not only structures      | A9.4 scores 2; no referral pathways or MOAs.                                                                                                                                        | SOP 5-06 sets one referral pathway across CSWDO, CHO, PESO, City Agriculture and the housing office.                                     |\n| Track to completion              | A9.3 scores 1; no recovery monitoring matrix.                                                                                                                                       | SOP 5-07 and form REC-F06 track every recovery PPA to closeout; evaluation feeds Chapter 7.                                              |\n| Privacy                          | MODE stores no names of evacuees, patients or informants (RA 10173).                                                                                                                | Beneficiary lists stay in the restricted records of the office that serves the person; MODE and public reports hold aggregates only.     |\n\n## 1.3 The Recovery Continuum\n\nRecovery overlaps with response. Early recovery starts while relief is still being delivered; rehabilitation and reconstruction continue long after the EOC has closed. Figure 5.2 places the phases, the Chapter 4 handover and the eight SOPs of this chapter on one line.\n\n**Figure 5.2**\n\n*The Recovery Continuum From the Chapter 4 Handover to Closeout*\n\n![Figure 2: 1.3 The Recovery Continuum](/library/chp5v10/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Phase durations follow the NEDA (2020) guide: short-term PPAs within a year and medium-term PPAs within the next three to four years; the actual timeframes are set by the planning committee for each event. Prepared for this chapter.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter sets the City''s minimum arrangements for planning recovery before a disaster, assessing damage, losses and needs after it, preparing and approving a rehabilitation and recovery program, delivering it to Build Back Better standards, and tracking it to completion. It does not create new legal powers. Decisions on plans, budgets, land use, relocation and procurement stay with the authorities named in law and in valid City issuances.\n\nThe intended institutional results are:\n\n-   A pre-disaster recovery framework, adopted by the CDRRMC and updated each year, that names roles, PDNA activation, temporary shelter, debris management and recovery fund sources before an event (A9.5).\n\n-   A designated and trained RDANA and PDNA capability, with a written decision route from rapid assessment to PDNA and to CDRRMC approval (A8.7, A9.1).\n\n-   A controlled handover from response to recovery in which every open item from the Chapter 4 transition register has a named recovery owner (SOP 4-09 to SOP 5-03).\n\n-   A rehabilitation and recovery program, prepared after each major event, approved through the proper route and containing build-back-better and resettlement provisions (A9.2).\n\n-   Build-back-better screening of recovery projects before funding, linked to the land use plan, the zoning ordinance and the Building Official''s assessments (C1.4, C2.1).\n\n-   One referral pathway for psychosocial, social protection, livelihood, agriculture and shelter services, with consent and privacy controls (A9.4).\n\n-   A recovery monitoring matrix that tracks damage, losses, livelihood and housing outputs to completion and reports them to the CDRRMC (A9.3).\n\n-   Transparent recovery communication with a feedback and grievance channel for affected people.\n\n-   Outcome evaluation and after-action learning that revise plans and standards (A10.6, Chapter 7).\n\n# 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\nThis chapter applies to recovery from any disaster that affects the City, whatever the hazard: tropical cyclones, floods and flash floods, landslides, earthquakes, tsunami, fire and industrial or geothermal incidents. It applies to the CDRRMC and its proposed Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee, the CDRRMO, the City offices that deliver recovery services, the barangays and their BDRRMCs, and the partners that work through City coordination arrangements. It covers pre-disaster recovery planning, damage and needs assessment, program formulation and approval, delivery, monitoring, evaluation and closeout.\n\nThe lead council depends on the area affected. The City leads when the disaster is confined to Ormoc and affects two or more barangays; a BDRRMC handles a disaster confined to one barangay. Because Ormoc is an independent component city, when the same disaster affects Ormoc and the Province of Leyte or another independent city, the RDRRMC VIII leads the overall rehabilitation and recovery coordination (NEDA, 2020). In that case the City still prepares its own local program, which feeds the regional program.\n\n**Table 5.7**\n\n*Institutional Authority Retained and Chapter 5 Interfaces*\n\n| **Institution / actor**                                  | **Authority and accountability retained**                                          | **Recovery interface under Chapter 5**                                                                                                                                                                         |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **City Mayor**                                           | Executive authority under the Local Government Code and valid City issuances.      | Oversees the overall recovery effort with the support of the CDRRMO Head; directs City offices; submits budget measures and requests for assistance.                                                           |\n| **Sangguniang Panlungsod**                               | Legislative authority, including appropriations and local legislation.             | Approves the local rehabilitation and recovery program and the related appropriations and ordinances (NEDA, 2020, citing the Local Government Code).                                                           |\n| **CDRRMC**                                               | Statutory policy, coordination and oversight body under RA 10121.                  | Adopts the pre-disaster recovery framework; decides whether a City PDNA is conducted; approves the PDNA; prepares the rehabilitation and recovery program; monitors implementation.                            |\n| **Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee (proposed)**     | None of its own; a committee of the CDRRMC.                                        | Coordinates sector sub-committees, consolidates PPAs, tracks progress and resolves implementation issues. Proposed technical lead: CPDO, the recovery thematic focal office of the CDRRMP TWG (EO 2022-00020). |\n| **CDRRMO Head and CDRRMO**                               | Permanent technical office and secretariat of the CDRRMC.                          | Assists the Mayor in overseeing rehabilitation; leads the PDNA; keeps the recovery baseline and the monitoring matrix; runs SOPs 5-01, 5-02, 5-03 and 5-07.                                                    |\n| **CPDO**                                                 | Development planning, CLUP and investment programming.                             | Technical lead for program formulation, land use framework and integration into the CDP, AIP and CLUP.                                                                                                         |\n| **City Engineering Office / Building Official**          | Infrastructure delivery; building permits and structural safety under PD 1096.     | Infrastructure sub-committee lead; structural assessments; build-back-better design review (SOP 5-05).                                                                                                         |\n| **CSWDO**                                                | Social welfare, protection and camp coordination (JMC No. 2 s. 2021).              | Social services sub-committee lead; beneficiary registration; social protection, emergency shelter assistance and protection referrals.                                                                        |\n| **City Health Office**                                   | Public health and mental health services (RA 11036).                               | Health and MHPSS recovery; referrals; health facility restoration needs.                                                                                                                                       |\n| **PESO / BPLO / City Agriculture Office**                | Employment facilitation; business permits and MSME support; agricultural services. | Livelihood and agriculture sub-committees; emergency employment, MSME loan program, crop insurance claims and inputs.                                                                                          |\n| **City Budget Office, Treasurer, Accountant, BAC**       | Budget, treasury, accounting and procurement controls.                             | Resource mobilisation sub-committee; all financing and procurement steps follow Chapter 6.                                                                                                                     |\n| **Barangays / BDRRMCs**                                  | Barangay governance and local DRRM.                                                | Report local needs; help validate damage data; take part in planning and monitoring; lead recovery confined to one barangay.                                                                                   |\n| **OCD VIII, DEPDev VIII, RDRRMC VIII and line agencies** | National and regional mandates and funds.                                          | Assist the PDNA on City request; review the PDNA and program when NDRRMF funding is sought; lead regional recovery coordination when more than one LGU of the required kind is affected.                       |\n| **Private sector, CSOs, academe, development partners**  | Their own resources, contracts and organisational rules.                           | Contribute through pre-agreed MOAs and through City coordination channels (Chapter 8); independent monitoring where agreed.                                                                                    |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The decision and approval roles above follow the NEDA Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide (2020), which is national guidance, not statute. The City Legal Office shall confirm the approval route for the local program, the role of the CDRRMC and the Sanggunian, and whether a Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee should be created by EO, by CDRRMC resolution or within the CDRRMP TWG under EO 2022-00020. NEDA has been reorganised as the Department of Economy, Planning, and Development (DEPDev) by RA No. 12145 (2025); references in the guide to NEDA are read as DEPDev.\n\nThree boundaries apply throughout. First, recovery begins inside response but does not take over response: life-safety actions stay under the Incident Commander and the EOC until the handover in SOP 4-09. Second, this chapter does not create land use restrictions, no-build zones, relocation orders or expenditure authority; these require the instruments named in Chapter 1 and Chapter 6. Third, Part C offices (CPDO, City Engineer, CENRO, utilities) keep ownership of their interface indicators; the CDRRMO coordinates and reports.\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\nThe provisions of this chapter shall be read with the legal and policy hierarchy established in Chapter 1. Table 5.8 lists the principal anchors. The City Legal Office, OCD Region VIII and DEPDev Region VIII shall verify current texts and local approval routes before adoption.\n\n**Table 5.8**\n\n*Principal Legal, Policy and Operational Instruments for Recovery*\n\n| **Instrument / guidance**                                                                                                      | **Relevance to recovery**                                                                                                                                      | **Application in this chapter**                                                                            |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA No. 10121 (2010) and its IRR                                                                                                | Rehabilitation and recovery is a thematic area of the DRRM system; local DRRMCs and offices; LDRRMF including the Quick Response Fund for relief and recovery. | Basis of the CDRRMC and CDRRMO roles; indicator A9.2 cites Sec. 12(c)(11). Funding rules are in Chapter 6. |\n| RA No. 7160 (1991), Local Government Code                                                                                      | Local executive and legislative powers; local development planning and appropriations.                                                                         | Sanggunian approval of the program and appropriations.                                                     |\n| NDRRM Framework and NDRRMP 2020–2030                                                                                           | Rehabilitation and recovery outcomes and the Build Back Better principle.                                                                                      | Program outcomes and indicators; A9.2 and A9.5 cite the recovery priority area.                            |\n| Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide (NEDA, 2020)                                                               | Recovery framework; pre-disaster activities; PDNA and RaDE; program contents; coordination structure; financing; communications; monitoring and evaluation.    | Primary guidance for every section and SOP of this chapter.                                                |\n| RA No. 12145 (2025), Economy, Planning and Development Act                                                                     | Reorganised NEDA into DEPDev.                                                                                                                                  | References to NEDA in national guidance are read as DEPDev.                                                |\n| OCD PDNA Guidance Notes; NDRRMC RDANA guidelines                                                                               | PDNA methodology, timeframes and team composition; RDANA triggers and forms.                                                                                   | SOP 5-02. Obtain the current editions from OCD VIII before the team is trained.                            |\n| Sendai Framework 2015–2030 (Priority 4; Targets C and D); UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard, Essential 10                    | Build Back Better; economic loss and critical infrastructure damage; expediting recovery.                                                                      | Good-practice benchmark for A9.3 and A9.5; data fields of the monitoring matrix.                           |\n| DSWD–DILG–DOH–DepEd JMC No. 2, s. 2021                                                                                         | Camp coordination and camp management; IDP protection, return and durable solutions.                                                                           | Closure of evacuation centres and transition of displaced families (SOP 5-03, 5-06).                       |\n| RA No. 11036 (Mental Health Act); RA No. 9710; RA No. 7277 as amended; RA No. 9994; RA No. 10821; RA No. 8371                  | Mental health and psychosocial support; gender, disability, older persons, children and indigenous peoples.                                                    | Inclusion safeguards and the referral pathway (sections 10–11, SOP 5-06).                                  |\n| RA No. 7279 (Urban Development and Housing Act) and DHSUD issuances                                                            | Socialised housing; conditions and due process for relocation and resettlement.                                                                                | Resettlement safeguards (section 9.3). Relocation is a last resort.                                        |\n| PD No. 1096 (National Building Code) and the structural code in force                                                          | Building permits and structural standards.                                                                                                                     | Build-back-better screening (SOP 5-05).                                                                    |\n| RA No. 12076 (Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act)                                                                                        | Standards for permanent evacuation centres.                                                                                                                    | Rebuilding or retrofitting evacuation centres.                                                             |\n| DHSUD/HLURB Supplemental Guidelines on Mainstreaming CC and DRR in the CLUP                                                    | Climate and disaster risk assessment; risk-sensitive land use.                                                                                                 | Post-disaster land use framework (section 9.2).                                                            |\n| RA No. 656 (Property Insurance Law); PCIC charter (PD 1467 as amended)                                                         | Insurance of government property through GSIS; crop and livestock insurance.                                                                                   | Recovery financing options (section 6.6), detailed in Chapter 6.                                           |\n| RA No. 12009 (New Government Procurement Act) and its IRR                                                                      | Procurement, including emergency and negotiated procurement.                                                                                                   | All recovery procurement follows Chapter 6.                                                                |\n| RA No. 10173 and applicable NPC issuances                                                                                      | Personal and sensitive personal information of beneficiaries.                                                                                                  | Data minimisation and restricted beneficiary records (section 13.4, SOP 5-06).                             |\n| Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032; CDP 2024–2030; CLUP 2017–2027 and Zoning Ordinance; LCCAP 2019–2030; City Shelter Plan | Local recovery PPAs, development priorities, land use and hazard zoning.                                                                                       | Source of local PPAs (Table 5.5) and of the land use framework.                                            |\n| EO No. 2022-00020 (CDRRMP Technical Working Group); EO No. 2024-032A (CDRRMC)                                                  | CPDO named as the thematic focal office for recovery in the CDRRMP TWG; CDRRMC composition and functions.                                                      | Proposed technical lead for the Rehabilitation and Recovery Committee.                                     |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The NEDA guide describes national and regional practice; several statements (for example, that the NDRRMF covers rehabilitation of disasters in the last two years and is open to an LGU only when its LDRRMF is insufficient) reflect the rules at the time of publication. The CDRRMO shall confirm current NDRRMF access rules with OCD VIII and DBM before any proposal is prepared. The DSWD administrative orders on cash-for-work and emergency shelter assistance cited in the guide may have been superseded; CSWDO shall confirm the current issuances.\n\n# 5. Systems-Thinking and TQM Principles for Recovery\n\n**Table 5.9**\n\n*Systems-Thinking and TQM Principles Applied to Recovery*\n\n| **Principle**                          | **Required application**                                                                                                                      | **Quality control**                                                                                     |\n|----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Recovery starts before the event**   | Baseline data, scenarios, committee roles, scalable PPAs and financing options are prepared in normal times.                                  | Framework adopted and reviewed each year; recovery annex in every contingency plan.                     |\n| **Build Back Better**                  | Reconstruction raises resilience standards and includes climate adaptation, not only restoration.                                             | Every funded project passes BBB screening (REC-F07) with a recorded decision.                           |\n| **Evidence before money**              | Needs and costs come from the PDNA (or a RaDE when the PDNA is delayed); targets come from the PDNA.                                          | Each PPA cites its PDNA line or justification; no unsourced figures.                                    |\n| **People-centred and inclusive**       | Needs of women, children, older persons, persons with disability, indigenous peoples and the poorest households are identified and addressed. | Sex-, age- and disability-disaggregated aggregates; inclusion check in BBB screening; feedback channel. |\n| **Risk-informed land use**             | Rebuilding follows the CLUP, the Zoning Ordinance and current hazard maps.                                                                    | Siting check with CPDO and the Zoning Administrator before approval.                                    |\n| **One recovery picture**               | One monitoring matrix for all PPAs regardless of fund source.                                                                                 | Monthly consolidation; reconciliation with budget and procurement records.                              |\n| **PDCA / TQM**                         | Plan, deliver, check outputs and outcomes, correct.                                                                                           | Progress reports, problem-solving sessions, field validation and outcome evaluation.                    |\n| **Local ownership and accountability** | The City leads; partners align with the City program.                                                                                         | Partner commitments recorded in the matrix; public progress reports.                                    |\n| **Privacy by design**                  | Only the data needed to serve a person is collected, and only by the office that serves them.                                                 | No names in MODE or in general reports; restricted records with access logs.                            |\n\n# 6. Pre-Disaster Recovery Planning\n\nMost of the work that makes recovery fast is done before the disaster. The NEDA guide lists six pre-disaster activities for local DRRMCs: build the recovery database, prepare science-based scenarios, identify critical areas and populations, set up the recovery organisation, list PPAs that can be scaled up or reprogrammed, and arrange recovery financing (NEDA, 2020). This section assigns each one to an owner. SOP 5-01 turns them into an annual cycle, and Annex 5-C gives the outline of the resulting framework document.\n\n## 6.1 Recovery Baseline Database\n\nThe CDRRMO, as CDRRMC secretariat, keeps the recovery baseline. It reuses the Chapter 3 risk-information base and adds the data that recovery planning needs: population disaggregated by sex, age and disability at barangay level; housing and settlement data; livelihoods, farms and fisheries; MSMEs; critical facilities and lifelines; and social services. Annex 5-E lists the minimum data, adapted from Annex A of the NEDA guide. The barangay unit is the PSGC barangay (85 barangays as of 31 July 2025), pending the master-list reconciliation recorded in Chapter 3.\n\n-   Keep the baseline as aggregates by barangay. Household lists stay with the office that owns them (for example, CBMS with CPDO and beneficiary lists with CSWDO).\n\n-   Record the source and date of every dataset. A blank value means "not reported", never zero.\n\n-   Update the baseline at least once a year as part of SOP 5-01, and after every event that triggers a PDNA.\n\n## 6.2 Recovery Scenarios\n\nRecovery scenarios describe what a disaster of a given size would do to people, buildings, lifelines and livelihoods. They are taken from the CDRA and the contingency-plan scenarios of Chapter 3, so that the same most-probable and most-severe scenarios drive preparedness, response and recovery (C1.1). For each priority hazard the framework records the likely number of damaged houses, interrupted lifelines, affected farms and businesses, and the recovery needs these imply. Scenarios are prepared by the City with technical support from PHIVOLCS, PAGASA, MGB and NAMRIA; local and indigenous knowledge may be used.\n\n## 6.3 Critical Areas, Assets and Populations\n\nFrom the scenarios the CDRRMO and CPDO identify, before any event, the areas, assets and population groups whose recovery comes first: lifeline roads and bridges named in the CDRA, water and power facilities, the port, hospitals, schools used as evacuation centres, settlements in high-hazard zones, and barangays with high poverty incidence. The list sets the order of the PDNA field work and of the first PPAs.\n\n## 6.4 Pre-agreed Recovery Organisation\n\nThe recovery organisation is agreed in normal times and activated when a program is needed. The NEDA guide recommends a planning committee with sector sub-committees that may be replicated at local level. Table 5.10 adapts that structure to City offices. It is a proposal for validation and adoption by the CDRRMC.\n\n**Table 5.10**\n\n*Proposed Rehabilitation and Recovery Sub-committees of the CDRRMC*\n\n| **Sub-committee**                     | **Proposed lead office**                                | **Members (as relevant) and responsibilities**                                                                                                                                                       |\n|---------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Infrastructure**                    | City Engineering Office                                 | Building Official, CPDO, utilities (LEYECO V, water district, telecoms), DPWH district office. Repair and reconstruction of roads, bridges, public buildings and lifelines to resilient standards.   |\n| **Shelter and settlement**            | City housing office (OCHO as named in the 10-Year Plan) | CPDO, City Legal Office, CSWDO, City Engineering, NHA, Pag-IBIG, SHFC. Repair and rebuilding of houses; transitional and permanent housing; resettlement as a last resort.                           |\n| **Social services**                   | CSWDO                                                   | CHO, DepEd Schools Division, Population Office. Continued relief to the most vulnerable, psychosocial support, social protection, restoration of social service facilities and education continuity. |\n| **Livelihood and business**           | PESO, with BPLO                                         | City Cooperative or Economic Enterprise office, DTI, DOLE, TESDA, Chamber of Commerce. Emergency employment, restoration of income, MSME recovery and access to finance.                             |\n| **Agriculture and fisheries**         | City Agriculture Office                                 | City Veterinary Office, DA, BFAR, PCIC, NIA, Visayas State University. Farm and fishing inputs, crop insurance claims, buffer seed stocks, irrigation repair.                                        |\n| **Environment and debris**            | ENRO / CENRO                                            | City Engineering, General Services, DENR. Debris and post-disaster waste, ecosystem rehabilitation, watershed and mangrove restoration (C3).                                                         |\n| **Resource mobilisation and support** | City Budget Office, with the City Treasurer             | City Accountant, BAC, CDRRMO, City Legal Office. Budget review, fund sources, procurement, audit and partner coordination (Chapter 6).                                                               |\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Box 7 and Part 3 of the NEDA (2020) guide, which lists infrastructure, shelter, social services, livelihood, agriculture and resource mobilisation sub-committees and allows others to be added. The environment and debris sub-committee is added for Ormoc because of the watershed and debris issues recorded in the CDRA and the 10-Year Plan. Lead offices are proposals.\n\n## 6.5 Scalable and Reprogrammable PPAs\n\nEach City office keeps a short list of its regular PPAs that can be scaled up or redirected to affected barangays after a disaster, with outputs, coverage, unit costs and the approval needed to realign them. Examples include the cash-for-work program, social pension and assistance programs, health programs, agricultural input programs and routine road and drainage maintenance. Regular programs are the fastest source of early recovery support because their beneficiary systems and budgets already exist (NEDA, 2020). The lists are consolidated in the framework and updated each year.\n\n## 6.6 Recovery Financing Options\n\nFinancing modalities are identified before the event so that the City does not wait for a budget cycle to start recovery. The detailed rules, ceilings and controls are in Chapter 6; Table 5.11 shows the options the framework must cover.\n\n**Table 5.11**\n\n*Recovery Financing Options to Be Identified in the Pre-disaster Framework*\n\n| **Source**                                                   | **Use in recovery**                                                                    | **Condition or control (see Chapter 6)**                                                   |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **LDRRMF 70% component**                                     | Rehabilitation and recovery PPAs programmed in the LDRRMF Investment Plan.             | Must be in the LDRRMFIP and AIP; utilisation reporting.                                    |\n| **Quick Response Fund (30%)**                                | Standby fund for relief and recovery so that living conditions are normalised quickly. | Use under the conditions set in Chapter 6, including the State of Calamity requirement.    |\n| **Special Trust Fund**                                       | Unexpended LDRRMF available for DRRM PPAs, including recovery.                         | Five-year use period; ageing ledger in Chapter 6.                                          |\n| **Realigned regular budgets of City offices**                | Scaled-up regular programs (section 6.5).                                              | Realignment authority and Sanggunian action where required.                                |\n| **Supplemental budget**                                      | Large financing gaps after the budget year has started.                                | Sanggunian appropriation.                                                                  |\n| **NDRRMF and national programs**                             | Rehabilitation PPAs that the LDRRMF cannot cover.                                      | Proposal through RDRRMC VIII to the NDRRMC; confirm current access rules (section 4 note). |\n| **GSIS property insurance (RA 656)**                         | Rebuilding of insured City buildings and assets.                                       | Assets must be insured before the event; claims filed promptly.                            |\n| **PCIC crop, livestock and fisheries insurance**             | Recovery of farm and fisheries investment.                                             | Farmer enrolment before the event; City Agriculture supports claims.                       |\n| **Development partners, private sector, CSOs and donations** | Co-financing or direct implementation of PPAs.                                         | Recorded in the City program; donations rules of Chapter 6.                                |\n\n*Note.* Sources are drawn from Part 4 and Table 1 of the NEDA (2020) guide and from the LDRRMF rules summarised in the MODE Platform Coherence Analysis (12 November 2026). Amounts, ceilings and section citations are governed by Chapter 6, including the open verification of the COA Circular 2012-002 section on trust-fund transfers.\n\n## 6.7 The Pre-disaster Recovery Framework\n\nThe six products above are brought together in one short document: the Ormoc Pre-disaster Recovery Framework. It is adopted by the CDRRMC, attached as the recovery annex of each hazard-specific contingency plan (Chapter 3, SOP 3-06), and reviewed every year. The IAT recommends the OCD and World Bank "Ready to Rebuild" workbook as the working tool (A9.5); the 10-Year Plan programs PlanSmart Ready to Rebuild training for this purpose. Annex 5-C gives the framework outline.\n\n# 7. Damage, Loss and Needs Assessment\n\nRecovery decisions depend on knowing what was damaged, what was lost and what people need. Three assessments are used, at different times and for different purposes (Table 5.12). The RDANA belongs to response (Chapter 4) and is carried out by the A8.7 team. The PDNA belongs to recovery and is the main source for the rehabilitation and recovery program.\n\n**Table 5.12**\n\n*Comparison of Damage and Needs Assessments Used by the City*\n\n| **Feature**    | **RDANA**                                                                        | **RaDE**                                                                                  | **PDNA**                                                                                 |\n|----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Purpose**    | Initial damage and immediate relief and response needs.                          | Quick desk estimate of damage, losses and recovery needs while a PDNA is being completed. | Detailed sectoral and cross-sectoral damage, losses, impacts and recovery needs.         |\n| **When**       | In the first days, during response.                                              | Optional; for large events when the PDNA will take long.                                  | After response stabilises; can run alongside the end of response.                        |\n| **Local lead** | CDRRMO RDANA team (A8.7).                                                        | CDRRMO with CPDO.                                                                         | CDRRMO, with OCD VIII support on City request.                                           |\n| **Method**     | Desk review and field validation; ASEAN ERAT Initial Needs Assessment Checklist. | Desk exercise on available data.                                                          | Desk review and field validation; UN-ECLAC damage and loss method; human recovery needs. |\n| **Coverage**   | Mostly public-sector damage and immediate needs.                                 | Public and private damage and losses; overall economic effect.                            | Public and private damage and losses; economic impact; recovery needs.                   |\n| **Feeds**      | SitReps, response decisions (Chapter 4).                                         | Early resource mobilisation and budget review.                                            | Rehabilitation and recovery program (section 8).                                         |\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Table 2 of the NEDA (2020) guide; at LGU level the lead is the LDRRMO. The guide cites NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 25, 2014 for RDANA triggers; the CDRRMO shall confirm the current RDANA issuance with OCD VIII.\n\n## 7.1 Deciding to Conduct a City PDNA\n\nThe CDRRMC decides whether the City conducts a PDNA when a disaster affects two or more barangays. The decision is based on the RDANA results and the extent of damage, and the usual triggers are a local declaration of a State of Calamity or a need for financial assistance from the national government (NEDA, 2020). When the CDRRMC decides not to conduct a PDNA, it records why and how recovery needs will be met (for example, through the regular programs of section 6.5). Figure 5.3 shows the decision and approval route.\n\n**Figure 5.3**\n\n*PDNA Decision, Approval and Program Route for a City-level Disaster*\n\n![Figure 3: 7.1 Deciding to Conduct a City PDNA](/library/chp5v10/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from Part 2 of the NEDA (2020) guide. Shaded boxes are decision bodies. The route through RDRRMC VIII applies only when national funding is requested. The approval route remains subject to City Legal Office confirmation.\n\n## 7.2 PDNA Team and Readiness\n\nThe CDRRMO leads the PDNA. The team is organised by sector, drawing on the offices that lead the recovery sub-committees (Table 5.10), with a CDRRMO coordinator and a data manager. The 10-Year Plan programs five PDNA technical positions and PDNA training for CDRRMC and BDRRMC members; until those positions are filled, the team is designated by office order from existing staff and trained with OCD VIII. Team composition, methods and timeframes follow the current OCD PDNA Guidance Notes. Templates are kept ready in the Library so that the team does not design forms after the event.\n\n## 7.3 Assessment Data Standards\n\n-   Use the PSGC barangay as the reporting unit and the pre-disaster baseline (section 6.1) as the reference.\n\n-   Keep figures as aggregates. Damage to individual houses may be recorded by the Building Official and CSWDO in their restricted records, but reports carry counts.\n\n-   Disaggregate affected population by sex, age and disability wherever the source allows.\n\n-   Separate confirmed figures from figures for validation, as in the Chapter 4 SitRep rules, and record the source of each figure.\n\n-   Record assumptions and unit costs used to estimate damage and losses, so that the figures can be reviewed and reproduced.\n\n# 8. Rehabilitation and Recovery Program\n\n## 8.1 Trigger and Timeframe\n\nThe CDRRMC prepares a local rehabilitation and recovery program for a disaster confined to the City when a State of Calamity is declared or national financial assistance is needed. It may also prepare one for a larger disaster, as the City''s input to the regional program (NEDA, 2020). Program preparation may start while response is still under way. For past typhoons, local and regional programs took an average of three months to prepare, excluding approval; this is a planning benchmark, not a legal deadline. Priority PPAs for the first year may be approved as an interim document before the full program, so that early recovery is not held up by the approval process.\n\n## 8.2 Program Contents\n\n**Table 5.13**\n\n*Minimum Contents of the Ormoc Rehabilitation and Recovery Program*\n\n| **Section**                                 | **Content**                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n|---------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Background and affected areas**           | Location, topography, climate, natural resources, pre-disaster economy, social and demographic data from the recovery baseline.                                                                                                          |\n| **Description of the hazard event**         | Hazard and immediate effects, from SitReps and the products of PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and MGB.                                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Assessment of effects**                   | Physical assessment (damage to infrastructure, changes to landscape) and socio-economic assessment (damage and losses by sector, affected, displaced, dead, missing and injured persons, situation of vulnerable groups), from the PDNA. |\n| **Recovery framework**                      | Goal, overall outcome, sector outcomes and cross-cutting concerns (Figure 5.4).                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Objectives and strategies**               | Achievable objectives with a timeframe; sector strategies consistent with Build Back Better.                                                                                                                                             |\n| **Targets**                                 | Taken from the PDNA, disaggregated by year and by barangay.                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| **Land use framework**                      | Locations for infrastructure, settlements and economic activities, based on the CLUP, Zoning Ordinance, shelter plan and updated hazard information (section 9.2).                                                                       |\n| **Phasing and implementation arrangements** | Short-term (within a year) and medium-term (three to four years) PPAs; lead and support offices.                                                                                                                                         |\n| **Financing and investment requirements**   | Investment requirement by source of fund and by sector; detailed PPA list (form REC-F04).                                                                                                                                                |\n| **Monitoring and evaluation**               | Monitoring unit, reporting arrangements and the monitoring matrix (section 13).                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Communication strategy**                  | Principles, core messages, spokesperson, materials and channels (section 12).                                                                                                                                                            |\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Part 2, Section B of the NEDA (2020) guide.\n\n**Figure 5.4**\n\n*Recovery Framework for an Ormoc Rehabilitation and Recovery Program*\n\n![Figure 4: 8.2 Program Contents](/library/chp5v10/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Adapted from the General Framework for Post-Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery (NEDA, 2020, Figure 3), which the Ormoc DRRM 10-Year Plan also uses as the base of its Framework for Local Resilience. Sector outcomes and cross-cutting concerns are tailored for each event; data privacy is added for Ormoc.\n\n## 8.3 Phasing of PPAs\n\nShort-term PPAs, implemented within a year, meet basic needs and restore social services so that families can return to normal life. Medium-term PPAs, implemented over the following three to four years, continue those programs and add measures that reduce future risk. Some reconstruction may run longer. In the 10-Year Plan, livelihood, social services and agriculture outcomes are targeted in the short term, while housing and infrastructure need phasing for studies, land acquisition and social preparation (City Government of Ormoc, 2023).\n\n## 8.4 Approval and Updating\n\nThe CDRRMC prepares and endorses the program; the Sangguniang Panlungsod approves it and the related appropriations. When funding from the national government is requested, the CDRRMC submits the Sanggunian-approved program through the RDRRMC VIII to the NDRRMC, and the RDRRMC reviews and validates the City PDNA (NEDA, 2020). The program includes an investment program that is revised as implementation progresses, based on the physical and financial accomplishment reports of SOP 5-07.\n\n# 9. Build Back Better\n\nBuild Back Better is applied as a decision rule, not a slogan. Every recovery project that rebuilds or repairs a structure, a lifeline, a settlement or an ecosystem is screened before it is funded (SOP 5-05, form REC-F07). The screen asks whether the project reduces risk compared with what was there before, and it records the decision and its reasons.\n\n**Table 5.14**\n\n*Build-Back-Better Criteria by Recovery Sector*\n\n| **Sector**                                  | **Build-back-better criterion**                                                                                                                                              | **Evidence and reviewer**                                                |\n|---------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Public buildings and evacuation centres** | Rebuilt or retrofitted to the structural code in force and the Building Official''s assessment; evacuation centres to RA 12076 standards; access for persons with disability. | Structural assessment and design review by the Building Official (C2.1). |\n| **Roads, bridges and drainage**             | Design accounts for the observed flood levels and hazard maps; alternate routes and critical segments identified in the CDRA are prioritised.                                | City Engineering and DPWH design review (C2.3).                          |\n| **Lifelines (power, water, telecoms)**      | Restoration priorities and redundancy agreed with providers; critical facilities first.                                                                                      | Restoration-priority arrangements with utilities (C2.2).                 |\n| **Housing and settlements**                 | Rebuilding only where the CLUP and Zoning Ordinance allow; floor elevation, setbacks and materials suited to the hazard; relocation only as a last resort with due process.  | CPDO, Zoning Administrator and housing office siting check (C1.4).       |\n| **Livelihoods**                             | Diversified or less hazard-exposed income; insurance or savings where available.                                                                                             | PESO, BPLO and City Agriculture; enrolment in PCIC or microinsurance.    |\n| **Ecosystems**                              | Watershed, forest and mangrove restoration that reduces hazard; safe debris and waste disposal.                                                                              | ENRO/CENRO and DENR (C3.1, C3.3).                                        |\n| **Social services**                         | Services restored and upgraded with inclusion standards; child-protection limits on schools used as shelters (RA 10821).                                                     | CSWDO, CHO, DepEd.                                                       |\n\n## 9.1 What Build Back Better Does Not Authorise\n\nThis Manual does not declare no-build zones, change land use classifications, order relocation or set design thresholds. Those require an ordinance, a valid executive issuance, a building permit decision or a technical standard issued by the competent body. The screen in SOP 5-05 records whether such an instrument exists and, if it does not, refers the issue to the authority that can issue it. One such instrument already exists: City Ordinance No. 37, s. 1993 declares parts of the Alegria–Punta shoreline and the Anilao and Malbasag river banks as high-risk areas where habitation, construction and building permits are prohibited (Chapter 1, section 4.5). Its boundaries must be reconciled with current hazard maps before the screen relies on it.\n\n## 9.2 Post-disaster Land Use Framework\n\nFor a disaster with major physical impact, the CPDO prepares a land use framework for the program that identifies where infrastructure, settlements and economic activities will be rebuilt, based on the CLUP, the Zoning Ordinance, the shelter plan and updated risk assessments. Where the disaster changes the landscape (for example, new landslide scars or river course changes), the framework proposes the changes needed in the CLUP or Zoning Ordinance for Sanggunian action. Where there is no significant physical change, the framework is used to enforce the existing land use policies (NEDA, 2020).\n\n## 9.3 Resettlement Safeguards\n\n-   Relocation is a last resort, after in-place rebuilding with risk reduction has been considered and found unsafe.\n\n-   Relocation follows RA 7279 and the applicable DHSUD rules, including consultation, adequate notice and due process.\n\n-   Resettlement sites include livelihood, social services, infrastructure and utilities, and are themselves screened for hazard.\n\n-   Affected families, including women, older persons and persons with disability, take part in site selection and design.\n\n-   The City records each decision, its legal basis and the consultation held; individual household records stay with the housing office and CSWDO.\n\n# 10. Sector Recovery Services\n\nRecovery services are delivered by the offices that hold the mandate. The CDRRMO coordinates and tracks; it does not take over service delivery. Table 5.15 sets the minimum service content for each sector.\n\n**Table 5.15**\n\n*Minimum Recovery Service Content by Sector*\n\n| **Sector**                         | **Lead office**                   | **Minimum service content**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n|------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Shelter and housing**            | Housing office with CSWDO         | Emergency shelter assistance for repair or rebuilding in safe sites; transitional shelter where needed; permanent housing through the LGU-funded housing program and NHA, Pag-IBIG or SHFC; land acquisition for settlement sites as programmed in the 10-Year Plan. |\n| **Livelihood and employment**      | PESO with BPLO                    | Cash-for-work using the City''s existing program; emergency employment with DOLE; MSME recovery and the planned LGU-managed MSME loan program; skills training with TESDA.                                                                                            |\n| **Agriculture and fisheries**      | City Agriculture Office           | Inputs and replanting; buffer seed stocks (programmed); support for PCIC claims; repair of farm and fisheries infrastructure with DA, BFAR and NIA.                                                                                                                  |\n| **Social services and protection** | CSWDO                             | Continued assistance to the most vulnerable until they recover; social protection; protection case management; closure of evacuation centres and support for return or durable solutions under JMC No. 2 s. 2021.                                                    |\n| **Health and MHPSS**               | City Health Office                | Mental health and psychosocial support under RA 11036; restoration of health services and facilities; disease surveillance in affected areas.                                                                                                                        |\n| **Education**                      | DepEd Schools Division with CSWDO | Return of schools used as shelters; repair of classrooms; learning continuity.                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Infrastructure and lifelines**   | City Engineering with utilities   | Priority restoration list; repair and reconstruction to Build Back Better criteria (Table 5.14).                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| **Environment and debris**         | ENRO / CENRO                      | Debris clearing and post-disaster waste management (programmed in the 10-Year Plan); ecosystem rehabilitation.                                                                                                                                                       |\n\n## 10.1 Referral Pathway\n\nPeople rarely need only one service. A family that lost its house may also have lost its income and may need psychosocial support. The referral pathway in Figure 5.5 gives every entry point the same screening and referral form (REC-F05) and the same consent and privacy rules. SOP 5-06 sets the steps. Referral pathways and MOAs among CSWDO, CHO and PESO are the recommended action for A9.4.\n\n**Figure 5.5**\n\n*Recovery Referral Pathway Across City Service Offices*\n\n![Figure 5: 10.1 Referral Pathway](/library/chp5v10/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. Personal details stay in the restricted record of the office that serves the person; the pathway shares only what the receiving office needs, with consent. BHW = barangay health worker; ESA = emergency shelter assistance; MHPSS = mental health and psychosocial support.\n\n# 11. Inclusion, Protection and Community Participation\n\nRecovery is hardest for those who were most vulnerable before the disaster: displaced families; orphaned, unaccompanied and separated children; older persons; persons with disability; single-parent households; indigenous peoples; and farmers, fisherfolk, farm workers and small traders who lose their income (NEDA, 2020). The program shall:\n\n-   Set priority beneficiaries using pre-disaster vulnerability, capacity to recover and access to services, and record the criteria publicly.\n\n-   Use sex-, age- and disability-disaggregated aggregates and gender-sensitive indicators in the monitoring matrix; the Harmonized Gender and Development Guidelines may be used to review PPAs.\n\n-   Respect culture, beliefs and cultural sites, and take account of the needs of indigenous peoples in restoring their assets.\n\n-   Consult affected communities, including women, youth, older persons and persons with disability, during assessment, planning, implementation and monitoring.\n\n-   Provide a feedback and grievance channel that is accessible and that does not depend on literacy or a mobile phone (SOP 5-08).\n\n-   Protect people from exploitation and abuse in the delivery of assistance, and refer protection cases to CSWDO.\n\n# 12. Recovery Communication and Accountability\n\nA recovery program builds trust only if people know what is planned, when it will happen and how to raise a problem. The CDRRMC designates a spokesperson and a communication focal person for recovery at the start of the program, and adopts a communication strategy that covers the principles, core messages, materials and channels below (NEDA, 2020).\n\n**Table 5.16**\n\n*Recovery Communication Strategy Components*\n\n| **Component**       | **Minimum content**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Principles**      | One communication protocol for all offices; one spokesperson; manage expectations with proactive messages about delays and beneficiary selection; a feedback mechanism with responses; a regular release schedule with consistent data.                               |\n| **Core messages**   | Roles of government, communities and partners; sector priorities; priority PPAs and targets; governing policies; available funds; start and completion dates; implementation issues and how they are being resolved; how communities can take part; overall progress. |\n| **Materials**       | Plain-language notices in Cebuano and Waray where needed, accessible formats, barangay posters, radio scripts and progress snapshots.                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Channels**        | Barangay assemblies, BDRRMC meetings, City public information channels, local radio, social media, and the feedback desk and hotline.                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Snapshot report** | A short, regular summary of the main recovery issues and trends for the Mayor and CDRRMC, shared with City offices so that all work from the same figures.                                                                                                            |\n\nPublic reports use the same aggregate figures as the monitoring matrix. Corrections are published when a figure changes. Full disclosure of DRRM funds and their use follows Chapter 1 and Chapter 6.\n\n# 13. Recovery Monitoring, Tracking to Completion and Evaluation\n\n## 13.1 Monitoring Unit\n\nThe CDRRMC designates the unit responsible for monitoring the program before implementation starts, so that monitoring is not left out (NEDA, 2020). This chapter proposes the CDRRMO Research and Planning function, working with the CPDO monitoring staff, as the recovery monitoring unit. Its responsibilities are to set key performance indicators; orient implementing offices on the monitoring tool; consolidate their reports; keep the project database up to date; take up implementation issues with the offices concerned; prepare periodic status reports to the CDRRMC; and validate reports through field visits to key projects. If a task force is created for a large event, the transfer of the monitoring function back to the permanent unit is agreed at the start.\n\n## 13.2 Recovery Monitoring Matrix\n\nAll recovery PPAs, whatever their fund source or implementing office, are tracked in one recovery monitoring matrix (form REC-F06). This closes the A9.3 gap and is the recommended action for that indicator ("add a recovery monitoring matrix to the event database"). Annex 5-D lists the minimum fields.\n\n## 13.3 Reporting and Problem Solving\n\n-   Each implementing office names a monitoring focal person and reports physical and financial accomplishment against targets at the frequency set by the CDRRMC. The NEDA guide requires at least an annual report; this chapter proposes monthly reports during the first year and quarterly reports afterwards, for CDRRMC decision.\n\n-   Issues that block implementation are raised as they arise, not only in the formal report.\n\n-   A regular inter-office problem-solving meeting, with the monitoring unit as secretariat, resolves bottlenecks.\n\n-   The monitoring unit and office focal persons validate key and problem projects in the field.\n\n## 13.4 Data Protection in Recovery Records\n\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch5v1_v1.0.md (Chapter 5, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('643af953-d4ff-5659-abee-7640461efab4'::uuid,'aec20d50-301b-57ce-87d4-64154502a0ef'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'**CHAPTER 7**\n\nMONITORING, EVALUATION, KNOWLEDGE MANAGEMENT AND CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)**\n\n**AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft*\n\n*Subject to institutional, technical, legal and data-privacy validation*\n\n**Draft 1.0 \\| 12 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# Document Control\n\n**Table 7.1**\n\n*Document Control*\n\n| **Document attribute**     | **Control information**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Document title**         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 7                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Chapter title**          | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Position in the Manual** | Seventh of ten chapters in the structure adopted on 12 November 2026. It follows Chapter 6 (DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability) and precedes Chapter 8 (Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships). It receives the reports, after-action records and corrective actions of Chapters 1 to 6 and returns lessons to all chapters. |\n| **Document status**        | Working draft for technical, institutional, legal and data-privacy validation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| **Document owner**         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| **Technical custodian**    | CDRRMO Head; Research and Planning Division as the proposed monitoring and evaluation (M&E) unit                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Reviewing offices**      | City Planning and Development Office (CPDO); City Legal Office; City Data Protection Officer (DPO); CDRRMP Technical Working Group thematic focal offices (CENRO, DILG City Office, CSWDO, CPDO); City Information and Communications Technology office; City Records Officer; Internal Audit Service, where constituted; OCD Region VIII                        |\n| **Version / date**         | Draft 1.0 / 12 November 2026                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| **Approval authority**     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements; in MODE, only the CDRRMO Head approves Manual versions                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| **Review cycle**           | At least annually, after the IAT cycle closes; after a major event or exercise whose AAR changes this chapter; when the NDRRMC issues the RA 12287 monitoring and evaluation framework                                                                                                                                                                           |\n\n## Revision and Approval Record\n\n**Table 7.2**\n\n*Revision and Approval Record*\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | **Prepared / reviewed by**        | **Approval** |\n|-------------|-------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | 12 Nov 2026 | Initial assessment-informed working draft under the ten-chapter structure. Built from IAT v1.7 rows A10.3, A10.4, A10.6, A10.8, A10.9, A5.4, A5.5, B7.3 and B7.4, and from the results-based M&E design of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032. Operates the CAPA register defined in Chapter 1, section 9.5 (GOV-10). Nine SOPs (7-01 to 7-09), nine ME forms, Annexes 7-A to 7-E. | Project drafting team with CDRRMO | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nChapters 1 to 6 set what the City does before, during and after a disaster and how it pays for it. This chapter asks whether those things are being done, whether they work, and what changes as a result. It is the "Check" and "Act" of the Plan–Do–Check–Act cycle that Chapter 1 (section 9) adopts for the whole Manual.\n\nA procedure that is written but never measured drifts. The Introduction (section 5.4) names the risk: with Mandatory compliance at 32.9%, the working standard can settle at "we responded" rather than "we met the standard". The remedy is a fixed standard, measured the same way every year, with gaps tracked to closure and lessons fed back into plans and the Manual. This chapter supplies those mechanisms.\n\nThe IAT v1.7 baseline shows that the City plans and reports, but rarely closes the loop (Figure 7.1). Domain A10 (Partnerships, M&E and Accountability) has an index of **1.86 (Developing)**, with 1 of 4 Mandatory items compliant. Regular reporting to OCD Region VIII and the DILG is in place (A10.3 at 3). The gaps are in the learning loop: no 45-day post-declaration report template (A10.8, score 0, High); no annual M&E report of the LDRRMP (A10.4); after-action reviews that rest on the FGD alone (A10.6, B7.4); no disaster event and loss database (A5.4, score 0); and data-protection arrangements for DRRM data not sighted (A5.5) (Table 7.3).\n\n**Figure 7.1**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Scores for Monitoring, Evaluation and Knowledge Indicators*\n\n![Figure 1: 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale](/library/chp7v10/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Scores are copied from the A_CDRRMO and B_EOC sheets of IAT v1.7. The A10 domain index is Σ(score × weight) ÷ Σweight over A10.1 to A10.9 = 26 ÷ 14 = 1.86, banded on the unrounded value. A5.4, A5.5, B7.3 and B7.4 belong to other domains and are shown because this chapter owns their procedures. All rows are pending validation at the workshop of 1–4 December 2026.\n\n**Table 7.3**\n\n*Assessment Findings That Shape This Chapter*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (IAT v1.7)**                                                               | **Score** | **Priority** | **Finding (summarised from the workbook)**                                                                                            |\n|--------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A10.8  | Post-declaration report within 45 days of lifting a State of Imminent Disaster (M, w2) | 0         | High (12.0)  | No 45-day report template; reporting route not confirmed with OCD VIII. New Mandatory item under RA 12287 IRR Rule VI Sec. 1.         |\n| A5.4   | DRRM information system with disaster event history, damage and losses (GP, w2)        | 0         | Medium (8.0) | Event records exist in reports (10-Year Plan, CDRA, RDANA and PDNA reports) but there is no database.                                 |\n| A10.4  | M&E framework for the LDRRMP with indicators and annual review (M, w2)                 | 2         | Medium (6.0) | The 10-Year Plan sets a results-based M&E design, Form 5D and annual reports; no annual report or baseline database was sighted.      |\n| A5.5   | Data privacy compliance for beneficiary and affected-population data (M, w2)           | 2         | Medium (6.0) | No DPO designation, data-sharing agreement or privacy notice sighted for DRRM data.                                                   |\n| B7.3   | Annual exercise program testing EOC activation (M, w2)                                 | 2         | Medium (6.0) | Exercise program and evaluation records to be validated.                                                                              |\n| A10.6  | After-action reviews after significant events, lessons integrated into plans (GP, w2)  | 2         | Medium (4.0) | FGD self-score of 4 lowered to 2 (Methodology §5.2); AAR reports and resulting plan revisions to be validated.                        |\n| B7.4   | After-action review after each activation, with improvement plan tracked (GP, w2)      | 2         | Medium (4.0) | EOC improvement plan tracking not sighted.                                                                                            |\n| A10.3  | Regular reporting to OCD Region VIII, RDRRMC and DILG (M, w2)                          | 3         | Low (3.0)    | In place; lowered from 4 for lack of a test or AAR. Use of the harmonized reporting template to be confirmed.                         |\n| A10.9  | Structured learning from other LGUs and networks, with changes documented (GP, w1)     | 2         | Low (2.0)    | MCR2030 commitment, NRC Resilient LGU partnership and the EDC Adopt-a-City MOA exist; changes resulting from them are not documented. |\n\n*Note.* Priority is the workbook band of (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 (Mandatory) or × 1.0 (Good Practice): High 9 or more, Medium 4 to 8.9, Low below 4. B7.3 is shared with Chapter 9, which runs the exercise program; this chapter evaluates exercises. Annex 7-A gives the full row set with master requirements and recommended actions.\n\n## 1.1 What the Records Show\n\n-   **A results-based M&E design exists.** The DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 (Chapter 5 of the plan) bases its monitoring on the UNDRR Ten Essentials for Making Cities Resilient, overlaid with NDRRMP 2020–2030 outcomes, the DILG capacity development pillars, the Sendai Framework targets and the Regional Development Plan of Region VIII (Figure 7.3). It sets a monitoring tool (Form 5D) and five outputs, including an annual progress report each January, a physical and financial report each December and a mid-term report in 2029, reported to the CDRRMC and the Local Development Council.\n\n-   **Mandates to monitor exist.** RA 10121 Sec. 11(b) tasks the CDRRMC to approve, monitor and evaluate the LDRRMP and to review and test it regularly. EO 2024-032A Sec. 2(a) repeats the task. EO 2022-00020 created the CDRRMP Technical Working Group for formulating, implementing, monitoring and evaluating the plan, with thematic focal offices: CENRO for prevention and mitigation, the DILG City Office for preparedness, the CSWDO for response and the CPDO for recovery. EO 2023-0026 tasks the Project TRANSFORM TWG to monitor indicators in the NRC resilience scorecard.\n\n-   **The loop is not yet closed.** No annual results report and no baseline database were found in the document set. The plan itself observes that the City''s performance has been assessed mainly through national award systems, without outcome-based M&E linked to sector results.\n\n-   **Event history is scattered.** The 10-Year Plan lists the six most significant disasters; the CDRA lists previous disasters with damage figures; RDANA and PDNA reports exist for 2017 to 2021. The CDRA recommends a disaster event and loss database. Chapter 5 found one damage figure in the plan that is not plausible (Yolanda), which a controlled database would have caught.\n\n-   **The assessment itself is the first full baseline.** The October–November 2026 IAT cycle scored 140 indicators, applied the evidence rule and produced the first Institutional Maturity Index (IMI 1.98). The validation workshop of 1–4 December 2026 will confirm or revise each score. That cycle is the model for the annual cycles this chapter sets.\n\n## 1.2 Design Considerations\n\n1.  **Measure the same way every year.** The IAT, the LDRRMP indicators and the reporting calendar stay stable so that change can be seen; when the instrument changes, the change is versioned and the old results stay readable.\n\n2.  **Evidence before score.** A score of 3 needs an approved document and proof of use; interviews and FGDs alone cap at 2. The same rule applies to M&E claims: an output is "done" only with its means of verification.\n\n3.  **One register.** Findings from the IAT, AARs, exercises, audits, compliance reports and feedback go into one corrective and preventive action (CAPA) register, defined in Chapter 1 (section 9.5, GOV-10) and run under this chapter.\n\n4.  **Closure means effective.** An action is closed when the problem it addressed has not recurred in the next test, exercise or event, not when work has started.\n\n5.  **Separate the scorer from the validator.** Whoever scores an indicator does not validate it; MODE enforces this in the database (ADR-029).\n\n6.  **Privacy by design.** M&E and knowledge products use aggregate counts; personal data stays with the office that collected it, under the Data Privacy Act.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter sets how the City monitors and evaluates its DRRM plan and its DRRM institutions, how it learns from events and exercises, how it tracks corrective action to closure, how it meets its reporting duties, and how it keeps and shares DRRM knowledge and data safely.\n\nIntended results:\n\n1.  The LDRRMP has an approved indicator set with baselines, and the CDRRMC receives an annual results report each January (A10.4).\n\n2.  The IAT is applied every year with the same instrument, evidence rules and separation of duties, and its results feed the action plan and the Manual (MR-12 to MR-15).\n\n3.  Every EOC activation at Blue or Red, every State of Calamity or State of Imminent Disaster, and every exercise is followed by an after-action review whose findings are tracked (A10.6, B7.4).\n\n4.  Every corrective action in the CAPA register has an owner, a due date and verification evidence, and is closed only when shown to be effective.\n\n5.  The 45-day post-declaration report is submitted on time after each State of Imminent Disaster (A10.8), and all other mandated reports follow a calendar.\n\n6.  A disaster event and loss database holds the City''s history from 1991 onwards in a standard format (A5.4).\n\n7.  Lessons from Ormoc''s own events and from other LGUs and networks are recorded with the changes they produced (A10.9).\n\n8.  DRRM data handling meets the Data Privacy Act: a designated DPO, privacy notices, data-sharing agreements and a breach procedure (A5.5).\n\n# 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\n**In scope:** monitoring and evaluation of the LDRRMP and of the CDRRMO and EOC; the annual IAT cycle; after-action reviews and exercise evaluation; the CAPA register; compliance and post-declaration reporting; the disaster event and loss database; records, lessons and peer learning; protection of DRRM data; management review and performance reporting.\n\n**Table 7.4**\n\n*Subjects Handled in Other Chapters*\n\n| **Subject**                                                                               | **Where it is handled**                          |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| Definition of the CAPA register, governance KPIs (GOV-09) and records management (GOV-08) | Chapter 1 (sections 9.4 and 9.5; SOP register)   |\n| Situation reports during an event and their cut-off times                                 | Chapter 4 (SOP 4-08; PR-SITREP-CYCLE)            |\n| Hot wash and the after-action record at deactivation                                      | Chapter 4 (SOP 4-09; form ER-F06), received here |\n| Recovery monitoring and closeout                                                          | Chapter 5 (SOP 5-07; REC-F06, REC-F09)           |\n| Financial and utilisation reports                                                         | Chapter 6 (SOP 6-02; FIN-F03)                    |\n| Planning, conduct and scheduling of drills and exercises                                  | Chapter 9                                        |\n| Gawad KALASAG and SGLG submissions                                                        | Chapter 10                                       |\n\n**Institutional boundaries.** The CDRRMC monitors and evaluates the LDRRMP; this chapter equips it to do so but does not take that function over. Thematic focal offices report on their own PPAs. The CDRRMO Research and Planning Division is proposed as the M&E unit; it compiles, checks and analyses, but does not score its own work in the IAT. The City DPO decides privacy questions. Part C lead offices own their interface indicators.\n\n**Table 7.5**\n\n*Roles in Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning*\n\n| **Role**                                                | **Responsibilities**                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **CDRRMC**                                              | Approves the LDRRMP indicator set and targets; receives the annual results report, the IAT results and the quarterly CAPA status; decides corrective actions beyond the CDRRMO''s authority (RA 10121 Sec. 11(b); EO 2024-032A Sec. 2(a)). |\n| **CDRRMO Head**                                         | Accountable for this chapter; chairs the quarterly management review; approves AAR reports, Manual versions and the closure of High-priority CAPA entries.                                                                                |\n| **Research and Planning Division (M&E unit, proposed)** | Keeps the indicator set, data and reports; runs the IAT cycle logistics; keeps the CAPA register, the reporting calendar, the event and loss database and the lessons register.                                                           |\n| **CDRRMP Technical Working Group**                      | Thematic focal offices report physical and financial accomplishment of their PPAs (EO 2022-00020).                                                                                                                                        |\n| **Assessors and validators**                            | Assessors score with evidence; validators confirm or revise; a person never validates a score they set (ADR-029).                                                                                                                         |\n| **AAR facilitator**                                     | A trained person who did not hold a command role in the event, named for each AAR.                                                                                                                                                        |\n| **City Data Protection Officer**                        | Advises on privacy notices, data-sharing agreements, suppression of small counts and breach notification.                                                                                                                                 |\n| **City Records Officer**                                | Retention and disposal of DRRM records under the National Archives rules.                                                                                                                                                                 |\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\nChapter 1 sets the general legal basis. Table 7.6 lists the instruments that govern monitoring, evaluation, reporting and data in DRRM. The official text governs.\n\n**Table 7.6**\n\n*Instruments Governing Monitoring, Evaluation, Reporting and DRRM Data*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                                                  | **What it requires**                                                                                                                                                          | **How this chapter applies it**                                      |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA 10121 Sec. 11(b)                                                                                             | The LDRRMC approves, monitors and evaluates the LDRRMP and regularly reviews and tests the plan.                                                                              | SOP 7-01; annual results report                                      |\n| RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)                                                                                             | LDRRMO functions, including consolidating local disaster risk information, knowledge management and reporting (items cited by IAT v1.7 for A5.4 and A10.4).                   | SOPs 7-01, 7-07, 7-08                                                |\n| NDRRMP 2020–2030 and its Results-Based Monitoring and Evaluation Plan                                           | National outcomes and indicators; M&E matrices with indicators, baselines, targets, data sources and responsible offices; annual accomplishment reporting; Sendai monitoring. | Indicator overlay (section 6); ME-F01 matrix                         |\n| NDRRMC LDRRMP Formulation Guidebook (2022)                                                                      | Stage 5 of LDRRMP formulation: a results-based M&E plan.                                                                                                                      | Section 6; SOP 7-01                                                  |\n| NDRRMC Memo No. 147, s. 2017                                                                                    | Review of LDRRMPs and their consistency with the AIP.                                                                                                                         | SOP 7-01 step 5                                                      |\n| RA 12287 IRR Rule VI Sec. 1                                                                                     | Report within 45 days of lifting a State of Imminent Disaster: declaration status, AA fund use, estimated economic and social costs minimised.                                | SOP 7-06; GOV-F13; Annex 7-D                                         |\n| DILG Region VIII harmonized disaster response reporting template and SOP (RDRRMC VIII Res. No. 12-006, s. 2023) | Report types and cut-off times for LGU reports.                                                                                                                               | Reporting calendar (section 10); Chapter 4 runs the SitReps          |\n| RA 10173 (Data Privacy Act) and IRR                                                                             | Lawful processing, proportionality, security, data-sharing agreements, rights of data subjects, a DPO.                                                                        | Section 12; SOP 7-09                                                 |\n| NPC Circular No. 16-03                                                                                          | Personal data breach management, including notification of the NPC and affected data subjects within 72 hours of knowledge of a breach.                                       | SOP 7-09 steps 5–7                                                   |\n| NPC Circular No. 2023-06                                                                                        | Security of personal data in government and the private sector.                                                                                                               | SOP 7-09 step 3                                                      |\n| RA 9470 (National Archives of the Philippines Act)                                                              | Records management and disposal under approved retention schedules.                                                                                                           | Section 11.2                                                         |\n| Sendai Framework 2015–2030; UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard (Detailed Level, 2017)                          | Global targets A–G and their indicators; scorecard criteria; disaster loss data for monitoring.                                                                               | Indicator overlay; event and loss database fields (Annex 7-E); MR-18 |\n| Local: EO 2024-032A Sec. 2(a); EO 2022-00020; EO 2023-0026                                                      | CDRRMC M&E function; CDRRMP TWG and thematic focal offices; Project TRANSFORM TWG.                                                                                            | Roles (section 3); SOP 7-01                                          |\n\n> PENDING NATIONAL RULES. The NDRRMC monitoring and evaluation framework for anticipatory action under RA 12287 has not been issued. Until it is, the 45-day report follows the IRR text and GOV-F13; SOP 7-06 will be revised when the framework is issued.\n\n# 5. Systems Thinking and the Learning Cycle\n\nThe CDRRMO learns in two ways. In single-loop learning it corrects how a procedure was carried out: a SitRep was late, so the cut-off reminder is moved earlier. In double-loop learning it questions the procedure or its assumptions: SitReps are late because the same staff run the EOC and the field, so the roster changes (Argyris and Schön, 1978). The IAT and the AAR find problems of both kinds; the CAPA register records which kind each action is, so that repeated single-loop fixes to the same problem trigger a deeper review.\n\nFigure 7.2 shows the learning system. Evidence arrives from five sources: the annual IAT cycle, LDRRMP monitoring, after-action reviews and exercises, audits and compliance reports, and feedback and peer learning. One register turns it into change in plans, the Manual, MODE and people''s capacity. The next cycle measures again, which is how effectiveness is verified.\n\n**Figure 7.2**\n\n*The City DRRM Learning System*\n\n![Figure 2: 5. Systems Thinking and the Learning Cycle](/library/chp7v10/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from the PDCA cycle in Chapter 1 (section 9.2) and the CAPA requirements in Chapter 1 (section 9.5).\n\n-   **Against drifting goals.** Standards are set by the IAT rubric and the law, not by last year''s performance. The register shows Mandatory gaps first.\n\n-   **Against shifting the burden.** An AAR finding that recurs in two events is escalated from a single-loop fix to a CDRRMC decision on the fundamental solution (staffing, issuance, budget).\n\n-   **Delays are named.** Each action states when its effect can be seen (the next drill, the next season, the next IAT cycle), so the CDRRMC does not abandon a correct fix before it has had time to work.\n\n# 6. Monitoring and Evaluation of the LDRRMP\n\n## 6.1 The Integrated M&E Design\n\nThe City already adopted the design in its 10-Year Plan (Figure 7.3). This chapter does not replace it; it operates it. The UNDRR Ten Essentials are the base. The NDRRMP outcomes, the DILG capacity pillars, the Sendai targets and the Regional Development Plan of Region VIII are overlays that align local indicators with national and regional reporting. This Manual adds one layer: the IAT Institutional Maturity Index as the measure of the CDRRMO and the EOC themselves.\n\n**Figure 7.3**\n\n*Base and Overlays of the LDRRMP Results-Based M&E*\n\n![Figure 3: 6.1 The Integrated M&E Design](/library/chp7v10/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Adapted from Table 33 (Integrated Criteria for Results-based Monitoring and Evaluation) of the Ormoc City DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032. Regional Development Plan indicators are marked "to be determined" in the plan.\n\n## 6.2 Indicator Set, Baselines and Targets\n\nEach indicator is recorded on the M&E matrix (ME-F01, aligned with the plan''s Form 5D): result level (output, outcome, impact), indicator and definition, unit, baseline value and year, target by year, data source, collection method and frequency, responsible office, and the Ten Essentials, NDRRMP, Sendai and IAT codes it maps to. Table 7.7 shows the levels. Targets are set by the CDRRMC; this chapter sets none.\n\n**Table 7.7**\n\n*Result Levels in the LDRRMP M&E Matrix*\n\n| **Level**         | **What is measured**                                                | **Examples of indicator types (targets set by the CDRRMC)**                                                                                                                                          |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Impact            | Change in disaster losses and resilience over the plan period       | Deaths, missing and affected persons per 100,000 (Sendai A, B); direct economic loss (Sendai C); damage to critical infrastructure and disruption of basic services (Sendai D); Ten Essentials score |\n| Outcome           | Change in institutions, systems and behaviour that the PPAs aim for | IAT IMI and Mandatory compliance; share of barangays with functional BDRRMCs; share of families reached by warnings who took protective action; share of recovery PPAs completed to BBB standards    |\n| Output            | Products and services delivered by the PPAs                         | Plans approved; drills held; people trained; early warning devices installed; evacuation centres upgraded; stocks at target                                                                          |\n| Input and process | Resources and how well they are used                                | LDRRMF utilisation (Chapter 6); staffing filled (Chapter 2); SitRep timeliness (Chapter 4)                                                                                                           |\n\n*Note.* Sendai indicators are reported as the City''s own figures for monitoring; they do not imply a national target allocation to the City.\n\n## 6.3 Reporting Products\n\n**Table 7.8**\n\n*LDRRMP M&E Reporting Products*\n\n| **Product**                        | **Timing**                          | **Content**                                                                                | **Recipient**                        |\n|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Annual progress and results report | Each January, for the previous year | ME-F01 values against baselines and targets; PPA status; IAT results; CAPA status; lessons | CDRRMC; LDC; posted on the City site |\n| Physical and financial report      | Each December                       | Physical accomplishment and LDRRMF use by PPA (with Chapter 6 FIN-F03)                     | CDRRMC; LDC                          |\n| Mid-term evaluation                | 2029                                | Progress at the midpoint; relevance of PPAs; revision of the plan                          | CDRRMC; SP                           |\n| End-of-plan evaluation             | 2032 (proposed)                     | Results against the plan''s outcomes; basis for the next LDRRMP                             | CDRRMC; SP                           |\n\n*Note.* The first three products are set by the 10-Year Plan; the end-of-plan evaluation is proposed by this chapter for CDRRMC decision.\n\n## 6.4 Data Quality\n\nEach value carries its source, date and the office that supplied it. A blank means "not reported" and is never entered as zero. Barangay figures use the 85-barangay PSGC list (Chapter 3). Values that change by more than half from the previous year are checked with the source office before publication. The M&E unit keeps the calculation for each composite indicator so that another person can repeat it.\n\n# 7. The Institutional Assessment Cycle\n\nThe IAT measures the CDRRMO and the EOC themselves. The Introduction (sections 5.1 to 5.3) describes the instrument and the baseline. This section sets how it is used every year.\n\n## 7.1 The Instrument\n\nIAT v1.7 has 140 indicators in 22 domains: Part A (CDRRMO), 82 indicators in 10 domains; Part B (EOC), 43 indicators in 7 domains; Part C (interface with other City offices and partners), 15 indicators in 5 domains. Parts A and B have 79 Mandatory and 46 Good Practice indicators and form the Institutional Maturity Index (IMI). Part C names a lead office other than the CDRRMO and is reported as a separate Interface Index, never added to the IMI. The 108-indicator sheet in the City''s earlier statement of design principles is IAT v1.0, which the Vault records as superseded.\n\n## 7.2 Scoring and Evidence Rules\n\n**Table 7.9**\n\n*IAT Scoring Rubric and Evidence Rule*\n\n| **Score** | **Level**      | **Meaning and minimum evidence**                                                                     |\n|-----------|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 0         | Absent         | Nothing exists.                                                                                      |\n| 1         | Initial        | Exists verbally or informally only.                                                                  |\n| 2         | Developing     | A draft or partial arrangement exists. The maximum for a score supported only by interviews or FGDs. |\n| 3         | Compliant      | An approved document exists **and** there is proof of use.                                           |\n| 4         | Advanced       | Level 3 plus a test, exercise or AAR showing improvement.                                            |\n| NA        | Not applicable | Only with a written justification.                                                                   |\n\n*Note.* From the IAT v1.7 Scoring_Lookups sheet and Methodology v1.1, section 5.2. Until validation, the lower score stands.\n\n**Table 7.10**\n\n*IAT Indices and Priority Formulas*\n\n| **Measure**                      | **Formula and rule**                                                                                                                                                                        |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Domain or part index**         | Σ(score × weight) ÷ Σ(weight) over scored items. Bands: below 0.5 Absent; 0.5–1.49 Initial; 1.5–2.49 Developing; 2.5–3.49 Compliant; 3.5 and above Advanced. The unrounded value is banded. |\n| **Institutional Maturity Index** | The index over all scored items in Parts A and B.                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Mandatory compliance**         | Scored Mandatory items at 3 or 4 ÷ scored Mandatory items. A domain is "Compliant" only at 100%.                                                                                            |\n| **Interface Index**              | The index over Part C, reported separately.                                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Gap priority**                 | (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 for Mandatory items; × 1.0 for Good Practice and all Part C items. High 9 and above; Medium 4 to 8.9; Low below 4.                                               |\n\n*Note.* The scoring rules are held once in SQL in MODE and mirrored in the application code under a parity test (ADR-005); a change to a formula is a change to the controlled instrument and needs a new IAT version.\n\n## 7.3 Validation and Separation of Duties\n\nEvery score is validated before the results are final. MODE records who set each score; a validation by the same person is refused. A validator either confirms the score or revises it; a revision needs a written note, and the submitted score is kept. Baseline rows loaded from the workbook have no recorded scorer, so the lead assessor does not validate them (ADR-029; Methodology v1.1, section 6.1). The agreement rate between scorers and validators is reported as a calibration signal, not as a performance measure of individuals.\n\n## 7.4 The Annual Cycle\n\nFigure 7.4 shows the cycle. MODE carries each cycle through five statuses: draft, fieldwork, validation, final and archived. A final cycle is not edited; corrections are made in the next cycle. The proposed timing places fieldwork and validation in the third and fourth quarters, so the results reach the January annual report and the budget call that follows.\n\n**Figure 7.4**\n\n*The Annual IAT Cycle*\n\n![Figure 4: 7.4 The Annual Cycle](/library/chp7v10/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from the MODE assessment_cycles statuses, ADR-005, ADR-029 and Methodology v1.1. The timing in the reporting calendar (Figure 7.6) is a proposal for CDRRMC decision.\n\n## 7.5 From Gap to Action to Manual\n\nEach gap routes to an improvement action with a responsible office, target date and budget source, and to the Manual section that addresses it (MR-14). MODE holds actions per indicator and cycle with a status (not started, ongoing, done, dropped) and shows the chain from indicator to master requirement to Manual document in the traceability view. Actions arising from the IAT are copied into the CAPA register so that one register holds all corrective work.\n\n> PLATFORM NOTE. The MODE actions table has no fields for root cause, verification evidence or effectiveness review, which Chapter 1 (Table 1.23) requires of a CAPA entry. Until they are added, the CAPA register is kept on ME-F05 and the MODE action carries the ME-F05 reference. The gap is listed in section 17.\n\n# 8. After-Action Reviews and Exercise Evaluation\n\nAn after-action review asks four questions: what was supposed to happen; what actually happened; why there was a difference; and what will be done about it. It examines systems and decisions, not individuals. Figure 7.5 shows the path from trigger to closure.\n\n**Table 7.11**\n\n*Events That Require an After-Action Review*\n\n| **Trigger**                                                          | **Minimum form**                                                                   | **Proposed timing**                                 |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|\n| EOC activation at Blue or Red                                        | Hot wash at deactivation (Chapter 4, SOP 4-09) and a full AAR (ME-F03)             | Hot wash on deactivation; AAR report within 30 days |\n| State of Calamity or State of Imminent Disaster                      | Full AAR; for a State of Imminent Disaster, linked to the 45-day report (SOP 7-06) | Within 30 days of lifting                           |\n| Functional or full-scale exercise                                    | Exercise evaluation (ME-F04)                                                       | Within 15 days of the exercise                      |\n| Tabletop exercise or drill                                           | Short evaluation (ME-F04, short form)                                              | Within 7 days                                       |\n| Near miss or significant procedural failure, even without activation | Focused AAR (ME-F03, focused form)                                                 | Within 15 days of discovery                         |\n\n*Note.* The timings are proposals of this chapter for CDRRMC adoption; they are not set by national rules.\n\n**Figure 7.5**\n\n*From After-Action Review to Verified Closure*\n\n![Figure 5: 8. After-Action Reviews and Exercise Evaluation](/library/chp7v10/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. ER-F06 is the Chapter 4 after-action and corrective action record; ME-F05 is the CAPA register entry.\n\n**Who takes part.** The EOC Manager, section chiefs, the Incident Commander or a representative, sector offices and barangays involved, and, where possible, community representatives and partners. A facilitator who did not hold a command role leads. Participants are told that the AAR is blame-free and that its record names positions, not people.\n\n**What is recorded.** The timeline from the official warning products and the logbook; decisions and their basis; what worked; what did not, with evidence; root or contributing causes; and recommended actions. Annex 7-C gives the report outline. Exercise evaluations follow the exercise objectives and the EOC Capability Assessment Checklist where it applies.\n\n# 9. Corrective and Preventive Action Management\n\nChapter 1 (section 9.5) defines the CAPA register and its fields; SOP GOV-10 names it. This chapter operates it (SOP 7-05). Every source in Figure 7.2 feeds it: IAT gaps, AAR and exercise findings, COA observations (Chapter 6, FIN-F11), compliance report gaps, recovery monitoring (Chapter 5), stakeholder feedback and changes in law.\n\n**Table 7.12**\n\n*CAPA Status Rules*\n\n| **Status**         | **Meaning**                                                                              | **Who may set it**                              |\n|--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| Open               | Entered with problem statement, source and risk consequence; owner and due date proposed | M&E unit                                        |\n| Planned            | Root cause, action, owner, due date and resources agreed                                 | Accountable owner, confirmed by the CDRRMO Head |\n| In progress        | Work has started                                                                         | Accountable owner                               |\n| Implemented        | Evidence of completion attached                                                          | Accountable owner                               |\n| Closed – effective | The problem has not recurred in the next test, exercise, event or IAT cycle              | CDRRMO Head; the CDRRMC for High-priority items |\n| Reopened           | The problem recurred; a new cause analysis is required                                   | M&E unit                                        |\n| Escalated          | Overdue twice, or beyond the CDRRMO''s authority                                          | CDRRMO Head to the CDRRMC                       |\n\n*Note.* An action is never closed because it has started (Chapter 1, section 9.5). Priority follows the IAT gap-priority bands where the source is an IAT indicator; otherwise the CDRRMO Head assigns High, Medium or Low by life-safety and legal consequence.\n\n# 10. Compliance Reporting and the Reporting Calendar\n\nThe City owes reports to several bodies. Missing one is a compliance gap; sending the same figures in different versions is a coherence gap. The M&E unit keeps one calendar (ME-F06) and one source for each figure (Figure 7.6; Table 7.13).\n\n**Table 7.13**\n\n*Mandated and Planned Reports*\n\n| **Report**                                                   | **Recipient**                                 | **Timing**                                    | **Owner and source**           |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| Situation reports (pre-, during and post-event; terminal)    | Province; OCD VIII and RDRRMC VIII; DILG VIII | Cut-off times of the DILG VIII harmonized SOP | EOC (Chapter 4, SOP 4-08)      |\n| DROMIC displacement reports                                  | DSWD Field Office VIII                        | Per DSWD schedule during events               | CSWDO                          |\n| 45-day post-declaration report under RA 12287                | OCD (route to be confirmed with OCD VIII)     | Within 45 days of lifting                     | CDRRMO Head; SOP 7-06; GOV-F13 |\n| LDRRMF utilisation report and Full Disclosure Policy posting | CDRRMC; public; DILG                          | Quarterly                                     | Chapter 6, SOP 6-02            |\n| Operation L!sto and other DILG preparedness reports          | DILG City Office and Region VIII              | Per DILG issuance                             | CDRRMO with DILG City Office   |\n| Annual LDRRMP results report                                 | CDRRMC; LDC                                   | January                                       | M&E unit; SOP 7-01             |\n| Physical and financial report of the LDRRMP                  | CDRRMC; LDC                                   | December                                      | M&E unit with Chapter 6        |\n| IAT results and action plan                                  | CDRRMC; CDRRMO staff                          | On cycle close                                | M&E unit; SOPs 7-02, 7-03      |\n| Gawad KALASAG and SGLG submissions                           | OCD; DILG                                     | Per annual guidelines                         | Chapter 10                     |\n\n*Note.* Report routes and schedules follow the issuing agency''s current rules; the M&E unit confirms them each January and updates ME-F06.\n\n**Figure 7.6**\n\n*Proposed Annual Reporting and Review Calendar*\n\n![Figure 6: 10. Compliance Reporting and the Reporting Calendar](/library/chp7v10/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. Orange marks the IAT cycle. Event-driven reports run on their own clocks and are not shown.\n\n# 11. Knowledge Management\n\n## 11.1 Disaster Event and Loss Database\n\nThe City needs one record of every disaster that affected it: what happened, where, to whom and at what cost (A5.4). The database uses the Sendai Framework and DesInventar fields (Annex 7-E), records barangay as the smallest unit using PSGC codes, and holds aggregate counts only. It starts with the events already documented (the 1991 flash flood, Yolanda in 2013, the 2017 earthquake, Urduja in 2017, Ursula in 2019 and Odette in 2021) and is updated after every event from the RDANA, DROMIC, PDNA and final SitRep. Each figure carries its source; implausible figures, such as the Yolanda damage printed in the 10-Year Plan, are flagged until verified (SOP 7-07).\n\n## 11.2 Records and the Vault\n\nDRRM records follow Chapter 1 (GOV-08). Issuances, plans, reports and evidence are kept in the MODE Vault; documents are superseded, never deleted, and each has an owner office and a date. Retention and disposal follow the schedules approved under RA 9470; nothing is disposed of by the CDRRMO on its own. Evidence files attached to IAT scores identify sources by office or position, never by an informant''s name (MR-15).\n\n## 11.3 Lessons Register and Manual Revision\n\nA lesson is recorded only when it changed something or when a change has been proposed. The lessons register (ME-F08) links each lesson to its source (AAR, exercise, IAT, peer exchange), the change made (plan, Manual section, form, training, MODE rule) and the CAPA entry. Changes to the Manual are made as new versions in the MODE Library; approved versions are never edited, and only the CDRRMO Head approves a version.\n\n## 11.4 Peer Learning\n\nOrmoc takes part in several networks: the Making Cities Resilient 2030 initiative, the National Resilience Council Resilient LGU program (SP Res. No. 2021-036), the EDC Adopt-a-City partnership and DENR–EDC Project TRANSFORM (EO 2023-0026), and OCD forums. Each exchange is recorded with what was learned and what the City changed as a result (A10.9). Partnerships themselves are managed under Chapter 8.\n\n# 12. Protection of DRRM Data\n\nDRRM work collects personal data: evacuee and beneficiary lists, casualty lists, contact rosters, volunteer records. The Data Privacy Act applies in full, including during emergencies. MODE is designed to hold aggregate counts only for affected populations; named lists stay with the office that collected them (CSWDO, CHO) and are shared only under a data-sharing agreement or as the law allows.\n\n**Table 7.14**\n\n*Data Protection Controls for DRRM Information*\n\n| **Control**             | **Requirement**                                                                                                                             | **Owner**                           |\n|-------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Data Protection Officer | The City DPO is designated and covers DRRM processing; a CDRRMO compliance officer for privacy is named.                                    | City Administrator; CDRRMO Head     |\n| Privacy notices         | Short notices on registration, evacuation and relief forms state purpose, sharing and retention.                                            | CSWDO; CDRRMO                       |\n| Data-sharing agreements | Signed with the CSWDO, CHO, CPDO and partners that exchange personal data with the CDRRMO.                                                  | City Legal Office; DPO              |\n| Aggregate rule          | Reports, SitReps, the event database and M&E products use counts; blanks are "not reported".                                                | M&E unit; EOC                       |\n| Small counts            | Where a count in a sex-, age- or disability-disaggregated table could identify a person, the DPO decides whether to suppress or combine it. | DPO                                 |\n| Security                | Role-based access; encryption in transit; access logs; shared-device sign-out (MODE ADR-004, ADR-014); NPC Circular No. 2023-06.            | City ICT office; platform custodian |\n| Breach response         | Contain, assess and, where required, notify the NPC and affected data subjects within 72 hours of knowledge (NPC Circular No. 16-03).       | DPO; CDRRMO Head                    |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The DPO designation, privacy notices and data-sharing agreements were not sighted in the document set (A5.5). The CPDO and the City Administrator''s Office are to confirm the current arrangements at the workshop.\n\n# 13. Management Review and Performance Reporting\n\nChapter 1 (section 9.4, GOV-09) sets governance KPIs. Each chapter of the Manual adds its own performance measures. The M&E unit consolidates them into one performance report for two reviews.\n\n**Table 7.15**\n\n*Management Reviews*\n\n| **Review**               | **Frequency**                                | **Inputs**                                                                                                   | **Outputs**                                                             |\n|--------------------------|----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRRMO management review | Quarterly                                    | Chapter KPIs; CAPA status; reporting calendar status; open AAR actions; stock and fund status from Chapter 6 | Decisions on overdue actions; items for the CDRRMC                      |\n| CDRRMC review            | Quarterly, with an annual session in January | Annual results report; IAT results; CAPA High-priority items; proposed Manual revisions                      | Resolutions on corrective actions, plan revisions and budget priorities |\n\n# 14. Standard Operating Procedures\n\nEach SOP has a control block and a step table. Every step states the actor (a position or office, never a person), the action and decision rule, inputs and outputs, and timing, control and escalation. Form codes are those of section 18. All MODE templates named here are proposals except where an existing template or form is cited; none of SOPs 7-01 to 7-09 is yet loaded in MODE.\n\n## SOP 7-01 \\| LDRRMP monitoring, annual results report and review\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning (M&E unit), with the CDRRMP Technical Working Group. **Purpose:** Measure the LDRRMP against its indicators each year and give the CDRRMC an evidence-based results report.\n\n**Table 7.16**\n\n*SOP 7-01 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                                                 |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP7-01 (proposed)                                                                                           |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMC approves the indicator set, targets and the annual report (RA 10121 Sec. 11(b)); LDC receives it                   |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                                                              |\n| **Trigger**                     | Approval of a new or revised LDRRMP (indicator set); each December (data call); each January (report)                     |\n| **Forms**                       | ME-F01; ME-F02                                                                                                            |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.4 (2, Medium); A3.1 (Chapter 3)                                                                                       |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-07; MR-11; MR-18                                                                                                       |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 10121 Sec. 11(b); NDRRMP 2020–2030 RBME Plan; NDRRMC LDRRMP Formulation Guidebook (2022); NDRRMC Memo No. 147, s. 2017 |\n| **Cross-references**            | Chapter 3 (LDRRMP); Chapter 6 (FIN-F03); SOPs 7-02, 7-05                                                                  |\n\n**Table 7.17**\n\n*SOP 7-01 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Set the matrix</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit with thematic focal offices</th>\n<th><p>Record each indicator on ME-F01 with definition, unit, baseline, target, source, frequency, responsible office and framework codes.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Every indicator has a baseline or a dated plan to establish one?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> LDRRMP; Form 5D</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F01 matrix</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 60 days of plan approval</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> CDRRMC approves the matrix</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Call for data</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Send the data call to focal offices with the ME-F01 extract for each.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Call sent to all focal offices?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> ME-F01</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Data call</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> First week of December</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Call logged</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Collect and check</th>\n<th>Focal offices; M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Offices submit values with means of verification; the M&amp;E unit checks source, date and arithmetic and queries large changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Value supported by a means of verification? If not, record "not verified".</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Office submissions</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Checked dataset; query log</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> By 31 December</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Blank means not reported, never zero</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Office head for missing data</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Analyse</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Compare values with baselines and targets; add IAT results (SOP 7-03), CAPA status (SOP 7-05) and financial data (Chapter 6).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Off-track results explained?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Checked dataset</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Draft ME-F02</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Second week of January</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Calculations kept for repetition</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Review consistency</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit with CPDO</th>\n<th><p>Check consistency of results and PPAs with the AIP and CDP (Memo No. 147, s. 2017).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Any PPA without an AIP line or vice versa?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> AIP; CDP</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Consistency note</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> With step 4</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Note attached to ME-F02</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CPDO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Present</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Present the annual report to the CDRRMC and the LDC.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Accepted? Decisions on off-track results?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> ME-F02</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> CDRRMC resolution; LDC minutes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> January meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Decisions entered as CAPA items</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Publish and file</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Post the summary report; file the full report and data in the Vault.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Posted?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Approved report</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Posting proof; Vault entry</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 15 days of approval</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> No personal data in published products</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** ME-F01 matrix; data call; checked dataset and query log; ME-F02 report; consistency note; CDRRMC resolution; posting proof.\n\n## SOP 7-02 \\| Annual institutional assessment cycle (IAT): planning, fieldwork and scoring\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning, with designated assessors. **Purpose:** Score all 140 IAT indicators each year with the same instrument and evidence rules, so that change in the CDRRMO and EOC can be measured.\n\n**Table 7.18**\n\n*SOP 7-02 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                      |\n|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | MODE Assess module: assessment cycle (status draft to fieldwork)               |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMO Head opens the cycle; the CDRRMC receives the results                   |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                   |\n| **Trigger**                     | Annual schedule (third quarter); or a CDRRMC request after a major change      |\n| **Forms**                       | MODE assessment responses and evidence; ME-F05 for gaps                        |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | All 140 indicators; A10.4 (2, Medium)                                          |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-12; MR-13; MR-14; MR-15                                                     |\n| **Policy basis**                | IAT v1.7 and Methodology v1.1; Traceability and Data Dictionary v1.2; RA 10173 |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOP 7-03; Introduction section 5                                               |\n\n**Table 7.19**\n\n*SOP 7-02 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Open the cycle</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Open the cycle in MODE with name, year, IAT version and period; confirm the instrument version.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Same instrument as last year? If changed, record the version change.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Previous cycle</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Cycle in draft</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> July</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Version recorded on the cycle</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Assign</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Assign assessors by domain and validators by domain, never the same person for the same indicator; name Part C lead-office focal persons.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Separation kept?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Staff list</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Assignment list</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> July</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> MODE enforces at validation</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Plan evidence</th>\n<th>Assessors</th>\n<th><p>List the means of verification for each indicator from the Data Dictionary; request documents from offices.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Request sent for every document?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Data Dictionary</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Evidence request log</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> August</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Requests logged</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Office heads</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Fieldwork</th>\n<th>Assessors</th>\n<th><p>Document review, key informant interviews, FGDs and observation; attach evidence with its source by office or position.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is the evidence attached, not just described?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Documents; interviews</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Evidence records in MODE</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> September–October</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> No informant names stored</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> M&amp;E unit</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Score</th>\n<th>Assessors</th>\n<th><p>Score 0–4 or NA by the rubric; apply the evidence gate (FGD or KII only: maximum 2); write the gap and recommended action.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Does the score match the strongest evidence?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Scored responses</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> By end of October</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> NA only with justification</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> M&amp;E unit</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Hand over to validation</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Move the cycle to validation; prepare the validation queue and printable sheets.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> All indicators scored or marked NA?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Scored responses</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Cycle in validation</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> November</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Completeness report</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Cycle record; assignment list; evidence request log; evidence records; scored responses with gaps and actions; completeness report.\n\n## SOP 7-03 \\| Validation of assessment scores and release of results\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with validators and the M&E unit. **Purpose:** Confirm or revise every score through people who did not set it, and release the IMI, compliance and gap priorities as final.\n\n**Table 7.20**\n\n*SOP 7-03 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                               |\n|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | MODE Assess module: validation workshop screens (v_validation_progress) |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMO Head finalises the cycle; CDRRMC receives the results            |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | M&E unit; validators                                                    |\n| **Trigger**                     | Cycle moved to validation (SOP 7-02 step 6)                             |\n| **Forms**                       | MODE validation records; printable validation sheets; ME-F05            |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | All 140 indicators                                                      |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-12; MR-13; MR-14                                                     |\n| **Policy basis**                | ADR-029; Methodology v1.1, section 6; IAT v1.7                          |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 7-02, 7-05; Introduction section 5.3                               |\n\n**Table 7.21**\n\n*SOP 7-03 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Prepare</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Brief validators on the rubric and evidence rule; issue the queue by domain.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Each validator has no indicator they scored?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Queue</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Briefing record</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before the workshop</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> MODE refuses self-validation</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Validate</th>\n<th>Validators</th>\n<th><p>Review evidence; confirm the score or revise it with a written note.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Evidence supports the score? A revision needs a note.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Scored responses; evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Confirmed or revised responses</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Workshop days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Submitted score kept on revision</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Lead validator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Resolve disputes</th>\n<th>Lead validator with the domain owner</th>\n<th><p>Settle disagreements on the evidence; record the reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Agreement reached? If not, the lower score stands.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Disputed items</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Resolution notes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> During the workshop</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Reason recorded</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Compute</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Let MODE compute the domain indices, IMI, Mandatory compliance, Interface Index and gap priorities.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Results match an independent recomputation from the export?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Validated responses</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Results set</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> After validation</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Second person recomputes</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Platform custodian for a mismatch</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Finalise</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Move the cycle to final; archive the previous cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> All items validated?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Results set</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Final cycle</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 10 days of the workshop</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Final cycles are not edited</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Act and report</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Copy High and Medium gaps into the CAPA register; report results to the CDRRMC and staff.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Every High gap has a CAPA entry?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Final results</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 entries; results report</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Next CDRRMC meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Agreement rate reported as calibration only</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Briefing record; validation records with notes; dispute resolutions; recomputation check; final cycle; CAPA entries; results report.\n\n## SOP 7-04 \\| After-action review and exercise evaluation\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with a designated AAR facilitator. **Purpose:** Learn from every significant event and exercise, and turn findings into tracked corrective actions.\n\n**Table 7.22**\n\n*SOP 7-04 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Protocol: PR-AAR (proposed)                                                                             |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMO Head approves the AAR report; CDRRMC decides actions beyond the CDRRMO''s authority               |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Head; AAR facilitator                                                                            |\n| **Trigger**                     | Any trigger in section 8 (Table 7.11); hot wash and ER-F06 from Chapter 4, SOP 4-09                     |\n| **Forms**                       | ER-F06 (input); ME-F03; ME-F04; ME-F05                                                                  |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.6 (2, Medium); B7.4 (2, Medium); B7.3 (2, Medium)                                                   |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-11; MR-04; MR-05                                                                                     |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 10121 Sec. 11(b); NDRRMC Memo No. 131, s. 2018 (EOC management); EOC Capability Assessment Checklist |\n| **Cross-references**            | Chapter 4 SOP 4-09; Chapter 9 (exercises); SOP 7-05                                                     |\n\n**Table 7.23**\n\n*SOP 7-04 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Register the trigger</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Record the event or exercise and the AAR due date.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Trigger in section 8?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Deactivation record; exercise plan</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> AAR register entry</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 1 day</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Due date set</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Name the facilitator</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Name a facilitator who did not hold a command role; set participants.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Facilitator independent of the event?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Register entry</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Facilitator and participant list</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 3 days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Partners and barangays invited where involved</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Reconstruct</th>\n<th>Facilitator with M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Build the timeline from warning products, the logbook, SitReps and resource requests.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Each entry sourced?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> MODE logbook; SitReps; ER-F06</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Timeline</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before the session</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Official product IDs cited</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Hold the session</th>\n<th>Facilitator</th>\n<th><p>Ask the four questions; record what worked, what did not and why; keep the discussion blame-free.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Causes identified, not only symptoms?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Timeline</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Session notes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within the timing in section 8</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Positions, not names, in the record</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Write the report</th>\n<th>Facilitator</th>\n<th><p>Write ME-F03 (or ME-F04 for exercises): findings with evidence, causes, recommended actions, owners.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Each finding has an action?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Notes</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F03 or ME-F04 draft</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 10 days of the session</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Template of Annex 7-C</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Approve</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Approve the report; send items beyond the CDRRMO''s authority to the CDRRMC.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Approved?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft report</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Approved report</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within the AAR due date</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Filed in the Vault</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Register actions</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Enter each action in the CAPA register; mark recurring findings for double-loop review.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Finding seen before? Then escalate.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Approved report</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 entries</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 5 days of approval</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Link to the report</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** AAR register; participant list; timeline; session notes; ME-F03 or ME-F04; CAPA entries.\n\n## SOP 7-05 \\| Corrective and preventive action management (CAPA register)\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning (register keeper); each accountable owner. **Purpose:** Track every finding from any source to verified, effective closure (operates GOV-10).\n\n**Table 7.24**\n\n*SOP 7-05 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                     |\n|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP7-05 (proposed); MODE actions for IAT-sourced items           |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMO Head closes; CDRRMC closes High-priority items and decides escalations |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                  |\n| **Trigger**                     | A new finding from any source in Figure 7.2                                   |\n| **Forms**                       | ME-F05; ER-F06 and FIN-F11 as inputs                                          |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.6 (2, Medium); B7.4 (2, Medium); all IAT gaps                             |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-11; MR-14                                                                  |\n| **Policy basis**                | Chapter 1, section 9.5 (Table 1.23); RA 10121 Sec. 11(b)                      |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 7-01, 7-03, 7-04; Chapter 6 SOP 6-10; Chapter 5 SOP 5-07                 |\n\n**Table 7.25**\n\n*SOP 7-05 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Enter</th>\n<th>Register keeper</th>\n<th><p>Enter source, problem statement and risk consequence; assign priority.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Duplicate of an open item? Then link instead.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Finding</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (Open)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 5 working days of the finding</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Source document linked</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Analyse and plan</th>\n<th>Accountable owner</th>\n<th><p>State the root cause, action, responsible actors, target date and resources; mark single- or double-loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Cause supported by evidence?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> ME-F05</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (Planned)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 15 working days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> CDRRMO Head confirms the plan</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Implement</th>\n<th>Responsible actors</th>\n<th><p>Carry out the action; update status.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> On schedule?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Plan</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (In progress)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> By the target date</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Status updated at least monthly</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Accountable owner</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Verify</th>\n<th>Register keeper</th>\n<th><p>Check evidence of completion.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Evidence complete?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (Implemented)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On notice of completion</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Evidence filed in the Vault</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Review effectiveness</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head (CDRRMC for High)</th>\n<th><p>Check at the next test, exercise, event or IAT cycle whether the problem recurred.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Effective? If not, reopen with a new cause analysis.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Next test result</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> ME-F05 (Closed – effective or Reopened)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> At the next test</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Closure only with effectiveness evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Report</th>\n<th>Register keeper</th>\n<th><p>Report status, overdue items and recurring findings to the quarterly reviews.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Overdue twice? Escalate.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Register</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> CAPA status report</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Quarterly</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Escalations recorded</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** ME-F05 register with status history; evidence of completion; effectiveness reviews; quarterly status reports.\n\n## SOP 7-06 \\| Post-declaration (45-day) report after a State of Imminent Disaster\n\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch7v1_v1.0.md (Chapter 7, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('bafb93ff-83cd-53aa-888f-c56553343bec'::uuid,'cb5fb5fd-5044-57b7-84fe-dff921b0c759'::uuid,'v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7)',E'**CHAPTER 8**\n\nSTAKEHOLDER ENGAGEMENT AND INSTITUTIONAL PARTNERSHIPS\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)**\n\n**AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft*\n\n*Subject to institutional, legal and partner validation*\n\n**Draft 1.0 \\| 12 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# Document Control\n\n**Table 8.1**\n\n*Document Control*\n\n| **Document attribute**     | **Control information**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Document title**         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 8                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| **Chapter title**          | Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| **Position in the Manual** | Eighth of ten chapters in the structure adopted on 12 November 2026. It follows Chapter 7 (Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement) and precedes Chapter 9 (Public Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development). Chapter 8 covers the City''s external relationships and its interfaces with other City offices; Chapter 9 covers building capacity in people, communities and institutions. |\n| **Document status**        | Working draft for technical, institutional, legal and partner validation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Document owner**         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Technical custodian**    | CDRRMO Head; Administration and Training Division (partner register and agreements); Research and Planning Division (Part C interface review)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| **Reviewing offices**      | City Legal Office; Office of the City Administrator; Sangguniang Panlungsod Secretariat; Part C lead offices (CPDO, Zoning Administrator, City Engineer and Building Official, CENRO, City Health Office, City Agriculture and Veterinary Offices, City Budget Office); CSWDO; DILG City Office; Province of Leyte PDRRMO; OCD Region VIII; partner organisations named in the register                                                  |\n| **Version / date**         | Draft 1.0 / 12 November 2026                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| **Approval authority**     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements; in MODE, only the CDRRMO Head approves Manual versions                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Review cycle**           | At least annually, at the CDRRMC meeting that reviews the partner register; after an event in which mutual aid or partner support was activated; when a partner agreement is signed, amended or ends                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n\n## Revision and Approval Record\n\n**Table 8.2**\n\n*Revision and Approval Record*\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | **Prepared / reviewed by**        | **Approval** |\n|-------------|-------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | 12 Nov 2026 | Initial assessment-informed working draft under the ten-chapter structure. Built from IAT v1.7 rows A10.1 and A10.2, the 15 Part C interface indicators (C1.1 to C5.1), and the related rows A1.2, A8.5 and B6.6; from the register of City issuances (1993–2025); and from RA 10121, RA 7160 and RA 12287 and its IRR. Eight SOPs (8-01 to 8-08), seven PTN forms, Annexes 8-A to 8-D. | Project drafting team with CDRRMO | For review   |\n\n> READING NOTE. A partnership allocates work; it does not transfer legal authority. Every office, agency, utility, LGU and organisation named in this chapter keeps its own mandate, chain of command and accountability. No agreement under this chapter binds the City until the Sangguniang Panlungsod has authorised it and the City Mayor has signed it (RA 7160 Sec. 22(c)).\n\n# 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nNo city manages disaster risk alone. In Ormoc, the decisions that most change risk are made outside the CDRRMO: where the CPDO allows development, whether the Building Official has checked an evacuation centre, how fast LEYECO V restores power to the hospital, whether the schools can keep teaching, whether a neighbouring town can lend a rescue team when every Ormoc team is deployed. Chapters 1 to 7 set what the CDRRMO and the EOC do. This chapter sets how they work with everyone else, before, during and after an event.\n\nThe chapter covers two kinds of relationship. The first is **external**: with the Province, the region and national agencies; with neighbouring LGUs; and with CSOs, volunteer groups, the private sector, utilities, academe, faith-based groups and communities (Figure 8.1). The second is the **interface** with other City offices that own systems on which disaster risk depends. The IAT measures this second kind separately, in Part C, and this chapter is its home in the Manual.\n\n**Figure 8.1**\n\n*The City''s DRRM Stakeholder Ecosystem*\n\n![Figure 1: 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale](/library/chp8v10/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from the CDRRMC membership in EO 2024-032A, the IAT v1.7 Part C lead offices and the register of City issuances. Placement shows the kind of relationship, not its importance.\n\nThe baseline shows a City that helps others and works with many partners, but mostly event by event and without standing agreements (Table 8.3). Partnerships with CSOs, the private sector and academe are documented and in use (A10.2, score 3). There is no reciprocal mutual aid agreement with any neighbouring LGU (A10.1, score 1), although the City has sent cash and rice to LGUs in Leyte, Southern Leyte, Cebu, Mindanao and Batangas after their disasters. Part C, the interface with other City offices, has an Interface Index of **1.41 (Initial)**, with two High gaps: the structural safety of the EOC, evacuation centres and critical facilities (C2.1) and health anticipatory action (C4.1) (Figure 8.2).\n\n**Table 8.3**\n\n*Assessment Findings That Shape This Chapter*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                                       | **Score** | **Priority** | **Finding (summarised from the workbook)**                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n|--------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A10.1  | MOAs and mutual aid agreements with neighbouring LGUs and the Province (GP, w2)                                                | 1         | Medium (6.0) | Assistance given after Odette (2021), Taal (2020) and the Mindanao earthquakes (2019), but no standing reciprocal agreement. The FGD noted political dynamics and suggested reframing as technical mutual aid.                |\n| A10.2  | Partnerships with CSOs, private sector, academe and faith-based groups documented, with roles and annual coordination (GP, w1) | 3         | Low (1.0)    | Documented and in use (NRC–CPRF–EDC MOA; CPR-RIMT MOA; tertiary institutions MOA; Project TRANSFORM). Lowered from 4: no annual review of MOAs with recorded improvements; CPR-RIMT copy unsigned; no faith-based instrument. |\n| B6.6   | Pre-arranged commitments from partners and suppliers in signed agreements (GP, w2)                                             | 1         | Medium (6.0) | No agreements listing item, quantity, trigger and contact. Recorded under Chapter 6 (SOP 6-12) once signed here.                                                                                                              |\n| A1.2   | CDRRMC constituted with complete membership, including CSO and private-sector seats (M, w2)                                    | 3         | Low (3.0)    | EO 2024-032A names 5 CSOs and 2 private-sector seats (EDC, LEYECO V); third private seat blank; academe seat dropped; SP accreditation of CSOs not shown. Owned by Chapter 1; the engagement steps are in SOP 8-06.           |\n| A8.5   | Response clusters organised at city level (M, w2)                                                                              | 3         | Low (3.0)    | Cluster lead offices sit on the CDRRMC; the CCCM Team EO of 2022 includes the Red Cross, an NGO and a civic group. Owned by Chapter 4; partner membership is agreed here.                                                     |\n| Part C | Interface Index over 15 indicators (lead offices other than the CDRRMO)                                                        | 1.41      | 2 High       | C2.1 (Building Official) and C4.1 (CHO and hospitals) are High; 9 Medium; 4 Low. No standing interface agreement with any lead office.                                                                                        |\n\n*Note.* Priority is the workbook band of (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 (Mandatory) or × 1.0 (Good Practice and all Part C items): High 9 or more, Medium 4 to 8.9, Low below 4. Part C is reported separately and never added to the IMI. Annex 8-A gives the full row set.\n\n**Figure 8.2**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Part C Interface Scores by Lead Office*\n\n![Figure 2: 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale](/library/chp8v10/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Scores copied from the C_Interface sheet of IAT v1.7. The Interface Index is Σ(score × weight) ÷ Σweight = 38 ÷ 27 = 1.41, banded on the unrounded value (Initial, 0.5–1.49). Orange bars are the two High gaps (weight 3, score 1). Part C has no Mandatory items. All rows are pending validation.\n\n## 1.1 What the Records Show\n\n-   **Helping neighbours, without an agreement.** After Typhoon Odette the CDRRMC and the SP sent cash to five Southern Leyte LGUs, five Leyte LGUs, Cebu City and the Province of Cebu (CDRRMC Res. Nos. 12, 14 and 15, s. 2021; SP Res. Nos. 2021-365, -367 and -369, the first authorising PHP 11.24 million from the 2021 QRF) and donated rice (SP Res. No. 2021-368). Earlier resolutions assisted LGUs hit by the Mindanao earthquakes (2019) and the Taal eruption (2020). CDRRMC Res. No. 13, s. 2021 adopted Proclamation No. 1267 so that the City could use its LDRRMF to assist other affected LGUs. None of these created a reciprocal arrangement.\n\n-   **Partnerships that exist.** SP Res. No. 2021-036 authorised the MOA among the City, the National Resilience Council, the Carlos P. Romulo Foundation and EDC. SP Res. No. 2021-357 authorised an MOU with the Oscar M. Lopez Center for climate and geo-hazard research and training. SP Res. No. 2023-347 authorised the MOA with the CPR-RIMT Rapid Rescue Rider Ormoc Chapter for volunteer augmentation of HEMS and the CDRRMO (the annexed copy is unsigned). CDRRMC Res. No. 04, s. 2025 created an Education Resilience and DRRM Coordination Group with all tertiary institutions.\n\n-   **Working groups with partners.** EO 2023-0025 (CDRA Technical Working Group, with LEYECO V and NGCP among partners); EO 2023-0026 (Project TRANSFORM Technical Working Group with DENR, EDC and the NRC under the Adopt-a-City program); EO 2023-0042 (El Niño Task Force, including CPR-RIMT and two farmers'' cooperatives); the City CCCM Team EO of 2022 (CSWDO-led, with the Red Cross, an NGO and a civic group; the EO number is to be confirmed).\n\n-   **Council seats.** EO 2024-032A gives the CDRRMC five CSO seats and two private-sector seats (EDC Leyte Geothermal and LEYECO V); a third private-sector seat is blank. The academe seat of the earlier EO was dropped; CDRRMC Res. No. 10, s. 2024 recommends the City College of Ormoc as a regular member, with no amending EO yet.\n\n-   **Volunteers.** CDRRMC Res. No. 07, s. 2024 approves the accreditation of community disaster volunteers under RA 10121 Sec. 13, and Res. Nos. 03 and 04, s. 2022 cover their guidelines and insurance. Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024 institutionalises the City volunteer program.\n\n-   **Utilities, event by event.** CDRRMC Res. No. 10, s. 2021 asked LEYECO V to run its 2 MW standby generator at Barangay Alta Vista during the Odette outage. There is no standing restoration-priority arrangement with power, water or telecom providers (C2.2).\n\n## 1.2 Design Considerations\n\n1.  **Mandates stay with their owners.** Agreements set who does what and how to ask; they never move command, legal powers or accountability.\n\n2.  **Agree before the event.** A partner who has signed, been briefed and been tested in a drill can be called in minutes; a partner who has not is a hope.\n\n3.  **Technical, reciprocal and apolitical.** Mutual aid is framed as an exchange between technical offices for defined resources, activated by request and governed by the host LGU''s command, so that it survives changes in leadership.\n\n4.  **One register.** Every agreement, its term, its focal person and its review date sit in one partner register, so that lapsed or unsigned agreements are seen and not counted.\n\n5.  **Interfaces are owned elsewhere.** For Part C, the lead office owns the indicator and the work; the CDRRMO coordinates, supplies risk information and reports. This chapter sets the interface, not the technical content, which stays in Chapters 3 to 6.\n\n6.  **Inclusion and accountability.** Groups that represent women, children, older persons, persons with disabilities and other at-risk people are partners, not only beneficiaries, and communities have a way to raise concerns.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter sets how the City identifies, agrees, authorises, registers, exercises, activates and reviews its DRRM relationships with other governments, organisations and City offices.\n\nIntended results:\n\n1.  A partner register lists every DRRM agreement with its legal authority, term, focal persons, roles before and after events, and review date (A10.2).\n\n2.  Technical mutual aid agreements are in force with Kananga, Albuera, Merida and Isabel and, where possible, the Province of Leyte (A10.1).\n\n3.  Each Part C lead office has an interface agreement naming its focal person, the actions it owns and how it reports, and the Interface Index rises from its baseline (MR-17).\n\n4.  Requests for augmentation follow the vertical route of the national response system, and incoming teams are received, briefed, tasked and demobilised in order.\n\n5.  Power, water and telecom providers have standing restoration-priority arrangements (C2.2).\n\n6.  The CDRRMC has its full membership, with SP-accredited CSOs, all private-sector seats filled and an academe seat (A1.2).\n\n7.  Partners meet at least once a year before the typhoon season, and their commitments are recorded in the Chapter 6 resource inventory (B6.6).\n\n8.  Assistance to other LGUs follows a lawful, documented route.\n\n# 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\n**In scope:** vertical coordination with the Province, the region and national agencies; horizontal mutual aid with other LGUs; partnerships with CSOs, volunteer groups, the private sector, utilities, academe, faith-based groups, development partners and networks; interface arrangements with Part C lead offices; partner membership of the CDRRMC and the response clusters; partner engagement in planning, exercises and reviews; assistance to other LGUs.\n\n**Table 8.4**\n\n*Subjects Handled in Other Chapters*\n\n| **Subject**                                                                                           | **Where it is handled**           |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| Composition of the CDRRMC and the issuances that create it                                            | Chapter 1 (A1.2; Annex 1-B)       |\n| Operation of response clusters and liaison desks during activation                                    | Chapter 4 (SOPs 4-04 to 4-07)     |\n| Technical content of Part C items (scenarios, zoning, structural checks, sector anticipatory actions) | Chapters 3, 4 and 5               |\n| Supplier framework agreements; recording partner commitments in the resource inventory; donations     | Chapter 6 (SOPs 6-06, 6-08, 6-12) |\n| Data-sharing agreements and privacy                                                                   | Chapter 7 (SOP 7-09)              |\n| Peer learning and networks as sources of lessons                                                      | Chapter 7 (SOP 7-08)              |\n| Training and accreditation of volunteers; BDRRMC capacity; public awareness                           | Chapter 9                         |\n| Recognition systems that assess partnerships                                                          | Chapter 10                        |\n\n**Table 8.5**\n\n*Legal Holders of Partnership Functions and the CDRRMO Role*\n\n| **Function**                                                              | **Holder (legal)**                                                     | **CDRRMO role**                                                     |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Authority to enter into an agreement on behalf of the City                | Sangguniang Panlungsod (RA 7160 Sec. 22(c))                            | Prepares the draft and the justification; the CDRRMC endorses       |\n| Signing the agreement                                                     | City Mayor                                                             | Routes for signature; files the signed copy                         |\n| Legal review of terms                                                     | City Legal Office                                                      | Requests review; records the advice                                 |\n| Accreditation of CSOs for local special bodies                            | Sangguniang Panlungsod, under the Local Government Code and DILG rules | Lists CSOs proposed for CDRRMC seats                                |\n| Accreditation of disaster volunteers                                      | CDRRMC under RA 10121 Sec. 13 and NDRRMC rules                         | Keeps the registry; Chapter 9 trains                                |\n| Requests for augmentation from the Province, region and national agencies | City Mayor as CDRRMC Chair                                             | Prepares the request; tracks the response                           |\n| Command of resources received under mutual aid                            | The host LGU''s Incident Commander, under ICS                           | Receives, briefs and tasks through the EOC (Chapter 4)              |\n| Assistance to other LGUs                                                  | CDRRMC recommends; SP authorises; Mayor approves                       | Prepares the request and the record (SOP 8-08)                      |\n| Part C indicators                                                         | Each lead office within its own mandate                                | Coordinates, supplies risk information, reports the Interface Index |\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\nTable 8.6 lists the instruments that govern partnerships and interfaces in DRRM. The official text governs.\n\n**Table 8.6**\n\n*Instruments Governing Stakeholder Engagement and Partnerships*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                        