, CSOs and the private sector.                                                                                 | Section 10; SOP 8-06; Chapter 9                |\n| RA 7160 (Local Government Code) Sec. 22(c)                                                                                                                                                                             | No contract may be entered into by the local chief executive on behalf of the LGU without prior authorisation by the sanggunian.                                                | SOPs 8-01, 8-02, 8-05                          |\n| RA 7160 Sec. 33                                                                                                                                                                                                        | LGUs may group themselves, consolidate or coordinate efforts, services and resources for commonly beneficial purposes, through agreements approved by their sanggunians.        | SOP 8-02                                       |\n| RA 7160 Secs. 34–36                                                                                                                                                                                                    | LGUs promote the establishment and operation of people''s and non-governmental organisations, may enter into joint ventures with them, and may assist them.                      | Section 10; SOP 8-01                           |\n| National Disaster Response Plan and the cluster approach (NDCC Circular No. 5, s. 2007 and later NDCC and NDRRMC issuances)                                                                                            | Response clusters with designated leads at national, regional and provincial levels; augmentation through the response system.                                                  | Section 7; SOP 8-03                            |\n| NDRRMC MC No. 4, s. 2012 (Incident Command System)                                                                                                                                                                     | Resources from other jurisdictions work under the incident''s command structure.                                                                                                 | SOPs 8-02, 8-03                                |\n| DSWD–DILG–DOH–DepEd JMC No. 02, s. 2021                                                                                                                                                                                | Camp coordination and camp management, and protection of internally displaced persons, with partner roles.                                                                      | Section 10 (CCCM partners)                     |\n| RA 12287 and IRR (signed 14 Sep 2026), Rules III to IV                                                                                                                                                                 | Request to the RDRRMC for a State of Imminent Disaster; anticipatory actions by sector and by lifeline, including pre-arranged agreements with suppliers and service providers. | Section 6 (Part C); SOPs 8-04, 8-05            |\n| RA 9418 (Volunteer Act of 2007)                                                                                                                                                                                        | Local volunteer programs.                                                                                                                                                       | Basis of Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024; Chapter 9 |\n| RA 10072 (Philippine Red Cross Act of 2009)                                                                                                                                                                            | The Red Cross as an auxiliary to the government in the humanitarian field.                                                                                                      | Section 10                                     |\n| UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard, Essentials 1, 6, 7 and 8                                                                                                                                                          | Organisation for resilience, institutional capacity, societal capacity and infrastructure resilience, including partner engagement and lifeline arrangements.                   | MR-18; Part C crosswalk                        |\n| Local: EO 2024-032A; EO 2023-0025; EO 2023-0026; EO 2023-0042; CCCM Team EO (2022); SP Res. Nos. 2021-036, 2021-357, 2023-347, 2024-102; CDRRMC Res. No. 04, s. 2025; Res. No. 07, s. 2024; Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024 | Council membership, working groups, partner agreements, donations authority, volunteer accreditation and program.                                                               | Baseline (section 1.1) and partner register    |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. IAT v1.7 cites "NDCC MC No. 12, s. 2008" for the cluster approach (A8.5), while the DILG VIII reporting SOP cites NDCC Circular No. 5, s. 2007 and No. 04, s. 2008. The CDRRMO is to confirm the controlling citation with OCD VIII before adoption.\n\n# 5. Systems Thinking and Partnership Principles\n\nPartnerships fail in predictable ways. A partner is called only during events, so neither side knows the other''s capacity; agreements are signed by one administration and forgotten by the next; the same few organisations are asked every time while others drift away. These are feedback problems: engagement that happens only under pressure builds little trust, and low trust makes the next request slower. The remedy is steady engagement between events, so that trust and readiness accumulate.\n\n-   **Relationships before resources.** Meet partners before the season, exercise with them, and review with them after events. The annual partner forum (SOP 8-07) is the main mechanism.\n\n-   **Reciprocity.** Mutual aid that only flows one way is charity; agreements state what each side can offer and how costs are settled.\n\n-   **Institutional, not personal.** Agreements name positions and offices, not persons, and survive changes in leadership.\n\n-   **Clear interfaces.** Every interface names who leads, who supports and who decides, so that partners do not wait for each other.\n\n-   **Accountability to affected people.** Partners working with affected communities follow the same feedback and protection rules as City offices (Chapter 5, SOP 5-08).\n\n**Figure 8.3**\n\n*Vertical and Horizontal Coordination*\n\n![Figure 3: 5. Systems Thinking and Partnership Principles](/library/chp8v10/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. The vertical route follows the national response system; the RA 12287 request goes from the CDRRMC to the RDRRMC VIII (Chapter 1, SOP GOV-05). Horizontal mutual aid is activated by request under standing agreements (SOP 8-02).\n\n# 6. Interface Governance with City Offices (Part C)\n\n## 6.1 Principle\n\nPart C of the IAT measures systems that reduce or manage disaster risk but sit outside the CDRRMO: land use and investment planning, building safety, lifelines, ecosystems, sector anticipatory actions and business continuity. Each indicator names a lead office. The lead office owns the indicator, the work and the evidence. The CDRRMO coordinates, supplies hazard and risk information, convenes the annual interface review and reports the Interface Index to the CDRRMC. The Interface Index is never added to the IMI, so a weak interface cannot be hidden by a strong CDRRMO score, or the reverse.\n\n## 6.2 The Fifteen Interfaces\n\n**Table 8.7**\n\n*Part C Interfaces, Lead Offices and CDRRMO Role*\n\n| **ID** | **Interface (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                 | **Lead office**                                    | **Score** | **Priority** | **CDRRMO role and Manual home**                                                |\n|--------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| C1.1   | Risk scenarios inform CDP and CLUP priorities and investment siting                                      | CPDO                                               | 2         | Medium (4)   | Supplies scenarios from Chapter 3                                              |\n| C1.2   | DRR-CCA screening in LDC and LFC project and budget approval                                             | CPDO; Budget                                       | 1         | Low (3)      | Signs off the screening form (Chapter 6, SOP 6-01 step 4)                      |\n| C1.3   | CDRA estimates losses beyond physical assets                                                             | CPDO                                               | 2         | Low (2)      | Provides event and loss data (Chapter 7, SOP 7-07)                             |\n| C1.4   | Risk-sensitive zoning and building permits based on hazard maps                                          | CPDO; Zoning Administrator                         | 2         | Medium (4)   | Sits on the Local Zoning Review Committee; supplies hazard maps                |\n| C2.1   | Structural safety of the EOC, evacuation centres and critical facilities; rapid pre-impact inspection    | City Engineer / Building Official                  | 1         | High (9)     | Lists facilities and requests inspections at the PDRA stage (Chapters 2 and 4) |\n| C2.2   | Pre-crisis and restoration-priority arrangements with power, water and telecom providers                 | CDRRMO with utilities                              | 2         | Medium (4)   | Leads the arrangements (SOP 8-05)                                              |\n| C2.3   | Transport anticipatory actions: bridges, alternate routes, traffic, port                                 | City Engineer; traffic office; DPWH; port operator | 1         | Medium (6)   | Convenes the agreement; Chapter 4 runs the actions                             |\n| C3.1   | Protective ecosystems identified and monitored                                                           | CENRO; DENR CENRO                                  | 2         | Medium (4)   | Receives the annual report for the CDRRMC                                      |\n| C3.2   | Watershed arrangements with neighbouring LGUs and DENR                                                   | CENRO                                              | 1         | Low (3)      | Links to mutual aid talks (SOP 8-02)                                           |\n| C3.3   | Environmental anticipatory actions: declogging, slope stabilisation, debris                              | CENRO; City Engineer; GSO                          | 1         | Medium (6)   | Places the protocol in the contingency plans (Chapter 3)                       |\n| C4.1   | Health anticipatory action: cluster activation, surge plans, prepositioned commodities, teams on standby | CHO; hospitals                                     | 1         | High (9)     | Convenes the health AA plan with the CHO and hospitals (Chapter 4)             |\n| C4.2   | Education anticipatory action and learning continuity                                                    | DepEd SDO Ormoc City                               | 1         | Medium (6)   | Agrees triggers for class suspension and school use (Chapter 4)                |\n| C4.3   | Agri-fisheries anticipatory action using RSBSA, FishR and livestock registries                           | City Agriculture; City Veterinary                  | 2         | Medium (4)   | Supplies forecasts and the PDRA schedule (Chapter 4)                           |\n| C4.4   | Pre-impact price monitoring of basic goods                                                               | DTI Leyte; city market office                      | 1         | Low (3)      | Requests monitoring from the PDRA stage                                        |\n| C5.1   | Business continuity outreach using shared hazard scenarios                                               | CDRRMO with the Chamber of Commerce                | 1         | Low (3)      | Leads outreach with the Chamber (section 9)                                    |\n\n*Note.* Scores and lead offices are copied from the C_Interface sheet of IAT v1.7. Gap priority for Part C is (4 − score) × weight × 1.0. Part C reaches High only at weight 3 and score 0 or 1.\n\n## 6.3 Interface Agreements and the Annual Review\n\nEach lead office signs an interface agreement with the CDRRMO (PTN-F04): a joint office order or memorandum of understanding between City offices, approved by the City Administrator, or included in an executive order where the Mayor prefers. It names the focal person by position, the Part C indicators the office owns, the actions it will take in the year, the information the CDRRMO will give it, and how it reports. Once a year, before the IAT cycle, the CDRRMO convenes the lead offices for an interface review (SOP 8-04): each office presents its evidence, the Interface Index is computed, and gaps go to the CAPA register (Chapter 7). In MODE, the indicator_lead_offices table links each Part C indicator to its lead office, so the lead office''s focal person sees and scores its own items.\n\n# 7. Vertical Coordination: Province, Region and National Agencies\n\nThe City is part of the national DRRM system. Its CDRRMC sits under the Provincial DRRMC of Leyte and the RDRRMC VIII, and many national agencies have offices and assets in or near the City. Most of them already sit on the CDRRMC (EO 2024-032A).\n\n**Table 8.8**\n\n*Vertical Partners and the Relationship With Each*\n\n| **Partner**                                                            | **Relationship**                                                                                                                           | **Where in the Manual**                            |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n| Province of Leyte (PDRRMC and PDRRMO)                                  | First level for augmentation and provincial reporting; provincial programmes and trainings.                                                | SOP 8-03; Chapter 4 SitReps                        |\n| RDRRMC VIII and OCD Region VIII                                        | Regional coordination; RA 12287 request and reply; reports, including the 45-day report; technical assistance and trainings; PDNA support. | Chapter 1 (GOV-05); Chapter 7 (SOP 7-06); SOP 8-03 |\n| DILG Region VIII and City Office                                       | Reporting templates and cut-off times; Operation L!sto; SGLG; local governance advice; CDRRMC member.                                      | Chapters 4, 7 and 10                               |\n| DSWD Field Office VIII                                                 | Family food packs and non-food item augmentation; DROMIC reporting; CCCM standards.                                                        | Chapters 4 and 6                                   |\n| DOH Center for Health Development VIII (HEMS)                          | Health cluster; health emergency augmentation; CDRRMC member.                                                                              | Chapter 4; Part C C4.1                             |\n| DepEd Schools Division of Ormoc City                                   | Schools as evacuation centres; class suspension; learning continuity; CDRRMC member.                                                       | Chapter 4; Part C C4.2                             |\n| PNP, BFP, PCG, AFP (802nd Brigade), DPWH, PPA, Philippine Navy reserve | Response assets and augmentation; law and order; search and rescue; road and port operations; CDRRMC members.                              | Chapter 4; Part C C2.3                             |\n| PAGASA and PHIVOLCS                                                    | Authoritative warning products; the City preserves their wording and meaning.                                                              | Chapter 4 (SOPs 4-01, 4-02)                        |\n\n**Requesting augmentation.** When City capacity is or will be exceeded, the EOC prepares a request that states the need, quantity, location, time needed and the City''s own resources already committed. The Mayor, as CDRRMC Chair, sends it to the Province, and through the Province to the region where national assets are needed, as the National Disaster Response Plan and the regional SOPs provide (SOP 8-03). The RA 12287 request for a State of Imminent Disaster is the one exception that goes directly from the CDRRMC to the RDRRMC VIII, under Chapter 1, SOP GOV-05. Incoming national or provincial teams are received at a staging area, briefed and tasked through the EOC, and work under the Incident Commander.\n\n# 8. Horizontal Coordination: Mutual Aid With Other LGUs\n\nOrmoc''s neighbours (Kananga, Albuera, Merida and Isabel) share its watersheds, roads and hazards. A flash flood in the Anilao and Malbasag catchment, an earthquake on the Philippine Fault or a geothermal incident can affect several of them at once, or overwhelm Ormoc while its neighbours are spared. RA 7160 Sec. 33 allows LGUs to coordinate efforts, services and resources by agreements approved by their sanggunians. The IAT recommends **technical mutual aid agreements**, framed between DRRM offices for defined resources, so that cooperation does not depend on political alignment.\n\n**Table 8.9**\n\n*Elements of a Technical Mutual Aid Agreement*\n\n| **Element**                     | **Content**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Parties and authority**       | The LGUs, each acting through its Mayor with prior sanggunian authority; the Province may join.                                                                                                                                                         |\n| **Scope**                       | Defined resource types: search and rescue teams, emergency medical teams, heavy equipment, water tankers, communications, evacuation centre capacity, technical staff (engineers, damage assessors, CCCM staff); information sharing on shared hazards. |\n| **Activation**                  | By written or recorded request from the requesting LGU''s Mayor or authorised DRRM officer; the assisting LGU may decline if its own area is threatened.                                                                                                 |\n| **Command and safety**          | Resources work under the host LGU''s Incident Commander under ICS, keep their own supervisors, and may be withdrawn by the sending LGU for its own emergency.                                                                                            |\n| **Duration and demobilisation** | Default period per deployment, renewable; demobilisation through the host EOC with a handover record.                                                                                                                                                   |\n| **Costs**                       | Who bears travel, fuel, food and lodging; how any reimbursement is settled; consumables replaced in kind or paid.                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Liability and insurance**     | Each LGU insures its own personnel and equipment; treatment of injury, damage and third-party claims.                                                                                                                                                   |\n| **Information**                 | Shared contact directory, warning and SitRep exchange; data protection under Chapter 7.                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| **Exercise and review**         | At least one joint exercise or call-down test a year; annual review; term and renewal.                                                                                                                                                                  |\n\n*Note.* Annex 8-B gives a model outline. Terms are to be negotiated by the parties and reviewed by the City Legal Office; this chapter fixes none of them.\n\nWatersheds that extend beyond the City (C3.2) are a natural first subject: the Anilao–Malbasag and Pagsangaan catchments link upstream land use to downstream flood risk, and an agreement on information sharing and joint monitoring with DENR, EDC and the upstream LGUs can be negotiated alongside mutual aid. The 10-Year Plan''s planned Leyte DRRMCs Telecom and Response Network is the route for joining a provincial network.\n\n# 9. Private Sector and Utilities\n\nPrivate firms and utilities own much of what keeps the City running: power generation and distribution, transmission, telecommunications, water, fuel, transport and supplies. Some are also hazard sources (the geothermal field, industrial sites) and all are themselves exposed.\n\n**Table 8.10**\n\n*Private-Sector and Utility Partners*\n\n| **Partner**                                         | **Before an event**                                                                                               | **During and after an event**                                                                      |\n|-----------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| EDC Leyte Geothermal (CDRRMC member)                | Shared hazard information; joint exercises for geothermal incidents; Project TRANSFORM and NRC programmes         | Emergency notification for incidents on its sites; equipment and personnel support under agreement |\n| LEYECO V (CDRRMC member) and NGCP                   | Restoration-priority list (hospitals, EOC, evacuation centres, water pumps); standby generation; contact protocol | Outage reports to the EOC; restoration in agreed priority (SOP 8-05)                               |\n| Telecommunications providers                        | Cell-on-wheels and backup power arrangements; emergency alert broadcasts under RA 10639                           | Network status reports; priority restoration                                                       |\n| Water service (City Waterworks and other providers) | Rationing and tanker plans                                                                                        | Water supply status; tanker deployment                                                             |\n| Chamber of Commerce and businesses                  | Business continuity outreach on shared hazard scenarios (C5.1); a seat for the private sector on the CDRRMC       | Information on business disruption; voluntary support through the donations process (Chapter 6)    |\n| Suppliers under framework agreements                | Agreements under Chapter 6 (SOP 6-06)                                                                             | Call-offs and deliveries                                                                           |\n\nEngagement with the private sector is transparent. No partner receives preference in procurement because of a partnership; purchases follow Chapter 6 and RA 12009. Members of the CDRRMC declare any interest in a matter before the Council.\n\n# 10. Civil Society, Volunteers, Faith-based and Humanitarian Organisations\n\n-   **CSOs on the CDRRMC.** The five CSO seats are filled by organisations accredited by the SP; their representatives bring community knowledge and reach. SOP 8-06 sets how seats are filled and renewed.\n\n-   **Volunteer groups.** Organised groups such as the CPR-RIMT Rapid Rescue Riders work under signed MOAs that state training, equipment, tasking, insurance and liability. Individual community disaster volunteers are accredited under RA 10121 Sec. 13 (Res. No. 07, s. 2024) and the City volunteer program (Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024); Chapter 9 trains them. Spontaneous volunteers in an event are registered and tasked through the EOC, never deployed unregistered.\n\n-   **Philippine Red Cross.** As an auxiliary to the government in the humanitarian field (RA 10072), the Red Cross sits on the CDRRMC and the CCCM Team; its roles in first aid, welfare, blood services and restoring family links are agreed in advance.\n\n-   **Faith-based groups.** Churches, mosques and other faith communities often shelter evacuees and distribute relief. No agreement with any faith-based group was found. The CDRRMO invites them to the annual partner forum and agrees, where they wish, on the use of their facilities as evacuation sites (subject to the Building Official''s safety check) and on relief coordination.\n\n-   **CCCM partners.** The City CCCM Team (EO of 2022) brings partners into camp management under the JMC No. 02, s. 2021 standards; their roles are part of the partner register.\n\n-   **Sectoral groups.** Organisations of persons with disabilities, older persons, women, children and youth are invited to planning, exercises and reviews, so that plans reflect their needs (with the PDAO, OSCA and GAD focal point).\n\n# 11. Academe, Science and Development Partners\n\nThe City''s academic and scientific partners give it research, data, training and technical review it could not afford alone: the tertiary institutions under the Education Resilience and DRRM Coordination Group (CDRRMC Res. No. 04, s. 2025), the City College of Ormoc and EVSU Ormoc, the Oscar M. Lopez Center (SP Res. No. 2021-357), the National Resilience Council and EDC through Project TRANSFORM. Their work is recorded in the partner register with its outputs. Research that uses City data follows a data-sharing agreement (Chapter 7, SOP 7-09). Development partners and donors are engaged through the same register; funds and goods they provide follow Chapter 6.\n\n# 12. Communities and Accountability to Affected People\n\nBarangays are the City''s closest partners. Their BDRRMCs plan, warn, evacuate and report; Chapter 9 builds their capacity and Chapter 4 coordinates them in events. This chapter adds two commitments. First, communities have a way to raise concerns and suggestions about DRRM services at any time, through the CDRRMO hotline and feedback desk, and partners working in communities use the same channel. Second, the City reports back: the annual results report (Chapter 7) is published, and the CDRRMO explains to barangays what changed after major events.\n\n# 13. The Partner Register and the Partnership Lifecycle\n\nEvery DRRM relationship with a written basis is recorded in the partner register (PTN-F01) and follows the lifecycle in Figure 8.4. An agreement is counted as a commitment only when it is authorised, signed, in force and filed. The register is reviewed at a CDRRMC meeting each year before the typhoon season.\n\n**Figure 8.4**\n\n*The Partnership Lifecycle*\n\n![Figure 4: 13. The Partner Register and the Partnership Lifecycle](/library/chp8v10/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from RA 7160 Sec. 22(c), the IAT v1.7 recommendation for A10.2 and the CDRRMC''s practice of approving partner agreements by resolution.\n\n**Table 8.11**\n\n*Partner Register Fields (PTN-F01)*\n\n| **Field**            | **Content**                                                                                                                   |\n|----------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Partner and type** | Name; type (LGU, national agency, utility, private, CSO, volunteer group, academe, faith-based, development partner, network) |\n| **Instrument**       | MOA, MOU, EO, resolution, interface agreement; number and date; SP authority; signatories; copy in the Vault                  |\n| **Term**             | Start, end and renewal date; status (draft, authorised, signed, in force, lapsed, ended)                                      |\n| **Focal persons**    | Positions on both sides; 24-hour contact                                                                                      |\n| **Roles**            | Before, during and after events; activation trigger; resources committed (copied to LOG-F03 in Chapter 6)                     |\n| **Testing**          | Last call-down or exercise and result                                                                                         |\n| **Review**           | Last review date; findings; CAPA references                                                                                   |\n\n> PLATFORM NOTE. MODE''s offices table records partner organisations by kind (national agency, utility, private, CSO, barangay), and response_clusters records lead offices. There is no table for agreements, terms or review dates. Until one is added, the register is kept on PTN-F01 and the agreements are filed in the Vault.\n\n# 14. Standard Operating Procedures\n\nEach SOP has a control block and a step table. Every step states the actor (a position or office, never a person), the action and decision rule, inputs and outputs, and timing, control and escalation. Form codes are those of section 18. All MODE templates named here are proposals; none of SOPs 8-01 to 8-08 is yet loaded in MODE.\n\n## SOP 8-01 \\| Partner register and partnership lifecycle\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Administration and Training Division (register keeper). **Purpose:** Bring every DRRM partnership under a written, authorised, registered and reviewed agreement.\n\n**Table 8.12**\n\n*SOP 8-01 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                                                   |\n|---------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP8-01 (proposed)                                                                                             |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | SP authorises; City Mayor signs; CDRRMC endorses and reviews                                                                |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Administration and Training                                                                                          |\n| **Trigger**                     | A need identified in a contingency plan, AAR or IAT gap; a partner''s offer; an agreement nearing its end; the annual review |\n| **Forms**                       | PTN-F01; PTN-F02                                                                                                            |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.2 (3, Low); B6.6 (1, Medium)                                                                                            |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-02; MR-08; MR-16; MR-18                                                                                                  |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 7160 Secs. 22(c), 34–36; RA 10121 Secs. 12(c), 13                                                                        |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 8-06, 8-07; Chapter 6 SOP 6-12; Chapter 7 SOP 7-09                                                                     |\n\n**Table 8.13**\n\n*SOP 8-01 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Identify</th>\n<th>Administration and Training with the requesting division</th>\n<th><p>State the need, the partner and the value to the City, citing the plan, AAR or IAT gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is a written agreement needed, or is coordination enough?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> CPs; AARs; IAT</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> PTN-F02 proposal</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On need</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Need traced to a source</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Agree terms</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head with the partner</th>\n<th><p>Draft roles before, during and after events, resources, activation trigger, costs, liability, data and review.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Does any term transfer legal authority? If so, rewrite.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> PTN-F02</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Draft agreement</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 30 days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Model outlines in Annexes 8-B and 8-C</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Legal review</th>\n<th>City Legal Office</th>\n<th><p>Review the draft.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Lawful and acceptable?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Legal advice</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 15 days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Advice filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Endorse and authorise</th>\n<th>CDRRMC; SP</th>\n<th><p>CDRRMC endorses by resolution; SP authorises the Mayor to sign.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Authorised?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft; advice</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> CDRRMC and SP resolutions</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Next sessions</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Resolution numbers recorded</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Sign and file</th>\n<th>City Mayor; Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Sign; obtain the partner''s signature; file the signed copy in the Vault.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Signed by both sides? Unsigned copies are not counted.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Authorised draft</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Signed agreement; Vault entry</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 15 days of authority</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Signed copy, not a draft, filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Register</th>\n<th>Register keeper</th>\n<th><p>Enter the agreement on PTN-F01; copy resource commitments to LOG-F03 (Chapter 6).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> All fields complete?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Signed agreement</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> PTN-F01 entry; LOG-F03 entries</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On signing</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> One entry per agreement</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Review and renew</th>\n<th>Register keeper; CDRRMC</th>\n<th><p>Review the register each year; renew, amend or end agreements; flag those within 90 days of expiry.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Still needed and working?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> PTN-F01; test results</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Updated register; CDRRMC minutes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Annually, before the typhoon season</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Lapsed agreements marked</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** PTN-F02 proposals; drafts; legal advice; resolutions; signed agreements; PTN-F01 register; annual review minutes.\n\n## SOP 8-02 \\| Inter-LGU technical mutual aid: agreement, activation and demobilisation\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with the EOC Manager during activation. **Purpose:** Have standing, reciprocal, technical mutual aid with neighbouring LGUs and use it in order when needed.\n\n**Table 8.14**\n\n*SOP 8-02 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                                                        |\n|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP8-02 (proposed) for the agreement; Protocol: PR-MUTUAL-AID (proposed) for activation                             |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | Each sanggunian authorises; each Mayor signs and approves requests; host Incident Commander directs deployed resources           |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Head; EOC Manager                                                                                                         |\n| **Trigger**                     | Agreement: annual plan or a gap found in an AAR. Activation: City capacity exceeded or about to be; a request from a partner LGU |\n| **Forms**                       | PTN-F03; EOC-F06; PTN-F01                                                                                                        |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.1 (1, Medium); C3.2 (1, Low)                                                                                                 |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-02 (A10.1); MR-03, MR-17, MR-18 (C3.2)                                                                                        |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 7160 Secs. 22(c), 33; RA 10121 Sec. 12(c); NDRRMC MC No. 4, s. 2012 (ICS)                                                     |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOP 8-01; Chapter 4 SOPs 4-05, 4-07; Chapter 6 SOP 6-12                                                                          |\n\n**Table 8.15**\n\n*SOP 8-02 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Propose</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Propose a technical mutual aid agreement to the DRRM officers of Kananga, Albuera, Merida, Isabel and the Province, using Annex 8-B.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Interest confirmed by each LGU''s DRRM office?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Annex 8-B</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Proposal and meeting record</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> First quarter 2027</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Positions, not persons, named</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Negotiate and authorise</th>\n<th>DRRM officers of each LGU; each sanggunian</th>\n<th><p>Agree the elements in Table 8.9; each sanggunian authorises its Mayor.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> All parties authorised?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft agreement</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Resolutions of each sanggunian</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Per sessions</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> City Legal Office review (SOP 8-01 step 3)</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Mayors</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Sign and register</th>\n<th>Mayors; register keeper</th>\n<th><p>Sign; register on PTN-F01 and LOG-F03; exchange contact directories.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Signed by all?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Authorised agreement</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Signed agreement; register entries</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On signing</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Vault copy</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Request aid</th>\n<th>EOC Manager; Mayor or authorised officer</th>\n<th><p>Send a written or recorded request on PTN-F03: resource, quantity, location, reporting time, expected duration.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Own and pre-arranged resources exhausted or insufficient?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> EOC-F06</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> PTN-F03 request</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> As soon as the need is foreseen</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Request logged in MODE</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Receive and task</th>\n<th>EOC Logistics and Operations</th>\n<th><p>Receive arriving resources at the staging area; brief on hazards, command and communications; assign to the Incident Commander.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Briefed and checked in?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Arrival</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Check-in record; assignment</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On arrival</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Resources work under the host IC</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Demobilise and settle</th>\n<th>EOC Logistics; Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Release resources with a handover record; settle costs as the agreement states; thank the partner.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Equipment returned in condition? Costs agreed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Assignment records</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Demobilisation record; settlement</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On release; settlement within 60 days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Records filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Review</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head with partner LGUs</th>\n<th><p>Review each activation in the AAR (Chapter 7) and the agreement each year; hold one joint exercise or call-down a year.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Changes needed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> AAR; test results</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Review record; CAPA entries</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Annually and after each activation</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Findings tracked</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Proposal; resolutions; signed agreement; PTN-F01 and LOG-F03 entries; PTN-F03 requests; check-in and demobilisation records; settlements; review records.\n\n## SOP 8-03 \\| Requesting augmentation from the Province, region and national agencies, and receiving incoming teams\n\n**Process owner:** EOC Manager, for the CDRRMC Chair. **Purpose:** Request outside help early, through the right route, and absorb it in order.\n\n**Table 8.16**\n\n*SOP 8-03 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                              |\n|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Protocol: PR-AUGMENT-REQUEST (proposed)                                                                |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | City Mayor as CDRRMC Chair requests; the Province, RDRRMC VIII and national agencies decide on support |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | EOC Manager; EOC Logistics                                                                             |\n| **Trigger**                     | City and mutual aid resources exceeded or about to be; an agency offers assistance                     |\n| **Forms**                       | PTN-F03; EOC-F06; SitRep                                                                               |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A10.1 (1, Medium); A8.5 (3, Low); A10.3 (3, Low)                                                       |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-02; MR-05; MR-07                                                                                    |\n| **Policy basis**                | National Disaster Response Plan; RA 10121; NDRRMC MC No. 4, s. 2012 (ICS); regional SOPs               |\n| **Cross-references**            | Chapter 4 SOPs 4-05, 4-07, 4-08; SOP 8-02                                                              |\n\n**Table 8.17**\n\n*SOP 8-03 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Assess the shortfall</th>\n<th>EOC Planning with Logistics</th>\n<th><p>Compare needs with City stocks, pre-arranged supply and mutual aid.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Shortfall confirmed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> SitRep; inventory; EOC-F06</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Shortfall statement</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Each operational period</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Figures sourced</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Prepare the request</th>\n<th>EOC Manager</th>\n<th><p>State resource, quantity, location, time needed, City resources already committed.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Specific enough to act on?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Shortfall statement</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> PTN-F03 request</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Same period</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Linked to the SitRep</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Approve and send</th>\n<th>City Mayor</th>\n<th><p>Approve and send to the Province (PDRRMO), copied to OCD VIII where national assets are needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Right route?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> PTN-F03</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Sent request; acknowledgement</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Immediately</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Time and recipient logged</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> —</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Track</th>\n<th>EOC Logistics</th>\n<th><p>Track acknowledgement, approval and arrival; follow up.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> No answer within the time set? Follow up and inform the Mayor.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Request log</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Status in MODE resource requests</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Continuous</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Status changes timestamped</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Receive incoming teams</th>\n<th>EOC Logistics and Operations</th>\n<th><p>Receive at staging; check in; brief on hazards, command, communications and protection rules; assign under the IC.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Self-sufficient? If not, arrange food, lodging and fuel.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Arrival</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Check-in records</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On arrival</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Teams work under the IC</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Demobilise</th>\n<th>EOC Logistics</th>\n<th><p>Release with a handover record; report the support received in the SitRep.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> All released resources accounted for?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Assignment records</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Demobilisation record</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> On release</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Reported in the terminal SitRep</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> EOC Manager</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Shortfall statements; PTN-F03 requests and acknowledgements; status records; check-in and demobilisation records; SitRep entries.\n\n## SOP 8-04 \\| Interface agreements and annual interface review with Part C lead offices\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning, with the Part C lead offices. **Purpose:** Give every Part C interface an owner, a plan and a yearly review, and report the Interface Index.\n\n**Table 8.18**\n\n*SOP 8-04 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                                                       |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP8-04 (proposed); MODE Assess module (indicator_lead_offices)                                                    |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | City Administrator approves interface agreements between City offices (or the Mayor by EO); CDRRMC receives the Interface Index |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                                                                    |\n| **Trigger**                     | Adoption of this chapter; change of a lead office; annual review before the IAT cycle                                           |\n| **Forms**                       | PTN-F04; PTN-F05                                                                                                                |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | C1.1 to C5.1 (Interface Index 1.41)                                                                                             |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-17, with MR-03, MR-04, MR-05, MR-16 and MR-18 by row                                                                         |\n| **Policy basis**                | IAT v1.7 Part C; RA 12287 IRR Rule IV; UNDRR Scorecard                                                                          |\n| **Cross-references**            | Section 6; Chapter 7 SOPs 7-02, 7-05                                                                                            |\n\n**Table 8.19**\n\n*SOP 8-04 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Confirm lead offices</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Confirm each Part C lead office and focal person by position; update MODE indicator_lead_offices.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Office accepts the indicator?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> IAT Part C</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Confirmed list</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 60 days of adoption</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Written acceptance</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Agree</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head with each lead office head</th>\n<th><p>Sign PTN-F04: indicators owned, actions for the year, information exchanged, reporting.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Actions match the IAT recommended actions?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Annex 8-A; Annex 8-C</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Signed PTN-F04</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 90 days</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Approved by the City Administrator</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Support</th>\n<th>CDRRMO</th>\n<th><p>Supply hazard maps, scenarios and event data; invite lead offices to PDRAs and exercises.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Requested information supplied?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Chapter 3 and 7 data</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Information log</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Continuous</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Requests answered within 10 working days</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Review</th>\n<th>Research and Planning with lead offices</th>\n<th><p>Hold the annual interface review; each office presents evidence; record findings on PTN-F05.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Evidence attached for each score?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Lead office evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> PTN-F05; IAT Part C responses</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before the IAT cycle</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Lead office scores its own items; a validator from another office validates</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Report</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Report the Interface Index and the High gaps to the CDRRMC; enter gaps in the CAPA register.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> High gaps have an owner and a date?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> PTN-F05</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> CDRRMC report; ME-F05 entries</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> With the IAT results</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Reported separately from the IMI</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Confirmed lead-office list; PTN-F04 agreements; information log; PTN-F05 review; CDRRMC report; CAPA entries.\n\n## SOP 8-05 \\| Restoration-priority and continuity arrangements with utilities\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with LEYECO V, NGCP, telecom providers and water services. **Purpose:** Agree before events which facilities are restored first, how utilities and the EOC exchange status, and what backup each will provide.\n\n**Table 8.20**\n\n*SOP 8-05 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                                                 |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP8-05 (proposed)                                                                                           |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | SP authorises agreements with private utilities; City Mayor signs; each utility decides within its regulatory obligations |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Head; EOC Logistics                                                                                                |\n| **Trigger**                     | Annual review before the typhoon season; change in critical facilities; an AAR finding on restoration                     |\n| **Forms**                       | PTN-F06; PTN-F01                                                                                                          |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | C2.2 (2, Medium); C2.1 (1, High) for the facility list                                                                    |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-03; MR-16; MR-17; MR-18                                                                                                |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 4(c)(ii)–(iv); UNDRR Scorecard Essential 8; RA 10639                                            |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOP 8-01; Chapter 2 section 2.8; Chapter 4                                                                                |\n\n**Table 8.21**\n\n*SOP 8-05 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. List critical facilities</th>\n<th>CDRRMO with CHO, Waterworks, CPDO</th>\n<th><p>List facilities by restoration priority: hospitals and health centres, EOC and alternate EOC, evacuation centres, water pumps, communications sites.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> List agreed by the offices concerned?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Facility data; CDRA</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> PTN-F06 priority list</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> By March</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Locations and account numbers checked</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Agree with each utility</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head with utility managers</th>\n<th><p>Agree priority, contact points, status reporting, standby generation, cell-on-wheels and water tankers.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Within the utility''s regulatory obligations?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> PTN-F06</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Arrangement or MOA</p></th>\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch8v1_v1.0.md (Chapter 8, v1.0 working draft (IAT v1.7))','draft')
